                <h4>Declarative vs Imperative — See the Difference</h4>
                <p>This distinction is the heart of working with Kubernetes (and a recurring exam theme). <strong>Imperative</strong> = you give step-by-step commands ("do this, now"). <strong>Declarative</strong> = you describe the end state in a file and let K8s reconcile it. Same result, very different philosophy:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header">
                            <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                            <span class="code-lang">imperative — fast, one-off</span>
                        </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Tell K8s exactly what to do, step by step</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj \
  --image=easypj:v2 --replicas=3
kubectl scale deployment easypj --replicas=5
kubectl set image deployment/easypj \
  easypj=easypj:v3
<span class="comment"># Great for speed on the exam,</span>
<span class="comment"># but nothing records your intent.</span></code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header">
                            <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                            <span class="code-lang">declarative — the K8s way</span>
                        </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Describe the end state in a file…</span>
<span class="comment"># deploy.yaml:  replicas: 5, image: easypj:v3</span>
kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml
<span class="comment"># Run it again? No harm — already matches.</span>
kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml   <span class="comment"># idempotent</span>
<span class="comment"># The file is the source of truth → Git,</span>
<span class="comment"># code review, and GitOps all work.</span></code></pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Best of both on the exam</h5>
                    <p>Generate YAML <em>imperatively</em>, then apply it <em>declaratively</em>: <code>kubectl create deployment easypj --image=easypj:v2 --dry-run=client -o yaml &gt; d.yaml</code>, tweak, then <code>kubectl apply -f d.yaml</code>. You get imperative speed <em>and</em> a declarative artifact.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>What Kubernetes Actually Gives You — the Official Capability List</h4>
                <p>The docs enumerate exactly what a cluster does for you. You'll build every one of these onto easypj as the guide progresses — so here's the whole checklist up front, mapped to where you'll meet it:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Capability (per kubernetes.io)</th><th>What it means for easypj</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>🔀 <strong>Service discovery &amp; load balancing</strong></td><td>a stable DNS name + VIP spreads traffic across the easypj-web pods</td><td>Part 5</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>💾 <strong>Storage orchestration</strong></td><td>auto-mount local, cloud, or network storage for PostgreSQL</td><td>Part 6</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>🚀 <strong>Automated rollouts &amp; rollbacks</strong></td><td>ship easypj:v2 gradually; revert instantly if it misbehaves</td><td>Ch 13</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>📦 <strong>Automatic bin packing</strong></td><td>pack pods onto nodes by their declared CPU/RAM requests</td><td>Ch 14–15</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>🔄 <strong>Self-healing</strong></td><td>restart failed containers; reschedule pods off dead nodes</td><td>Ch 13, 31</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>🔐 <strong>Secret &amp; config management</strong></td><td>inject DB passwords &amp; settings without rebuilding the image</td><td>Ch 23</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>🧮 <strong>Batch execution</strong></td><td>run migrations &amp; nightly backups as Jobs / CronJobs</td><td>Ch 16</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>📈 <strong>Horizontal scaling</strong></td><td>add/remove easypj replicas by command or on CPU load (HPA)</td><td>Ch 13, 15</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>🌍 <strong>IPv4/IPv6 dual-stack</strong></td><td>pods &amp; Services can receive both an IPv4 and an IPv6 address</td><td>Part 5</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>🧩 <strong>Designed for extensibility</strong></td><td>add features (CRDs/operators) without forking Kubernetes</td><td>Ch 38</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 The flip side — "not a mere orchestrator"</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">The docs make a subtle point: Kubernetes <em>"is not a mere orchestration system. In fact, it eliminates the need for orchestration"</em> — there's no central script sequencing steps; independent controllers each drive their piece toward the desired state and the result <em>emerges</em>. That's the reconciliation model from §1.2, restated.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>What Kubernetes Is <em>NOT</em></h4>
                <p>Understanding the boundaries is as important as understanding the features — it stops you from expecting things K8s doesn't do:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏗️</div>
                        <h5>Not a build tool</h5>
                        <p>It does <strong>not</strong> build container images. You build with Docker/BuildKit/Kaniko in CI; K8s only <em>runs</em> the resulting images.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔁</div>
                        <h5>Not a CI/CD system</h5>
                        <p>It doesn't run your test pipeline. Tools like GitHub Actions, Jenkins, or Argo/Flux sit <em>around</em> K8s to deliver into it.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">☁️</div>
                        <h5>Not a PaaS</h5>
                        <p>Unlike Heroku, it won't "just run my code." It orchestrates <em>containers</em> — you still package the app and declare how it runs.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🗄️</div>
                        <h5>Not a database (and won't manage your data)</h5>
                        <p>etcd stores <em>cluster</em> state, not your app data. Persisting easypj's PostgreSQL data is <em>your</em> job (PersistentVolumes, backups).</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Reconciliation Isn't Abstract — Meet the Controllers Doing It</h4>
                <p>"Control loops reconcile desired vs actual" stays fuzzy until you see that Kubernetes ships <strong>dozens of named controllers</strong>, each owning one kind of object and all running the <em>identical</em> OBSERVE → DIFF → ACT loop inside the <code>kube-controller-manager</code>. Here are the ones you'll depend on constantly:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Controller</th><th>Watches</th><th>Ensures (its reconcile goal)</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Deployment</strong></td><td>Deployment objects</td><td>the right ReplicaSet exists; drives rolling updates &amp; rollbacks</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>ReplicaSet</strong></td><td>ReplicaSets + their Pods</td><td>exactly N pods match the template — recreates any that vanish</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Node</strong> (lifecycle)</td><td>Node heartbeats (Leases)</td><td>marks a silent node <code>NotReady</code>, then evicts its pods after a grace period</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Job / CronJob</strong></td><td>Jobs &amp; schedules</td><td>runs pods to completion / on a cron schedule, with retries</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>EndpointSlice</strong></td><td>Services + Pods</td><td>keeps the list of <em>Ready</em> pod IPs behind each Service current</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Namespace</strong></td><td>Namespaces being deleted</td><td>deletes everything inside first, then removes the namespace (via finalizers)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>ServiceAccount / Token</strong></td><td>ServiceAccounts</td><td>provisions the default SA &amp; its API token in every namespace</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <p>Notice the pattern: none of them takes orders imperatively. Each simply watches its object type and works to close a gap — which is why you manage Kubernetes by <em>editing desired state</em> and letting the matching controller do the rest.</p>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Control plane vs data plane — a distinction that pays off all guide long</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">The <strong>control plane</strong> (apiserver, etcd, scheduler, these controllers) <em>decides</em> what should happen; the <strong>data plane</strong> (your pods on the nodes, plus kube-proxy &amp; the CNI moving their packets) <em>does the actual work</em>. When troubleshooting, first ask <em>which plane</em>: "the app returns errors" is data-plane; "I can't create anything / nothing self-heals" is control-plane. A surprising share of Part 9 resolves to that single question.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Remember the equation</h5>
                    <p><strong>Desired State → [control loops] → Actual State</strong>. Every Kubernetes feature you'll learn — Deployments, self-healing, autoscaling, rollouts — is just a controller closing the gap between what you declared and what's actually running.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §1.3 ─────────── -->
