                <h4>How Scoring Actually Works — and the Time Math That Follows</h4>
                <p>The CKA is scored by <strong>automated grading of the end state</strong>, not by watching how you got there. Each task carries a weight; <strong>partial credit is real</strong> (a task with three checks can earn ⅓ or ⅔). You need <strong>66%</strong> overall. Two consequences shape your entire strategy:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🧮</div>
                        <h5>The point math</h5>
                        <p>~15–20 tasks in 120 minutes ≈ <strong>6–8 min/task</strong>. To clear 66% you can afford to <em>completely miss about ⅓ of the weight</em> — so never sink 25 minutes into one hard task while three quick ones sit undone.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">✅</div>
                        <h5>Only end-state counts</h5>
                        <p>The grader inspects the cluster, not your keystrokes. Imperative or declarative, elegant or ugly — if the object ends up correct, you score. Optimize for <em>a correct result, fast</em>, and always <strong>verify</strong> (<code>kubectl get</code>/<code>describe</code>) before moving on.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                    <h5>⚡ The two-pass strategy this implies</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>Pass 1:</strong> go top to bottom, solve everything you can in a few minutes, <em>flag</em> anything sticky, and bank the guaranteed points. <strong>Pass 2:</strong> return to flagged tasks with the time you have left. A blank task is the only guaranteed zero — always attempt <em>something</em>, because partial credit adds up.</p>
                </div>

