            <section class="section-block" id="s1-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">1.4 Kubernetes Core Concepts — The Big Picture</h3>
                <p>Everything in Kubernetes is built from five ideas. Lock these in now — they're the mental model you'll use for the entire CKA journey.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-5">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🗂️</div>
                        <h5>1 · Cluster</h5>
                        <p>A set of machines (nodes) Kubernetes manages as a single unit.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🧠</div>
                        <h5>2 · Control Plane</h5>
                        <p>The "brain" — makes all scheduling &amp; orchestration decisions.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">💪</div>
                        <h5>3 · Worker Nodes</h5>
                        <p>The "muscle" — actually run your containerized apps.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div>
                        <h5>4 · Pod</h5>
                        <p>Smallest deployable unit — 1+ containers sharing IP &amp; storage.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🎯</div>
                        <h5>5 · Desired State</h5>
                        <p>You declare <em>what</em> you want; K8s continuously keeps it that way.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>The Words You'll Keep Hearing — a Beginner's Glossary</h4>
                <p>Before the diagrams, here's a plain-English decoder for the terms that appear on every page from here on. Bookmark this — you won't feel lost again:</p>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>📖 Core vocabulary</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🐋</span> container</div><div class="anatomy-value">A packaged, isolated process — your app + its dependencies. The thing that actually runs easypj.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🖼️</span> image</div><div class="anatomy-value">The read-only template a container is started from, e.g. <code>easypj:v2</code>. Built once, run anywhere.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📦</span> pod</div><div class="anatomy-value">The smallest thing K8s schedules — one (or a few) containers that share an IP &amp; storage.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💪</span> node</div><div class="anatomy-value">A machine (VM or physical) that runs pods. The cluster is a set of nodes.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🗂️</span> cluster</div><div class="anatomy-value">All your nodes + the control plane, managed as one system.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🏷️</span> namespace</div><div class="anatomy-value">A virtual folder that partitions a cluster (e.g. <code>easypj</code>, <code>kube-system</code>) for isolation &amp; quotas.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📄</span> manifest</div><div class="anatomy-value">A YAML file describing a desired object (a Deployment, Service…). The "what you want."</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🧩</span> object / resource</div><div class="anatomy-value">Any thing K8s tracks in etcd — Pod, Service, ConfigMap, Node. Everything is an object.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔄</span> controller</div><div class="anatomy-value">A loop that watches objects and drives actual → desired (e.g. the Deployment controller).</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">⌨️</span> kubectl</div><div class="anatomy-value">The command-line tool you use to talk to the API server ("koob-cuddle" / "koob-control").</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>How the Objects Nest — the Workload Hierarchy</h4>
                <p>Beginners often blur Pod, ReplicaSet, and Deployment. They're <strong>layers</strong>, each wrapping the one below and adding a capability. You almost always create the <em>outermost</em> one and let it manage the rest:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod → Container (inside a Namespace)</div>
<pre>
  🏷️ Namespace: easypj   (a virtual folder that isolates these objects)
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ 🚀 Deployment  "easypj-web"                       ← you create THIS     │
  │    adds ▶ rolling updates · rollback · revision history                │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │
  │  │ 🎛️ ReplicaSet  "easypj-web-6d8f9"   (one per pod-template version) │ │
  │  │    adds ▶ "keep exactly N pods alive"  → self-healing              │ │
  │  │  ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐   N = 3      │ │
  │  │  │ 📦 Pod         │  │ 📦 Pod         │  │ 📦 Pod         │            │ │
  │  │  │  …-6d8f9-x7k2 │  │  …-6d8f9-m9p4 │  │  …-6d8f9-q2j7 │            │ │
  │  │  │ ┌──────────┐ │  │ ┌──────────┐ │  │ ┌──────────┐ │            │ │
  │  │  │ │🐋 web :8000│ │  │ │🐋 web :8000│ │  │ │🐋 web :8000│ │ ← container│ │
  │  │  │ └──────────┘ │  │ └──────────┘ │  │ └──────────┘ │            │ │
  │  │  └──────────────┘  └──────────────┘  └──────────────┘            │ │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │
  │  🕓 old ReplicaSet "easypj-web-5a1c" → scaled to 0, kept for rollback   │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   naming chain ▶  Deployment ──(+ pod-template-hash)──▶ ReplicaSet ──(+ random)──▶ Pod
   rule of thumb ▶  you create the Deployment; it makes the ReplicaSet; the RS makes the
                    Pods; each Pod runs your container(s). You almost never make a bare Pod.
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Why not just create Pods directly?</h5>
                    <p>A bare Pod is a <em>pet</em> — if it dies, it's gone for good (nothing recreates it). Wrap it in a ReplicaSet and you get self-healing; wrap that in a Deployment and you also get rolling updates &amp; rollback. That's why in real life you create <strong>Deployments</strong>, almost never naked Pods.</p>
                </div>

