                <h4>What "Cloud Native" Actually Means</h4>
                <p>"Cloud native" is the phrase behind the CNCF's name and gets thrown around constantly — but it's rarely defined. It's not "runs in the cloud." It's a <strong>way of building software</strong> so it can be operated by software (like Kubernetes). Five properties define it, and easypj will gain each one as we go:</p>
                <div class="card-grid">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div><h5>Containerized</h5><p>Each piece is packaged with its dependencies into an image — portable and isolated. (easypj → a container in Ch 2.)</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🧩</div><h5>Loosely coupled</h5><p>Independent services talk over the network (web ↔ DB), so each can scale, fail, and deploy on its own.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🤖</div><h5>Dynamically orchestrated</h5><p>A platform schedules, scales, and heals it automatically — no human placing processes on machines.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📝</div><h5>Declarative</h5><p>Desired state lives in version-controlled manifests, not in someone's head or a runbook.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🛡️</div><h5>Resilient &amp; observable</h5><p>It expects failure and recovers; it exposes metrics, logs, and traces so you can see what it's doing.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Why the term matters to you</h5>
                    <p>Every tool in the orbit above exists to deliver one of these five properties. So "cloud native" isn't marketing — it's the <em>checklist</em> the whole ecosystem is built around. When you finish this guide, easypj will satisfy all five, and you'll be the administrator who keeps the platform that makes that possible running.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Three "Sockets" — How Kubernetes Stays Pluggable (CRI · CNI · CSI)</h4>
                <p>Why does such a huge ecosystem exist? Because Kubernetes itself doesn't run containers, wire up networks, or attach disks. Instead it defines three <strong>standard interfaces</strong> — think of them as universal sockets — and lets vendors plug in. This is <em>the</em> reason Kubernetes runs identically on your laptop, on bare metal, and on every cloud.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Kubernetes defines the socket; you choose the plug</div>
<pre>
                       ☸️  KUBERNETES  (defines 3 standard interfaces)
                                       │
        ┌──────────────────────┬───────┴───────────────┬──────────────────────┐
        ▼                      ▼                        ▼
   🔌 CRI                  🔌 CNI                    🔌 CSI
   Container Runtime IF    Container Network IF      Container Storage IF
   kubelet asks:           K8s asks:                 K8s asks:
   "run / stop this        "give this pod            "attach this disk
    container"              an IP &amp; connect it"        to the pod"
        │                      │                         │
   any plug speaking ▼     any plug speaking ▼       any plug speaking ▼
   🐋 containerd · CRI-O   🌐 Calico · Cilium ·       💾 AWS EBS · GCP PD ·
                             Flannel                    Ceph · NFS
        │                      │                         │
        ▼                      ▼                         ▼
   starts the easypj       easypj pod 10.244.1.5     postgres data lives on a
   container on the node   reaches any pod, no NAT   real disk, survives restarts

   💡 Swap any plug (e.g. containerd → CRI-O) and Kubernetes ITSELF doesn't change.
      THAT is why one set of easypj manifests runs on a laptop, bare metal &amp; every cloud.
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="card-grid">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🐋</div>
                        <h5>CRI — Runtime</h5>
                        <p>How the kubelet asks something to <strong>run a container</strong>. Default: <strong>containerd</strong>. (You'll configure this in Chapter 9 when installing the cluster.)</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🌐</div>
                        <h5>CNI — Network</h5>
                        <p>How pods get an <strong>IP and reach each other</strong>. You install a CNI (Calico/Cilium) right after <code>kubeadm init</code> — without one, nodes stay NotReady (Chapter 17).</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">💾</div>
                        <h5>CSI — Storage</h5>
                        <p>How a <strong>real disk</strong> gets attached to a pod for durable data — exactly how easypj's PostgreSQL keeps its files (Chapter 22).</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <h4>How the Three Sockets Are Actually Wired — the Files an Admin Touches</h4>
                <p>The socket model is elegant in theory; as an <em>admin</em> you also need the concrete plumbing, because when one socket is misconfigured the cluster breaks in a very specific, recognizable way. Here is where each plug physically lives and how the kubelet reaches it:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Socket</th><th>Who calls it &amp; how</th><th>Where it lives on the node</th><th>Break it and…</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>🐋 <strong>CRI</strong></td><td>the <strong>kubelet</strong> calls the runtime over a local gRPC <strong>Unix socket</strong></td><td><code>/run/containerd/containerd.sock</code> (or <code>crio.sock</code>); debug with <code>crictl</code>, not <code>docker</code></td><td>runtime down → node <code>NotReady</code>, pods can't start</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>🌐 <strong>CNI</strong></td><td>the kubelet execs a CNI <strong>binary</strong> when a pod sandbox is created</td><td>plugin binaries in <code>/opt/cni/bin/</code>; config in <code>/etc/cni/net.d/*.conf</code></td><td>no/broken CNI → nodes <code>NotReady</code>, pods stuck <code>ContainerCreating</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>💾 <strong>CSI</strong></td><td>a <strong>controller</strong> plugin provisions/attaches; a <strong>node</strong> plugin mounts</td><td>runs as pods (a Deployment + a DaemonSet); registers under <code>/var/lib/kubelet/plugins/</code></td><td>driver missing → PVCs stay <code>Pending</code>, pods can't mount storage</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">One pod starting — all three sockets fire, in sequence</div>
<pre>
   🤖 kubelet is told "run easypj-web on this node"
        │
        │ ①  CRI  → gRPC to containerd.sock:  RunPodSandbox   (creates the 🫙 pause container)
        ▼
        │ ②  CNI  → exec /opt/cni/bin/&lt;plugin&gt; using /etc/cni/net.d config:
        │            "give this sandbox an IP" → 10.244.1.5, wire it into the pod network
        ▼
        │ ③  CRI  → PullImage easypj:v2 → CreateContainer → StartContainer
        ▼
        │ ④  CSI  (only if the pod has a PVC) → node plugin mounts the volume into the container
        ▼
   📦 easypj-web RUNNING :8000 — on the pod network, with its storage attached
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Why this ordering is a troubleshooting shortcut</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">The failure <em>symptom</em> tells you which socket broke: a <code>ContainerCreating</code> that never finishes is almost always <strong>CNI</strong> (no IP); <code>Pending</code> tied to a PVC is <strong>CSI</strong> (no volume); a node stuck <code>NotReady</code> is <strong>CRI</strong> (runtime) or CNI. You'll configure CRI in Ch 9, CNI in Ch 17, and CSI in Ch 22 — but this map is worth carrying now.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 The big idea</h5>
                    <p>Kubernetes is a <strong>platform for building platforms</strong>: it owns the orchestration logic and delegates the "hardware-touching" parts to pluggable drivers. Whenever you see "install a CNI" or "pick a StorageClass," you're choosing a plug for one of these sockets. Keep this model in your head — it makes Parts 5 (networking) and 6 (storage) click instantly.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

