                <h4>A Day in the Life of a CKA Administrator</h4>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Morning Check</h5>
                        <p>Review dashboards; check node health, pod restarts, and overnight alerts.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Troubleshoot</h5>
                        <p>Investigate failed pods/nodes: <code>describe</code>, <code>logs</code>, <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Ship Changes</h5>
                        <p>Apply upgrades/RBAC/config via GitOps or PRs — staging first, then prod.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Secure &amp; Audit</h5>
                        <p>Review audit logs, rotate certificates that are due, verify NetworkPolicies, and check RBAC for over-permissioned accounts.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>Maintain</h5>
                        <p>Drain → patch → uncordon a node; verify the latest etcd backup restores.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">6</div>
                        <h5>Capacity Plan</h5>
                        <p>Watch resource trends; add nodes or right-size requests/limits.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Real Companies Running Kubernetes at Scale</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🎵</div><h5>Spotify</h5><p>Migrated 150+ services to K8s; deployments dropped from hours to minutes.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🛒</div><h5>Shopify</h5><p>Survives Black Friday by auto-scaling clusters to ~10× normal load.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🎮</div><h5>Pokémon GO</h5><p>Launched on GKE; scaled from 1× to ~50× expected traffic in days.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🏦</div><h5>Capital One</h5><p>Runs regulated banking workloads on K8s — proof it works under compliance.</p></div>
                </div>

                <h4>Why Organizations Actually Adopt Kubernetes (and What It Costs)</h4>
                <p>Spotify and Capital One didn't adopt Kubernetes for the logo — they adopted it because it pays back three concrete dividends. Understanding these tells you <em>when</em> K8s is the right call (and, honestly, when it isn't):</p>
                <div class="card-grid">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🚀</div>
                        <h5>1 · Velocity</h5>
                        <p>Ship more often, safely. Rolling updates + instant rollback + a declarative pipeline turn risky, hours-long deploys into routine, minutes-long ones — so teams release many times a day instead of monthly.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🛡️</div>
                        <h5>2 · Resilience</h5>
                        <p>Self-healing + rescheduling + HA mean a crashed pod or a dead node is a non-event, not a 3 AM page. Uptime stops depending on a human being awake.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">💰</div>
                        <h5>3 · Efficiency</h5>
                        <p>Bin-packing many containers onto shared nodes (and autoscaling them) drives up utilization and drives down the cloud bill — versus one fat VM per app sitting mostly idle.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p>There's a fourth, quieter dividend: <strong>portability</strong>. Because workloads target the Kubernetes API (not a specific cloud), the same easypj manifests run on your laptop, on bare metal, and on any cloud — which is real leverage against lock-in.</p>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚖️ The honest trade-off — it isn't free</h5>
                    <p>Those dividends are bought with <strong>operational complexity</strong>: a control plane to run (or pay a cloud to run), certificates that expire, upgrades to perform, networking and storage to wire up, and a real learning curve. That complexity <em>is the administrator's job</em> — which is precisely why the CKA exists and why this skill is valuable. The rule of thumb: Kubernetes pays off when you have <em>multiple services, real scale/uptime needs, and frequent releases</em>; for a single low-traffic app, it's usually more machine than the job requires (see the "when NOT to use it" box below).</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Managed vs Self-Managed — Who Runs the Control Plane?</h4>
                <p>There are two ways to get a cluster, and the difference decides what's <em>your</em> job. The CKA deliberately tests the <strong>self-managed</strong> path (you run everything), because once you understand that, managed clusters are easy.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Aspect</th><th>🛠️ Self-managed (kubeadm) — what the CKA tests</th><th>☁️ Managed (EKS / GKE / AKS)</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Control plane</strong></td><td>You install &amp; run it (API server, etcd, scheduler…)</td><td>The cloud runs &amp; upgrades it for you</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>etcd backups</strong></td><td>Your responsibility (Chapter 29)</td><td>Handled by the provider</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Upgrades</strong></td><td>You run <code>kubeadm upgrade</code> (Chapter 28)</td><td>Click/CLI; you still upgrade node pools</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Nodes</strong></td><td>You join &amp; maintain them</td><td>Managed node pools / autoscaling</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>You still own</strong></td><td colspan="2" class="tac">Workloads · RBAC · NetworkPolicy · storage · ingress — <strong>everything in this guide transfers to both</strong></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Why learn the hard (self-managed) way?</h5>
                    <p>Because it makes you <em>unbreakable</em>. When a managed cluster misbehaves, the engineers who understand etcd, certificates, and the kubelet are the ones who fix it. Chapter 36 maps every skill here onto EKS/GKE/AKS — same concepts, the provider just runs the control plane.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚫 Honest Take: When NOT to Use Kubernetes</h5>
                    <p>Maturity means knowing the limits. Kubernetes is <em>overkill</em> when:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>A single small app, low traffic</strong> — a managed PaaS (Heroku, Render, Fly.io) or one VM is simpler and cheaper.</li>
                        <li><strong>No one to operate it</strong> — K8s adds real operational complexity; without an admin (you!) it becomes a liability.</li>
                        <li><strong>Static workloads that never scale</strong> — if you'll never need scaling, healing, or rolling updates, you're paying complexity for nothing.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p class="mb-0">Use it when you have <strong>multiple services</strong>, need <strong>resilience &amp; scale</strong>, ship <strong>frequently</strong>, or want a <strong>portable</strong> platform across clouds — which is exactly where easypj is headed.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>What "Operating" a Cluster Actually Means — the Day-2 Task Matrix</h4>
                <p>Installing a cluster is <em>Day 1</em>; keeping it healthy for years is <em>Day 2</em> — and Day-2 work is the bulk of both the job and the exam. Here is the recurring task set: how often each comes up, how risky it is when botched, and where this guide drills it — so "administration" becomes a concrete checklist rather than a vague word:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Day-2 task</th><th>How often</th><th>Risk if botched</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Watch health — nodes, pods, events, <code>kubectl top</code></td><td>daily</td><td>low (read-only)</td><td>Ch 32–33</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Deploy / roll out / roll back workloads</td><td>daily–weekly</td><td>medium (user-facing)</td><td>Ch 13</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Manage access — RBAC, ServiceAccounts</td><td>weekly</td><td>high (security)</td><td>Ch 24–25</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Drain → patch → uncordon a node (OS/kernel)</td><td>monthly</td><td>medium (needs PodDisruptionBudgets)</td><td>Ch 30</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Back up etcd &amp; <em>test</em> the restore</td><td>daily backup / periodic test</td><td>🔴 critical (the whole cluster)</td><td>Ch 29</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Upgrade Kubernetes, one minor at a time</td><td>~every few months</td><td>high (control plane)</td><td>Ch 28</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Rotate / renew certificates before expiry</td><td>yearly (or on alert)</td><td>🔴 critical (API lockout)</td><td>Ch 26</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Capacity plan — add nodes, right-size requests/limits</td><td>ongoing</td><td>medium (cost / evictions)</td><td>Ch 15</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Incident response — diagnose &amp; fix breakage</td><td>whenever it breaks</td><td>varies</td><td>Ch 31–34</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ The two tasks that can lose the entire cluster</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">Notice the two 🔴 rows — <strong>etcd backups</strong> and <strong>certificate renewal</strong>. An un-tested backup and a silently-expired cert are the two classic ways admins lose (or lock themselves out of) a cluster, which is exactly why the CKA weights them heavily and this guide gives each a full chapter. Most other mistakes you can recover from; these two you must plan <em>ahead</em> of.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Where you fit in — the easypj journey</h5>
                    <p>This guide follows <strong>easypj</strong> — our Django <code>jobpost</code> board — from a single-server app to a production-grade cluster. You'll containerize it, deploy it, scale it, secure it, break it, fix it, and ship it. By the end you'll pass the CKA <em>and</em> be able to run real clusters.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 1.7 OBJECT MODEL & API — official-docs enrichment ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s1-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">1.7 The Object Model &amp; the Kubernetes API</h3>
                <p>Everything you will ever create is a <strong>Kubernetes object</strong> — a persisted record of intent the cluster works to make real. Four ideas make the whole API click: the <em>shape</em> of every object, how you <em>name</em> it, how the API is <em>organised &amp; versioned</em>, and the <em>ways you apply</em> it. Master these and manifests stop being magic.</p>

                <h4>① The four required fields — in every manifest, no exceptions</h4>
                <p>Every object you write carries the same four top-level fields. Omit one — or mis-case <code>kind</code> — and the apiserver rejects the manifest before it ever reaches etcd.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What it is</th><th>easypj example</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>apiVersion</code></td><td>Which API group + version defines this object (so the server knows its schema)</td><td><code>apps/v1</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kind</code></td><td>The object type — what you're creating</td><td><code>Deployment</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>metadata</code></td><td>Identity: <code>name</code> (required), <code>namespace</code>, <code>labels</code>, <code>annotations</code>, and the server-set <code>uid</code></td><td><code>name: easypj-web</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>spec</code></td><td>Your <strong>desired state</strong> — the shape varies per <code>kind</code></td><td><code>replicas: 3</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>② <code>spec</code> vs <code>status</code> — desired vs actual, inside the object itself</h4>
                <p>Almost every object has two halves: a <code>spec</code> you write (the goal) and a <code>status</code> the cluster writes (reality). The reconciliation loop from §1.4 is just "keep <code>status</code> matching <code>spec</code>." You never edit <code>status</code> — <em>reading</em> it is how you troubleshoot.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">kubectl get deploy easypj-web -o yaml (trimmed)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:                 <span class="comment"># 👈 YOU declare this — desired state</span>
  replicas: 3
status:               <span class="comment"># 👈 KUBERNETES writes this — actual state (read-only)</span>
  replicas: 3
  readyReplicas: 2    <span class="comment"># only 2 are Ready right now → a controller is closing the gap</span>
  conditions:
  - type: Available
    status: "False"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>③ Naming rules — why <code>easypj_web</code> is rejected</h4>
                <p>Object names aren't free-form. <strong>Most</strong> objects — Pods, Deployments, ConfigMaps, and even Nodes — follow the <strong>DNS Subdomain</strong> rule; only a few — <strong>namespaces</strong> (and <strong>Services</strong>, under the related RFC 1035) — must be a stricter <strong>DNS Label</strong>. Break the rule and you get a validation error, not a running object.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Used by</th><th>Max</th><th>Allowed</th><th>✅ valid · ❌ invalid</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>DNS Subdomain</strong></td><td>most objects: Deployments, ConfigMaps, Secrets…</td><td>253</td><td>lowercase <code>a–z 0–9 - .</code>; start/end alphanumeric (may start with a digit)</td><td>✅ <code>easypj-web</code>, <code>app.prod</code> · ❌ <code>easypj_web</code>, <code>EasyPJ</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>DNS Label</strong></td><td>namespaces (RFC 1123 label)</td><td>63</td><td>lowercase <code>a–z 0–9 -</code> (<strong>no dots</strong>); <strong>start with a letter</strong>, end alphanumeric</td><td>✅ <code>easypj-prod</code> · ❌ <code>my.ns</code>, <code>NS-1</code>, <code>9ns</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>DNS Label (RFC 1035)</strong></td><td>Service names</td><td>63</td><td>same, but <strong>must start with a letter</strong> (a feature gate relaxes this)</td><td>✅ <code>easypj-web</code> · ❌ <code>9web</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>UID</strong></td><td>every object (set by the server)</td><td>—</td><td>cluster-unique, auto-generated, never user-supplied</td><td>tells apart two objects that reused a name over time</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>④ API groups, versions &amp; stability levels</h4>
                <p>The API is split into <strong>groups</strong>, each <strong>versioned independently</strong>. The <code>apiVersion</code> you write picks the group + version; the stability ladder tells you what's safe to depend on.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">how apiVersion resolves to an API path</div>
<pre>
   apiVersion: v1          → CORE group    → /api/v1           Pod · Service · ConfigMap · Namespace · Node
   apiVersion: apps/v1     → "apps" group  → /apis/apps/v1     Deployment · ReplicaSet · StatefulSet · DaemonSet
   apiVersion: batch/v1    → "batch" group → /apis/batch/v1    Job · CronJob
   apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1                    Role · RoleBinding · ClusterRole
   └─ kubectl api-resources   lists every kind → its group, version &amp; whether it's namespaced
</pre>
                </div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Means</th><th>On by default?</th><th>In a name</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Alpha</strong></td><td>experimental — may change or vanish, may have bugs</td><td>no (needs a feature gate)</td><td><code>v1alpha1</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Beta</strong></td><td>well-tested; details may still change before GA</td><td>varies (newer betas off)</td><td><code>v1beta1</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Stable / GA</strong></td><td>production-ready, backward-compatible</td><td>yes</td><td><code>v1</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box note mb-0"><p class="mb-0"><strong>Deprecation policy:</strong> a <strong>GA</strong> (stable) API version is <strong>never removed within a major version</strong> — a <code>v1</code> API lasts for all of Kubernetes 1.x; a deprecated <strong>beta</strong> version is dropped after <strong>9 months or 3 releases</strong> (whichever is longer); <strong>alpha</strong> can change or vanish in any release. There's <strong>no LTS</strong>: each minor release is maintained ~14 months (≈12 months support + a ~2-month maintenance window) — read the release notes and upgrade before EOL.</p></div>

                <h4>⑤ Three ways to manage objects — and why the exam loves <code>apply</code></h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Command</th><th>Idempotent?</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Imperative command</td><td><code>kubectl create deploy easypj-web --image=easypj:v2</code></td><td>no — errors if it exists</td><td>exam speed &amp; quick fixes; keeps no file</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Imperative object config</td><td><code>kubectl create -f f.yaml</code> / <code>replace -f</code></td><td>no</td><td>rarely — forces delete-then-recreate</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Declarative</strong></td><td><code>kubectl apply -f f.yaml</code></td><td><strong>yes</strong> — run 100×, same result</td><td>production &amp; GitOps; the default to reach for</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box tip"><p class="mb-0">💡 <strong>Server-Side Apply</strong> (<code>kubectl apply --server-side</code>) moves the merge into the apiserver and tracks <em>field ownership</em>, so several controllers can co-own one object without clobbering each other. ⚡ <strong>Exam speed:</strong> <code>kubectl create deploy … --dry-run=client -o yaml &gt; d.yaml</code> scaffolds a manifest you then edit &amp; <code>apply</code>.</p></div>

                <h4>⑥ Add-ons — what a fresh cluster still needs</h4>
                <p>A brand-new cluster can schedule pods but is missing pieces you'll assume are there. These ship as <strong>add-ons</strong> (themselves pods), not part of the core binaries.</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><h5>🧭 CoreDNS</h5><p>In-cluster DNS — every pod resolves <code>name.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code> here. Normally installed; no DNS → no name-based service discovery.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>📊 metrics-server</h5><p>Feeds CPU/memory to the API. <strong>Required</strong> for <code>kubectl top</code> and the HorizontalPodAutoscaler. Not installed by default.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>🌍 Ingress controller</h5><p>Terminates &amp; routes external HTTP(S) to Services (e.g. ingress-nginx). You must pick &amp; install one — none ships by default.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>🖥️ Dashboard</h5><p>Optional web UI. Handy, never required for admin work — and one more thing to secure if you deploy it.</p></div>
                </div>

                <h4>⑦ Labels, selectors, annotations &amp; ownership — the glue that wires objects together</h4>
                <p>The four required fields give an object its identity; three more <code>metadata</code> ideas are how objects <em>find and own each other</em>. These aren't cosmetic — they're the wiring behind Services, controllers, and cascading deletes, and confusing them is the single most common cause of "my Service has no endpoints."</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>What it is</th><th>Kubernetes uses it to…</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>labels</strong></td><td>key/value tags <em>meant for selection</em> (<code>app: easypj</code>, <code>tier: web</code>)</td><td>let selectors find groups of objects — the basis of all wiring</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>selectors</strong></td><td>a query over labels (<code>app=easypj</code>)</td><td>connect a Service to its Pods, a Deployment to its Pods, a NetworkPolicy to its targets</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>annotations</strong></td><td>key/value metadata <em>not</em> for selection (URLs, checksums, tool config)</td><td>carry non-identifying info tools read, e.g. <code>kubectl.kubernetes.io/last-applied-configuration</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>ownerReferences</strong></td><td>a pointer from child → parent (Pod → ReplicaSet → Deployment)</td><td>drive <strong>cascading deletion</strong> &amp; adoption of orphans</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Labels wire a Service to Pods; ownerReferences chain the delete</div>
<pre>
   WIRING (by label selector)                      OWNERSHIP (by ownerReferences)
   ┌────────────────────────────┐                   Deployment easypj-web
   │ Service easypj-web           │                        │ owns
   │   selector:  app=easypj      │                        ▼
   └──────────────┬─────────────┘                    ReplicaSet easypj-web-6d8f9
                  │ matches the label                       │ owns
        ┌─────────┼─────────┐                               ▼
        ▼         ▼         ▼                        📦 Pod   📦 Pod   📦 Pod
   📦 app=easypj 📦 app=easypj 📦 app=easypj          delete the Deployment ⇒ the whole
   (Ready pods → EndpointSlice → traffic)            chain is garbage-collected ⬇ (cascade)

   ⚠️ if the pod labels ≠ the Service selector → 0 endpoints → the Service "works" but nothing answers
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Finalizers — why a namespace sometimes hangs in "Terminating"</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">A <strong>finalizer</strong> is a key in <code>metadata.finalizers</code> that tells the API server "run cleanup <em>before</em> you actually delete me." A delete sets <code>deletionTimestamp</code> and then <em>waits</em> until the responsible controller removes its finalizer. It's the mechanism behind orderly teardown — and the reason a stuck controller can leave a namespace in <code>Terminating</code> indefinitely (you'll diagnose exactly that in Ch 32).</p>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note mb-0">
                    <h5>📖 The takeaway that saves you hours later</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>Labels are load-bearing.</strong> Services, Deployments, NetworkPolicies, and DaemonSets all target pods <em>by label selector</em>, so a one-character label typo silently breaks the wiring with <em>no error</em> — the object looks healthy, it just selects nothing. Whenever something is mysteriously unreachable, an early check is <code>kubectl get pods --show-labels</code> against the selector.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Label &amp; selector rules worth knowing (they bite on the exam)</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li>A label <strong>value</strong> is ≤63 chars; the <strong>key</strong> may carry an optional DNS-subdomain <strong>prefix</strong> (≤253 chars) before a <code>/</code>.</li>
                        <li>The <code>kubernetes.io/</code> and <code>k8s.io/</code> prefixes are <strong>reserved</strong> for Kubernetes core components — don't invent your own keys under them.</li>
                        <li>Selectors support equality (<code>=</code>, <code>!=</code>) and set-based (<code>in</code>, <code>notin</code>, <code>exists</code>) conditions; multiple conditions are AND-ed by commas. There is <strong>no OR</strong> operator.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h4>Where objects live — the four built-in namespaces</h4>
                <p>Every namespaced object lives in exactly one <strong>namespace</strong> (a virtual cluster for isolation &amp; quotas). A fresh cluster ships with four you must recognize:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Namespace</th><th>What's in it</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>default</code></td><td>where your objects land if you don't specify one (easypj should get its <em>own</em> namespace instead)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kube-system</code></td><td>the control plane &amp; add-ons — CoreDNS, kube-proxy, the static-pod mirrors, Leases</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kube-public</code></td><td>world-readable (even to unauthenticated clients) cluster info; rarely used directly</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kube-node-lease</code></td><td>one <code>Lease</code> per node for the fast node heartbeats (see Under the Hood ⑥)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box note mb-0">
                    <p class="mb-0">📖 Namespaces can't be nested, and a resource lives in only one. The control plane stamps every namespace with an immutable <code>kubernetes.io/metadata.name</code> label (equal to its own name), so you can select namespaces by name. Set your working namespace once with <code>kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=easypj</code> — full depth in Chapter 4.</p>
                </div>
