            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 2 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the mental model — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">State two concrete differences between a VM and a container.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>(1) A VM runs a <strong>full guest OS</strong> on a hypervisor (gigabytes, boots in minutes); a container <strong>shares the host kernel</strong> (megabytes, starts in milliseconds). (2) VM isolation is hardware-level/strong; container isolation is process-level via namespaces + cgroups.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th></th><th>🖥️ Virtual Machine</th><th>📦 Container</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Kernel</td><td>its own full guest OS on a hypervisor</td><td>shares the host kernel</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Size · start</td><td>GBs · boots in minutes</td><td>MBs · starts in milliseconds</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Isolation</td><td>hardware-level (strong)</td><td>process-level — namespaces + cgroups</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The shared kernel is the root cause of every "light &amp; fast" property — and of the weaker isolation that later security chapters harden.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod is <code>ImagePullBackOff</code>. Give three likely causes and how to confirm.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Likely: (1) wrong image name/tag, (2) private registry with no <code>imagePullSecrets</code>, (3) registry unreachable / rate-limited. Confirm with <code>kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt;</code> and read the <strong>Events</strong> — it states the exact pull error.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Likely cause</th><th>Event says…</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>wrong image name/tag</td><td><code>not found</code> / <code>manifest unknown</code></td><td>correct the image reference</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>private registry, no creds</td><td><code>unauthorized</code></td><td>add an <code>imagePullSecrets</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>registry unreachable / throttled</td><td><code>timeout</code> / <code>429</code></td><td>fix networking · mirror the image</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Events is always the first stop. "unauthorized" → credentials; "not found" → name/tag typo; "timeout" → network/registry.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why does a multi-stage build produce a smaller, safer image?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The <strong>builder</strong> stage holds compilers and <code>-dev</code> headers; the <strong>runtime</strong> stage copies only the installed result and discards the builder. Less in the final image = smaller size, faster pulls, and a smaller attack surface / fewer CVEs.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">what ships vs what's thrown away</div>
<pre>
  STAGE 1 builder (~900MB)                  STAGE 2 runtime (~150MB)  ← the ONLY stage shipped
  ┌───────────────────────────┐   COPY      ┌───────────────────────────┐
  │ gcc · make · -dev headers │  --from=    │ python:3.13-slim base     │
  │ pip caches · build deps   │  builder    │ /install (the packages)   │
  │ /install  ────────────────┼──────────▶  │ easypj source · USER app  │
  └───────────── DISCARDED ✂️ ┘             └───────────── SHIPPED ✅ ──┘
</pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>easypj's image drops from ~900 MB to ~150 MB this way, and nothing exploitable (gcc, build deps) ships to production.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question"><code>kubectl</code> is unavailable on a node. How do you list and log the containers?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Use <strong><code>crictl</code></strong>, which talks to the CRI directly:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">on the node — talk to the CRI directly</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">crictl ps                  <span class="comment"># list running containers</span>
crictl logs &lt;id&gt;           <span class="comment"># view a container's logs</span>
crictl inspect &lt;id&gt;        <span class="comment"># full details: mounts, env, state</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>When the apiserver/kubelet is down, the containers still run — crictl is the node-level lifeline (Ch 32).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the CRI, and what replaced dockershim in v1.24?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The <strong>Container Runtime Interface</strong> is the standard gRPC API the kubelet uses to drive a runtime. v1.24 removed the <code>dockershim</code> translation layer; nodes now run containers via a CRI-native runtime — <strong>containerd</strong> (default) or <strong>CRI-O</strong>.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">before vs after v1.24 — Docker leaves the data path</div>
<pre>
  BEFORE v1.24                          AFTER v1.24
  🤖 kubelet                            🤖 kubelet
     │ dockershim (translation)            │ CRI (native gRPC)
     ▼                                     ▼
  🐳 Docker daemon                      🐋 containerd / CRI-O ──▶ 📦 containers
     │
     ▼                                  (one hop fewer; same OCI images run unchanged)
  containerd ──▶ 📦 containers
</pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Docker-built images still work (they're OCI) — only the node-side runtime path changed.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Reorder a Dockerfile so <code>pip install</code> stays cached across code edits.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Copy the deps list and install <em>before</em> copying the source, so editing code only busts the last layer:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">Dockerfile — deps before source</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-docker">COPY requirements.txt .
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt   <span class="comment"># cached unless deps change</span>
COPY . .                                             <span class="comment"># only THIS layer busts on a code edit</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Layers cache top-down by content; put what changes <em>least</em> (deps) low, what changes <em>most</em> (your code) high.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name three ways to shrink the easypj image.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>(1) Multi-stage build (drop build tools); (2) a slim/distroless base (<code>python:3.13-slim</code> not full <code>python</code>); (3) a <code>.dockerignore</code> to exclude <code>.git</code>, caches, venvs. (Bonus: combine <code>RUN</code> layers, <code>--no-cache-dir</code> for pip.)</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Technique</th><th>What it removes</th><th>Typical saving</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>multi-stage build</td><td>compilers, headers, pip caches (the whole builder)</td><td>hundreds of MB</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>slim / distroless base</td><td>the OS packages a full base drags in</td><td>~800MB → ~120MB base</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>.dockerignore</code></td><td><code>.git</code>, <code>venv/</code>, <code>db.sqlite3</code>, <code>.env</code> from the build context</td><td>MBs + keeps secrets out</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>pip --no-cache-dir</code> / merged <code>RUN</code>s</td><td>package caches; dead intermediate layers</td><td>tens of MB</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Smaller images pull faster (quicker pod starts/scaling) and expose fewer packages to scan/exploit.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why run as non-root, and how do you do it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>If an attacker escapes a root container, they're root on the node. Declare a user in the image <em>and</em> enforce it in the pod:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">image (Dockerfile) + pod (securityContext)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># Dockerfile
RUN useradd app
USER app
---
# Pod spec
securityContext:
  runAsNonRoot: true
  runAsUser: 1000</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Defense in depth — the image declares intent; the cluster enforces it cluster-wide via Pod Security Standards, so a bad image can't slip through (Ch 27).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why is <code>:latest</code> dangerous, and what should you use instead?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>:latest</code> is a moving pointer — two nodes can run different builds, and it defaults <code>imagePullPolicy</code> to <code>Always</code>. Use <strong>pinned tags</strong> (<code>easypj:v1</code>) or, best, an immutable <strong>digest</strong> (<code>easypj@sha256:…</code>).</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Reference</th><th>Mutable?</th><th>Default pull policy</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>easypj:latest</code> (or no tag)</td><td>✏️ yes — re-pushable</td><td><code>Always</code></td><td>❌ non-reproducible; surprise pulls</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>easypj:v1</code></td><td>✏️ technically yes</td><td><code>IfNotPresent</code></td><td>🟡 fine if your registry forbids re-push</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>easypj@sha256:…</code></td><td>🔒 no — content-addressed</td><td><code>IfNotPresent</code></td><td>✅ exact bytes, tamper-evident — pin prod</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Reproducibility &amp; rollback depend on knowing <em>exactly</em> which bytes ran. Digests are tamper-evident too.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A node's kubelet won't start, citing cgroups. What's the fix?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The runtime and kubelet disagree on the cgroup driver. Set the runtime to <code>systemd</code>, then restart both:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">/etc/containerd/config.toml → systemd driver</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">[plugins."io.containerd.grpc.v1.cri".containerd.runtimes.runc.options]
  SystemdCgroup = true

sudo systemctl restart containerd kubelet    <span class="comment"># node should go Ready</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>On systemd hosts both must use the <code>systemd</code> driver; a mismatch leaves the node NotReady (Ch 9).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch2-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 2 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Exam-style and hands-on — a broken state, and you produce the fix with real commands.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod is stuck in <code>ImagePullBackOff</code>. You run <code>kubectl describe pod easypj-web</code> and see the event: <em>"Failed to pull image 'myregistry.io/app:v2': rpc error: code = Unknown desc = Error response from daemon: unauthorized"</em>. What is the most likely cause and how do you fix it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The image is stored in a <strong>private registry</strong> and the pod doesn't have credentials configured. Fix by creating an <code>imagePullSecret</code> and referencing it in the pod spec:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">fix the pull auth</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create the secret with registry credentials</span>
kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred \
  --docker-server=myregistry.io \
  --docker-username=myuser \
  --docker-password=mypass

<span class="comment"># Add to pod spec under spec.imagePullSecrets:</span>
<span class="comment"># imagePullSecrets:</span>
<span class="comment">#   - name: regcred</span></code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>ImagePullBackOff</code> means the kubelet failed to pull the container image. The "unauthorized" error specifically indicates authentication failure. Common causes: (1) Private registry without <code>imagePullSecrets</code>, (2) Expired/wrong credentials, (3) Wrong registry URL. On the CKA exam, always check the <strong>Events</strong> section of <code>kubectl describe pod</code> — it tells you exactly why the pull failed. Other ImagePullBackOff causes include typos in the image name or tag not existing.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between <code>crictl</code> and <code>docker</code> commands? On a Kubernetes node running containerd, which tool should you use to list running containers and why?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Use <strong><code>crictl</code></strong>. It communicates directly with the CRI (Container Runtime Interface) and works with any CRI-compliant runtime (containerd, CRI-O).</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th>Task</th><th>crictl Command</th><th>Docker Equivalent</th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>List containers</td><td><code>crictl ps</code></td><td><code>docker ps</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>List images</td><td><code>crictl images</code></td><td><code>docker images</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>View logs</td><td><code>crictl logs &lt;id&gt;</code></td><td><code>docker logs &lt;id&gt;</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Inspect container</td><td><code>crictl inspect &lt;id&gt;</code></td><td><code>docker inspect &lt;id&gt;</code></td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Since Kubernetes v1.24 removed <code>dockershim</code>, Docker is no longer a supported runtime. Most clusters now use <code>containerd</code> directly. The <code>crictl</code> tool is the standard debugging tool for CRI runtimes. On the CKA exam, if you SSH to a node and need to inspect containers at the runtime level, <code>crictl</code> is the expected tool. Configure it via <code>/etc/crictl.yaml</code> to point at the runtime socket (e.g., <code>unix:///run/containerd/containerd.sock</code>).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Explain the difference between the three <code>imagePullPolicy</code> values: <code>Always</code>, <code>IfNotPresent</code>, and <code>Never</code>. What is the default policy when you use the <code>:latest</code> tag versus a specific tag like <code>:v1.2</code>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong><code>Always</code></strong> — kubelet always contacts the registry to check for newer image. Re-pulls if digest changed.</li>
                            <li><strong><code>IfNotPresent</code></strong> — kubelet uses the local cached image if available; pulls only if not cached.</li>
                            <li><strong><code>Never</code></strong> — kubelet never pulls; uses local image only. Fails if image isn't cached.</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p><strong>Defaults:</strong></p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><code>:latest</code> tag → defaults to <code>Always</code></li>
                            <li>Specific tag (e.g., <code>:v1.2</code>) → defaults to <code>IfNotPresent</code></li>
                            <li>No tag specified → treated as <code>:latest</code> → defaults to <code>Always</code></li>
                        </ul>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>Effective policy</th><th>Node behaviour per container start</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>image: easypj</code> (no tag)</td><td><code>Always</code></td><td>contacts the registry every time</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>image: easypj:latest</code></td><td><code>Always</code></td><td>contacts the registry every time</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>image: easypj:v1.2</code></td><td><code>IfNotPresent</code></td><td>uses the node cache; pulls once</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>image: easypj@sha256:…</code></td><td><code>IfNotPresent</code></td><td>uses the node cache; exact bytes</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>explicit <code>imagePullPolicy: Never</code></td><td><code>Never</code></td><td>cache only — fails if absent</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is a common trap on the CKA. If you specify <code>image: nginx</code> without a tag, Kubernetes treats it as <code>nginx:latest</code> with <code>imagePullPolicy: Always</code>. This means every pod restart will contact the registry. In air-gapped or exam environments, this can cause unexpected <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> errors. Best practice: always use specific tags and explicitly set <code>imagePullPolicy</code> to avoid surprises.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is a <strong>container image layer</strong>? Why are layers important for both storage efficiency and pod startup speed in Kubernetes?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A container image is built from <strong>read-only layers</strong>. Each layer represents a filesystem change from a Dockerfile instruction (<code>FROM</code>, <code>RUN</code>, <code>COPY</code>, etc.). Layers are:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>Cached and shared</strong> — If two images share a base layer (e.g., <code>python:3.11-slim</code>), the layer is stored and downloaded only once</li>
                            <li><strong>Content-addressable</strong> — Each layer is identified by its SHA256 digest, enabling deduplication</li>
                            <li><strong>Immutable</strong> — once built, a layer never changes; containers add a thin writable layer on top</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>For Kubernetes: shared layers mean that if node already has the base image cached, pulling a new version only downloads changed layers, dramatically speeding up pod startup.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">v1 → v2: only the changed layer moves</div>
<pre>
  easypj:v1 (on the node)          easypj:v2 (being pulled)
  ┌─────────────────────┐         ┌─────────────────────┐
  │ L3 app code  sha:jkl │         │ L3' app code sha:xyz │ ◄── ONLY this layer downloads
  │ L2 pip deps  sha:ghi │  ═══▶   │ L2 pip deps  sha:ghi │ ✅ cached — reused
  │ L1 base      sha:def │         │ L1 base      sha:def │ ✅ cached — reused
  └─────────────────────┘         └─────────────────────┘
  content-addressed by digest → identical layers are stored once, shared by all images
</pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>While the CKA doesn't test image building, understanding layers helps diagnose issues: (1) Large images cause slow pulls and startup — use slim/alpine bases. (2) <code>imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent</code> leverages the layer cache. (3) If a node's disk fills up with cached images, use <code>crictl rmi --prune</code> to clean unused images. The kubelet also has garbage collection settings (<code>imageGCHighThresholdPercent</code>) that automatically clean old images when disk pressure is detected.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod spec references a <strong>multi-container pod</strong> with an init container and a sidecar container. Explain the difference between init containers and regular sidecar containers in terms of execution order and lifecycle.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Init containers:</strong></p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Run <strong>sequentially before</strong> any app containers start</li>
                            <li>Must complete successfully (exit 0) before the next init container or app containers start</li>
                            <li>If an init container fails, the pod restarts according to its <code>restartPolicy</code></li>
                            <li>Common uses: wait for a dependency, clone a git repo, populate a shared volume</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p><strong>Sidecar containers:</strong></p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Run <strong>alongside</strong> the main app container for the entire pod lifetime</li>
                            <li>Share the pod's network namespace (communicate via <code>localhost</code>) and volumes</li>
                            <li>Common uses: log shipping, service mesh proxy, monitoring agent</li>
                        </ul>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:
  initContainers:            <span class="comment"># Run first, sequentially</span>
    - name: init-db-check
      image: busybox
      command: ['sh', '-c', 'until nslookup db-service; do sleep 2; done']
  containers:                <span class="comment"># Run together after init completes</span>
    - name: easypj-web       <span class="comment"># Main container</span>
      image: easypj:v1
    - name: log-shipper      <span class="comment"># Sidecar</span>
      image: fluentd:latest</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The CKA frequently tests multi-container pod patterns. Key points: (1) Init containers share volumes with app containers but NOT the same array — they're under <code>initContainers:</code>. (2) As of Kubernetes v1.28+, there's a native <code>restartPolicy: Always</code> for sidecar containers declared in <code>initContainers</code> that makes them true sidecars. (3) All containers in a pod share the same IP address and port space. A sidecar on port 8080 conflicts with the app on 8080.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to troubleshoot a pod on a worker node. The <code>kubectl</code> command is not available on the node. What tools can you use to inspect containers, view logs, and check images directly on the node?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">on the worker node</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># List all running containers on the node</span>
sudo crictl ps

<span class="comment"># List all containers (including stopped/failed)</span>
sudo crictl ps -a

<span class="comment"># View logs of a specific container</span>
sudo crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;
sudo crictl logs --tail 50 &lt;container-id&gt;

<span class="comment"># Inspect container details (config, state, mounts)</span>
sudo crictl inspect &lt;container-id&gt;

<span class="comment"># List all pods on this node</span>
sudo crictl pods

<span class="comment"># List cached images</span>
sudo crictl images

<span class="comment"># Check kubelet status and logs</span>
sudo systemctl status kubelet
sudo journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager | tail -50</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>On the CKA exam, you will encounter scenarios where you must SSH to a node and troubleshoot without <code>kubectl</code>. The two essential tools are: (1) <strong><code>crictl</code></strong> — for container-level inspection, and (2) <strong><code>journalctl -u kubelet</code></strong> — for kubelet logs showing why pods fail to start. Remember that <code>crictl</code> talks to the container runtime (containerd) via the CRI socket, while <code>kubectl</code> talks to the API server. They show different views of the same containers.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the OCI (Open Container Initiative) and why does it matter that Docker images are OCI-compliant? Can you use Docker-built images on a Kubernetes cluster running containerd?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Yes, absolutely.</strong> Docker-built images work perfectly on containerd or CRI-O clusters because they conform to the <strong>OCI Image Specification</strong>.</p>
                        <p>The OCI defines two standards:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>OCI Image Spec</strong> — how container images are structured (layers, manifests, configs)</li>
                            <li><strong>OCI Runtime Spec</strong> — how containers are executed (runc, crun)</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>Docker, containerd, CRI-O, and Podman all produce and consume OCI-compliant images. The tool you use to <em>build</em> the image is irrelevant — what matters is the resulting image format.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">OCI is the neutral middle — any builder, any runtime</div>
<pre>
  BUILD with any of…                 ONE standard artifact              RUN on any of…
  🐳 Docker / BuildKit ─┐                                             ┌─▶ 🐋 containerd (runc)
  🧰 Buildah / Podman  ─┼──▶  📀 OCI image (manifest + config +  ────┼─▶ 📦 CRI-O (runc/crun)
  ☸️ kaniko (in-cluster)─┘        content-addressed layers)           └─▶ 🛡️ gVisor / Kata (RuntimeClass)
                              no conversion, ever — the FORMAT is the contract
</pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>A common misconception is that removing Docker from Kubernetes means Docker images no longer work. This is false. The removal of <code>dockershim</code> only affects the <em>runtime layer</em>, not the <em>image format</em>. You can build with Docker, Buildah, Kaniko, or any OCI-compliant tool and run the images on any CRI-compliant runtime. The CKA may test this conceptual understanding in a scenario where someone incorrectly believes images need to be "converted" for containerd.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod is in <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> state. What does this status mean, and what are the systematic steps to diagnose the root cause?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> means the container starts, crashes, and Kubernetes keeps restarting it with exponentially increasing delays (10s, 20s, 40s... up to 5 minutes).</p>
                        <p><strong>Diagnostic steps:</strong></p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bash</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check pod status and restart count</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-web -o wide

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check events for clues</span>
kubectl describe pod easypj-web | grep -A 20 "Events:"

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check container logs (current attempt)</span>
kubectl logs easypj-web

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check previous crash logs</span>
kubectl logs easypj-web --previous

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Check if it's an OOMKill</span>
kubectl describe pod easypj-web | grep -i "OOMKilled"

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Check if command/args are wrong</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-web -o yaml | grep -A 5 "command\|args"</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> is one of the most common CKA troubleshooting scenarios. Common root causes: (1) <strong>Application error</strong> — app crashes on startup (check <code>kubectl logs --previous</code>), (2) <strong>Wrong command/args</strong> — container entrypoint fails, (3) <strong>Missing ConfigMap/Secret</strong> — referenced env vars don't exist, (4) <strong>OOMKilled</strong> — container exceeds memory limit, (5) <strong>Liveness probe failure</strong> — probe kills the container before it's ready. The <code>--previous</code> flag is crucial because it shows logs from the <em>last crashed</em> instance, not the currently starting one.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is a <strong>container registry</strong>? Explain the full image reference format <code>registry/repository:tag@sha256:digest</code> and what each part means.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A container registry is a storage and distribution system for container images. The full reference format:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">anatomy</span></div>
<pre><code>registry.example.com/namespace/image-name:tag@sha256:abc123...

│                    │          │          │    │
├─ Registry host     │          │          │    └─ Digest (immutable hash)
│  (docker.io if     │          │          └─ Tag (mutable label)
│   omitted)         │          └─ Image name
│                    └─ Namespace/org
└─ Full reference</code></pre></div>
                        <p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><code>nginx</code> → <code>docker.io/library/nginx:latest</code></li>
                            <li><code>mycompany/app:v2</code> → <code>docker.io/mycompany/app:v2</code></li>
                            <li><code>gcr.io/my-project/app:v1</code> → explicit registry</li>
                        </ul>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Understanding image references is critical for troubleshooting <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> errors on the CKA. Common mistakes: (1) Omitting the registry for private images, (2) Using <code>:latest</code> when a specific tag is needed, (3) Not specifying <code>imagePullSecrets</code> for private registries. The <strong>digest</strong> (<code>@sha256:...</code>) is the most reliable way to pin an image — tags are mutable and can be overwritten, but digests are content-addressable and immutable.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You SSH to a Kubernetes worker node and run <code>crictl ps</code> but get the error: <em>"connect: no such file or directory"</em>. What is wrong and how do you fix it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The <code>crictl</code> tool cannot find the container runtime socket. Fix by configuring the runtime endpoint:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">on the worker node</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Option 1: Set environment variable</span>
export CONTAINER_RUNTIME_ENDPOINT=unix:///run/containerd/containerd.sock

<span class="comment"># Option 2: Create/edit crictl config file</span>
sudo cat &gt; /etc/crictl.yaml &lt;&lt;EOF
runtime-endpoint: unix:///run/containerd/containerd.sock
image-endpoint: unix:///run/containerd/containerd.sock
timeout: 10
debug: false
EOF

<span class="comment"># Now try again</span>
sudo crictl ps</code></pre></div>
                        <p><strong>Common socket paths:</strong></p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>containerd: <code>unix:///run/containerd/containerd.sock</code></li>
                            <li>CRI-O: <code>unix:///var/run/crio/crio.sock</code></li>
                        </ul>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The CKA exam may present a node where <code>crictl</code> isn't configured. The tool needs to know which socket to connect to — this is the CRI endpoint. If <code>/etc/crictl.yaml</code> doesn't exist or has the wrong socket path, <code>crictl</code> will fail. To find the correct socket, check what runtime is installed: <code>systemctl status containerd</code> or <code>systemctl status crio</code>. Then set the endpoint accordingly. This is a quick fix that can save you from being stuck on a troubleshooting question.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            
