                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What is the role of <strong>CoreDNS</strong> in a Kubernetes cluster? If DNS resolution fails inside a pod, what troubleshooting steps would you take?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p><strong>CoreDNS</strong> provides cluster-internal DNS resolution. It runs as a Deployment in the <code>kube-system</code> namespace and resolves:</p>
                                <ul>
                                    <li><code>service-name.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code> → ClusterIP</li>
                                    <li><code>pod-ip-dashed.namespace.pod.cluster.local</code> → Pod IP</li>
                                </ul>
                                <p><strong>Troubleshooting DNS failure:</strong></p>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check if coredns pods are running</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Check if CoreDNS pods are running</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns

<span class="comment"># 2. Check CoreDNS logs</span>
kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns

<span class="comment"># 3. Verify the kube-dns Service exists</span>
kubectl get svc kube-dns -n kube-system

<span class="comment"># 4. Test DNS from inside a pod</span>
kubectl run dns-test --image=busybox --rm -it --restart=Never \
  -- nslookup kubernetes.default

<span class="comment"># 5. Check pod's /etc/resolv.conf</span>
kubectl exec easypj-web -- cat /etc/resolv.conf
<span class="comment"># Should point to kube-dns ClusterIP (usually 10.96.0.10)</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>DNS is critical infrastructure in Kubernetes. Every pod gets a <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> pointing to the CoreDNS service's ClusterIP. If DNS fails, almost everything breaks — service discovery, external API calls, etc. Common causes: (1) CoreDNS pods not running, (2) kube-dns Service ClusterIP changed, (3) Network policy blocking DNS traffic on port 53, (4) CoreDNS ConfigMap misconfigured, (5) kube-proxy not programming iptables rules for the kube-dns service. This is a high-priority CKA troubleshooting topic.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Explain the difference between the <code>kube-apiserver</code>'s <strong>authentication</strong>, <strong>authorization</strong>, and <strong>admission control</strong> stages. What happens at each stage when a request arrives?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p>Every API request passes through three stages in order:</p>
                                <ol>
                                    <li><strong>Authentication</strong> — <em>"Who are you?"</em> — Verifies identity via client certificates, bearer tokens, or service account tokens. If authentication fails → <code>401 Unauthorized</code></li>
                                    <li><strong>Authorization</strong> — <em>"Are you allowed to do this?"</em> — Checks RBAC rules (Role/ClusterRole + RoleBinding/ClusterRoleBinding). If denied → <code>403 Forbidden</code></li>
                                    <li><strong>Admission Control</strong> — <em>"Should we modify or reject this?"</em> — Webhooks and built-in plugins that can mutate (change defaults) or validate (reject invalid) requests. Examples: <code>LimitRanger</code> (set default resource limits), <code>NamespaceLifecycle</code> (reject requests to terminating namespaces)</li>
                                </ol>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bash</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash">Request → [Authentication] → [Authorization] → [Admission] → etcd
              401?                  403?              Reject/Mutate</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Understanding this pipeline is critical for CKA RBAC and security questions. When you get a "forbidden" error, it's authorization (RBAC). When you get "unauthorized", it's authentication (bad/expired cert or token). Admission controllers can silently modify your objects — for example, <code>DefaultStorageClass</code> adds a storage class to PVCs that don't specify one. The CKA tests RBAC extensively (creating Roles, ClusterRoles, Bindings). Remember: Roles are namespace-scoped, ClusterRoles are cluster-wide.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">A Kubernetes node's kubelet is configured with <code>--pod-cidr=10.244.1.0/24</code>. What does this mean, and how does the CNI plugin use this information to assign IP addresses to pods?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p>The <code>--pod-cidr</code> defines the <strong>subnet of IP addresses</strong> allocated to pods on that specific node. Each node gets a unique pod CIDR from the cluster's overall pod network CIDR (e.g., <code>10.244.0.0/16</code>).</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">one cluster CIDR, carved into per-node /24s</div>
<pre>
  cluster pod CIDR  10.244.0.0/16   (set at kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr)
        │ controller-manager hands each node a unique slice
        ├────────────────┬────────────────┬────────────────┐
        ▼                ▼                ▼
  node-1: 10.244.0.0/24  node-2: 10.244.1.0/24  node-3: 10.244.2.0/24
  📦 .0.2  📦 .0.3        📦 .1.2  📦 .1.4        📦 .2.7
        └──────── CNI assigns pod IPs from its OWN node's slice ────────┘
  💡 read an IP like an address: 10.244.1.x lives on node-2 — handy when tracing traffic
</pre>
                        </div>
                                <p><strong>How it works:</strong></p>
                                <ul>
                                    <li>Controller-manager assigns each node a unique <code>/24</code> subnet from the cluster CIDR</li>
                                    <li>The CNI plugin (Flannel, Calico, Cilium) reads the node's pod CIDR</li>
                                    <li>When a pod is created, the CNI assigns an IP from that node's subnet</li>
                                    <li>Node 1: <code>10.244.0.0/24</code> → pods get <code>10.244.0.2</code>, <code>10.244.0.3</code>, etc.</li>
                                    <li>Node 2: <code>10.244.1.0/24</code> → pods get <code>10.244.1.2</code>, <code>10.244.1.3</code>, etc.</li>
                                </ul>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Understanding pod networking is essential for the CKA's "Services & Networking" domain (20%). The pod CIDR is configured during cluster initialization (<code>kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16</code>). Each node gets a unique subnet. The CNI plugin handles the actual IP assignment and inter-node routing. If pods can't communicate across nodes, check: (1) CNI plugin is installed, (2) pod CIDRs don't overlap with node IPs, (3) firewall rules allow inter-node pod traffic.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You run <code>kubectl get componentstatuses</code> and see that <code>scheduler</code> shows <code>Unhealthy</code>. What could cause this, and how would you fix it?</div>
