                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A pod is never repaired — when it dies it's replaced by a <em>new</em> pod with a new name and a new IP. So you must never hard-code a pod IP; reach pods through a <strong>Service</strong> (stable VIP + DNS) instead.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">pods churn; the Service address never does</div>
<pre>
  🔌 Service easypj-web   VIP 10.96.142.10  ·  DNS easypj-web.easypj.svc   ← STABLE, forever
        │ endpoints track whichever pods currently match + are Ready
        ▼
  t0:  📦 …-x9k2 (10.244.1.5)   💥 dies
  t1:  📦 …-m4p8 (10.244.2.9)   ← NEW name, NEW IP — clients never noticed
  ❌ hard-coded 10.244.1.5 → broken at t1      ✅ easypj-web (Service DNS) → always works
</pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the entire reason Services exist — stable networking over a moving set of pods.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
