kubectl get svc api-server -n exam-prep
<span class="comment"># Note the NodePort (e.g., 31234)</span>
<span class="comment"># Access via: http://&lt;node-ip&gt;:31234</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>This 3-command sequence is a <strong>bread-and-butter CKA task</strong>. Timing tip: this should take under 60 seconds. Key points: (1) Always specify <code>-n &lt;namespace&gt;</code> or use <code>kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=exam-prep</code> to avoid mistakes. (2) <code>--port</code> is the service port, <code>--target-port</code> is the container port. (3) Kubernetes auto-assigns a NodePort from 30000-32767 unless you specify <code>--node-port=XXXXX</code>. (4) <code>kubectl get all</code> shows deployments, replicasets, pods, and services in one view.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between <code>kubectl create</code> and <code>kubectl apply</code>? When would you use each one on the CKA exam?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>Feature</th>
                                            <th><code>kubectl create</code></th>
                                            <th><code>kubectl apply</code></th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Type</td>
                                            <td>Imperative</td>
                                            <td>Declarative</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>If exists</td>
                                            <td>Error: "already exists"</td>
                                            <td>Updates (merges changes)</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Idempotent</td>
                                            <td>No</td>
                                            <td>Yes</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Best for</td>
                                            <td>First-time creation, speed</td>
                                            <td>Updates, CI/CD, repeatability</td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">cka strategy: use create for speed, apply for edits</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># CKA strategy: use create for speed, apply for edits</span>
kubectl create deployment web --image=nginx --dry-run=client -o yaml > web.yaml
vim web.yaml  <span class="comment"># add resources, probes, volumes</span>
kubectl apply -f web.yaml

<span class="comment"># Later, edit and re-apply</span>
vim web.yaml  <span class="comment"># change replicas, image</span>
kubectl apply -f web.yaml  <span class="comment"># applies only the diff</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>On the CKA, use <code>create</code> with <code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code> to generate quick scaffolds, then <code>apply</code> to submit them. If a question says "create," either works. If a question says "update" or "modify," you need <code>apply</code> or <code>kubectl edit</code> or <code>kubectl patch</code>. Pro tip: <code>kubectl apply</code> stores the last-applied configuration as an annotation, enabling three-way merge diffs on subsequent applies.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">A pod is in <code>Pending</code> state. When you describe it, events show: <em>"0/3 nodes are available: 3 Insufficient cpu"</em>. What does this mean and what are your options to fix it?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p>The scheduler cannot find any node with enough free CPU to satisfy the pod's <code>resources.requests.cpu</code>. Options to fix:</p>
                                <ol>
                                    <li><strong>Reduce the pod's CPU request:</strong>
                                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">or edit the deployment</span></div>
                                        <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl edit pod &lt;name&gt;  <span class="comment"># or edit the deployment</span>
<span class="comment"># Change resources.requests.cpu to a lower value</span></code></pre>
                                        </div>
                                    </li>
                                    <li><strong>Free up resources</strong> by deleting/scaling down other workloads:
                                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bash</span></div>
                                        <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl top pods -A --sort-by=cpu | head -10
kubectl scale deployment heavy-app --replicas=1</code></pre>
                                        </div>
                                    </li>
                                    <li><strong>Add more nodes</strong> to the cluster to provide additional capacity</li>
                                    <li><strong>Check ResourceQuotas</strong> — a namespace quota may be artificially limiting available resources:
                                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bash</span></div>
                                        <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get resourcequota -n &lt;namespace&gt;</code></pre>
                                        </div>
                                    </li>
                                </ol>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The "Insufficient" message is from the scheduler's <strong>filtering phase</strong>. It checked all 3 nodes and none had enough allocatable CPU. On the CKA, <code>kubectl describe pod</code> events section is your best friend for diagnosing Pending pods. Other common Pending causes: node taints without matching tolerations, node selector/affinity not matching, PVC unbound (waiting for PV). Always read the specific message — it tells you exactly which filter failed and on how many nodes.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What are <strong>Annotations</strong> in Kubernetes? How do they differ from Labels, and when would you use annotations instead of labels?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>Feature</th>
                                            <th>Labels</th>
                                            <th>Annotations</th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Purpose</td>
                                            <td>Identify and select objects</td>
                                            <td>Attach non-identifying metadata</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Used in selectors</td>
                                            <td>Yes (<code>selector: app=web</code>)</td>
                                            <td>No</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Size limit</td>
                                            <td>63 chars value</td>
                                            <td>256KB total</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Example</td>
                                            <td><code>app: web, tier: frontend</code></td>
                                            <td><code>description: "Main web app"</code></td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">add annotation</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Add annotation</span>
kubectl annotate deployment web kubernetes.io/change-cause="Updated to v2"

<span class="comment"># Add label</span>
kubectl label pod web-pod tier=frontend

<span class="comment"># Filter by label (annotations can't be used in selectors)</span>
kubectl get pods -l tier=frontend</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Labels are for <strong>identification and grouping</strong>. Annotations are for <strong>metadata storage</strong>. Common annotations you'll see on the CKA: (1) <code>kubernetes.io/change-cause</code> — records why a rollout happened, (2) <code>kubectl.kubernetes.io/last-applied-configuration</code> — stored by <code>kubectl apply</code>, (3) Ingress controller annotations (e.g., <code>nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target</code>). The CKA may ask you to annotate objects or add labels for selection — know the syntax for both.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You need to scale a Deployment from 3 replicas to 5, then verify the rollout completed. What are three different ways to accomplish this on the CKA exam?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">kubectl scale (fastest)</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Method 1: kubectl scale (fastest)</span>
kubectl scale deployment easypj-web --replicas=5

<span class="comment"># Method 2: kubectl edit (in-place edit)</span>
kubectl edit deployment easypj-web
<span class="comment"># Change spec.replicas from 3 to 5, save and exit</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 3: kubectl patch (JSON patch)</span>
kubectl patch deployment easypj-web -p '{"spec":{"replicas":5}}'

<span class="comment"># Verify (all methods)</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment easypj-web
kubectl get deployment easypj-web
<span class="comment"># Ready: 5/5</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><code>kubectl scale</code> is the fastest method for the CKA exam. However, <code>kubectl edit</code> is useful when you need to change multiple fields at once. <code>kubectl patch</code> is scriptable and good for automation. Key insight: <strong>scaling does NOT trigger a rollout</strong> — it only changes the replica count on the <em>current</em> ReplicaSet. Only image changes, env changes, or other spec updates trigger new rollouts. Verify scaling with <code>kubectl get deployment</code> — check that READY shows <code>5/5</code>, not <code>3/5</code> (which would indicate pods are still starting). You can also use <code>kubectl get pods -l app=easypj-web</code> to see all 5 pods.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>
            </div>

            
