            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 4.2 — Roll Forward, Roll Back (Zero Downtime)</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 7 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>easypj-web runs 3 replicas of <code>easypj:v1</code>. Ship <code>v2</code> with a rolling update, watch it complete, inspect the revision history — then discover v2 is buggy and roll back <em>instantly</em>. Finally, prove the old ReplicaSet machinery made the rollback cheap.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> <code>set image</code> triggers the roll; <code>rollout status/history/undo</code> is the lifecycle trio. After the rollback, <code>kubectl get rs</code> shows the trick — the "old" ReplicaSet never died, it was just scaled to 0.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">roll → verify → undo → understand</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 0. Baseline (annotate the cause so history is readable)</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=3

<span class="comment"># 1. ROLL FORWARD to v2 — pods replaced in waves (maxSurge/maxUnavailable 25%)</span>
kubectl set image deploy/easypj-web easypj-web=easypj:v2
kubectl rollout status deploy/easypj-web        <span class="comment"># waits until the roll completes ✅</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. INSPECT the history</span>
kubectl rollout history deploy/easypj-web       <span class="comment"># revision 1 (v1), revision 2 (v2)</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. v2 is bad → ROLL BACK instantly</span>
kubectl rollout undo deploy/easypj-web          <span class="comment"># or --to-revision=1</span>
kubectl rollout status deploy/easypj-web

<span class="comment"># 4. THE TRICK — the old ReplicaSet was kept at 0 replicas the whole time</span>
kubectl get rs -l app=easypj-web
<span class="comment">#  easypj-web-6d8f9  3  3  3   ← v1, scaled back UP by the undo</span>
<span class="comment">#  easypj-web-7c4b2  0  0  0   ← v2, now parked at 0 for a future redo</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> a rollback isn't a rebuild — the Deployment just <em>rebalances replicas between ReplicaSets</em> it kept around (<code>revisionHistoryLimit</code>, default 10). That's why <code>undo</code> is instant and safe, and why the RS naming (<code>-&lt;pod-template-hash&gt;</code>) matters: one RS per template version. This exact sequence is a guaranteed exam task class.</p>
