                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 4.3 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 4.3 — "Running but No Traffic": the Readiness Cut-off</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 8 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Users say easypj is down, yet <code>kubectl get pods</code> shows all pods <code>Running</code>. Reproduce the classic cause: give a pod a readiness probe that fails, and watch Kubernetes silently pull it out of the Service — <em>without restarting it</em>. Then explain the liveness-vs-readiness distinction from what you observed.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> <code>Running</code> is a <em>phase</em>; joining a Service requires the <code>Ready</code> <em>condition</em>. Watch <code>kubectl get endpoints</code> and the pod's <code>READY 0/1</code> column — that's where the story is.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">break readiness → watch the endpoints drain</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. A deployment + service with a readiness probe that will FAIL</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=nginx --replicas=2 \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml &gt; d.yaml
<span class="comment"># edit d.yaml — add to the container:</span>
<span class="comment">#   readinessProbe:</span>
<span class="comment">#     httpGet: { path: /nope, port: 80 }   # 404 → probe fails</span>
<span class="comment">#     periodSeconds: 5</span>
kubectl apply -f d.yaml
kubectl expose deploy easypj-web --port=80

<span class="comment"># 2. OBSERVE — Running, but 0/1 Ready, and the Service has NO backends</span>
kubectl get pods                       <span class="comment"># STATUS Running · READY 0/1  ← the tell</span>
kubectl get endpoints easypj-web       <span class="comment"># ENDPOINTS: &lt;none&gt;</span>
kubectl describe pod -l app=easypj-web | grep -A3 "Readiness"
<span class="comment">#   Readiness probe failed: HTTP probe failed with statuscode: 404</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. FIX the probe path (path: /) and re-apply</span>
sed -i 's|/nope|/|' d.yaml &amp;&amp; kubectl apply -f d.yaml
kubectl get endpoints easypj-web       <span class="comment"># pod IPs appear ✅ traffic flows</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Probe</th><th>Question it asks</th><th>On failure</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><strong>readiness</strong></td><td>"open for business?"</td><td>remove from Service endpoints — <em>no restart</em></td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>liveness</strong></td><td>"alive at all?"</td><td><em>restart</em> the container</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>startup</strong></td><td>"still booting?"</td><td>holds the other probes off until it passes</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> the pod never restarted (RESTARTS stayed 0) — readiness only gates <em>traffic</em>. The observable chain is: probe fails → <code>Ready</code> condition false → <code>READY 0/1</code> → EndpointSlice drops the IP → users get nothing while the pod "looks" fine. That chain <em>is</em> the answer to one of the most common troubleshooting questions on the exam.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

