            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 4.5 — Label Surgery: Orphan a Pod, Watch the Controller React</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 8 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Labels aren't decoration — they're <em>membership</em>. Prove it: on a 3-replica easypj Deployment, change one pod's <code>app</code> label. Predict first, then observe: what does the ReplicaSet do? What happens to the relabeled pod? Then use the trick for what it's actually good for — <strong>quarantining a sick pod for debugging</strong> without losing it.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> The ReplicaSet counts only pods matching its selector. Relabel one and the RS suddenly sees 2/3 — it creates a replacement. The relabeled pod keeps running, but now belongs to nobody (and left the Service too).
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">membership is a label match</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Baseline: 3 pods, all labeled app=easypj-web</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=nginx --replicas=3
kubectl get pods -l app=easypj-web            <span class="comment"># 3 pods</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. SURGERY — relabel ONE pod out of the selector</span>
POD=$(kubectl get pods -l app=easypj-web -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
kubectl label pod "$POD" app=quarantine --overwrite

<span class="comment"># 3. OBSERVE — the RS instantly backfills; the orphan keeps running</span>
kubectl get pods --show-labels
<span class="comment">#   easypj-web-xxx-NEW    app=easypj-web   ← replacement (RS saw 2/3)</span>
<span class="comment">#   easypj-web-xxx-OLD    app=quarantine   ← orphaned: still Running, out of the</span>
<span class="comment">#                                            Service, owned by nobody</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. WHY it's useful — debug the sick pod at leisure, no user impact</span>
kubectl exec -it "$POD" -- sh                 <span class="comment"># poke around the quarantined pod</span>
kubectl delete pod "$POD"                     <span class="comment"># clean up when done (nothing respawns it)</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> three deep facts surfaced at once — (1) a ReplicaSet's <em>membership is the label match</em>, nothing more; (2) the Service dropped the pod for the same reason (selectors again); (3) the orphan didn't respawn when deleted, because no controller claimed it anymore. The quarantine move — relabel to detach, debug, delete — is a genuinely useful production technique built entirely on Chapter 4's mental model.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 4 Visual Summary — the objects, wired together</div>
<pre>
   🌍 Ingress/Gateway ─routes─▶ 🔌 Service ─selector(app=easypj)─▶ 📦 Pods
                                    ▲                                ▲ owned by
                               🚀 Deployment ─manages─▶ 🎛️ ReplicaSet ┘ creates (self-heal)
                                    │ pod template pulls in:
                                    ├─ ⚙️ ConfigMap / 🔒 Secret (envFrom)
                                    ├─ 💾 PVC ─▶ PV ─▶ 🧱 StorageClass
                                    └─ 🛡️ ServiceAccount ◀─ RBAC governs access
   🗂️ Namespace contains it all   ·   🧯 NetworkPolicy firewalls it   ·   🔖 LABELS are the glue
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   You create a Deployment → it owns a ReplicaSet → which owns Pods; a Service finds those Pods
   by LABEL and gives them a stable address. That sentence is 80% of every CKA workload task.
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> The objects nest. You create a <strong>Deployment</strong>; it <em>manages</em> a <strong>ReplicaSet</strong>; the ReplicaSet <em>creates and self-heals</em> the <strong>Pods</strong>. You never make the lower layers by hand — you describe the top one and ownership cascades down.</p>
                    <p>A pod's template pulls in everything it needs: <strong>ConfigMap/Secret</strong> for configuration (<code>envFrom</code>), a <strong>PVC → PV → StorageClass</strong> chain for storage, and a <strong>ServiceAccount</strong> (governed by RBAC) for API access. Out front, a <strong>Service</strong> uses a <em>label selector</em> (<code>app=easypj</code>) to find the ever-changing pods and give them one stable address; <strong>Ingress/Gateway</strong> routes external traffic to that Service. Around it all, a <strong>Namespace</strong> contains the objects and a <strong>NetworkPolicy</strong> firewalls them.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> <em>labels are the glue</em> — nothing is wired by name, everything is matched by label. "Create a Deployment → it owns a ReplicaSet → which owns Pods → a Service selects them by label" is the single sentence behind roughly 80% of every CKA workload task.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
