            <section class="section-block" id="b-s1-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">1.1 The Problem Backstage Solves</h3>
                <p>You've built <strong>easypj</strong> — a Django <code>jobpost</code> app where employers list jobs filtered by Skills, Location, and Author. It runs in a container, it's on GitHub, it has CI/CD, it's deployed to Kubernetes. That's <em>plenty</em> of tooling. So why does anyone need <em>one more</em> tool on top of all of that?</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Term — Developer Experience (DX) &amp; Cognitive Load</h5>
                    <p><strong>Cognitive load</strong> is the mental effort required just to <em>find</em> information before you can start real work. Every extra tool a developer must remember, log into, and search separately adds load — not because any one tool is bad, but because the <em>number</em> of places to look grows faster than any one person can track. Backstage's entire reason to exist is collapsing that number back down to one.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Spotify's Story — Where This Problem Was First Solved at Scale</h4>
                <p>Backstage wasn't dreamed up in the abstract — it was built at <strong>Spotify</strong> to survive Spotify's own scale: <strong>100+ autonomous squads</strong> shipping <strong>1,000+ microservices</strong>, each squad free to pick its own stack. That freedom is great for shipping speed and terrible for discoverability — with no central portal, a new engineer had no way to answer "does a service for X already exist?" or "who do I even ask?" without pinging people. Backstage is Spotify's fix for its own growing pains, later open-sourced so every org hitting the same wall could use it.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">A Developer's Day — WITHOUT Backstage vs WITH Backstage (the easypj story)</div>
<pre>
  😰 WITHOUT BACKSTAGE — the "Where's Waldo?" problem
  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ "Where is the easypj repo?"          → Search GitHub...       10 min   │
  │ "What's the CI/CD pipeline?"         → Check Jenkins...       15 min   │
  │ "Who owns the jobpost service?"      → Ask in Slack...        20 min   │
  │ "Where are the API docs?"            → Search Confluence...   30 min   │
  │ "How do I create a new service?"     → Find a template...     60 min   │
  │ "What's the PagerDuty for this?"     → Ask the ops team...    15 min   │
  │ ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────│
  │  TOTAL TIME LOST:  ~2.5 HOURS — just finding information, zero code   │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                              │
                          ────▼────   the SAME questions, one portal:
  🎉 WITH BACKSTAGE — one search box, one pane of glass
  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ "Search for easypj"                  → Software Catalog...    30 sec  │
  │ "CI/CD status"                        → built-in plugin card... 10 sec│
  │ "Who owns jobpost?"                   → Entity page → Owner... 5 sec  │
  │ "API docs"                            → TechDocs tab, auto-pub 10 sec │
  │ "New microservice"                    → Create → Template...   2 min  │
  │ "On-call info"                        → PagerDuty plugin card... 5 sec│
  │ ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────│
  │  TOTAL TIME:  ~3 MINUTES — not 2.5 hours                               │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <p>That's the same developer, the same six questions, same org — only the <em>lookup path</em> changes. Play it forward one level and the case for a portal writes itself:</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="scenario-box">
                        <h5>😰 A New Hire on the easypj Team, WITHOUT Backstage</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>🔴 Spends their first 2 weeks just <em>locating</em> things, not building</li>
                            <li>🔴 Learns who owns what by trial, error, and Slack DMs</li>
                            <li>🔴 Finds 3 versions of the "API docs" — 2 are stale</li>
                            <li>🔴 Copies an old repo by hand to start a new service — misses steps</li>
                            <li>🔴 Doesn't know PagerDuty on-call exists until their first incident</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-box positive">
                        <h5>🎉 A New Hire on the easypj Team, WITH Backstage</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>✅ Searches the Catalog, sees every service, API, and owner on day 1</li>
                            <li>✅ Opens jobpost's entity page → ownership, docs, CI/CD, all in one place</li>
                            <li>✅ Reads TechDocs — generated straight from the repo's own <code>/docs</code></li>
                            <li>✅ Scaffolds a brand-new service from a Software Template in minutes</li>
                            <li>✅ Sees the on-call card before they ever need it</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Why This Isn't "Just Add a Wiki Page"</h5>
                    <p>A wiki is <em>written once, by a human, and rots</em> — nobody updates the "owners" page when a team reorgs. Backstage's Catalog is closer to a <strong>live index</strong>: entities are declared next to the code they describe (a <code>catalog-info.yaml</code> file in the repo, §1.13), so the "who owns this" answer is versioned, reviewed, and lives where the code already lives — not in a separate document someone forgets to update.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Cost Doesn't Stay Flat — It Compounds With Team Size</h4>
                <p>§1.1's ~2.5-hours/day figure is per developer. That number doesn't just add up — it <em>multiplies</em>, because a bigger team means more people each independently re-discovering the same scattered answers, and more cross-team questions ("who owns THAT service?") that only a human can currently answer:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Team size</th><th>Hours lost/day (at ~2.5h each)</th><th>Approx. cost/year (at $75/hr loaded rate)</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>5 developers</td><td>12.5 hours/day</td><td>~$230,000/year</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>25 developers</td><td>62.5 hours/day</td><td>~$1.15 million/year</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>100 developers</td><td>250 hours/day</td><td>~$4.6 million/year</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box note mb-0">
                    <h5>📖 Reading This Table Honestly</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">These are illustrative order-of-magnitude figures, not a formal study — the exact minutes-per-task in §1.1 will vary by org. The point isn't the precise dollar figure; it's that "hunting for information" is a <strong>cost that scales with headcount</strong>, which is exactly why the pain gets impossible to ignore well before Spotify's scale (§1.6) — and exactly why §1.13 calls the "only huge companies need this" myth out directly.</p>
