            <!-- ─────────── §1.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s1-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">1.2 Backstage Defined — The Simple Answer</h3>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Official Definition (backstage.io)</h5>
                    <p><strong>"Backstage is an open source framework for building developer portals. Powered by a centralized software catalog, Backstage restores order to your microservices and infrastructure and enables your product teams to ship high-quality code quickly — without compromising autonomy."</strong></p>
                    <p>Read that sentence twice — the operative word is <strong>framework</strong>, not "platform" or "product." Backstage doesn't ship you a finished developer portal; it ships you the <em>pieces</em> (a Catalog engine, a Scaffolder, a docs pipeline, a plugin API) and you assemble <em>your org's</em> portal from them. That's exactly why "Customizing Backstage" is <strong>32%</strong> of the CBA exam — the single biggest domain — because using Backstage well always means building on it, not just installing it.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   BACKSTAGE SHIPS YOU                      YOUR ORG STILL ASSEMBLES
   📦 Catalog engine              ──▶       your own entities (Ch 3, 7-9)
   🚀 Scaffolder                  ──▶       your own Templates (Ch 11-13)
   📖 TechDocs pipeline           ──▶       your own docs content (Ch 14-15)
   🔌 Plugin API                  ──▶       your own installed/custom Plugins (Ch 16-18)
                    </pre>
                    <p class="mb-0">The official docs add one more precise sentence worth memorizing verbatim: <strong>"Backstage unifies all your infrastructure tooling, services, and documentation to create a streamlined development environment from end to end."</strong> Notice the phrase "without compromising autonomy" — that's the key tension Backstage resolves: centralizing <em>discovery</em> (the Catalog) without centralizing <em>control</em> (each squad still owns and ships its own service).</p>
