                <h3 class="section-h">1.5 Where Backstage Fits in Your Workflow</h3>
                <p>Backstage isn't a stop on the developer workflow — it's the <strong>hub the workflow flows through</strong>. easypj's code still lives in Git, CI/CD still runs in Jenkins/GitHub Actions, and Kubernetes still runs the pods; Backstage's job is to make every step of that pipeline <em>visible from one place</em> instead of six.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The easypj Developer Workflow — Code → Git → CI/CD → Deploy → Monitor, all visible in Backstage</div>
<pre>
   👨‍💻 Developer writes code for jobpost
        │
        ▼
   🔀 Git (push to the jobpost repo)  ──────────────▶  📦 Software Catalog picks up
        │                                                catalog-info.yaml automatically
        ▼
   🚀 CI/CD pipeline runs (build, test, lint)  ───────▶  🔌 CI/CD plugin card shows the
        │                                                 latest run status on jobpost's
        ▼                                                 entity page — no separate login
   📦 Image built, pushed to the registry
        │
        ▼
   ☸️ Deployed to Kubernetes  ─────────────────────────▶  🔌 Kubernetes plugin card shows
        │                                                 live pod health on the same page
        ▼
   📈 Monitoring / alerts fire (Sentry, PagerDuty)  ───▶  🔌 Those plugin cards ALSO render
                                                           on jobpost's entity page

   ── Every arrow above is a DIFFERENT tool. Every arrowhead lands on ONE Backstage page. ──
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="analogy-box">
                    <h5>🏬 Analogy: Backstage Is a Shopping Mall</h5>
                    <p>Picture your engineering tools as individual stores — GitHub, Jenkins, Sentry, Jira — each with its own entrance, its own layout, its own login. A shopping mall doesn't replace any of those stores; it puts them under <strong>one roof</strong> with <strong>one directory</strong> at the entrance:</p>
<pre>
   🏬 THE MALL                              ☸️ BACKSTAGE
   ──────────────────────────────────────   ──────────────────────────────
   Individual stores (Nike, Apple, Zara)     Individual tools (GitHub, Jenkins,
                                              Sentry, Jira) — untouched, still
                                              running exactly as before
   🗺️ Mall directory                         📦 Software Catalog
      "find any store in seconds"               "find any service in seconds"
   🏗️ New-store build-out service            🚀 Software Templates
      "open a new store in weeks, not          "scaffold a new service in
       months, using the mall's standard        minutes, using the org's
       electrical/plumbing hookups"              standard CI/CD + K8s hookups"
   🛍️ Every store, one visit                 🔌 Every tool's status, one page
</pre>
                    <p class="mb-0">Just like a mall brings every store under one roof without renovating any of them, Backstage brings every developer tool under one UI without you rewriting any of them (§1.13's biggest myth-buster). This is the third and final zoom: the Airport gave you the whole-system map, the App Store gave you the build/extend angle, and this Mall gives you the <strong>daily-integration</strong> angle — how already-existing tools (GitHub, Jenkins, Sentry) sit inside the one UI without any of them changing.</p>
