            <!-- ─────────── §1.10 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s1-10">
                <h3 class="section-h">1.10 How Backstage Actually Runs — A Beginner's Walkthrough</h3>
                <p>Set the jargon aside for a moment: what <em>literally</em> happens when you click "jobpost" in the Catalog? This section is the simplified, beginner version — Chapter 2 gives you the full technical version with real code.</p>

                <h4>A Day in the Life of One Click — Simplified for Beginners</h4>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active"><div class="step-number">1</div><h5>Open the URL</h5><p>You open <code>backstage.easypj.com</code> in your browser.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">2</div><h5>Just a React app</h5><p>The page you see is a React app, like any website.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">3</div><h5>You click</h5><p>You click "jobpost" in the Catalog.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">4</div><h5>Browser asks server</h5><p>Your browser asks the Backstage SERVER: "tell me about jobpost."</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">5</div><h5>Server checks the DB</h5><p>The server checks PostgreSQL for that entity.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">6</div><h5>Server responds</h5><p>The server sends the answer back as JSON data.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">7</div><h5>Browser renders</h5><p>Your browser turns that data into the page you see.</p></div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note mb-0">
                    <h5>📖 The One Rule to Remember</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">Your browser <strong>never</strong> talks to the database directly. It always asks the server first. Always. Every single interaction — clicking jobpost, running a Template, opening a TechDocs page — is really <strong>two hops</strong>: browser → server, then server → database.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Same Walkthrough, as an HTTP Exchange</h4>
                <p>"Your browser asks the server" is a real HTTP request under the hood — nothing magical happens between steps 4 and 6. Seeing the actual shape of that exchange makes the two-hop rule concrete instead of abstract:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">what the browser actually sends &amp; receives</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 4. Browser → Server: a plain HTTP GET, same as any REST API call</span>
GET /api/catalog/entities/by-name/component/default/jobpost HTTP/1.1
Host: backstage.easypj.com
Authorization: Bearer &lt;your-session-token&gt;

<span class="comment"># 6. Server → Browser: the entity, as JSON — this is ALL the browser gets</span>
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "kind": "Component",
  "metadata": { "name": "jobpost", "description": "Job listings service" },
  "spec": { "type": "service", "owner": "team-easypj", "lifecycle": "production" }
}</code></pre>
                </div>
                <p>Notice what's absent: no SQL, no database host, no credentials — the browser only ever sees a URL and a JSON blob. The server is the only thing that ever holds a database connection string.</p>

                <h4>Why This Rule Actually Matters — Not Just Correctness</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔐</div>
                        <h5>Security</h5>
                        <p>Database credentials live only on the server — a compromised browser session can't leak them, because it never had them.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔌</div>
                        <h5>Every Plugin Reuses the Same Path</h5>
                        <p>A GitHub plugin, a Kubernetes plugin, and a PagerDuty plugin all follow the identical browser→server→(external API) shape — learn the rule once, and you've understood how every plugin talks to the world.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Preview: Chapter 2 Names This Properly</h5>
                    <p>This chapter deliberately calls it "the browser" and "the server" in plain English. Chapter 2 gives these the same split its own formal name — the <strong>frontend/backend split</strong> — and shows you the real Express routes and React components involved. Hold onto the plain-English version now; the vocabulary upgrade later is easy once the shape is already familiar.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>"Isn't Two Hops Slower Than One?" — Addressing the Obvious Objection</h4>
                <p>A fair question: doesn't inserting a server in the middle make everything slower than the browser just reading a database directly? In practice, no — for three concrete reasons that matter more as your Catalog grows:</p>
                <div class="card-grid">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⚡</div>
                        <h5>The extra hop is local-network fast, not internet-slow</h5>
                        <p>Browser → server is typically the only hop crossing the public internet; server → database usually happens inside the same data center or cluster, adding single-digit milliseconds.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⚡</div>
                        <h5>The server can cache</h5>
                        <p>A direct-to-database model can't cache anything for the browser — every client re-queries fresh. The server hop means popular entities (like jobpost's page) can be cached once and served instantly to everyone after.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⚡</div>
                        <h5>The server can pre-compute</h5>
                        <p>Search indices, permission checks, and cross-entity joins (§1.12's target architecture) all happen once on the server, not once per browser tab.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="uth-callout mb-0"><strong>🔑 The two-hop rule isn't a compromise — it's what makes the system faster at real scale</strong>, not just safer. This is the same reason virtually every production web app on earth (not just Backstage) is built this way.</div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §1.11 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s1-11">
                <h3 class="section-h">1.11 Backstage + Kubernetes — Two Different Relationships</h3>
                <p>This guide deploys Backstage <em>onto</em> Kubernetes from day one — but novices routinely conflate two entirely separate relationships. Untangle them now, because the CBA exam tests each one independently.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The Dual Relationship — Don't Mix These Up</div>
<pre>
   RELATIONSHIP #1 — Backstage RUNS ON Kubernetes
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Backstage itself is just a Node.js app, packaged into a container.
   We deploy it as Pod(s) — Chapter 19 shows exactly how. Kubernetes gives
   the Backstage app itself self-healing, scaling, and rolling updates —
   the SAME benefits any containerized app gets from K8s (see the CKA
   Template Part 1 chapters for that general mechanism).

   RELATIONSHIP #2 — Backstage OBSERVES Kubernetes
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   The Kubernetes PLUGIN (Chapters 10, 20) lets Backstage DISPLAY live pod
   health for jobpost — or ANY service — on its entity page, regardless of
   where Backstage itself happens to be running. This is just another
   Plugin following §1.10's browser→server→(K8s API) pattern.
</pre>
                </div>

                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th></th><th>Relationship #1 — Runs On</th><th>Relationship #2 — Observes</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>What it means</td><td>Backstage's own Pods are scheduled &amp; managed by K8s</td><td>Backstage's K8s <em>plugin</em> reads the cluster's API and renders it</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Where it's built</td><td>Chapter 19 — Deploying Backstage on K8s</td><td>Chapters 10, 20 — the Kubernetes plugin</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Can you have one without the other?</td><td>Yes — Backstage can run on a VM/Docker Compose with no plugin installed</td><td>Yes — the K8s plugin can point at a cluster Backstage itself doesn't run on</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>What breaks if it fails</td><td>Backstage itself goes down (it's the workload)</td><td>Only the pod-health <em>card</em> goes blank — Backstage keeps running</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ CBA Gotcha</h5>
                    <p>"Backstage runs on K8s" and "Backstage shows K8s data" are <strong>not</strong> the same fact — you can have either one without the other. A scenario question that describes only ONE of these relationships is not implying the other; the exam tests them separately, and this is a favorite place to plant a wrong-but-tempting answer choice.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>A Third Scenario That Makes the Independence Undeniable</h4>
                <p>To fully separate these two relationships in your head, picture the most extreme version: an org runs Backstage on plain AWS ECS (no Kubernetes anywhere in Backstage's own hosting) — yet still installs the Kubernetes plugin, pointed at a <em>completely different</em> team's Kubernetes cluster elsewhere in the company, purely to display that other team's pod health.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Maximally independent — zero overlap between the two relationships</div>
<pre>
   🖥️ Backstage itself runs on:  AWS ECS  (NOT Kubernetes — Relationship #1 is absent)
        │
        └── 🔌 Kubernetes plugin installed anyway, configured to read:
                ☸️ a DIFFERENT team's cluster, that Backstage never touches for its own hosting
                (Relationship #2 is present, fully independent of #1)
</pre>
                </div>
                <p>This is an unusual setup, but a perfectly valid one — which is exactly why the exam can ask about either relationship in isolation without the other ever being implied.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">✅</div>
                        <h5>Quick self-check — Relationship #1</h5>
                        <p>Ask: "if the Kubernetes plugin were uninstalled right now, would Backstage itself go down?" If no, you're reasoning about hosting (Relationship #1), not the plugin.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">✅</div>
                        <h5>Quick self-check — Relationship #2</h5>
                        <p>Ask: "if Backstage moved to a different host tomorrow, would the pod-health card disappear?" If no (the plugin just points elsewhere), you're reasoning about display (Relationship #2), not hosting.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

