            <section class="section-block" id="b-s1-13">
                <h3 class="section-h">1.13 Common Beginner Misconceptions — Myth vs Reality</h3>
                <p>New Backstage learners repeat the same handful of mix-ups. Clear them up before they become bad habits that cost you exam points or an awkward architecture conversation with your team.</p>

                <div class="diagram-title myth-table-title">🧭 Myth vs Reality — Backstage Edition</div>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>❌ Myth</th><th>✅ Reality</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>"Backstage is a monitoring tool"</td><td>It's a <strong>portal framework</strong> that <em>embeds</em> monitoring tools via plugins — it doesn't collect metrics itself.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>"Backstage replaces Kubernetes"</td><td>It runs <em>on</em> K8s and can <em>display</em> K8s data — two separate relationships (§1.11), and neither one is a replacement.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>"You must rewrite your apps to use it"</td><td>You add <strong>one</strong> YAML file (<code>catalog-info.yaml</code>) — zero application code changes required (§1.12).</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>"It's only worth it for huge companies"</td><td>Any team with 10+ services already benefits — the ROI curve in §1.6 starts on day one, not at Spotify scale.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>"SQLite is fine for production"</td><td>SQLite is <strong>single-writer only</strong>. Production always needs PostgreSQL — covered again in Chapters 2, 4, and 19.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>"Backstage requires Kubernetes to run"</td><td>False — it's a Node.js app deployable anywhere (VM, Docker Compose, ECS). §1.11's Relationship #1 is a choice, not a requirement.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>"You need to be a TypeScript expert to use Backstage day to day"</td><td>Only <strong>customizing</strong> Backstage (writing Plugins) needs TypeScript. Using the Catalog, running a Template, or reading TechDocs needs none.</td></tr>
