            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch1-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — the two-hop rule that powers everything</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>§1.10's one-click walkthrough hides one crucial architectural fact worth restating on its own: your browser is <strong>never</strong> trusted to talk to the database. Every single interaction — clicking jobpost in the Catalog, running a Template, opening a TechDocs page — is actually <strong>two hops</strong>: browser → server, server → database. It is this two-hop rule, not any one feature, that lets Backstage add auth, caching, and multiple plugins in front of the same data without your browser code ever knowing the difference.</p>

                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   ┌──────────────── TWO-HOP RULE (every Backstage interaction) ───────────────┐
   │                                                                          │
   │   🌐 BROWSER  ──①HTTP req──▶  🖥️ SERVER  ──②SQL query──▶  🗄️ DATABASE     │
   │   (React app)  ◀──④JSON res──   (Node.js)   ◀──③rows───    (PostgreSQL)   │
   │                                                                          │
   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   The browser NEVER does hop ② or ③. If a bug report describes the UI
   reading/writing something impossible without a round trip to the server,
   that assumption is broken somewhere — this is your first debugging reflex.
                    </pre>

                    <h5>① The Full Trace — Clicking "jobpost," Step by Step</h5>
                    <p>Here's the two-hop rule playing out for real, with every intermediate stop named:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
 1. You click "jobpost" in the Catalog UI (a React component)
 2. The React app calls its own frontend API client:
      catalogApi.getEntityByRef('component:default/jobpost')
 3. That client issues a real HTTP GET to the BACKEND, never to a database:
      GET /api/catalog/entities/by-name/component/default/jobpost
 4. The backend's catalog plugin receives the request, checks the caller's
    auth token (⑤ below), then queries ITS OWN database:
      SELECT * FROM final_entities WHERE entity_ref = 'component:default/jobpost'
 5. PostgreSQL returns the stored entity row (JSON column + metadata)
 6. The backend serializes it and responds with the JSON body you saw in §1.10
 7. The React app receives that JSON and renders the entity page you see

 ── At NO step does your browser open a database connection, hold a DB
    password, or run SQL. Steps 4-5 are invisible to it by DESIGN. ──
                    </pre>

                    <h5>② Why the Frontend Is "Just a Website" — the Plugin Shell Pattern</h5>
                    <p>Every card you see on an entity page — the CI/CD status, the Kubernetes pod health, the PagerDuty schedule — is a separate <strong>frontend plugin</strong> rendered into the same page shell. None of them talk to their external tool (Jenkins, K8s, PagerDuty) directly from your browser; each one follows the identical two-hop shape, just with a different backend plugin and a different external system on the far end:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   🌐 Entity page (ONE React shell)
        ├── 📦 Catalog card       → hop → 🖥️ catalog backend  → 🗄️ PostgreSQL
        ├── 🚀 CI/CD status card  → hop → 🖥️ CI/CD backend    → 🌐 Jenkins API
        ├── ☸️ K8s health card    → hop → 🖥️ K8s backend      → ☸️ Cluster API
        └── 📞 PagerDuty card     → hop → 🖥️ PagerDuty backend → 🌐 PagerDuty API

   Four completely different data sources. ONE identical shape: browser
   asks ITS OWN server, the server asks the outside world, never the reverse.
                    </pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Learn the shape once, understand every plugin.</strong> Chapters 16-18 build a real custom plugin — and it follows exactly this pattern. There is no "special case" plugin that's allowed to skip the backend hop.</div>

                    <h5>③ What the Entity JSON Actually Looks Like, End to End</h5>
                    <p>§1.10 showed a trimmed response. Backstage entities always carry the <strong>same four top-level shape</strong> no matter which Kind they are — <code>apiVersion</code>, <code>kind</code>, <code>metadata</code>, <code>spec</code> — a deliberate design echo of Kubernetes' own object model, so if you've ever read a K8s manifest this will look immediately familiar:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   # This is catalog-info.yaml, the ONLY file jobpost needs (full detail in Ch 3)
   apiVersion: backstage.io/v1alpha1
   kind: Component
   metadata:
     name: jobpost
     description: Job listings service — filter by Skills, Location, Author
     annotations:
       github.com/project-slug: easypj-org/jobpost
   spec:
     type: service
     lifecycle: production
     owner: team-easypj
                    </pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What it holds</th><th>Kubernetes equivalent (if you know it)</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>apiVersion</code></td><td>Which schema version this entity follows</td><td><code>apiVersion</code> — identical idea</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kind</code></td><td>The entity's Kind — <code>Component</code>, <code>API</code>, <code>Resource</code>…</td><td><code>kind</code> — identical idea</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>metadata</code></td><td>Name, description, and <strong>annotations</strong> (§1.9's "sticky notes")</td><td><code>metadata</code> — identical idea</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>spec</code></td><td>Your intent — type, lifecycle stage, owning team</td><td><code>spec</code> — identical idea (desired state)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why this matters beyond trivia.</strong> Once you can read one entity's four fields, you can read <em>any</em> entity — an API, a Resource, a System — because the shape never changes, only the values inside <code>spec</code> do. Chapter 3 is entirely about mastering this one shape.</div>

                    <h5>④ Where Auth Actually Sits — the Header You Saw in §1.10</h5>
                    <p>Look back at §1.10's HTTP request — it carried an <code>Authorization: Bearer</code> header. That token is checked at hop ①→② (server receiving the request), never by the browser itself, and never by the database. The server is the single choke point that decides "is this caller even allowed to see jobpost," which is exactly why database credentials and auth logic both live in one place: the backend, never the browser.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   🌐 browser sends token   ──▶  🖥️ server VALIDATES token   ──▶  🗄️ database
                                  (Ch 26 in the full guide covers
                                   Backstage's real auth providers)
                                  If invalid → 401, request stops HERE.
                                  The database never even sees a rejected request.
                    </pre>

                    <h5>⑤ CNCF Governance Under the Hood — What "Donated" Actually Changed</h5>
                    <p>§1.7 established Backstage is CNCF Incubating. Concretely, "donating to the CNCF" changed three specific things, not just a logo on the website:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Before donation (Spotify-internal)</th><th>Today, under CNCF governance</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Spotify alone decided the roadmap</td><td>A cross-company Technical Steering Committee (TSC) governs it</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Spotify alone owned the trademark/IP</td><td>The Linux Foundation holds project assets on the community's behalf</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>No formal maturity bar to clear</td><td>Must eventually pass a security audit + prove multi-company use to reach Graduated</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 So "CNCF Incubating" is a governance fact, not a marketing badge.</strong> It tells you specifically: multiple companies now steer Backstage, not just its creator — which is a meaningfully different risk profile than a single-vendor open-source project.</div>

                    <h5>⑥ Entity References — Why Every Entity Has a Three-Part Address</h5>
                    <p>Look back at §① 's walkthrough and §1.10's HTTP request — both used a string shaped like <code>component:default/jobpost</code> (or, as a URL path, <code>component/default/jobpost</code>). This is called an <strong>entity reference</strong>, and once you can parse it on sight, every cross-entity relationship in later chapters (an API's <code>providesApis</code>, a Component's <code>dependsOn</code>) becomes trivial to read instead of mysterious — Lab Q&amp;A Q13 has you parse one directly.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   ENTITY REFERENCE ANATOMY:      component : default / jobpost
                                      │           │        │
                                      ▼           ▼        ▼
                                    KIND     NAMESPACE    NAME
                              (§1.9's Kind)  (a grouping   (the entity's
                                             boundary —     unique name
                                             "default" if   inside that
                                             unspecified)   namespace)

   Full form:   &lt;kind&gt;:&lt;namespace&gt;/&lt;name&gt;      e.g.  api:default/jobpost-api
   Short form (kind implied by context):  &lt;namespace&gt;/&lt;name&gt;  or just &lt;name&gt;
                    </pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Reference</th><th>Reads as</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>component:default/jobpost</code></td><td>The Component named "jobpost" in the "default" namespace</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>api:default/jobpost-api</code></td><td>The API named "jobpost-api" in the "default" namespace</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>resource:default/postgres-db</code></td><td>The Resource named "postgres-db" in the "default" namespace</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>group:default/team-easypj</code></td><td>The Group (team) named "team-easypj"</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why namespaces exist at all.</strong> Two different teams can each have a service literally named "api" without colliding, because <code>team-a/api</code> and <code>team-b/api</code> are different entity references. Almost every org just uses the single <code>default</code> namespace for everything — Chapter 3 covers the rare cases where more than one is worth the added complexity.</div>

                    <h5>⑦ From Click to Pixel — How React Actually Turns JSON Into the Page You See</h5>
                    <p>§1.10's step 7 said "your browser turns that data into the page you see" — true, but worth unpacking, because it explains how an entity page can show DIFFERENT cards for a Component vs an API vs a Resource, all from the same generic frontend code.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   1. The React app receives the entity JSON (kind: "Component", spec: {...})
   2. It looks up which PAGE LAYOUT is registered for that Kind
        Component → EntityPage with tabs: Overview / CI-CD / Kubernetes / Docs
        API       → EntityPage with tabs: Overview / Definition / Consumers
   3. Each TAB is really just... a Plugin's frontend component, slotted in
   4. Each of THOSE components independently does its own hop-①-through-④
      (Under the Hood §②) to fetch ITS OWN data (a CI/CD status, a K8s
      pod list) and renders itself into its tab
   5. React assembles all these independently-fetched tabs into one page

   ── The page doesn't "know" what a Component is. It just knows: for
      Kind X, render Plugin list [A, B, C] — and hands each one a blank
      tab to fill in however it wants. ──
                    </pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 This is WHY Backstage is "extensible" (§1.3).</strong> Adding a brand-new tab to every Component's entity page never means editing Backstage's core frontend — it means registering one more Plugin against the <code>Component</code> Kind. Chapters 16-18 build exactly this.</div>

                    <p><strong>🔬 Deeper (exam + beyond):</strong> every fact above is really the same two-hop rule, seen from seven different angles. The full trace (①) is the two-hop rule played out literally, step by step. The plugin-shell pattern (②) is that same rule repeated once per card on a page — four plugins, four independent two-hop trips, one shared shell. The entity's four-field shape (③) is simply <em>what</em> travels across the hop. The auth header (④) shows <em>where</em> the hop is checked — always at the browser→server boundary, never before, never by the database. CNCF governance (⑤) is a fact about who steers the project that built this architecture, not the architecture itself — worth keeping separate in your head. The entity reference (⑥) is just the address you hand the server so it knows which hop's target you mean. And the render step (⑦) is what your browser does with whatever JSON that hop handed back. Once you can name which of these seven ideas a bug report, a code review comment, or an exam question is really pointing at, "how does Backstage actually work" stops being one large fact to memorize and becomes seven small, connected ones — and Chapter 3's entity model, Chapters 16-18's custom plugin, and Chapter 26's real auth providers are each just a deeper look at one of them.</p>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch1-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Backstage is an open source FRAMEWORK</strong> for building developer portals — not a finished product you just install (§1.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Created at Spotify</strong>, open-sourced 2020, donated to the <strong>CNCF</strong> the same year — currently <strong>Incubating</strong> (since March 2022), <em>not</em> Graduated (§1.2, 1.7).</li>
                    <li><strong>The 4 Pillars</strong> — Catalog, Templates, TechDocs, Plugins — map to 4 different questions: find, create, understand, extend (§1.3, 1.8).</li>
                    <li><strong>No single competitor</strong> combines all four as one integrated system — Backstage sits <em>on top of</em> GitLab/Jenkins/Confluence rather than replacing them (§1.4, 1.5).</li>
                    <li><strong>Proven at real scale</strong> — Spotify, Netflix, American Airlines, Expedia, Zalando all run Backstage in production (§1.6).</li>
                    <li><strong>Core vocabulary</strong> — Entity, Kind, Component, Resource, Annotation, Plugin, Template — unlocks nearly every later chapter (§1.9).</li>
                    <li><strong>Your browser never talks to the database directly</strong> — every action is browser → server → database, the two-hop rule (§1.10).</li>
                    <li><strong>"Runs on Kubernetes" and "displays Kubernetes data"</strong> are two separate relationships, not one (§1.11).</li>
                    <li><strong>easypj's jobpost</strong> Django app will become a <code>Component</code> + <code>API</code> + <code>Resource</code> in the Catalog (§1.12).</li>
                    <li><strong>A single <code>catalog-info.yaml</code></strong> is the only app-code change required to onboard an existing service — zero rewrites (§1.13).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch1-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Calling Backstage "a monitoring tool."</strong> It's a portal <em>framework</em> — monitoring shows up only because a plugin embeds a monitoring tool's data (§1.13).</li>
                    <li><strong>Calling Backstage "CNCF Graduated."</strong> It is CNCF <strong>Incubating</strong> — Graduated is a higher, unreached bar (security audit + 2+ years multi-company production use) (§1.7).</li>
                    <li><strong>Assuming "runs on K8s" implies "shows K8s data" (or the reverse).</strong> They're independent relationships and the exam tests them separately (§1.11).</li>
                    <li><strong>Confusing "Component" with "Resource."</strong> A <code>Component</code> is software <em>you build</em> (jobpost); a <code>Resource</code> is infrastructure <em>you use</em> (its Postgres database) (§1.9, 1.12).</li>
                    <li><strong>Thinking SQLite is fine for production</strong> because the create-app wizard defaults to it. It's single-writer, dev-only — production always needs PostgreSQL (§1.13, and again in Ch 2, 4, 19).</li>
                    <li><strong>Believing adoption means rewriting the app.</strong> Onboarding an existing service is additive — one <code>catalog-info.yaml</code> file, zero application code changes (§1.12, 1.13).</li>
                    <li><strong>Treating Annotations and Labels as the same idea before Ch 3 formally splits them.</strong> §1.9 already plants the "sticky note" rule for Annotations — don't skip it.</li>
                    <li><strong>Assuming Backstage replaces GitLab/Jenkins/Confluence.</strong> It sits on top of them and surfaces their data in one place — it doesn't compete with what they each do best (§1.4, 1.5).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

