                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A teammate says: "the frontend just reads straight from Postgres when you click an entity — it's faster that way." What's wrong with this claim?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>It's false — the browser <strong>never</strong> talks to the database directly. Every interaction is two hops: browser → server, then server → database. The server is the only thing holding a database connection.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">Correcting the claim</div>
<pre>
  ❌ claimed:  🌐 browser ───────────────────▶ 🗄️ database
  ✅ actual:   🌐 browser ──▶ 🖥️ server ──▶ 🗄️ database
</pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The two-hop rule (§1.10) isn't a performance choice — it's what lets the server enforce auth, run one query pattern, and swap the database engine without ever touching browser code. A "the browser reads the DB directly" claim always signals a broken mental model, never a real optimization.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

