                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Catalog</strong> (to see what services/teams/APIs exist) and <strong>TechDocs</strong> (to read how things actually work) — exactly the Day-1 onboarding stage from §1.6's developer journey diagram.</p>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
   DAY 1  📦 Catalog + 📖 TechDocs   →  DAY 2  🚀 Templates  →  ONGOING  🔌 Plugins
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Templates and Plugins matter later — once the new hire is contributing (Day 2) or living in the dashboard daily (Ongoing). Day 1 is purely about orientation, which is Catalog + TechDocs's job.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Your engineering manager says: "We already have Confluence for docs and GitLab for CI/CD — why would we add Backstage?" Give a precise, non-dismissive answer.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Backstage doesn't replace either — it sits on top of both. Confluence stays your long-form docs tool (or gets replaced specifically for API docs by TechDocs, which is code-linked and auto-published); GitLab keeps hosting code and running pipelines. What Backstage adds is the piece neither one provides: a single, code-aware, searchable index of every service, its owner, and its live status — pulling GitLab's CI results onto the same page as Confluence's (or TechDocs') content.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Keeps doing</th><th>Backstage adds</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Confluence</td><td>Long-form docs</td><td>Code-linked TechDocs, on the same entity page</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>GitLab</td><td>Hosting + CI/CD</td><td>CI status surfaced on the entity page too</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is §1.4's exact table in conversation form — the correct answer names what Backstage adds (a unified, code-aware Catalog) rather than claiming false superiority over tools that are genuinely good at their own job.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A developer wants their service's PagerDuty on-call schedule to show up on its entity page. Which Pillar builds this, and what pattern does it follow under the hood?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Plugins</strong> — specifically, a PagerDuty plugin card. Under the hood it follows the exact two-hop pattern from Under the Hood §②: browser asks the Backstage backend, the backend's PagerDuty plugin calls PagerDuty's own API, and the result renders as a card. No direct browser-to-PagerDuty call ever happens.</p>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
   🌐 browser ──▶ 🖥️ Backstage backend ──▶ 🔌 PagerDuty plugin ──▶ 🌐 PagerDuty API
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>"I want X tool's info on this page" is always the Plugins Pillar — the one-word test from §1.8 (extending) applies directly.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You have 10 minutes in a leadership meeting to pitch Backstage adoption to a new-hire developer, an engineering manager, and a platform engineer sitting side by side. Per §1.3, which single benefit do you lead with for each — and why does reciting the full 8-benefit list undersell the pitch?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Lead with the ONE benefit each role already feels the pain of, not the full list: the new hire gets <strong>🔍 Centralized Discovery</strong> (they're living §1.1's "Where's Waldo?" problem right now), the engineering manager gets <strong>🖥️ Single Pane of Glass</strong> (they want one view across everything their team owns), and the platform engineer gets <strong>🔌 Plugin Ecosystem + ☸️ Kubernetes Native</strong> (they're the one who'll build and maintain the integrations).</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Stakeholder</th><th>Benefit that lands</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>New hire / junior developer</td><td>🔍 Centralized Discovery</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Engineering manager</td><td>🖥️ Single Pane of Glass</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Platform/DevOps engineer</td><td>🔌 Plugin Ecosystem + ☸️ K8s Native</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>§1.3's "which benefit sells it, by role" table exists for exactly this situation — reciting all 8 benefits to everyone waters the pitch down, while naming the one pain each specific person already feels makes the case land in one sentence instead of eight.</p>
