                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Your team needs to spin up a brand-new microservice with the org's standard CI/CD wiring and Kubernetes manifests already in place. Which Pillar, and roughly how long should it take per §1.6's data?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Software Templates</strong>, via the Scaffolder. Per §1.6's "Week 2" stage of the developer journey, this is a ~5-minute form-fill, not a manual multi-hour setup.</p>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
   👤 Dev → 🚀 Create → 📋 Pick Template → ✏️ Fill form → ✅ repo+CI/CD+K8s ready  (~5 min)
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>"I need a brand-new microservice" is the canonical Templates trigger phrase from §1.8's decision guide — the Scaffolder engine (§1.9) is what actually runs the Template.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Your platform team says: "We're installing the Kubernetes plugin next quarter, so Backstage itself will finally run more reliably." What's the flaw in this reasoning?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The flaw: installing the Kubernetes <em>plugin</em> (Relationship #2 — displaying K8s data) has nothing to do with Backstage's own reliability. Backstage's own uptime/self-healing depends on Relationship #1 — whether Backstage itself is <em>deployed on</em> Kubernetes (or another resilient platform), which is a completely separate decision covered in Chapter 19.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Claim</th><th>Actually affects</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>"Install the K8s plugin"</td><td>Relationship #2 — data display only</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>"Backstage runs more reliably"</td><td>Relationship #1 — a separate hosting decision (Ch 19)</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is §1.11's CBA Gotcha in disguise: conflating "we can now see K8s data" with "we now run more reliably on K8s" — one is a data-display feature, the other is a hosting decision, and neither implies the other.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A teammate finds a plugin on the Backstage plugin marketplace with 3 GitHub stars and no commits in 14 months, and reasons: "it's listed on the marketplace, so it must be safe and maintained." What's the flaw, and what should they check first?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The flaw: marketplace listing alone is not a maintenance or safety guarantee. Backstage explicitly separates <strong>core plugins</strong> (ship with every install, maintained by the core team) from <strong>community plugins</strong> (independently authored, quality and upkeep vary widely). Before depending on any community plugin in production, check its own repo directly — recent commits, open issues, and maintainer responsiveness.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Plugin type</th><th>Maintained by</th><th>Guarantee</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Core</td><td>Backstage core team</td><td>✅ Ships with every install</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Community</td><td>Independent authors</td><td>⚠️ Varies — verify the repo yourself</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is §1.13's newest myth, tested precisely because "it's in the ecosystem" quietly gets rounded up to "it's supported" — the 200+ plugin count (§1.3) is a strength, but it also means due diligence on any one community plugin is the adopting team's job, not the Backstage project's (Chapter 16).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A startup with exactly 12 microservices and no dedicated platform team asks whether Backstage is "overkill" for their size. Using §1.1 and §1.6's numbers, make the case either way.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Not overkill — §1.13 explicitly states any team with 10+ services already benefits, and this startup is past that threshold. §1.1's ~2.5-hours-a-day pain doesn't require Spotify's scale to be real; it starts accumulating the moment more than a handful of services exist and nobody remembers who owns what.</p>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
   ROI threshold:  10+ services  ──▶  this startup: 12 services  ──▶  past the line ✅
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The myth "it's only for huge companies" (§1.13) is directly aimed at exactly this kind of question — the ROI curve in §1.6 starts on day one, it doesn't require thousands of services to pay off.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A developer asks which entity Kind to use for jobpost's PostgreSQL database — <code>Component</code> or <code>Resource</code>? Justify the choice.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Resource</strong>. §1.9's rule: a Component is software <em>you build</em>; a Resource is infrastructure <em>you use</em>. jobpost's team didn't write PostgreSQL — they consume it, which makes it a Resource, exactly as §1.12's mapping table shows.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Test</th><th>PostgreSQL DB</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Component</td><td>Did we build it?</td><td>❌ No</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Resource</td><td>Do we consume it?</td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This build-vs-use distinction is the single fastest way to classify any new entity correctly, and it's tested precisely because Component/Resource is the most commonly confused Kind pair.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Two different teams each want to register a service literally named "api" in the Catalog. Will this collide? Why or why not?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Only if they're in the SAME namespace. <code>team-a/api</code> and <code>team-b/api</code> are different entity references and coexist fine; two teams both using the bare <code>default</code> namespace with the same name WOULD collide.</p>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
   team-a/api   ✅ coexists with   team-b/api      (different namespaces)
   default/api  ❌ collides with   default/api     (same namespace, same name)
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Under the Hood §⑥'s namespace explanation directly answers this — most orgs never hit this because they rarely reuse identical short names, but it's exactly why namespaces exist as an escape hatch.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Your org is rolling out Backstage top-down by mandate next quarter. Per §1.6's adoption-pattern table, what's the single biggest risk of this approach, and what mitigates it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The risk: Catalog quality suffers because auto-generated entities get stale ownership data nobody personally maintains. The mitigation: pair the rollout with a clear golden-path Software Template (Ch 11-13) so onboarding isn't a manual, easily-neglected chore.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Risk</th><th>Mitigation</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Stale, auto-generated ownership data</td><td>Golden-path Software Template (Ch 11-13)</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Top-down rollouts move fast but risk becoming "just another checkbox" — the fix isn't slowing down, it's giving people a Template that makes doing it right the path of least resistance.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A junior engineer says: "I need to learn TypeScript before I can even use Backstage day to day." Correct this, precisely.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>False for daily use. Only <em>customizing</em> Backstage (writing Plugins) requires TypeScript. Searching the Catalog, running a Template, or reading TechDocs requires none — §1.13's newly-added myth on this exact point.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Activity</th><th>Needs TypeScript?</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Search Catalog / run a Template / read TechDocs</td><td>❌ No</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Write a Plugin (customize Backstage)</td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This distinction matters for onboarding messaging — telling every developer "learn TypeScript first" needlessly gatekeeps the 3 Pillars (Catalog, Templates, TechDocs) that need zero code knowledge to use.</p>
