                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q13</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A stakeholder pushes back: "even if we adopt Backstage, won't the two extra network hops (browser→server→database) make everything feel slower?" Answer using Under the Hood §④'s reasoning.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>No — the browser→server hop is the only one crossing the public internet; server→database typically stays inside the same data center, adding single-digit milliseconds. The server hop also enables caching and pre-computation that a direct-to-database model couldn't offer at all.</p>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
   🌐 browser ──(internet, the slow part)──▶ 🖥️ server ──(same data center, ~ms)──▶ 🗄️ db
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>§1.10's "why 2 hops is faster, not slower" argument reframes a naive performance objection — the two-hop rule is what makes the system faster at scale, not a compromise for safety alone.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q14</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A teammate wants to add a brand-new "Compliance Status" tab to every Component's entity page. Per Under the Hood §⑦, does this require editing Backstage's core frontend code?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>No. It requires registering a new Plugin against the <code>Component</code> Kind's page layout — the core frontend never needs to change, because it only knows "for Kind X, render Plugin list [A, B, C]."</p>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
   Component page tabs:  Overview · CI/CD · Kubernetes · Docs · [+ Compliance ← new Plugin]
                                                                    core frontend: unchanged
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is exactly why Backstage is called "extensible" — new functionality is additive (a new Plugin), never a modification of shared core code.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q15</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Your org runs Backstage on AWS ECS with no Kubernetes cluster anywhere near it, yet a different team's Kubernetes plugin still shows live pod health on their own entity pages. Is this a contradiction?</div>
