                <p><strong>knex.js</strong>, labeled on the Backend↔Database connector above, is the Node.js library that translates backend code into the actual SQL each database understands — §2.4 covers exactly what it does and why migrations depend on it.</p>

                <div class="analogy-box">
                    <h5>🍽️ Analogy: A Restaurant</h5>
                    <p>Picture a restaurant. The <strong>frontend</strong> is the dining room — the menu, the tables, what the customer actually sees and touches. The <strong>backend</strong> is the kitchen — where the real work happens, orders get cooked, nobody in the dining room can see it directly. The <strong>database</strong> is the walk-in pantry — where every ingredient is stored, and only the kitchen (never the dining room) is allowed to go in and grab something.</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>🍽️ Restaurant</th><th>☸️ Backstage Layer</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Dining room, menu, waiter</td><td>🖥️ Frontend</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Kitchen, chefs, cooking</td><td>⚙️ Backend</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Walk-in pantry</td><td>💾 Database</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p class="mb-0">A customer never walks into the pantry themselves — they order through the waiter, who tells the kitchen, who fetches from the pantry. That's <em>exactly</em> Chapter 1's two-hop rule, just with real names attached to each stop.</p>
