                <h4>The Full Request Lifecycle — From Browser to Database and Back</h4>
                <p>This is Chapter 1's "browser → server → database" two-hop rule, now with every real stop named. Trace it once here and you'll recognize this exact shape in every plugin you meet later:</p>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active"><div class="step-number">1</div><h5>Browser</h5><p>You visit <code>https://backstage.easypj.com/catalog/default/component/jobpost</code></p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">2</div><h5>React Router</h5><p>Matches the URL to the Catalog Plugin's route</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">3</div><h5>Catalog Plugin UI</h5><p>Renders <code>&lt;EntityPage&gt;</code>, calls <code>useApi(catalogApiRef)</code></p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">4</div><h5>API Client</h5><p><code>fetch</code> sends <code>POST /api/catalog/entities/by-name</code></p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">5</div><h5>Auth Middleware</h5><p>Validates the JWT token, checks permissions</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">6</div><h5>Catalog Backend</h5><p>Processes the request, queries the database</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">7</div><h5>PostgreSQL</h5><p><code>SELECT * FROM entities WHERE name = 'jobpost'</code></p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">8</div><h5>Response flows back</h5><p>DB → Backend → API → Frontend → React renders the page</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 The Number Worth Memorizing</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">All 8 hops above complete in <strong>~50ms with proper caching</strong> — fast enough that a user never perceives "a request happened," they just see the page. §2.7 dedicates a full deep dive to exactly where those milliseconds go, and what breaks when the number climbs to seconds instead.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Terms — Decoding Steps 3-5's Vocabulary</h5>
                    <p>If "REST API," "JWT," and <code>useApi()</code> are new terms, here's exactly what each one means before you see them again in every later chapter:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Plain-English meaning</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>REST API</strong></td><td>A convention for structuring HTTP requests around resources and verbs — <code>GET</code> = "read this," <code>POST</code> = "create/send this," <code>PUT</code>/<code>DELETE</code> = "update/remove this." Step 4's <code>POST /api/catalog/entities/by-name</code> is a REST call asking the backend to look up one specific entity.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>JWT</strong> (JSON Web Token)</td><td>A signed, tamper-proof block of text that encodes "who you are" (your identity) so the server doesn't need to look you up in a session database on every single request. Under the Hood §② covers exactly how it's checked.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>useApi(catalogApiRef)</code></td><td>A React hook — Backstage's standard way for any frontend component to "ask for" a fully-configured API client (here, the Catalog's) without constructing one by hand. Every plugin's UI uses this exact same pattern to reach its own backend.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p class="mb-0">Notice the shape: a REST call carries a JWT to prove identity, and <code>useApi()</code> is just the frontend's tidy way of firing that REST call. Three terms, one mechanism.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Term — Utility API (the Official Name for What <code>catalogApiRef</code> Actually Is)</h5>
                    <p><code>catalogApiRef</code> above is an instance of a <strong>Utility API</strong> — Backstage's official term for a small, typed, framework-agnostic object that provides one specific piece of functionality (here: "talk to the Catalog backend") to whichever component calls <code>useApi()</code> for it. Every core plugin publishes its own Utility API this way — <code>scaffolderApiRef</code>, <code>techdocsApiRef</code>, <code>searchApiRef</code> — and one plugin's UI can call ANOTHER plugin's Utility API directly, in-process, with no network hop required.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0">This is the frontend's exact counterpart to §2.8's backend Extension Points: both are explicit, typed "here's how you may plug into me" interfaces — Extension Points let a backend plugin extend another plugin's server-side behavior; Utility APIs let a frontend component consume another plugin's client-side functionality. Same philosophy (declare it, don't hide it), applied to opposite sides of the stack.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §2.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s2-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">2.3 Backend Architecture — Plugin System</h3>
                <p>Every backend capability — Catalog, Scaffolder, TechDocs, Search, Auth — is a <strong>backend plugin</strong>. Each one follows the exact same 4-stage lifecycle, and (this is the section's single most important, most-tested fact) each one runs as its own independent unit.</p>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active"><div class="step-number">1</div><h5>Install</h5><p><code>npm</code>/<code>yarn</code> adds <code>@backstage/plugin-xxx</code></p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">2</div><h5>Configure</h5><p>Its settings are added to <code>app-config.yaml</code></p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">3</div><h5>Register</h5><p><code>createBackendPlugin({ pluginId, register })</code> wires it in</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">4</div><h5>Run</h5><p>The plugin is active, serving its own routes</p></div>
                </div>

                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Backend plugin type</th><th>What it owns</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Catalog</td><td>Entity ingestion, storage, querying</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Scaffolder</td><td>Template execution engine</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>TechDocs</td><td>Documentation generation &amp; serving</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Search</td><td>Full-text search across entities</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Auth</td><td>Authentication &amp; authorization</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ The Exam's Favorite Backend Myth: "There's an Internal Event Bus"</h5>
                    <p>It's tempting to assume backend plugins are secretly wired together by a message broker or pub/sub event bus, quietly broadcasting "entity created!" events to anyone listening. <strong>They are not.</strong> The official architecture docs describe no such generic internal bus. Instead:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Backend plugins run as <strong>independent units</strong> and only reach each other <em>"over the wire"</em> via their own HTTP endpoints.</li>
                        <li>When one plugin needs to react to another's output, it does so through explicitly declared <strong>Extension Points</strong> and <strong>modules</strong> — never a hidden broadcast channel.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p class="mb-0">The official architecture docs put it in exactly these words: <strong>"If plugins want to communicate with each other, they must do so over the wire. There can be no direct communication between plugins through code."</strong> That second sentence is stronger than most people expect — it's not just "avoid it," it's "structurally not a supported path."</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Plugin Communication — HTTP APIs, Not a Hidden Bus</div>
<pre>
   ┌──────────────────┐
   │  🚀 Scaffolder    │
   │  Plugin           │
   └────────┬─────────┘
            │  1. POST /api/catalog/entities
            │     (register the entity it just scaffolded)
            ▼
   ┌──────────────────┐
   │  📦 Catalog       │
   │  Plugin           │
   └────────┬─────────┘
            │  2. Catalog processes it, emits it into the search index
            ▼
   ┌──────────────────┐
   │  🔍 Search        │
   │  Plugin           │
   └──────────────────┘

   💡 Plugins call each other's REST APIs directly (as shown above), or extend
      one another through explicit Extension Points/modules — there is no
      hidden pub/sub event bus wiring them together.
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Why This Matters Operationally, Not Just for Trivia</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">If the Scaffolder finishes a task and the Catalog doesn't immediately show the new entity, you are <strong>not</strong> looking for a broken "event" — there isn't one to break. You're looking for whichever explicit integration (an Extension Point, or the Catalog's own periodic refresh cycle) is supposed to bridge the two. This single reframing turns a vague "something's not syncing" bug into a specific, searchable one.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Anti-Pattern: Importing Another Plugin's Internals Directly</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">Because every backend plugin lives in the same monorepo (§2.9), it's technically <em>possible</em> to <code>import</code> a function straight out of another plugin's source and call it in-process — skipping the HTTP hop entirely. <strong>Don't.</strong> This silently recreates the "hidden coupling" problem the whole Extension Point design exists to prevent: the moment either plugin changes its internal code (not its public API), the other breaks with no warning. If you find yourself reaching for a direct import across plugin boundaries, that's the signal to either call the owning plugin's REST API or register a proper Extension Point/module instead.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The 3 Official Plugin Architecture Types</h4>
                <p>Per the official Backstage architecture docs, every plugin — frontend or backend — falls into exactly one of 3 categories, based on <em>where its data comes from</em>. It's placed here rather than in §2.2 because the distinction is really about a plugin's <strong>backend data source</strong> — a frontend-only ("Standalone") plugin is defined by having <em>no</em> backend call at all, which only makes sense once you've seen what backend calls normally look like. Knowing which type a plugin is tells you instantly what it depends on and what can break it:</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>What it does</th><th>Real example</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Standalone</strong></td><td>Runs entirely in the browser — renders its own data, makes NO API requests to anything</td><td>Tech Radar plugin (a static, hardcoded visualization)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Service-backed</strong></td><td>Calls a service that lives <em>inside</em> your own org's ecosystem</td><td>A Lighthouse plugin calling your org's own <code>lighthouse-audit-service</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Third-party-backed</strong></td><td>Calls an external SaaS service <em>outside</em> your org — routed through Backstage's own proxy (§2.5's <code>proxy:</code> config) because a browser can't call most third-party APIs directly (CORS)</td><td>A CircleCI plugin calling CircleCI's public API</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="uth-callout mb-0"><strong>🔑 Why "third-party" needs a proxy specifically.</strong> Browsers block most cross-origin requests to external domains (CORS). Backstage's backend proxy exists precisely to make that third-party call <em>on the browser's behalf</em>, then hand the result back — which is exactly what §2.5's <code>proxy:</code> config section wires up.</div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §2.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s2-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">2.4 Database Schema Overview</h3>
                <p>Everything the backend persists lands in one of a handful of core tables. You don't need to memorize a full schema — you need to know which table owns which job, because that's what tells you where to look when something's wrong.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Table</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Owned by</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>entities</code></td><td>All catalog entities, <strong>raw</strong> — exactly as ingested</td><td>Catalog</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>final_entities</code></td><td>The <strong>processed/stitched</strong> entity data — what the UI actually reads</td><td>Catalog</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>locations</code></td><td>Where to find each entity's YAML file</td><td>Catalog</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>search_index</code></td><td>Full-text search index</td><td>Search</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>scaffolder_tasks</code></td><td>Template execution history/status</td><td>Scaffolder</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>user_settings</code></td><td>Per-user preferences</td><td>Core</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 The Real Reason Plugin A Can't Read Plugin B's Tables</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">This isn't just a convention — it's how knex.js is wired up. Per Backstage's own architecture docs, <strong>"the Backstage backend and its built-in plugins are based on the Knex library, and set up a separate logical database per plugin."</strong> Catalog's tables (<code>entities</code>, <code>final_entities</code>, <code>locations</code>) live in a logically separate database from Search's <code>search_index</code> or Scaffolder's <code>scaffolder_tasks</code> — which is the concrete, physical reason §2.3's "no direct communication between plugins through code" rule holds even at the database layer, not only at the API layer.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ <code>entities</code> vs <code>final_entities</code> — the Exam's Favorite Database Trap</h5>
                    <p><code>entities</code> holds the <strong>raw</strong>, just-ingested row — exactly what a <code>catalog-info.yaml</code> file said. <code>final_entities</code> holds the <strong>processed</strong> result after Catalog processors run (resolving relations, applying defaults, stitching in computed fields). When jobpost's entity page shows something that doesn't match its YAML file verbatim, <code>final_entities</code> — not <code>entities</code> — is where the discrepancy lives.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">How a Write Flows Through the Tables</div>
<pre>
   catalog-info.yaml  ──ingest──▶  entities (raw)  ──process──▶  final_entities (stitched)
                                                                        │
                                                                        ▼
                                                              📖 UI reads THIS one
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Migrations — How Backstage Handles DB Upgrades</h4>
                <p><strong>knex.js</strong> is a Node.js "query builder" library — it lets backend code describe a database change (like "add this column") once in JavaScript, and knex translates that into the correct SQL for whichever database you're running (PostgreSQL or SQLite), so the same migration file works on both. Backstage uses knex.js migrations under the hood (§2.9 shows exactly which package owns this). Upgrading Backstage's version runs any new migrations automatically on backend startup — you don't hand-write <code>ALTER TABLE</code> statements yourself.</p>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Day-2 Ops Note</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">Before any major version upgrade, back up the database first — migrations run automatically and are rarely reversible by hand. This is the exact same instinct as etcd backups in the Kubernetes Template chapters: a migration is a one-way door.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning mb-0">
                    <h5>⚠️ Never Hand-Edit <code>final_entities</code></h5>
                    <p class="mb-0"><code>final_entities</code> is a <strong>derived</strong> table — the Catalog processor chain (Under the Hood §①) regenerates it from <code>entities</code> on every processing pass. Manually editing a row in <code>final_entities</code> to "fix" something is pointless: the next processing cycle silently overwrites your edit with a freshly re-derived value. If something in <code>final_entities</code> is wrong, the fix always belongs upstream — either in the source <code>catalog-info.yaml</code>, or in whichever processor produced the bad value.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §2.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s2-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">2.5 Configuration System — app-config.yaml</h3>
                <p><code>app-config.yaml</code> is the single central configuration file every layer reads from. Rather than each plugin inventing its own config file, Backstage funnels everything — database credentials, auth providers, catalog sources — through one YAML tree, split into top-level sections that each own exactly one concern.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">app-config.yaml — annotated skeleton</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># WHAT the app is called and where it's served from</span>
