                <h4>Per-Environment Configs &amp; Secrets</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🌍</div>
                        <h5>Environment-Specific Overrides</h5>
                        <p><code>app-config.local.yaml</code> and <code>app-config.production.yaml</code> layer on top of the base file — local overrides for dev, production overrides for prod, without duplicating the whole file.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔒</div>
                        <h5>Secrets Never Live in the YAML</h5>
                        <p>Notice every sensitive value above is <code>${ENV_VAR}</code> — substituted from environment variables at startup, never committed to the YAML file itself.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Why Environment-Variable Substitution Matters</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0"><code>app-config.yaml</code> is typically checked into Git — that's the whole point, so config changes get reviewed like code. But a Git-committed file can never safely hold a real database password or GitHub client secret. The <code>${VAR}</code> syntax is how Backstage lets the <em>structure</em> of config live in Git while the actual <em>secret values</em> stay in environment variables, injected only at runtime.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning mb-0">
                    <h5>⚠️ The Most Common Real-World Mistake: a Hard-Coded Secret</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">It's easy to type <code>clientSecret: abc123realSecretValue</code> directly into <code>app-config.yaml</code> while testing locally — "just for now" — and then commit it. Once a secret is in Git history, rotating the credential is <strong>mandatory</strong>, not optional: removing the line in a later commit does NOT remove it from history. Always start with <code>${VAR}</code> syntax, even for local-only testing, and keep the real value in an untracked <code>.env</code> file or your shell environment.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

