            <!-- ─────────── §2.7 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s2-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">2.7 Request Flow Lifecycle — The Deep Dive</h3>
                <p>§2.2 walked the 8-hop request lifecycle at a beginner's pace. Now go one level deeper: <em>where</em> does each millisecond of that ~50ms actually go, and what happens when a hop is slow or fails?</p>

                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Hop</th><th>Typical latency</th><th>What's actually happening</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>1-2</td><td>Browser → React Router</td><td>&lt;1ms</td><td>Pure client-side routing, no network involved yet</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>3-4</td><td>Component render → API call</td><td>~1-2ms</td><td>React renders, <code>fetch</code> fires the HTTP request</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>—</td><td>Network transit (round trip)</td><td>~15-25ms</td><td>The actual browser↔server hop over the network — the single biggest cost, and the one earlier steps can't shrink</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>5</td><td>Auth middleware</td><td>~2-5ms</td><td>JWT signature check + permission lookup (cached after first hit)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>6-7</td><td>Backend → Database</td><td>~5-15ms</td><td>The SQL query itself — the single biggest legitimate <em>server-side</em> cost</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>8</td><td>Response → render</td><td>~1-2ms</td><td>JSON serializes back, React re-renders with real data</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <p>Add these up and the rows span roughly <strong>25-50ms</strong> depending on where each hop lands within its own range — the low end on a quiet network with a warm cache, the high end under real-world conditions. Either way you land in the same neighborhood as §2.2's rounded "~50ms" figure, and notice which row dominates: <strong>network transit</strong>, not any of the application code. This is exactly why §2.7's later caching discussion targets the backend/DB hops specifically — they're the only ones your own code can actually influence.</p>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Where Caching Actually Buys You Speed</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">Auth's permission lookup (hop 5) and the Catalog's <code>final_entities</code> reads (hop 6-7) are the two most cacheable steps — Backstage caches both aggressively, which is why repeated visits to the same entity page feel instant while the very first load of a session is a touch slower.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>What Happens When a Hop Fails — the Same Flow, in Reverse</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Failure Modes, Mapped to the Hop That Causes Them</div>
<pre>
   Symptom                              Failing hop            Where to look
   ────────────────────────────────     ──────────────────     ──────────────────────
   Blank page, console errors           1-3 (frontend)          Browser devtools console
   "401 Unauthorized" toast             5 (auth middleware)     Backend auth logs, JWT expiry
   "403 Forbidden" toast                5 (permissions)         RBAC/permission policy (Ch 24)
   Spinner forever, no error            6-7 (backend/DB)        Backend logs, DB connection pool
   Page loads, data looks stale         6-7 (cached read)       Cache TTL, final_entities refresh
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Terms — "Connection Pool" and "Cache TTL," Unpacked</h5>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Plain-English meaning</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Connection pool</strong></td><td>Opening a fresh database connection per request is slow, so the backend keeps a small stock of already-open connections ready to reuse. If every connection in the pool is busy, new requests queue up and wait — which is exactly why "spinner forever" can mean the pool is exhausted, not that the database itself is down.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Cache TTL</strong> (Time To Live)</td><td>A cached value is only trusted for a set duration (its TTL) before it's re-fetched from the source of truth. "Data looks stale" usually means you're still inside that window — the cache hasn't expired yet, so it's still serving the old value on purpose, not by accident.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                    <h5>⚡ Speed Tip — Bisect, Don't Guess</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">When a page is slow or broken, open browser devtools' Network tab FIRST. If the API call itself is slow, the problem is hops 5-7 (backend/DB) — go check backend logs. If the API call is fast but the page still looks wrong, the problem is hops 1-4 or 8 (frontend rendering) — go check the browser console instead. This one triage step cuts your search space in half immediately.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §2.8 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s2-8">
                <h3 class="section-h">2.8 Plugin Communication &amp; Extension Points</h3>
                <p>§2.3 already told you WHAT plugins don't have (a hidden event bus). This section shows you what they use INSTEAD on the <strong>backend</strong> — the real, named pattern: <strong>Extension Points</strong> and <strong>modules</strong>, part of Backstage's backend system. (§2.2's Utility API is the equivalent story for the <strong>frontend</strong> half of the stack — same philosophy, opposite side.)</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Term — Extension Point</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">An <strong>Extension Point</strong> is an explicit interface one plugin publishes, saying "here's exactly how you may extend or configure me." Nothing implicit, nothing broadcast — a plugin that wants to extend another must import that specific Extension Point and register against it on purpose.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">the real pattern, simplified</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-typescript"><span class="comment">// 1. The Catalog plugin PUBLISHES an Extension Point —</span>
<span class="comment">//    an explicit, typed way for others to add custom entity processors</span>
export const catalogProcessingExtensionPoint =
  createExtensionPoint&lt;CatalogProcessingApi&gt;({
    id: 'catalog.processing',
  });

<span class="comment">// 2. A SEPARATE module explicitly registers against it —</span>
<span class="comment">//    this is the ONLY way to hook into Catalog processing</span>
export const jobpostCatalogModule = createBackendModule({
  pluginId: 'catalog',
  moduleId: 'jobpost-custom-processor',
  register(reg) {
    reg.registerInit({
      deps: { catalog: catalogProcessingExtensionPoint },
      async init({ catalog }) {
        catalog.addProcessor(new JobpostEntityProcessor());
      },
    });
  },
});</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Extension Point Registration — Nothing Implicit, Every Step On Purpose</div>
<pre>
   ┌──────────────────────┐
   │  📦 Catalog Plugin    │  1. PUBLISHES an Extension Point on purpose
   │                       │     export const catalogProcessingExtensionPoint = createExtensionPoint(...)
   └──────────┬────────────┘
              │
              │  2. Your module explicitly IMPORTS that Extension Point
              ▼
   ┌──────────────────────┐
   │  🧩 jobpostCatalog    │  3. REGISTERS against it — deps: { catalog: catalogProcessingExtensionPoint }
   │  Module               │
   └──────────┬────────────┘
              │
              │  4. init() runs, adds your processor to the chain
              ▼
   ┌──────────────────────┐
   │  ⚙️ Catalog Plugin    │  Your processor now runs INSIDE Catalog's own processing chain
   │  (runtime)            │
   └──────────────────────┘

   💡 Compare to §2.3's diagram: there, two independent plugins talk over HTTP.
      Here, a module extends ONE specific plugin from the inside — but both paths
      share the same rule: nothing is implicit, and nothing is ever broadcast.
</pre>
                </div>

                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>What it is</th><th>Why it's not "a hidden event bus"</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Extension Point</td><td>A typed interface a plugin publishes on purpose</td><td>You must explicitly import it — nothing subscribes silently</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Module</td><td>A separate package that extends exactly ONE plugin, via its Extension Points</td><td>The dependency is visible in <code>package.json</code>, not hidden at runtime</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Core Service</td><td>Built-in dependency every plugin can request (logger, database, httpRouter)</td><td>Requested explicitly in a plugin's <code>register()</code> function, not auto-injected magically</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <div class="cka-gotcha mb-0">
                    <h5>⚠️ "Modules Extend Plugins" — Get the Direction Right</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">A common mix-up: thinking a module and a plugin are peers that "talk to each other." They're not — a <strong>module always extends exactly one specific plugin</strong> (notice <code>pluginId: 'catalog'</code> above). Two unrelated plugins (say, Catalog and Scaffolder) never share modules; if Scaffolder needs to react to something Catalog does, it goes back to §2.3's answer — an HTTP call to Catalog's own API, not a module.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

