            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch2-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — how the layers, services, and plugins really fit together</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>§2.1-2.9 gave you the map. This section goes one level deeper into the machinery that makes the map hold together: how a raw entity becomes a "final" one, how Auth actually validates a request, how Core Services get handed to a plugin without magic, why there's no event bus by design (not by accident), how the backend system itself was rebuilt in 2023, and how this all scales past one server.</p>

                    <h5>① The Catalog Processing Pipeline — How <code>entities</code> Becomes <code>final_entities</code></h5>
                    <p>§2.4 named the two tables; here's the pipeline that turns one into the other. A chain of <strong>processors</strong> runs on every ingested entity, each one adding or validating something:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   catalog-info.yaml
        │  ingest
        ▼
   entities table (raw)
        │
        ▼
   ┌─────────────────── PROCESSOR CHAIN (runs in order) ───────────────────┐
   │  1. Placeholder resolver   — resolves $text/$json refs in the YAML    │
   │  2. Location processor      — records WHERE this entity came from    │
   │  3. Annotation processors   — validate known annotations (e.g. K8s)  │
   │  4. Relation processor      — resolves ownedBy/providesApis links    │
   │  5. Your custom processor   — anything a module registered (§2.8)    │
   └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
        │  stitched result
        ▼
   final_entities table  ──▶  what the UI and API actually read
                    </pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Every processor is independently pluggable</strong> — exactly the same "explicit Extension Point" pattern from §2.8, just applied specifically to Catalog processing. There's no special-case code path; your custom jobpost processor from §2.8's code sample runs in this exact chain.</div>

                    <h5>② How Auth Middleware Actually Validates a Request</h5>
                    <p>§2.2's step 5 said "validates the JWT token, checks permissions" in one breath. Unpacked, it's a precise 3-check sequence, and knowing the order tells you exactly which error message maps to which failure:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Failure you'd see</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>1. Signature</td><td>Was this token issued by us, unmodified?</td><td><code>401 Unauthorized</code> — invalid signature</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>2. Expiry</td><td>Is the token still valid right now?</td><td><code>401 Unauthorized</code> — token expired</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>3. Permission policy</td><td>Is THIS identity allowed to do THIS action?</td><td><code>403 Forbidden</code> — permission denied</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 401 vs 403, precisely.</strong> A <code>401</code> means "we don't know who you are" (checks 1-2). A <code>403</code> means "we know exactly who you are, and the answer is no" (check 3). Conflating these two wastes debugging time — a <code>403</code> is never fixed by logging in again.</div>

                    <h5>③ Core Services — How a Plugin Gets a Logger Without Importing One</h5>
                    <p>§2.8's code sample used <code>deps: { catalog: catalogProcessingExtensionPoint }</code>. The same pattern is how EVERY plugin gets its basic tools — a database connection, a logger, an HTTP router — without ever writing <code>new Logger()</code> itself:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   register(reg) {
     reg.registerInit({
       deps: {
         logger: coreServices.logger,       ← ask for a logger
         database: coreServices.database,   ← ask for a DB connection
         httpRouter: coreServices.httpRouter, ← ask to expose routes
       },
       async init({ logger, database, httpRouter }) {
         logger.info('jobpost plugin starting');
         httpRouter.use('/jobpost-status', myRouter);
       },
     });
   }
                    </pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 This is dependency injection, not magic.</strong> A plugin declares exactly what it needs in <code>deps</code>, and the backend hands it over during <code>init()</code>. Nothing is globally available or auto-imported — if a plugin didn't ask for <code>coreServices.database</code> in its deps, it structurally CANNOT touch the database, which is a real security/isolation boundary, not just tidiness.</div>

                    <h5>④ Why No Event Bus Is a Deliberate Design Choice, Not a Missing Feature</h5>
                    <p>§2.3 and §2.8 both stated the fact. Here's the actual engineering reasoning behind it, because "why not just add one?" is a fair question:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>With a generic event bus</th><th>With explicit Extension Points (Backstage's real choice)</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Any plugin can silently subscribe to any event</td><td>Every extension is declared, typed, and visible in <code>package.json</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Debugging means asking "who's listening to this event?" org-wide</td><td>Debugging means reading one plugin's published Extension Point interface</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>A renamed event silently breaks unrelated listeners</td><td>TypeScript's compiler catches a breaking Extension Point change at build time</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Explicitness is the whole point.</strong> A generic event bus optimizes for "easy to add a listener." Backstage optimizes for "easy to know everything that can possibly happen to your plugin" — a deliberate trade-off favoring debuggability at scale over convenience.</div>

                    <h5>⑤ The 2023 Backend System Rewrite — Legacy vs New</h5>
                    <p>Chapter 1's CNCF timeline mentioned "2023 — new backend system." Here's concretely what changed: Backstage's backend used to be assembled with a more manual <code>createRouter</code>/plugin-environment pattern; the new system introduced exactly the <code>createBackendPlugin</code> / <code>createBackendModule</code> / <code>createExtensionPoint</code> / <code>coreServices</code> vocabulary this section has been using.</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Legacy backend system</th><th>New backend system (current)</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Wiring plugins together</td><td>Manual, imperative <code>createRouter()</code> calls per plugin in <code>index.ts</code></td><td>Declarative <code>backend.add(catalogPlugin())</code> calls</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Getting a logger/DB</td><td>Passed manually through a shared "PluginEnvironment" object</td><td>Explicitly requested via <code>deps</code> (③ above)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Extending another plugin</td><td>Ad-hoc, plugin-specific patterns</td><td>Standardized Extension Points (§2.8)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why this matters for a real deployment.</strong> If you ever read Backstage documentation or a plugin's README that mentions a "PluginEnvironment," you're looking at legacy-system material — new plugins and new setups (Chapter 4) always use the new backend system's vocabulary from this Under the Hood.</div>

                    <h5>⑥ Scaling the Backend Horizontally</h5>
                    <p>Because the backend is a stateless Node.js process (all real state lives in Postgres, §2.4), running Backstage in production almost always means multiple backend replicas behind a load balancer — the exact same pattern as any 12-factor app, and the exact same pattern Chapter 19 uses when deploying Backstage onto Kubernetes.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   🌐 Load Balancer
        │
        ├──▶ ⚙️ Backend replica 1 ──┐
        ├──▶ ⚙️ Backend replica 2 ──┼──▶ 💾 ONE shared PostgreSQL
        └──▶ ⚙️ Backend replica 3 ──┘     (the single source of truth)

   Any replica can serve any request — none of them holds session state locally.
                    </pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 This is WHY §1.13's "SQLite is dev-only" myth-bust matters so much here.</strong> SQLite is a single file on ONE disk — it structurally cannot be shared safely across multiple backend replicas. The moment you need more than one backend instance (any real production deployment), PostgreSQL isn't a nice-to-have, it's a hard requirement.</div>

                    <p>The database isn't the only thing that must be shared once you're multi-replica — §2.7's caching (the thing that gets those hops down to ~50ms) has the exact same problem. An in-memory cache lives inside ONE replica's process; replica 2 has no idea what replica 1 just cached. Backstage officially supports 5 cache stores, and the choice matters for exactly this reason:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Cache store</th><th>Shared across replicas?</th><th>When to use</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Memory (default)</td><td>❌ No — local to one replica</td><td>Local development only</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Memcache</td><td>✅ Yes</td><td>Production, simple key-value caching</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Redis</td><td>✅ Yes</td><td>Production, the most common real-world choice</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Valkey</td><td>✅ Yes</td><td>Production — the open-source Redis fork</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Infinispan</td><td>✅ Yes</td><td>Production, Java/enterprise-heavy environments</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout mb-0"><strong>🔑 Same lesson, different component.</strong> Just like the "Memory" cache option is the SQLite of caching — perfectly fine on your laptop, silently wrong the moment a second replica exists — the fix is identical in shape: swap the default for a shared, network-reachable store (Redis, in most real deployments) before you ever run more than one backend replica.</div>

                    <h5>⑦ Service Scope — Why Some Core Services Are "Per-Plugin" and Others Are "Whole-Backend"</h5>
                    <p>③ showed a plugin asking for <code>coreServices.logger</code>, <code>coreServices.database</code>, and <code>coreServices.httpRouter</code> — but not every core service behaves the same way once it's handed over. Each one is declared with a <strong>scope</strong>, and the scope changes what "one instance" actually means:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Scope</th><th>What you get</th><th>Real example</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>'plugin'</code> (the default)</td><td>A <strong>fresh instance per plugin</strong>, automatically tagged with that plugin's ID</td><td><code>coreServices.logger</code> — every log line the Catalog plugin writes is auto-prefixed <code>[catalog]</code>; Scaffolder's own logger prefixes <code>[scaffolder]</code>, with zero extra code</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>'root'</code></td><td><strong>Exactly ONE shared instance</strong> for the entire backend process, handed identically to every plugin that asks</td><td><code>coreServices.rootLogger</code> — the single unformatted log stream the whole backend writes to before per-plugin tagging is applied</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   'plugin'-scoped service                       'root'-scoped service
   ┌─────────────┐  ┌─────────────┐              ┌─────────────────────────┐
   │ Catalog     │  │ Scaffolder  │              │  ONE shared instance    │
   │ gets its    │  │ gets its    │              │  handed to EVERY plugin │
   │ OWN logger  │  │ OWN logger  │              │  that asks for it       │
   │ [catalog]   │  │ [scaffolder]│              │  (e.g. rootLogger,      │
   └─────────────┘  └─────────────┘              │   rootConfig)           │
                                                  └─────────────────────────┘
   A crash/leak in one plugin's copy            A root-scoped service is the
   never touches another plugin's copy          ONE thing every plugin shares
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why this split exists.</strong> Plugin-scoped services give you free isolation and free attribution — you never have to remember to tag your own log lines with "which plugin is this," and one plugin's misbehaving logger instance can't corrupt another's. Root-scoped services exist for the handful of things that are only meaningful ONCE per backend — there is only one process config, so <code>coreServices.rootConfig</code> is root-scoped by definition; there's no such thing as "Catalog's own copy of the process config."</div>

                    <div class="info-box warning">
                        <h5>⚠️ Don't Confuse This With §2.6's "5 Core Services"</h5>
                        <p class="mb-0">§2.6's "5 Core Services" (Catalog, Scaffolder, TechDocs, Search, Auth) are <strong>whole backend plugins</strong> — user-facing features. <code>coreServices</code> here is a completely different, unrelated concept: a small set of <strong>dependency-injection primitives</strong> (logger, database, httpRouter…) that EVERY plugin — including all 5 of those — asks for internally just to do its job. The word "core" does double duty in Backstage's own vocabulary; keep the two apart by remembering one is a <em>feature</em> and the other is a <em>tool a feature uses</em>.</p>
                    </div>

                    <p>③ and this section each showed 1-3 examples so far. Here's the fuller, real reference — the services you'll actually recognize inside any Backstage backend plugin's source, not just the handful used in this chapter's own code samples:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Service</th><th>Scope</th><th>What it gives you</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>logger</code> / <code>rootLogger</code></td><td>plugin / root</td><td>Structured logging, auto-tagged per plugin (above)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>database</code></td><td>plugin</td><td>A ready knex.js connection, scoped to that plugin's own tables (§2.4)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>httpRouter</code> / <code>rootHttpRouter</code></td><td>plugin / root</td><td>Where a plugin mounts its own REST endpoints (§2.3)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>config</code> / <code>rootConfig</code></td><td>plugin / root</td><td>Read-only access to <code>app-config.yaml</code> (§2.5)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>cache</code></td><td>plugin</td><td>A namespaced cache client — ⑥ above covers WHICH store backs it</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>discovery</code></td><td>plugin</td><td>Looks up ANOTHER plugin's base URL, so one plugin can call another's API (§2.3's HTTP-not-event-bus rule) without hardcoding an address</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>auth</code> / <code>httpAuth</code></td><td>plugin</td><td>Issue/verify the plugin-to-plugin and user tokens behind ②'s 401/403 checks</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>permissions</code></td><td>plugin</td><td>Ask "is this identity allowed to do X?" — the actual mechanism a PermissionPolicy (Chapter 23) plugs into</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>userInfo</code></td><td>plugin</td><td>Resolve "who is making this request" into a full identity, not just a raw token</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>scheduler</code></td><td>plugin</td><td>Run a recurring background task — e.g. the Catalog's own periodic refresh (§2.3)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>lifecycle</code> / <code>rootLifecycle</code></td><td>plugin / root</td><td>Startup/shutdown hooks (next)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Every one of these is opt-in.</strong> A plugin that never lists <code>discovery</code> in its <code>deps</code> structurally cannot look up another plugin's URL; one that never lists <code>permissions</code> cannot check an identity's access. This is the same isolation boundary ③ already established for <code>database</code> — it's not a special case, it's how ALL of <code>coreServices</code> works.</div>

                    <h5>⑧ Lifecycle Hooks — How a Plugin Starts Up and Shuts Down Cleanly</h5>
                    <p>Every plugin's <code>init()</code> function (③) runs once at boot — but real plugins often need to do more than that single burst of setup: wait for a dependency to become reachable before serving traffic, or clean up (close connections, flush a queue) the moment the process is asked to stop. Backstage's backend system exposes exactly this through a root-scoped lifecycle service:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   register(reg) {
     reg.registerInit({
       deps: {
         lifecycle: coreServices.rootLifecycle,
         logger: coreServices.logger,
       },
       async init({ lifecycle, logger }) {
         lifecycle.addStartupHook(async () =&gt; {
           logger.info('waiting for downstream dependency...');
         });
         lifecycle.addShutdownHook(async () =&gt; {
           logger.info('closing connections gracefully');
         });
       },
     });
   }
                    </pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Hook</th><th>Runs when</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Startup hook</strong></td><td>Before the backend starts accepting traffic</td><td>Wait for a downstream service to be reachable; warm a cache</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Shutdown hook</strong></td><td>When the process receives a stop signal (e.g. SIGTERM)</td><td>Flush an in-flight queue; close DB/HTTP connections cleanly</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 This is the exact same idea as a Kubernetes graceful shutdown</strong> (Template Part 1's Chapter 4 covers <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> and <code>preStop</code> hooks) — just one layer up the stack. Kubernetes gives the <em>Pod</em> a grace window before <code>SIGKILL</code>; the shutdown hook is how the <em>Backstage process running inside that Pod</em> uses its slice of that window to close things properly instead of dying mid-request.</div>

                    <h5>⑨ TechDocs' Own Mini-Pipeline — a Second Real Example of §2.1's Layers</h5>
                    <p>TechDocs (one of the 5 core services, §2.6) is a great closing example because it exercises all 3 layers in a slightly unusual order — generation happens OFFLINE, not on-request:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   1. 📄 jobpost's /docs (Markdown) + mkdocs.yml
           │  CI pipeline runs `mkdocs build` (Ch 14-15)
           ▼
   2. 📦 Static HTML site produced
           │  uploaded to storage (local disk or cloud bucket)
           ▼
   3. ⚙️ TechDocs BACKEND serves the pre-built HTML on request
           │  (no live Markdown-to-HTML conversion happens per-request)
           ▼
   4. 🖥️ TechDocs FRONTEND reader renders it inside the Backstage UI shell
                    </pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why this design choice.</strong> Generating docs on EVERY page view would be slow and wasteful — TechDocs instead treats doc generation like a build artifact (closer to a CI step than a live database query), which is exactly why a doc update needs a new commit + CI run, not just a page refresh, to appear.</div>

                    <p><strong>🔬 Deeper (exam + beyond):</strong> every architectural fact in this chapter reduces to the same handful of primitives reused everywhere — a <strong>processor chain</strong> (①) is just a pipeline of functions run in order; a <strong>core service</strong> (③) is just a typed object handed to whoever declares they need it, scoped either <code>'plugin'</code> or <code>'root'</code> (⑦); a <strong>lifecycle hook</strong> (⑧) is just a function registered to run at a fixed moment in the process's life; an <strong>Extension Point</strong> (§2.8) is just a published interface plus an explicit registration — the same shape as a plugin architecture in any large system (VS Code extensions, browser extensions all use this pattern for the same reason: explicitness scales, implicit magic doesn't). The 2023 backend rewrite (⑤) didn't invent new concepts — it took patterns that already existed informally and made them the <em>only</em> sanctioned way to wire a backend together. Concretely, all of this lives in exactly two packages: <code>@backstage/backend-plugin-api</code> ships <code>createBackendPlugin</code>, <code>createBackendModule</code>, <code>createExtensionPoint</code>, and <code>coreServices</code> itself; <code>@backstage/backend-defaults</code> ships <code>createBackend()</code> — the function a real <code>packages/backend/src/index.ts</code> (§2.9) calls once, before chaining <code>.add(catalogPlugin())</code>-style calls for every service you want running. And the same "explicit dependency, no magic" philosophy is now spreading to the frontend too: Backstage's own <strong>New Frontend System</strong> (alpha as of this writing) applies the identical <code>createFrontendPlugin</code>/<code>createFrontendModule</code> shape to the React side — so a plugin's UI extension points will eventually read exactly like its backend Extension Points do today. Once you see "declare your dependency, receive it explicitly" as the one idea underneath services, lifecycle hooks, extensions, and modules alike, every new plugin's source code becomes readable on sight instead of a fresh puzzle.</p>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch2-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>3 layers, always</strong> — Frontend (React + MUI), Backend (Node.js + Express), Database (Postgres or SQLite) (§2.1).</li>
                    <li><strong>Every user action is a REST round trip</strong> — Frontend → auth middleware → the right backend plugin → DB → back (§2.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Plugins follow install → configure → register → run</strong>, and each one is independently deployable (§2.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Core catalog tables</strong>: <code>entities</code> (raw), <code>final_entities</code> (processed), and <code>locations</code> (where each entity's YAML lives) — the Catalog service owns all three (§2.4).</li>
                    <li><strong><code>app-config.yaml</code>'s top sections</strong> (app, organization, backend, auth, catalog, scaffolder, techdocs, proxy/integrations) each own one job (§2.5).</li>
                    <li><strong>The 5 Core Services</strong> are Catalog, Scaffolder, TechDocs, Search, Auth — and Auth is the one every other service depends on (§2.6).</li>
                    <li><strong>The full request lifecycle is 8 hops, ~50ms with caching</strong> — bisect frontend vs backend/DB first when debugging (§2.7).</li>
                    <li><strong>Backend plugins talk via HTTP APIs and explicit Extension Points/modules</strong>, NOT a generic internal event bus (§2.3, §2.8).</li>
                    <li><strong>The real repo is a yarn-workspaces monorepo</strong> — <code>packages/app</code>, <code>packages/backend</code>, and <code>plugins/</code> (§2.9).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch2-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Assuming a generic internal "event bus" wires all plugins together.</strong> It doesn't — they talk via HTTP APIs plus explicit Extension Points/modules (§2.3, §2.8).</li>
                    <li><strong>Confusing <code>entities</code> with <code>final_entities</code>.</strong> The latter is the processed/stitched result — the one the UI actually reads — not the raw ingested row (§2.4).</li>
                    <li><strong>Thinking the frontend "is" Backstage.</strong> The frontend is the thinnest of the 3 layers — nearly all real logic lives in the backend (§2.1).</li>
                    <li><strong>Forgetting which of <code>app-config.yaml</code>'s 8 sections owns a given field</strong> and hunting through the wrong one (§2.5).</li>
                    <li><strong>Confusing a 401 with a 403.</strong> 401 = we don't know who you are (auth); 403 = we know exactly who you are, and the answer is no (permissions) (Under the Hood §②).</li>
                    <li><strong>Thinking a module can extend ANY plugin it likes.</strong> A module always extends exactly ONE specific plugin, declared via its Extension Point — modules aren't peer-to-peer (§2.8).</li>
                    <li><strong>Reading legacy-system docs (a "PluginEnvironment") and assuming it still applies.</strong> The 2023 backend-system rewrite replaced that pattern with <code>createBackendModule</code>/<code>coreServices</code> (Under the Hood §⑤).</li>
                    <li><strong>Assuming TechDocs renders Markdown live, per request.</strong> It serves pre-built static HTML generated offline in CI — a doc edit needs a new build, not just a refresh (Under the Hood §⑨).</li>
                    <li><strong>Assuming <code>yarn dev</code>'s fast local reload means your types are correct.</strong> Local dev runs in <strong>transpile-only mode</strong> for speed — it strips TypeScript types without checking them. A real type error can run fine in <code>yarn dev</code> and only surface later in CI's dedicated type-check step (or production). Run a full type check locally before trusting "it works on my machine."</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch2-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Bisect by layer first.</strong> Slow/broken page? Check the Network tab: slow API call → backend/DB (§2.7); fast API call, still broken → frontend rendering.</li>
                    <li><strong>"Which app-config.yaml section?" is a fast lookup, not a search.</strong> Memorize §2.5's anatomy-card once — app/organization/backend/auth/catalog/scaffolder/techdocs/proxy+integrations, all 8 — and you'll never hunt the whole file again.</li>
                    <li><strong>401 vs 403 tells you where to look immediately.</strong> 401 → check token/login. 403 → check RBAC/permission policy, never re-login.</li>
                    <li><strong>"Which service owns this?" collapses most Chapter 2 questions to one word.</strong> Catalog / Scaffolder / TechDocs / Search / Auth — name the service first, then the fix follows.</li>
                    <li><strong>See "PluginEnvironment" in a doc or README?</strong> That's legacy-system material — mentally translate to the new system's <code>createBackendModule</code>/<code>coreServices</code> vocabulary (Under the Hood §⑤).</li>
