                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q15</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why must a production Backstage backend be stateless, and what does "stateless" actually mean here?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Production typically runs multiple backend replicas behind a load balancer for reliability. "Stateless" means no replica holds session/data locally — ALL real state lives in the one shared PostgreSQL database, so any replica can serve any request.</p>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
🌐 Load Balancer ──▶ ⚙️ replica 1 ┐
                 ──▶ ⚙️ replica 2 ├──▶ 💾 ONE shared PostgreSQL
                 ──▶ ⚙️ replica 3 ┘
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Under the Hood §⑥ — this is also exactly why SQLite (a single local file) cannot work once you need more than one backend instance.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
