                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A teammate insists a shared "notification bus" would make plugins easier to extend, and pushes back when you tell them Backstage has no such thing. What's the concrete engineering downside they're not seeing?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Discoverability at scale.</strong> With HTTP calls and Extension Points, every integration between two plugins is <em>static</em> — visible in an import statement or a <code>package.json</code> dependency, so <code>grep</code> or an IDE's "find references" answers "who calls this?" instantly. A generic broadcast bus makes that question unanswerable without a live runtime trace: once a dozen plugins are wired to the same channel, nobody can tell who's listening, why, or what breaks if a message's shape changes.</p>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
❌ proposed:  Plugin A ──(broadcast to anyone listening)──▶ ???            (who? how many? why?)
✅ actual:    Plugin A ──HTTP call to a named endpoint──▶ Plugin B         (grep finds this)
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Under the Hood §④ lays out the actual engineering trade-off — explicitness over convenience, chosen specifically so dependencies stay auditable as the plugin count grows.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A user reports "Forbidden" when trying to view a Catalog entity, right after their token was refreshed 2 minutes ago. Auth or RBAC issue?</div>
