                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Nobody actually wired the new plugin into the running backend — writing the code only satisfies "Install"; someone still has to add it via <code>backend.add(...)</code> in <code>packages/backend/src/index.ts</code> to complete the "Register" stage. A plugin that's never registered doesn't exist to the server at all, so every route on it 404s, indistinguishable from a typo in the URL.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Working plugins</th><th>New plugin</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Present in <code>packages/backend/src/index.ts</code> via <code>backend.add(...)</code></td><td>Written, but never added to <code>index.ts</code></td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>§2.3's 4-stage lifecycle (install → configure → <strong>register</strong> → run) — "Install" just gets the package onto disk; "Register" is the separate, easy-to-forget step that actually turns code into a running plugin.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

