            <!-- ─────────── §3.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s3-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">3.1 Key Backstage Terminology</h3>
                <p>Before you can build with Backstage, you need to speak its language. This section covers the essential terminology — Entity, Kind, Spec, Location, Annotation, Plugin, Template — the foundation for everything that follows.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Entity</strong></td><td>Any thing tracked in the Catalog — a service, API, person, team, database, etc. Every entity has a <code>kind</code> and is defined in a YAML file.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Kind</strong></td><td>The type of entity. Backstage has 9 built-in kinds: Component, API, System, Domain, Resource, User, Group, Template, Location.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Spec</strong></td><td>Type-specific properties of an entity. A Component spec has <code>type</code>, <code>lifecycle</code>, <code>owner</code>. An API spec has <code>type</code> (openapi, asyncapi, etc.) and <code>definition</code>.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Metadata</strong></td><td>Common properties: name, description, labels, annotations, tags, links. Every entity has metadata.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Annotation</strong></td><td>A <code>key: value</code> pair in metadata that links an entity to an external system. Example: <code>github.com/project-slug: easypj/jobpost</code>.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Location</strong></td><td>A pointer to where entity YAML files can be found — a GitHub URL, a local file path, or an integration source.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Plugin</strong></td><td>An extensible module that adds features to Backstage. Can be frontend-only, backend-only, or full-stack.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Template</strong></td><td>A Scaffolder blueprint that defines how to create new projects. Specifies parameters (user inputs) and steps (actions to execute).</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Entity Anatomy — What Makes Up a Backstage Entity</div>
<pre>
   apiVersion: backstage.io/v1alpha1
   kind: Component
   metadata:
     name: jobpost                  ← UNIQUE IDENTIFIER
     description: Job posting app   ← Human description
     annotations:
       github.com/project-slug: easypj/jobpost  ← Links
       backstage.io/techdocs-ref: dir:.              ← Docs
     labels:
       language: python
       tier: backend
     tags: [django, postgres, rest-api]
     links:
       - url: https://jobpost.easypj.com
         title: Production
   spec:                     ← TYPE-SPECIFIC PROPERTIES
     type: service
     lifecycle: production
     owner: team-platform
     system: easypj-system
     providesApis: [jobpost-api]
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §3.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s3-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">3.2 Entity Kinds — Complete Reference</h3>
                <p>Every entity you'll ever register is exactly one of these 9 Kinds. Getting the Kind right is the single most common early mistake — the decision usually comes down to one question per pair, called out below.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight" id="b-s3-2a">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div><h5>Component</h5>
                        <p>A piece of software — a service, website, library, or CLI tool. The most common entity kind.</p>
                        <p><strong>Example:</strong> jobpost (Django service), easypj-frontend (React website)</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card" id="b-s3-2b">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📡</div><h5>API</h5>
                        <p>An API definition — OpenAPI (REST), AsyncAPI (async), GraphQL, or gRPC. Describes the contract between services.</p>
                        <p><strong>Example:</strong> jobpost-api (OpenAPI spec for jobpost's REST endpoints)</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card" id="b-s3-2c">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🖥️</div><h5>System</h5>
                        <p>A collection of Components and Resources that work together. Like a "product" or "application suite."</p>
                        <p><strong>Example:</strong> easypj-system (contains jobpost, frontend, postgres-db)</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card" id="b-s3-2d">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏢</div><h5>Domain</h5>
                        <p>A grouping of Systems by business domain. Like a department or business unit.</p>
                        <p><strong>Example:</strong> hr-domain (contains recruitment system, payroll system)</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card" id="b-s3-2e">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">💾</div><h5>Resource</h5>
                        <p>Infrastructure that Components depend on — databases, caches, queues, S3 buckets.</p>
                        <p><strong>Example:</strong> postgres-db (PostgreSQL database), redis-cache (Redis cache)</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card" id="b-s3-2f">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">👤</div><h5>User</h5>
                        <p>A person in the organization. Typically auto-discovered from your auth provider (GitHub, Okta).</p>
                        <p><strong>Example:</strong> jane.doe (developer on team-platform)</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card" id="b-s3-2g">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">👥</div><h5>Group</h5>
                        <p>A team, squad, tribe, or organizational unit. Can have parent/child hierarchy.</p>
                        <p><strong>Example:</strong> team-platform (parent of team-fe, team-data)</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card" id="b-s3-2h">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🚀</div><h5>Template</h5>
                        <p>A Scaffolder blueprint for creating new projects. Defines parameters and actions.</p>
                        <p><strong>Example:</strong> django-service (template for creating new Django microservices)</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card" id="b-s3-2i">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📍</div><h5>Location</h5>
                        <p>A pointer to where entity YAML files can be found. Can be a GitHub URL, file path, or integration.</p>
                        <p><strong>Example:</strong> URL pointing to easypj's catalog-info.yaml on GitHub</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Required <code>spec</code> fields</th><th>Example (easypj/jobpost)</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Component</td><td><code>type</code>, <code>lifecycle</code>, <code>owner</code></td><td>jobpost (type: service)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>API</td><td><code>type</code>, <code>lifecycle</code>, <code>owner</code>, <code>definition</code></td><td>jobpost-api (type: openapi)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>System</td><td><code>owner</code></td><td>easypj-system</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Domain</td><td><code>owner</code></td><td>hr-domain</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Resource</td><td><code>type</code>, <code>owner</code></td><td>postgres-db (type: database)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>User</td><td><code>memberOf</code></td><td>jane.doe</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Group</td><td><code>type</code>, <code>children</code></td><td>team-platform</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Template</td><td><code>type</code>, <code>steps</code></td><td>django-service</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Location</td><td><em>none</em> (<code>spec</code> may be an empty object)</td><td>github.com/easypj/jobpost</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📌 <code>spec.type</code> and <code>spec.lifecycle</code> — Free Text, But Not Random</h5>
                    <p>Neither field is a hard-enforced enum — both are plain strings, and Backstage will happily store whatever you type. But nearly every real organization (and the CBA exam) sticks to the same small, well-known set of values, because the Catalog UI uses them to pick icons and colored badges, and a made-up value just shows up unstyled.</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Well-known values</th><th>What it drives</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>spec.type</code> (Component)</td><td><code>service</code> · <code>website</code> · <code>library</code> · <code>documentation</code></td><td>Which icon shows next to the entity in the Catalog</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>spec.lifecycle</code></td><td><code>experimental</code> · <code>production</code> · <code>deprecated</code></td><td>The colored maturity badge on the entity's page</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip mb-0">
                    <h5>📌 Group's <code>children</code> and Template's <code>owner</code>/<code>parameters</code> — Required-But-Empty vs. Truly Optional</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0"><code>User.spec.memberOf</code> and <code>Group.spec.children</code> must both be <em>present</em> in the schema, but are allowed to be an empty array (<code>[]</code>) — a Group with no children yet still needs the key. Template is the opposite case: its JSON Schema only strictly requires <code>type</code> and <code>steps</code> — <code>owner</code> and <code>parameters</code> are schema-optional, even though nearly every real Template sets both (no <code>owner</code> means no clear catalog ownership; no <code>parameters</code> means the Scaffolder form has nothing for the user to fill in). <code>Location</code> takes this furthest: its <code>spec</code> itself is the only required field, and the minimal valid value is an empty object — <code>target</code>/<code>targets</code> and <code>type</code> are schema-optional, even though a Location with none of them can't actually point at anything useful.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ Component vs Resource — the Single Most Confused Pair</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">A common mistake is using Component for databases. <strong>Components are for software YOU build.</strong> <strong>Resources are for infrastructure you USE.</strong> Key discriminator: "Did we write the code for this?" Yes → Component. No → Resource. postgres-db is a Resource; jobpost (which reads from it) is a Component.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §3.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s3-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">3.3 The Entity Lifecycle</h3>
                <p>An entity doesn't appear in the Catalog the instant you write its YAML — it passes through 6 real stages first. Understanding this lifecycle is exactly what lets you troubleshoot "why isn't my entity showing up?" methodically instead of guessing.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Entity Lifecycle — From YAML to Searchable</div>
<pre>
  1. LOCATION        2. INGESTION       3. PROCESSING
  ┌──────────┐      ┌──────────┐      ┌──────────┐
  │ Git URL  │─────▶│ Fetch    │─────▶│ Validate │
  │ File path│      │ YAML     │      │ & Parse  │
  │ URL      │      │ from src │      │ entity   │
  └──────────┘      └──────────┘      └──────────┘
                                            │
                                            ▼
  4. STORAGE          5. STITCHING       6. SEARCH
  ┌──────────┐      ┌──────────┐      ┌──────────┐
  │ Save to  │◀─────│ Link     │◀─────│ Index    │
  │ Database │      │ relations│      │ for      │
  │ (PG/SQL) │      │ (owner,  │      │ search   │
  └──────────┘      │  APIs)   │      └──────────┘
                    └──────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active"><div class="step-number">1</div><h5>Registration</h5><p>Entity YAML is discovered at a Location (GitHub URL, file path)</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">2</div><h5>Ingestion</h5><p>YAML is fetched and parsed from the source</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">3</div><h5>Processing</h5><p>Entity is validated, enriched with additional data</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">4</div><h5>Storage</h5><p>Entity is saved to the PostgreSQL database</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">5</div><h5>Stitching</h5><p>Relationships between entities are resolved</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">6</div><h5>Search</h5><p>Entity is indexed for full-text search</p></div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip mb-0">
                    <h5>💡 These 6 Stages Are the Only Lifecycle Model — Don't Confuse Them with <code>spec.lifecycle</code></h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">The 6 steps above (Registration → Ingestion → Processing → Storage → Stitching → Search) are the complete pipeline — there's no separate "status" model layered on top. Don't confuse this pipeline with the unrelated <code>spec.lifecycle</code> field from §3.2 (<code>experimental</code> / <code>production</code> / <code>deprecated</code>), which describes a Component's maturity, not where it is in this ingestion pipeline. An entity can be fully Stitched and Searchable while its <code>spec.lifecycle</code> still reads <code>deprecated</code> — the two are independent.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §3.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s3-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">3.4 Annotations Reference</h3>
                <p>Annotations are <code>key: value</code> pairs in an entity's metadata that link it to external systems. They're how Backstage knows which GitHub repo, PagerDuty service, or Kubernetes deployment belongs to each entity.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Annotation</th><th>What it unlocks</th><th>Example value</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>github.com/project-slug</code></td><td>GitHub repo card + README on entity page</td><td><code>easypj/jobpost</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>backstage.io/techdocs-ref</code></td><td>The "Docs" tab (TechDocs)</td><td><code>dir:.</code> or <code>url:https://...</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>backstage.io/kubernetes-id</code></td><td>The "Kubernetes" tab (pod health)</td><td><code>jobpost</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>backstage.io/view-url</code> / <code>edit-url</code></td><td>Custom "view/edit source" links</td><td>a URL</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>jenkins.io/job-full-name</code></td><td>Jenkins CI/CD status card</td><td><code>easypj/jobpost-build</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>pagerduty.com/service-id</code></td><td>On-call schedule card</td><td><code>P12345</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>sentry.io/project-slug</code></td><td>Error tracking card</td><td><code>easypj/jobpost</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>sonarqube.org/project-key</code></td><td>Code-quality card</td><td><code>easypj-jobpost</code></td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Labels vs Annotations — the One-Line Rule</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">🏷️ <strong>Labels</strong> = for HUMANS to filter/search in the Catalog UI. 🔗 <strong>Annotations</strong> = for MACHINES (plugins) to find external tools. If a value helps a person browse, it's a Label; if a plugin reads it to make an API call, it's an Annotation.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip mb-0">
                    <h5>🧩 Custom Annotations — Your Own Organization Can Define These Too</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">The table above is only the built-in annotations that Backstage's own core plugins recognize — nothing stops easypj from defining its own. A custom key like <code>easypj.com/service-tier: gold</code> or <code>easypj.com/cost-center: eng-42</code> is exactly as valid as <code>github.com/project-slug</code>; a homegrown plugin simply reads it the same way the GitHub plugin reads its own key. The convention is always <code>your-domain.com/key-name</code>, which keeps custom keys from ever colliding with a built-in or third-party integration's namespace.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §3.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s3-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">3.5 The YAML Format — catalog-info.yaml</h3>
                <p>Every entity is declared in a <code>catalog-info.yaml</code> file. This section shows its complete, real anatomy — and two full, valid examples for jobpost's Component and its Resource.</p>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>📋 catalog-info.yaml — Complete Anatomy</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label">apiVersion</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>backstage.io/v1alpha1</code> for Component/API/System/Domain/Resource/User/Group/Location — but <strong>Template is the one exception</strong>, using <code>scaffolder.backstage.io/v1beta3</code> (its own plugin-owned schema, not the core catalog-model one)</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label">kind</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>Component</code> | <code>API</code> | <code>System</code> | <code>Domain</code> | <code>Resource</code> | <code>User</code> | <code>Group</code> | <code>Template</code> | <code>Location</code></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label">metadata.name</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Unique identifier. Must be unique per kind and namespace. <code>jobpost</code></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label">metadata.annotations</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Key-value pairs linking to external systems (GitHub, K8s, PagerDuty, etc.)</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label">metadata.labels</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Key-value pairs for filtering and grouping. <code>language: python</code>, <code>tier: backend</code></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label">metadata.tags</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Simple string tags. <code>[django, postgresql, rest-api]</code></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label">spec</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Type-specific properties. Varies by kind. Component has <code>type</code>, <code>lifecycle</code>, <code>owner</code>.</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip mb-0">
                    <h5>📏 Naming Rules — What Actually Makes a <code>metadata.name</code> Valid</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">Names aren't free text: Backstage requires letters, numbers, and the separators <code>-</code>, <code>_</code>, or <code>.</code> only — no spaces, no other symbols — up to 63 characters total. <code>jobpost</code> and <code>easypj-frontend</code> both pass; <code>job post!</code> or a name over 63 characters would fail schema validation before the entity is ever stored (the same Processing-stage check Under the Hood §④ describes).</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">catalog-info.yaml — jobpost Component</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: backstage.io/v1alpha1
kind: Component
metadata:
  name: jobpost
  description: Job posting and management application
  annotations:
    github.com/project-slug: easypj/jobpost
    backstage.io/techdocs-ref: dir:.
    backstage.io/kubernetes-id: jobpost
  labels:
    language: python
    tier: backend
  tags: [django, postgresql, rest-api]
  links:
    - url: https://jobpost.easypj.com
      title: Production
spec:
  type: service
  lifecycle: production
  owner: team-platform
  system: easypj-system
  providesApis: [jobpost-api]
  dependsOn:
    - resource:postgres-db
    - resource:redis-cache</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">catalog-info.yaml — postgres-db Resource</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: backstage.io/v1alpha1
kind: Resource
metadata:
  name: postgres-db
  description: PostgreSQL database for jobpost
spec:
  type: database
  owner: team-platform
  system: easypj-system</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip mb-0">
                    <h5>💡 Multi-Entity Files and Relationships</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">A single YAML file can declare multiple entities, separated by <code>---</code>. Relationship fields — <code>owner</code>, <code>system</code>, <code>dependsOn</code>, <code>providesApis</code>, <code>consumesApis</code> — are what §3.3's "Stitching" stage resolves into the dependency graph you see on an entity page.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch3-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — why "Stitching" gets its own lifecycle stage</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>§3.3's lifecycle diagram names "Stitching" as its own stage for a real reason: an entity's relations aren't knowable at ingestion time. This section explains exactly why, plus five more internals of the entity model worth understanding precisely.</p>

                    <h5>① Why Relations Can't Be Resolved at Ingestion</h5>
                    <p>An entity's relations (its owner, the APIs it provides, the resources it depends on) are not known at ingestion time — they only become resolvable once ALL related entities have been ingested and their names cross-referenced. That is why a freshly-registered jobpost Component might briefly show no owner or no APIs immediately after registration but self-corrects once processing catches up.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   t=0s   jobpost.yaml ingested   →  spec.owner: "team-platform" is READ, but not yet LINKED
   t=0s   team-platform.yaml doesn't exist in the Catalog yet (still being ingested elsewhere)
   t=2s   team-platform.yaml ingested
   t=2s   STITCHING pass runs   →  jobpost ←→ team-platform link is WRITTEN both ways
   t=2s   jobpost's entity page now shows its owner correctly
                    </pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Annotations and <code>spec.owner</code> are read at ingestion; the two-way relationship itself is only written during stitching.</strong> This is why a brand-new entity can look "incomplete" for a few seconds — it isn't broken, it just hasn't been stitched yet.</div>

                    <h5>② Entity References — the Address Every Relation Field Uses</h5>
                    <p>Every relation field (<code>owner</code>, <code>dependsOn</code>, <code>providesApis</code>) points at another entity using the same 3-part address: <code>kind:namespace/name</code>. This is the exact same entity-reference format covered in Chapter 1's Under the Hood §⑥ — Chapter 3 is where you see it used for real, in every direction.</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Example value</th><th>Points at</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>spec.owner</code></td><td><code>team-platform</code></td><td>A <code>Group</code> (namespace assumed <code>default</code>)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>spec.dependsOn</code></td><td><code>resource:postgres-db</code></td><td>A <code>Resource</code> entity</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>spec.providesApis</code></td><td><code>jobpost-api</code></td><td>An <code>API</code> entity</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 If a reference is misspelled or the target entity was never ingested, stitching simply can't complete that one link</strong> — the entity itself still exists, it just shows a broken or missing relation on its page. This is the #1 real-world cause of "why doesn't my dependency graph show anything?"</div>

                    <h5>③ Namespaces — the Silent Third Part of Every Reference</h5>
                    <p>Every entity reference is really a 3-part address, not 2: <code>kind:namespace/name</code>. §3.1's Entity Anatomy and §3.5's YAML never set a <code>metadata.namespace</code> for jobpost — and that's normal, because when it's omitted Backstage assumes the namespace <code>default</code>. Namespaces exist so two entities can share a <code>name</code> without colliding, exactly as promised back in Chapter 1's Under the Hood §⑥.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   metadata:
     name: api
     namespace: team-a        ← optional; assumed "default" if you omit it

   component:team-a/api   ≠   component:team-b/api
   (identical "name", different namespace → two completely distinct, non-colliding entities)

   Uniqueness rule: kind + namespace + name together, not name alone
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Almost every real org just uses <code>default</code> for everything, forever.</strong> Multiple namespaces only earn their complexity in large, multi-tenant setups — e.g. one Backstage instance shared by two companies post-acquisition, each keeping their own "api" or "frontend" names without renaming anything. For easypj's single-team setup, every entity in this guide safely lives in <code>default</code>, and you can leave <code>namespace</code> out entirely.</div>

                    <h5>④ JSON Schema Validation — Where §3.2's "Required Fields" Is Actually Enforced</h5>
                    <p>§3.2's required-<code>spec</code>-fields table isn't just a style guide — every Kind ships a real, machine-readable JSON Schema (bundled in Backstage's <code>catalog-model</code> package) that the Processing stage (§3.3, stage 3) validates every incoming entity against, using the <code>ajv</code> validator library. A Resource missing <code>owner</code> doesn't just look incomplete — it fails schema validation and never reaches Storage at all.</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>What you write</th><th>What actually happens at Processing</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>A Resource with no <code>spec.owner</code></td><td>Fails its Kind's JSON Schema → rejected before it's ever saved to the database</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>A Component with an unknown <code>lifecycle</code> value</td><td>Same rejection — the schema, not a human reviewer, is the actual gatekeeper</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 This is the exact mechanism Chapter 9 §9.6 lets you run yourself.</strong> The <code>ValidateEntity</code> API and <code>@backstage/cli</code>'s validation command check a YAML file against this identical schema locally — so a failure on your laptop is guaranteed to be the same failure the real Processing stage would hit later.</div>

                    <h5>⑤ System-Generated Metadata — Fields You Never Author Yourself</h5>
                    <p>Every entity's metadata has two more fields that never appear in a <code>catalog-info.yaml</code> you write, because the Catalog itself generates them — the sixth "invisible" thing that gets added once processing finishes.</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Set by</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>metadata.uid</code></td><td>The catalog, once, the moment an entity is first stored</td><td>A globally unique internal ID — useful when you must be certain two lookups returned the exact same underlying record</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>metadata.etag</code></td><td>The catalog, on every update</td><td>Changes whenever ANY part of the entity is updated — clients use it to detect "did this change since I last read it?" (optimistic concurrency)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Authored vs. generated is the real dividing line.</strong> Everything in §3.5's anatomy card (<code>name</code>, <code>annotations</code>, <code>labels</code>, <code>spec</code>) is something YOU write. <code>uid</code> and <code>etag</code> are something the Catalog computes and hands back when you fetch the entity — never put them in your own YAML, and don't be surprised to see them in an API response you didn't write yourself.</div>

                    <h5>⑥ Beyond the Built-in 9 — Custom Entity Kinds</h5>
                    <p>§3.2's 9 Kinds are the ones Backstage's own core plugins recognize out of the box — they are not a hard ceiling on the model itself. An organization can register its own additional Kind (a "Team" alias, a domain-specific "MLModel," etc.) by shipping its own validation schema and wiring it in through a custom Entity Provider/Processor pair — the exact same Extension Point pattern Chapter 2 §2.8 showed for any other backend customization, just pointed at the catalog instead of another plugin.</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Built-in Kind (the 9 in §3.2)</th><th>Custom Kind</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Recognized by</td><td>Backstage's own core plugins, no setup required</td><td>Only your own Entity Provider/Processor pair</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Validation schema</td><td>Ships with the catalog-model package</td><td>You author and register it yourself</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Needed for easypj / this guide</td><td>Yes — every example uses one of the 9</td><td>No — never required for any scenario in this guide</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Know this exists, don't expect to need it.</strong> For the CBA exam and for easypj's own catalog throughout this guide, the 9 built-in Kinds cover every real scenario — custom Kinds are a real, supported escape hatch for large organizations with genuinely novel entity types, not something a typical instance ever requires.</div>

                    <p><strong>🔬 Deeper (exam + beyond):</strong> all six of the above are really one idea, viewed from six angles — an entity is a piece of AUTHORED data (§3.1's anatomy, validated by Kind-specific schemas in ④) that the Catalog identifies precisely (③'s namespace + name, ⑤'s generated <code>uid</code>) and progressively enriches (①'s stitched relations, using ②'s address format) as it moves through the lifecycle in §3.3. The "9 Kinds" of §3.2 are simply the vocabulary Backstage ships by default (⑥) — the underlying mechanism (a schema, a namespace, a reference format, a processing pipeline) is generic enough to describe a Kind nobody has invented yet. Once you see identity (kind+namespace+name), authored data (spec/metadata), and derived data (relations, uid, etag) as three separate concerns, every entity you'll meet in Chapters 7-9 is just this same shape with different values inside it.</p>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch3-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Entity = a THING, Kind = its TYPE, Spec = its kind-specific properties, Location = where its YAML lives</strong> (§3.1).</li>
                    <li><strong>9 Kinds exist:</strong> Component, API, System, Domain, Resource, User, Group, Template, Location (§3.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>The lifecycle is</strong> Location → Ingestion → Processing → Storage → Stitching → Search (§3.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Annotations are machine-readable pointers</strong> — <code>github.com/project-slug</code>, <code>backstage.io/techdocs-ref</code>, <code>backstage.io/kubernetes-id</code> (§3.4).</li>
                    <li><strong>Every <code>catalog-info.yaml</code> needs</strong> <code>apiVersion</code>, <code>kind</code>, <code>metadata</code>, <code>spec</code> — nothing else is required (§3.5).</li>
                    <li><strong>Labels = humans filtering in the UI; Annotations = machines and plugins finding external tools</strong> (§3.4, §3.5).</li>
                    <li><strong>Relations resolve during Stitching, not Ingestion</strong> — a brand-new entity can briefly show incomplete relations (Under the Hood §①).</li>
                    <li><strong>A full entity reference has 3 parts</strong> — <code>kind:namespace/name</code> — and <code>namespace</code> silently defaults to <code>default</code> if you never set it (Under the Hood §③).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch3-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Mixing up Labels (for humans) and Annotations (for machines).</strong> The exam tests this distinction directly (§3.4, §3.5).</li>
                    <li><strong>Forgetting a Kind's REQUIRED <code>spec</code> fields</strong> — e.g. a Resource still needs <code>type</code> + <code>owner</code>, not just a name (§3.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Assuming an entity appears in the Catalog the instant you write its YAML</strong> — it must first be registered as a Location and pass ingestion (§3.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Using <code>dependsOn</code> without the <code>resource:</code> or <code>component:</code> prefix</strong> on the target reference (§3.5).</li>
                    <li><strong>Treating Component and Resource as interchangeable.</strong> "Did we write the code?" Yes → Component. No → Resource (§3.2).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch3-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Read YAML in this order:</strong> <code>metadata.name</code> then <code>spec.type</code>/<code>lifecycle</code>/<code>owner</code> FIRST — those 4 alone answer most identification questions.</li>
                    <li><strong>Recite the one-sentence rule</strong> whenever Entity, Kind, and Annotation blur together: "An Entity is a THING, a Kind is its TYPE, an Annotation is a sticky note for a Plugin" (Chapter 1 §1.9).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch3-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 3)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
