                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You have a PostgreSQL database that multiple services depend on. Which entity kind should you use to represent it in the Catalog?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Use the <strong>Resource</strong> kind with <code>spec.type: database</code>. Resources represent infrastructure that Components depend on. Other Components would reference it via <code>spec.dependsOn: [resource:postgres-db]</code>.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Answer</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Did the team write this code?</td><td>❌ No → it's infrastructure → Resource</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A common mistake is using Component for databases. Components are for software YOU build. Resources are for infrastructure you USE. Key discriminator: "Did we write the code for this?" Yes → Component. No → Resource.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between labels and annotations in entity metadata?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th></th><th>Labels</th><th>Annotations</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>For</td><td>Humans, filtering/grouping in the Catalog UI</td><td>Machines/plugins, linking to external systems</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Example</td><td><code>language: python</code>, <code>tier: backend</code></td><td><code>github.com/project-slug: easypj/jobpost</code></td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Think of labels as "user-facing tags for filtering" and annotations as "machine-facing pointers to external systems." Labels help humans browse the Catalog. Annotations help plugins integrate with GitHub, K8s, PagerDuty, etc.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What are the 4 required top-level fields in every catalog-info.yaml file?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
apiVersion: backstage.io/v1alpha1   ← always this value
kind: (one of the 9 entity kinds)
metadata: { name: ... }             ← name is the only required metadata field
spec: { ... }                       ← varies by kind
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Every catalog-info.yaml MUST have these 4 fields. If any is missing, the entity will fail validation during catalog ingestion. <code>name</code> in metadata is the only required metadata field, though <code>description</code> is highly recommended.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What annotation links a Backstage entity to a GitHub repository?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>github.com/project-slug</code> with the value in the format <code>owner/repo</code>. Example: <code>github.com/project-slug: easypj/jobpost</code>. This annotation enables the GitHub plugin to show repository information, README, and CI/CD status on the entity page.</p>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
metadata:
  annotations:
    github.com/project-slug: easypj/jobpost
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is one of the most commonly used annotations. Without it, Backstage can't connect the entity to its GitHub repo, and the GitHub plugin cards won't appear on the entity page. The format is always <code>owner/repository-name</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Describe the 6 stages of the entity lifecycle in Backstage.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Stage</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>1</td><td>Registration</td><td>Entity YAML is discovered at a Location (GitHub URL, file path)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>2</td><td>Ingestion</td><td>YAML is fetched from the source</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>3</td><td>Processing</td><td>Entity is validated and enriched with additional data</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>4</td><td>Storage</td><td>Entity is saved to the PostgreSQL database</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>5</td><td>Stitching</td><td>Relationships between entities are resolved (owner, system, APIs)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>6</td><td>Search</td><td>Entity is indexed for full-text search</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Understanding the entity lifecycle helps you troubleshoot catalog issues. If an entity isn't showing up, check: (1) Is the location configured? (2) Is the YAML valid? (3) Did processing succeed? (4) Is it in the database? (5) Are relationships resolved? (6) Is the search index updated?</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between a System and a Domain in Backstage?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
Domain (hr-domain)  →  business area
   └── System (easypj-system)  →  product/app
          └── Component (jobpost) / Resource (postgres-db)
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The hierarchy goes: Domain (business area) → System (product/app) → Component (service/library) / Resource (infrastructure). A Domain can contain multiple Systems. A System can contain multiple Components and Resources.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you define that a Component depends on a Resource in catalog-info.yaml?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">spec.dependsOn</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:
  dependsOn:
    - resource:postgres-db
    - resource:redis-cache</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The <code>dependsOn</code> field accepts references in the format <code>kind:name</code>. For Resources, it's <code>resource:name</code>. For other Components, it's <code>component:name</code>. This builds the dependency graph that helps developers understand service relationships.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the purpose of the <code>backstage.io/techdocs-ref</code> annotation?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>It tells Backstage's TechDocs system <strong>where to find the documentation files</strong> for this entity. Common values: <code>dir:.</code> (docs are in the root of the repository, in a <code>/docs</code> folder), or <code>url:https://github.com/easypj/docs</code> (docs are at a specific URL). Without this annotation, TechDocs won't know where to look for an entity's documentation.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>dir:.</code></td><td>Docs live in this same repo's root</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>url:https://...</code></td><td>Docs live at a specific external URL</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This annotation is essential for TechDocs to work. Every entity that wants documentation in Backstage needs this annotation. <code>dir:.</code> is the most common value — it tells TechDocs to look for an <code>mkdocs.yml</code> and <code>/docs</code> folder in the same repo.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to represent the "easypj" platform, which consists of a Django backend (jobpost), a React frontend, and a PostgreSQL database. Show the entity kinds and relationships you would use.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
hr-domain (Domain)
  └── easypj-system (System)
        ├── jobpost (Component, type: service — Django backend)
        ├── easypj-frontend (Component, type: website — React frontend)
        ├── postgres-db (Resource, type: database)
        └── jobpost-api (API, type: openapi)

Relationships: jobpost PROVIDES jobpost-api; frontend CONSUMES jobpost-api;
both Components DEPEND ON postgres-db.
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is a classic CBA scenario question. The key is choosing the right kind for each piece: software you build → Component, infrastructure → Resource, API definitions → API, logical grouping → System, business grouping → Domain. The hierarchy and relationships between them create the full picture.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch3-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 3 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Real workplace situations, not just definitions — 10 scenarios that mirror how the CBA exam actually asks: a situation, and you name the right Kind, field, or fix.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A new hire writes a <code>catalog-info.yaml</code> for jobpost's Redis cache and sets <code>kind: Component</code>. What's wrong, and what's the fix?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Wrong Kind. Redis is infrastructure the team USES, not software they wrote — it should be <code>kind: Resource</code> with <code>spec.type: cache</code>, not <code>database</code> — <code>spec.type</code> is free text (§3.2), so it has to be corrected to match what the Resource actually is, not just copy-pasted from another Resource.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">corrected</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">kind: Resource
metadata:
  name: redis-cache
spec:
  type: cache        # not "database" — matches what it IS
  owner: team-platform</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>§3.2's discriminator applies directly: Component = built by you, Resource = used by you. This exact mistake is the most common real-world Kind error — and once corrected to Resource, other Components reference it as <code>dependsOn: [resource:redis-cache]</code>, the same pattern as jobpost's <code>postgres-db</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">jobpost's entity page shows no owner immediately after you register its <code>catalog-info.yaml</code>. A teammate says "the YAML must be broken." What's the more likely explanation?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Not necessarily broken — the owner relationship is written during the <strong>Stitching</strong> stage, which runs after ingestion. A brand-new entity can briefly show no owner before stitching catches up.</p>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
t=0s  ingested, spec.owner READ but not yet LINKED  →  t=2s  STITCHING pass  →  owner shows correctly
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Under the Hood §① — check again in a few seconds before assuming the YAML is invalid.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You want jobpost's page to show a "view source" link pointing at a specific internal wiki page instead of GitHub. Which annotation do you use?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>backstage.io/view-url</code>, set to the wiki page's URL.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Annotation</th><th>Overrides</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>backstage.io/view-url</code></td><td>The "view source" link target</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>§3.4's annotation table — <code>view-url</code>/<code>edit-url</code> exist exactly for overriding the default source link.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A teammate writes <code>dependsOn: [postgres-db]</code> instead of <code>dependsOn: [resource:postgres-db]</code>. What breaks?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The reference is missing its Kind prefix — Backstage can't resolve which entity <code>postgres-db</code> refers to without knowing its Kind. The dependency silently fails to stitch.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Written</th><th>Correct</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>postgres-db</code></td><td><code>resource:postgres-db</code></td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>§3.5's Gotcha — every relation reference needs its <code>kind:</code> prefix (Under the Hood §②'s entity-reference format).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Your manager asks you to group jobpost, the frontend, and 2 other HR-related products under one umbrella for a leadership dashboard. Which Kind, and where does it sit in the hierarchy?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A <strong>Domain</strong> (e.g. <code>hr-domain</code>) — the top of the hierarchy, grouping multiple Systems by business area. Since jobpost and the frontend already live inside <code>easypj-system</code> (a System, not a Component/Resource directly), the new Domain groups <em>Systems</em>, not individual products — so the "2 other HR-related products" only count if they're each already modeled as their own System.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Required <code>spec</code> field</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Domain</td><td><code>owner</code> only — creating <code>hr-domain</code> is a two-line spec</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>§3.2/Q7's hierarchy: Domain is business-area-level, one step above System. Per §3.2's required-fields table, Domain (like System) needs nothing but <code>owner</code> — the leadership-dashboard umbrella itself is cheap to create; the real work is making sure each HR product is already a System underneath it.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A developer tags jobpost with <code>label: production-ready</code> expecting it to trigger a Slack alert when the label is added. Why won't this work as they expect?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Labels are for human filtering in the Catalog UI — no plugin reads them to trigger actions. Real alerting integrations are wired through an Annotation instead: <code>pagerduty.com/service-id</code> (§3.4) is what actually connects an entity to an on-call/alerting system, because a plugin reads that key to make the API call. Adding a Label never fires a webhook by itself.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Wants</th><th>Needs</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Human filtering in the UI</td><td>🏷️ Label</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>A plugin to call an external alerting API</td><td>🔗 Annotation, e.g. <code>pagerduty.com/service-id</code></td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>§3.4's one-line rule: Labels = humans, Annotations = machines. This mix-up is one of the exam's most repeated distinctions — the fix isn't "use an Annotation instead," it's specifically wiring up the annotation the target plugin (here, PagerDuty) actually reads.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You write a Template entity but forget its <code>spec.steps</code> field. What happens?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The entity fails validation — <code>type</code> and <code>steps</code> are the two <code>spec</code> fields the Template Kind's schema strictly requires, and <code>steps</code> is the one you just dropped.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Template's required spec fields</th><th>Schema-optional but conventional</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>type</code>, <code>steps</code></td><td><code>owner</code>, <code>parameters</code></td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>§3.2's required-fields table — each Kind has its own required <code>spec</code> fields, and skipping one blocks ingestion (§3.3's Processing stage). <code>owner</code> and <code>parameters</code> aren't schema-required for a Template, but skipping them in practice means no clear ownership and a Scaffolder form with nothing to fill in.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">jobpost's dependency graph on its entity page shows nothing, even though its YAML has a <code>dependsOn</code> field. What are the 2 most likely causes?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Likely cause</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>1</td><td>The reference is missing its <code>kind:</code> prefix (Q4 above)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>2</td><td>The target entity (e.g. <code>postgres-db</code>) was never actually ingested, so stitching has nothing to link to</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Under the Hood §②'s callout — a misspelled or nonexistent reference just leaves that one relation unresolved, not the whole entity broken.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write the annotation that would make jobpost's Kubernetes pod health show up on its entity page.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">Kubernetes tab annotation</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">metadata:
  annotations:
    backstage.io/kubernetes-id: jobpost</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>§3.4's table — this is the exact annotation Chapter 1 §1.11's "Backstage observes Kubernetes" relationship depends on.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A skeptical teammate asks why Backstage needs a separate "Stitching" step at all — why not just resolve relationships during Ingestion?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Because at ingestion time, the entities a relationship points to might not exist in the Catalog yet — Stitching runs as a separate pass specifically so it can wait until all related entities have been ingested before cross-referencing them.</p>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
Ingestion: read spec.owner (a NAME, not yet a link)  →  Stitching: cross-reference ALL entities → LINK
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Under the Hood §①'s full timeline — this is the precise "why," not just the "what," behind §3.3's lifecycle diagram.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

