            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch4-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — yarn dev vs yarn tsc vs yarn build, precisely</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>§4.4 named 3 commands. Here's exactly what each one does differently, and why mixing them up causes real bugs.</p>
                    <h5>① Transpile-Only vs Type-Checked vs Bundled</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   yarn dev (development)              yarn tsc (type-check)          yarn build (production)
   ────────────────────────            ─────────────────────          ───────────────────────
   ts-node/webpack TRANSPILE            TypeScript compiler CHECKS     Full compile + bundle:
   on the fly — strips types,           types across ALL packages     runs tsc, THEN
   does NOT verify correctness          using project references      webpack/esbuild bundles
   ⚡ Fast startup, unsafe               🐢 Slower, catches every        for production serving
                                        type error before you commit
                    </pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 This is exactly Chapter 2 Gotcha's "yarn dev's transpile-only mode is NOT a type check."</strong> A real type error can run fine under <code>yarn dev</code> and only surface when <code>yarn tsc</code> or CI runs for real.</div>
                    <h5>② Project References &amp; Incremental Builds</h5>
                    <p>Each package's <code>tsconfig.json</code> extends the root config and lists its dependencies under <code>references</code>. TypeScript caches compile state in <code>.tsbuildinfo</code> files (one per package) — only CHANGED packages (and their dependents) recompile, not the whole monorepo every time.</p>
                    <div class="uth-callout mb-0"><strong>🔑 Why this matters day to day.</strong> A large monorepo would be unusably slow to type-check from scratch every time — project references are what make <code>yarn tsc</code> fast enough to run before every commit.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch4-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Prerequisites:</strong> Node 22/24 (Active LTS), Yarn 4.4.1 via Corepack, Git — Docker optional (§4.1).</li>
                    <li><strong><code>npx @backstage/create-app@latest</code></strong> scaffolds the entire project — frontend, backend, and DB choice (§4.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>The directory structure mirrors Chapter 2's monorepo</strong> — <code>packages/app</code>, <code>packages/backend</code>, <code>plugins/</code> (§4.3).</li>
                    <li><strong><code>yarn dev</code> = SQLite + hot reload; <code>yarn build &amp;&amp; yarn start</code> = PostgreSQL + production</strong> (§4.4).</li>
