            <!-- ─────────── §5.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s5-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">5.3 Environment Variables &amp; Secrets</h3>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Secret Management Pattern</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># NEVER do this:</span>
auth:
  providers:
    github:
      clientId: "abc123"    <span class="comment">← ❌ HARDCODED SECRET!</span>

<span class="comment"># ALWAYS do this:</span>
auth:
  providers:
    github:
      clientId: ${GITHUB_CLIENT_ID}      <span class="comment">← ✅ From env var</span>

<span class="comment"># Set in your terminal or .env file:</span>
export GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=abc123
export GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=xyz789

<span class="comment"># On Kubernetes, use Secrets:</span>
kubectl create secret generic backstage-secrets \
  --from-literal=GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=abc123</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning mb-0"><h5>⚠️ Never commit secrets to Git!</h5><p class="mb-0">Environment variables keep secrets out of your config files and out of your Git history. On Kubernetes, use <code>Secret</code> resources and reference them in your Deployment's <code>env</code> section.</p></div>

                <h4>Using .env Files for Local Development</h4>
                <p>Backstage doesn't automatically load <code>.env</code> files — you need to set environment variables manually or use a package like <code>dotenv-cli</code>. Here's the recommended approach:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Local Development Secret Management</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Create a .env file (ADD TO .gitignore!)
# .env — NEVER COMMIT THIS FILE</span>
GITHUB_TOKEN=ghp_xxxxxxxxxxxx
GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=abc123def456
GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=xyz789secret
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=mysecretpassword

<span class="comment"># 2. Load env vars before starting Backstage:
# Option A: Use export (Linux/macOS)</span>
$ export $(cat .env | xargs) && yarn dev

<span class="comment"># Option B: Use dotenv-cli (cross-platform)</span>
$ npm install -g dotenv-cli
$ dotenv -- yarn dev</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">Kubernetes — Deployment secret injection pattern</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: backstage
spec:
  template:
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: backstage
        image: backstage:latest
        env:
        - name: GITHUB_TOKEN        <span class="comment"># ← Maps K8s Secret to env var</span>
          valueFrom:
            secretKeyRef:
              name: backstage-secrets
              key: github-token
        - name: POSTGRES_PASSWORD
          valueFrom:
            secretKeyRef:
              name: backstage-secrets
              key: postgres-password</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note mb-0">
                    <p class="mb-0">On the CBA exam, remember these secret patterns: <code>${ENV_VAR}</code> — use in app-config.yaml for ALL secrets (never hardcode); <code>.env</code> file — local dev only, MUST be in <code>.gitignore</code>; <code>secretKeyRef</code> — the Kubernetes pattern for injecting secrets into pods; <code>kubectl create secret generic</code> — create K8s Secrets from literal values or files. Backstage has NO built-in secret manager — all secrets come from environment variables.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §5.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s5-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">5.4 Multiple Configuration Files</h3>
                <p>Backstage merges configuration from multiple files. The merge order (later overrides earlier):</p>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">1</div><h5>app-config.yaml</h5><p>Base/default config</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">2</div><h5>app-config.production.yaml</h5><p>Production overrides</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">3</div><h5>app-config.local.yaml</h5><p>Local dev overrides — HIGHEST priority</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">4</div><h5>Environment Variables</h5><p><code>${ENV_VAR}</code> substitution wins any remaining value</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Config File Merge Hierarchy</div>
<pre>
  app-config.yaml                 (1) BASE — checked into Git, defaults
        │  merged with
        ▼
  app-config.production.yaml      (2) PRODUCTION overrides — checked in
        │  merged with
        ▼
  app-config.local.yaml           (3) LOCAL overrides — gitignored,
        │                             per-developer, HIGHEST priority
        ▼
  FINAL MERGED CONFIG used by the running Backstage instance

  🔑 RULE: later files WIN on conflicting keys. local.yaml can override
     anything — that's exactly why it must never be committed.
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip mb-0">
                    <h5>💡 Deep Merge, Not File Replacement</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">If <code>app-config.yaml</code> sets <code>backend.database.client</code> and <code>app-config.local.yaml</code> only overrides <code>backend.cors.origin</code>, BOTH survive — only truly conflicting keys get overwritten. This is why the exact same checked-in <code>app-config.yaml</code> can run unmodified on a laptop and in production (Under the Hood below).</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch5-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — how the 3-file deep merge actually works</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>§5.4 states the merge order, but the mechanism is worth internalizing.</p>
                    <h5>① Load Order &amp; Deep Merge</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
1. Load app-config.yaml                    (base)
2. DEEP-MERGE app-config.production.yaml   (in the prod environment)
3. DEEP-MERGE app-config.local.yaml LAST   (gitignored, per-developer)
                    </pre>
                    <p>"Deep merge" matters here — it's not whole-file replacement. If the base sets <code>backend.database.client</code> and local only overrides <code>backend.cors.origin</code>, both survive; only truly conflicting keys get overwritten by the later file.</p>
                    <div class="uth-callout mb-0"><strong>🔑 Combined with §5.3's environment-variable substitution, this is how the exact same checked-in app-config.yaml can run unmodified on a laptop and in production</strong> — the file is identical, only the environment and the local overlay differ.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch5-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong><code>yarn dev/tsc/lint/test/build</code> cover the whole daily loop; <code>backstage-cli create-plugin/migrate</code> are for structural changes</strong> (§5.1).</li>
                    <li><strong><code>app-config.yaml</code>'s 8 core sections each own one concern</strong> — app, backend, catalog, scaffolder, techdocs, auth, proxy, integrations (§5.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Environment-variable substitution is how real secrets stay OUT of app-config.yaml entirely</strong> (§5.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>In Kubernetes, secrets are injected via <code>Secret</code>, never <code>ConfigMap</code></strong> (§5.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>3 config files, one merge order:</strong> app-config.yaml → .production.yaml → .local.yaml, LAST file wins (§5.4).</li>
                    <li><strong><code>app-config.local.yaml</code> must never be committed</strong> — it's the highest-priority override by design (§5.4).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch5-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Assuming <code>app-config.yaml</code> alone is "the config."</strong> In a real deployment it's always a 3-file merge (§5.4).</li>
                    <li><strong>Hardcoding a real secret value directly into <code>app-config.yaml</code></strong> instead of an environment-variable placeholder (§5.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Forgetting <code>local.yaml</code> OVERRIDES <code>production.yaml</code></strong> if both are present locally — it is the highest priority, always (§5.4).</li>
                    <li><strong>Editing the wrong app-config.yaml section</strong> because two features sound similar (e.g. <code>auth</code> vs <code>integrations</code>) (§5.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Committing a <code>.env</code> file</strong> — it must always be in <code>.gitignore</code>, never in Git history (§5.3).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch5-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
