            <!-- ─────────── §7.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s7-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">7.2 How the Catalog Works — Under the Hood</h3>
                <p>The Catalog works through a <strong>3-stage ingestion pipeline</strong>: Location → Provider → Processor → Database. Understanding this pipeline is critical for the CBA exam and for troubleshooting when entities don't appear.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Catalog Ingestion Pipeline — 3 Stages</div>
<pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  📥 CATALOG INGESTION FLOW                                          │
  │                                                                     │
  │  STAGE 1: LOCATION          STAGE 2: PROVIDER       STAGE 3: PROCESSOR
  │  ┌──────────────┐          ┌──────────────┐        ┌──────────────┐ │
  │  │ GitHub URL   │─────────▶│ GithubEntity │───────▶│ Placeholder  │ │
  │  │ (YAML file)  │          │ Provider     │        │ Processor    │ │
  │  └──────────────┘          └──────────────┘        └──────┬───────┘ │
  │                                                           │         │
  │  ┌──────────────┐          ┌──────────────┐               ▼         │
  │  │ Local File   │─────────▶│ UrlReader    │        ┌──────────────┐ │
  │  │ (./catalog/) │          │ Processor    │───────▶│ Entity       │ │
  │  └──────────────┘          └──────────────┘        │ Processor    │ │
  │                                                     └──────┬───────┘ │
  │  ┌──────────────┐          ┌──────────────┐               ▼         │
  │  │ K8s Cluster  │─────────▶│ Kubernetes   │        ┌──────────────┐ │
  │  │ (auto-disc)  │          │ Provider     │───────▶│ 💾 DATABASE  │ │
  │  └──────────────┘          └──────────────┘        │    STORE     │ │
  │                                                     └──────────────┘ │
  │                                                                     │
  │  🔑 KEY: Location = WHERE to find entities                          │
  │          Provider = HOW to read/generate entities                    │
  │          Processor = HOW to transform & validate entities            │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📍</div>
                        <h5>Stage 1: Location</h5>
                        <p><strong>Where</strong> entities come from. A location is a pointer to a YAML file (URL, local file, or integration). Think of it as the "address" where Backstage should look. Configurable in <code>app-config.yaml</code> under <code>catalog.locations</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📡</div>
                        <h5>Stage 2: Entity Provider</h5>
                        <p><strong>How</strong> entities are read. Each location type has a matching provider: <code>GithubEntityProvider</code> reads from GitHub, <code>UrlReaderProcessor</code> reads local files, <code>KubernetesEntityProvider</code> auto-discovers from K8s.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⚙️</div>
                        <h5>Stage 3: Processor</h5>
                        <p><strong>Transforms</strong> raw data into valid entities. Validates YAML syntax, resolves placeholders (<code>$text</code>), enriches with external data, and stores the final entity in the database. Runs on every catalog refresh cycle.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">💾</div>
                        <h5>Final: Database Store</h5>
                        <p>Entities are stored in <strong>PostgreSQL</strong> (or SQLite for dev). The database is the ultimate source of truth. The Catalog API (<code>/api/catalog/entities</code>) queries this database. Entities refresh on a configurable schedule.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning mb-0">
                    <h5>⚠️ CBA Exam Alert: Ingestion Pipeline</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">The CBA will test your understanding of why an entity might not appear. Common failure points: (1) Wrong location URL, (2) Invalid YAML syntax in catalog-info.yaml, (3) Missing required fields (name, kind), (4) GitHub token not configured for private repos, (5) Provider not enabled in app-config.yaml. Always check backend logs first!</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Catalog Refresh Cycles — How Entities Stay Up-to-Date</h4>
                <p>The catalog doesn't just ingest entities once — it <strong>continuously refreshes</strong> them on a schedule. This ensures your Catalog always reflects the latest state of your repositories and infrastructure.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Refresh Aspect</th><th>Default</th><th>What Happens</th><th>Config Location</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Scheduled Refresh</strong></td><td>Every 100 seconds</td><td>All locations are re-read, entities re-processed</td><td><code>backend.reading.allow</code> config</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>GitHub Org Scan</strong></td><td>Every 30 minutes</td><td>Re-scans GitHub org for new/changed catalog-info.yaml files</td><td><code>catalog.providers.github.schedule</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Manual Refresh</strong></td><td>On demand</td><td>Immediate re-read via <code>POST /api/catalog/refresh</code></td><td>API call — no config needed</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Entity-level Refresh</strong></td><td>On demand</td><td>Refresh a single entity: <code>POST /refresh/{kind}/{namespace}/{name}</code></td><td>API call</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

