            <!-- ─────────── §7.5 (NEW — from Backstage.txt, no HTML equivalent existed) ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s7-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">7.5 Working with Manually Registered Entity Locations</h3>
                <p>A manual Location is a single, explicit pointer YOU add — nothing discovers it automatically. There are 3 equivalent ways to add one. Manual registration is best for: one-off entities, early testing, or repos an automated discovery scan wouldn't reach.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Registering jobpost Manually — 3 Equivalent Methods</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  METHOD 1: The UI Wizard (fastest for one entity)                             │
│  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────                            │
│  1. Click CREATE in the sidebar                                               │
│  2. Click Register Existing Component (bottom of the template list)           │
│  3. Paste: https://github.com/easypj/jobpost/blob/main/catalog-info.yaml      │
│  4. Backstage fetches + PREVIEWS the entity before you confirm                │
│  5. Click Import — entity now appears in the Catalog                          │
│                                                                               │
│  METHOD 2: app-config.yaml (best for a fixed, known set of repos)             │
│  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────            │
│  catalog:                                                                     │
│    locations:                                                                 │
│      - type: url                                                              │
│        target: https://github.com/easypj/jobpost/blob/main/catalog-info.yaml  │
│                                                                               │
│  METHOD 3: Catalog API (best for scripting/automation)                        │
│  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────                     │
│  curl -X POST http://localhost:7007/api/catalog/locations \                   │
│    -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \                                      │
│    -d '{"type":"url","target":"https://github.com/easypj/jobpost/             │
│         blob/main/catalog-info.yaml"}'                                        │
└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note mb-0">
                    <p class="mb-0">🔑 A Location just POINTS to a file — it does NOT create the entity itself. The entity only appears after the Catalog fetches and successfully PROCESSES the YAML at that location.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §7.6 (NEW — from Backstage.txt, no HTML equivalent existed) ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s7-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">7.6 Working with Automated Ingestion — Discovery &amp; Providers</h3>
                <p>Manual Locations don't scale past a handful of repos. AUTOMATED ingestion uses an EntityProvider to discover MANY entities on a recurring schedule — no one has to add each repo by hand. The GitHub Organization Provider scans an entire GitHub org for <code>catalog-info.yaml</code> files matching a glob; similar discovery providers exist for GitLab, Bitbucket, AWS, and Kubernetes clusters.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Manual Location</th><th>Automated Provider</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Scope</td><td>You add ONE explicit URL</td><td>Scans an ENTIRE org/account</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>New repos</td><td>Static — never finds new repos</td><td>Finds NEW repos automatically</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Good for</td><td>1-5 known repos</td><td>50+ repos, whole orgs</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Config</td><td><code>catalog.locations</code></td><td><code>catalog.providers.*</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Timing</td><td>Runs once when Backstage starts</td><td>Runs on a recurring SCHEDULE</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Configuring GitHub Org Auto-Discovery for easypj</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># app-config.yaml
catalog:
  providers:
    github:
      providerId:
        organization: 'easypj'
        catalogPath: '/catalog-info.yaml'   <span class="comment"># file to look for in each repo</span>
        filters:
          repository: '.*'                  <span class="comment"># regex — which repos to scan</span>
        schedule:
          frequency: { minutes: 30 }         <span class="comment"># how often to re-scan</span>
          timeout: { minutes: 3 }</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip mb-0">
                    <p class="mb-0">💡 CBA Tip — Provider vs Processor: a PROVIDER finds WHICH entities exist (discovery). A PROCESSOR transforms/enriches an entity ONCE it's already been found (e.g. resolving placeholders, validating schema). Every entity passes through processors — only some entities arrive via a provider instead of a manual Location.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §7.7 (NEW — from Backstage.txt, no HTML equivalent existed) ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s7-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">7.7 Troubleshooting Entity Ingestion — A Systematic Method</h3>
                <p>"My entity isn't showing up" is the #1 real-world Catalog problem. Debug in this EXACT order — each step rules out one entire cause.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The 5-Step Ingestion Debug Flowchart</div>
<pre>
  STEP 1 — Is the Location even configured?
  Check catalog.locations / catalog.providers in app-config.yaml.
  No location = Backstage never even LOOKS for your YAML.
       │ if configured, continue ▼
  STEP 2 — Can Backstage REACH the file?
  curl the raw file yourself. Private repo? Check the integration token
  in app-config.yaml has 'repo' scope and hasn't expired.
       │ if reachable, continue ▼
  STEP 3 — Is the YAML actually VALID?
  Run it through a YAML linter. Common killers: tabs instead of spaces,
  missing apiVersion/kind, a trailing comma copied from JSON examples.
       │ if valid, continue ▼
  STEP 4 — Has ingestion actually RUN yet?
  Force it: curl -X POST localhost:7007/api/catalog/refresh
    -d '{"entityRef":"component:default/jobpost"}'
  Or just wait for the next scheduled processing interval.
       │ if refreshed, continue ▼
  STEP 5 — Check the backend LOGS for the real error
  Ingestion failures are logged, not silently swallowed. Look for
  'PlaceholderResolutionError', 'InputError', or 'NotFoundError'.
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Root Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Entity never appears</td><td>Location not configured</td><td>Add to <code>catalog.*</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>"404" fetching the YAML</td><td>Wrong branch/path in the URL</td><td>Fix the URL</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>"401/403" fetching the YAML</td><td>Missing/expired GitHub token</td><td>Rotate token</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Entity appears then vanishes</td><td>Provider re-scan can't find it anymore</td><td>Repo renamed/deleted</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Entity shows but fields blank</td><td>YAML valid but incomplete</td><td>Add missing spec fields</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Two entities collide</td><td>Duplicate <code>metadata.name</code></td><td>Rename one</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning mb-0">
                    <p class="mb-0">🎯 CBA Exam Pattern — a scenario question almost always boils down to ONE of these 5 steps. "Entity registered a week ago but never appeared" = Step 1 (never configured) or Step 3 (invalid YAML), never Step 5 — the exam rewards ruling out the SIMPLE causes first, not jumping straight to logs.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch7-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — the single journey every entity takes, regardless of how it entered</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>Every entity's journey is the same regardless of how it entered.</p>
                    <h5>① Location/Provider → Processor → Database → API</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
Location (§7.3/7.5, manual) or EntityProvider (§7.6, automated) — WHERE to look
                              ↓
Processor (§7.2) — transforms/enriches, resolves placeholders, validates schema
                              ↓
PostgreSQL DB — queryable via API, visible in the UI
                    </pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout mb-0"><strong>🔑 A Provider only ever answers "which entities exist"; a Processor answers "is this one valid, and what does it mean."</strong> Every entity passes through a Processor, but only some arrive via a Provider instead of a hand-added Location.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch7-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>The Catalog is the single source of truth</strong> — services, APIs, websites, libraries, databases and teams all live in it.</li>
                    <li><strong>Ingestion is always Location/Provider → Processor → DB → API</strong> (§7.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Manual registration has 3 equivalent forms:</strong> the UI wizard, app-config.yaml locations, and a Catalog API POST (§7.5).</li>
                    <li><strong>Automated ingestion (an EntityProvider)</strong> scans an entire org on a recurring schedule — it scales where manual Locations don't (§7.6).</li>
                    <li><strong>A Location only POINTS to a file</strong>; it does not create the entity — the entity appears only after successful processing (§7.5).</li>
                    <li><strong>"Entity isn't showing up" is always debugged in the same 5-step order:</strong> configured? → reachable? → valid? → ingested? → logs (§7.7).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch7-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Assuming an entity appears with NO Location or Provider configured at all</strong> — nothing is automatic without one (§7.5/7.6).</li>
                    <li><strong>Jumping straight to backend logs (Step 5)</strong> before ruling out the simpler Steps 1-4 — the exam rewards ruling out simple causes first (§7.7).</li>
                    <li><strong>Confusing a Provider</strong> (discovers WHICH entities exist) <strong>with a Processor</strong> (transforms an entity already found) (Under the Hood §①).</li>
                    <li><strong>Forgetting that a private repo needs a token with 'repo' scope</strong> — a 401/403 almost always means an expired or missing token (§7.7).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch7-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong><code>curl /api/catalog/entities</code> first</strong> as a sanity check that ANYTHING is registered before debugging a single entity.</li>
                    <li><strong>Force a re-ingestion with the <code>/refresh</code> endpoint</strong> instead of waiting for the next scheduled scan.</li>
                    <li><strong>Past a handful of repos, always prefer GitHub org auto-discovery</strong> over hand-adding Locations one at a time.</li>
                    <li><strong>Chapter 7 quick win:</strong> the ingestion pipeline is Location → Provider → Processor → Database — always verify with <code>curl :7007/api/catalog/entities</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

