            <section class="section-block" id="b-s8-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.1 Component Entity — The Most Common Kind</h3>
                <p>A <strong>Component</strong> is any piece of software — a service, website, library, CLI tool, or mobile app. It's the most frequently used entity kind. Every microservice in your organization should be a Component.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Component Entity — jobpost Example</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># catalog-info.yaml for easypj/jobpost
apiVersion: backstage.io/v1alpha1
kind: Component
metadata:
  name: jobpost
  description: Job posting and recruitment management application
  annotations:
    github.com/project-slug: easypj/jobpost
    backstage.io/techdocs-ref: dir:.
  tags: [python, django, postgresql]
  links:
    - url: https://jobpost.easypj.com
      title: Production
      icon: dashboard
spec:
  type: service              <span class="comment"># ← service | website | library | CLI</span>
  lifecycle: production      <span class="comment"># ← experimental | production | deprecated</span>
  owner: team-platform       <span class="comment"># ← WHO is responsible (User or Group)</span>
  system: easypj-system      <span class="comment"># ← Which System this belongs to</span>
  providesApis:
    - jobpost-api            <span class="comment"># ← APIs this component exposes</span>
  dependsOn:
    - resource:postgres-db   <span class="comment"># ← Resources this component depends on</span>
    - resource:redis-cache</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>spec.type</th><th>When to Use</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>service</code></td><td>Backend services, APIs, microservices</td><td>jobpost, auth-service</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>website</code></td><td>Frontend apps, SPAs, static sites</td><td>easypj-dashboard</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>library</code></td><td>Shared code libraries, npm packages</td><td>easypj-utils</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>CLI</code></td><td>Command-line tools</td><td>easypj-cli</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §8.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s8-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.2 API Entity — Document Your Interfaces</h3>
                <p>An <strong>API</strong> entity represents a formal API definition — REST (OpenAPI), GraphQL, gRPC, or AsyncAPI. APIs are separate from Components because multiple Components might consume the same API, and the API spec can be auto-generated from code.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">API Entity — jobpost-api Example</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: backstage.io/v1alpha1
kind: API
metadata:
  name: jobpost-api
  description: REST API for job posting management — filter by skills/location
  tags: [rest, openapi]
spec:
  type: openapi              <span class="comment"># ← openapi | asyncapi | graphql | grpc</span>
  lifecycle: production
  owner: team-platform
  system: easypj-system
  definition:
    $text: https://github.com/easypj/jobpost/blob/main/openapi.yaml</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §8.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s8-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.3 System &amp; Domain Entities — Organize at Scale</h3>
                <p><strong>System</strong> groups related Components and Resources. <strong>Domain</strong> groups related Systems (business area). Together they create a hierarchy: Domain → System → Components + Resources.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">System &amp; Domain Hierarchy for easypj</div>
<pre>
  🏢 hr-domain (Domain)
   └── 🖥️ easypj-system (System)
        ├── 📦 jobpost (Component: service)
        ├── 📡 jobpost-api (API: openapi)
        ├── 💾 postgres-db (Resource: database)
        └── 🔴 redis-cache (Resource: cache)
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip mb-0">
                    <h5>💡 Rule of Thumb</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">Create a <strong>System</strong> when 2+ Components/Resources work together as one product. Create a <strong>Domain</strong> only when you have MULTIPLE Systems that belong to the same business area.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §8.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s8-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.4 User &amp; Group Entities — People and Teams</h3>
                <p><strong>User</strong> represents a person (auto-discovered from auth provider). <strong>Group</strong> represents a team, squad, or organizational unit. Groups can have parent/child relationships to model your org chart.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Group Entity — team-platform Example</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: backstage.io/v1alpha1
kind: Group
metadata:
  name: team-platform
  description: Platform Engineering Team
spec:
  type: team
  profile:
    displayName: Platform Engineering
    email: platform@easypj.com
  parent: engineering-department
  children: [team-fe, team-data]
  members: [jane.doe, mike.smith]</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §8.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s8-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.5 Resource Entity — Infrastructure Components</h3>
                <p>A <strong>Resource</strong> represents infrastructure that Components depend on — databases, caches, message queues, S3 buckets, CDNs. Resources are separate from Components because multiple services might share the same database.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Resource Entity — postgres-db for easypj</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: backstage.io/v1alpha1
kind: Resource
metadata:
  name: postgres-db
  description: Primary PostgreSQL database for jobpost
spec:
  type: database             <span class="comment"># ← database | s3-bucket | pubsub-topic</span>
  owner: team-data
  system: easypj-system
  dependencyOf:
    - component:jobpost      <span class="comment"># ← Which Components depend on this</span></code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §8.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s8-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.6 Entity Relationships — How Everything Connects</h3>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">easypj Entity Relationship Map</div>
<pre>
                  ┌─────────────────────┐
                  │  🏢 hr-domain       │ ← Domain
                  └──────────┬──────────┘
                             │
                  ┌──────────▼──────────┐
                  │  🖥️ easypj-system   │ ← System
                  └──────────┬──────────┘
                             │
        ┌────────────────────┼────────────────────┐
        │                    │                     │
  ┌─────▼─────┐       ┌──────▼──────┐       ┌─────▼─────┐
  │ 📦 jobpost│       │ 💾 postgres │       │ 🔴 redis  │
  │ Component │──▶──▶│  Resource   │       │ Resource  │
  │ (service) │depends│ (database)  │       │ (cache)   │
  └─────┬─────┘       └─────────────┘       └───────────┘
        │
        │ providesApis
        ▼
  ┌─────────────┐
  │ 📡 jobpost  │ ← API
  │    -api     │
  │ (openapi)   │
  └─────────────┘

  Owner: 👥 team-platform (Group)
  Members: 👤 jane.doe, 👤 mike.smith (Users)
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §8.7 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s8-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.7 Entity Relationship Types — Complete Reference</h3>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Relationship</th><th>Direction</th><th>Example</th><th>Used In</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>owner</code></td><td>Entity → User/Group</td><td>jobpost owned by team-platform</td><td>Component, API, System, Resource, Domain</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>system</code></td><td>Component/API/Resource → System</td><td>jobpost part of easypj-system</td><td>Component, API, Resource</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>domain</code></td><td>System → Domain</td><td>easypj-system in hr-domain</td><td>System</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>providesApis</code></td><td>Component → API</td><td>jobpost provides jobpost-api</td><td>Component</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>consumesApis</code></td><td>Component → API</td><td>frontend consumes jobpost-api</td><td>Component</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>dependsOn</code></td><td>Component → Resource/Component</td><td>jobpost depends on postgres-db</td><td>Component</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>dependencyOf</code></td><td>Resource → Component</td><td>postgres-db dependency of jobpost</td><td>Resource</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>parent/children</code></td><td>Group → Group</td><td>engineering parent of platform</td><td>Group</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>memberOf</code></td><td>User → Group</td><td>jane.doe member of team-platform</td><td>User</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>subcomponentOf</code></td><td>Component → Component</td><td>jobpost-admin subcomponent of jobpost</td><td>Component</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning mb-0">
                    <h5>⚠️ KEY RULE for CBA</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0"><code>owner</code> is <strong>MANDATORY</strong> for Component, API, System, Resource, and Domain. All other relationships are optional but recommended for full visibility. The CBA exam will test your understanding of which relationships are required vs optional.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note mb-0">
                    <h5>🔑 CBA Precision — Spec Field Name ≠ Relation Type Name</h5>
                    <p>The table above shows SPEC fields (what you write in YAML). Backstage auto-generates a DIFFERENT pair of relation names in the catalog graph itself:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>YAML spec field</th><th>Auto-generated graph relation (pair)</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>owner</code></td><td><code>ownedBy</code> / <code>ownerOf</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>system</code>, <code>domain</code>, <code>subcomponentOf</code></td><td><code>partOf</code> / <code>hasPart</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>providesApis</code> / <code>consumesApis</code></td><td><code>apiProvidedBy</code>/<code>providesApi</code>, <code>apiConsumedBy</code>/<code>consumesApi</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>dependsOn</code></td><td><code>dependsOn</code> / <code>dependencyOf</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>memberOf</code></td><td><code>memberOf</code> / <code>hasMember</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Group <code>parent</code>/<code>children</code></td><td><code>parentOf</code> / <code>childOf</code></td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p class="mb-0">The exam can ask for either the YAML field name or the underlying graph relation name — know both.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §8.8 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s8-8">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.8 Entity Kind Decision Tree — Which Kind Should You Use?</h3>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Choosing the Right Entity Kind — Ask In Order, Top to Bottom</div>
<pre>
  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  🌳 ENTITY KIND DECISION TREE                                  │
  │                                                                │
  │  1. Is it a piece of software you build?                      │
  │       YES → 📦 COMPONENT (service, website, library, CLI)      │
  │  2. Is it an API definition?                                   │
  │       YES → 📡 API (openapi, asyncapi, graphql, grpc)          │
  │  3. Is it infrastructure you use (not build)?                  │
  │       YES → 💾 RESOURCE (database, cache, queue, bucket)       │
  │  4. Is it a person?                                            │
  │       YES → 👤 USER                                            │
  │  5. Is it a team/squad/org unit?                                │
  │       YES → 👥 GROUP                                            │
  │  6. Is it a grouping of related Components + Resources?        │
  │       YES → 🖥️ SYSTEM                                          │
  │  7. Is it a grouping of related Systems (a business area)?     │
  │       YES → 🏢 DOMAIN                                           │
  │  8. Is it a blueprint for creating new projects?                │
  │       YES → 🚀 TEMPLATE                                         │
  │                                                                │
  │  Ask in this order — a "yes" earlier in the list ALWAYS wins   │
  │  (e.g. a database is infrastructure, so it stops at #3 as a    │
  │  Resource, never reaching #6).                                 │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch8-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — every relationship's mirror-image graph relation name</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>Every relationship you write in <code>spec</code> has a mirror-image relation name that Backstage generates automatically in the catalog graph.</p>
                    <h5>① The Full Field-to-Relation Map</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
spec.owner                              →  ownedBy / ownerOf
spec.system, spec.domain, .subcomponentOf →  partOf / hasPart
spec.providesApis / consumesApis         →  apiProvidedBy/providesApi, apiConsumedBy/consumesApi
spec.dependsOn                            →  dependsOn / dependencyOf
spec.memberOf                             →  memberOf / hasMember
Group parent/children                     →  parentOf / childOf
                    </pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout mb-0"><strong>🔑 The YAML field name and the graph relation name are not always the same word</strong> — the exam can ask for either, so §8.7's info box and this list are worth memorizing side by side.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch8-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>9 entity kinds total:</strong> Component, API, System, Domain, Resource, User, Group, Location, Template.</li>
                    <li><strong>Component's <code>type</code> field</strong> distinguishes service/website/library/CLI; <code>lifecycle</code> tracks experimental → production → deprecated.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>owner</code> is MANDATORY</strong> on Component, API, System, Resource and Domain.</li>
                    <li><strong>System groups Components + Resources</strong> that ship together; Domain groups multiple Systems in the same business area.</li>
                    <li><strong>Group models teams with a parent/children hierarchy;</strong> User is usually auto-discovered from the auth provider, not hand-written.</li>
                    <li><strong>Every spec relationship field has a paired auto-generated graph relation name</strong> (§8.7's info box).</li>
                    <li><strong>Use the entity kind decision tree (§8.8)</strong> whenever unsure which of the 9 kinds applies.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch8-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Forgetting <code>owner</code> is mandatory</strong> on Component/API/System/Resource/Domain — a missing owner is a common validation failure (§8.7).</li>
                    <li><strong>Confusing System</strong> (groups Components/Resources) <strong>with Domain</strong> (groups Systems) (§8.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Mixing up a relationship's direction</strong> — <code>dependsOn</code> vs <code>dependencyOf</code>, <code>providesApis</code> vs <code>consumesApis</code> (§8.6/8.7).</li>
                    <li><strong>Mixing up the YAML field name</strong> (e.g. <code>providesApis</code>) <strong>with its underlying relation name</strong> (<code>apiProvidedBy</code>/<code>providesApi</code>) (Under the Hood §①).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch8-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Create a System as soon as 2+ Components/Resources ship together</strong> as one product — don't wait until it's messy.</li>
                    <li><strong>Reach for the §8.8 decision tree first</strong> whenever you're unsure between Component/Resource/System for something new.</li>
                    <li><strong>Chapter 8 quick win:</strong> memorize the 7 main entity kinds and their use cases — <code>owner</code> is mandatory for 5 of them.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch8-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 8)</h3>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Owner Required?</th><th>Key spec fields</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Component</td><td>YES</td><td>type, lifecycle, owner, system, providesApis</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>API</td><td>YES</td><td>type, lifecycle, owner, system, definition</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Resource</td><td>YES</td><td>type, owner, system, dependsOn</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>System</td><td>YES</td><td>owner, domain</td></tr>
