                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Every Component MUST have: apiVersion, kind, metadata.name, spec.type, spec.lifecycle, spec.owner. Annotations are optional but essential for integrations.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between spec.type and spec.lifecycle on a Component?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Answers</th><th>Example values</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>spec.type</code></td><td>WHAT kind of software (doesn't change often)</td><td>service, website, library, CLI</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>spec.lifecycle</code></td><td>WHERE in its dev journey (changes over time)</td><td>experimental, production, deprecated</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>A new service starts as <code>experimental</code>, becomes <code>production</code>, eventually <code>deprecated</code> — the <code>type</code> (service) stays the same throughout.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Your jobpost component depends on a PostgreSQL database. Should you model the database as a Component or a Resource? Why?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Model it as a <strong>Resource</strong> — infrastructure Components depend on, not software you write.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">Resource entity</span></div><pre><code class="language-yaml">kind: Resource
metadata:
  name: postgres-db
spec:
  type: database
  owner: team-platform
  system: easypj-system
  dependencyOf: [component:jobpost]</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Key distinction: Components = software YOU write. Resources = infrastructure you USE. If it's provisioned by Terraform or a managed service, it's a Resource.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between spec.providesApis and spec.consumesApis on a Component?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Component is the API…</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>providesApis</code></td><td>Producer (exposes it)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>consumesApis</code></td><td>Consumer (calls it)</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>jobpost provides jobpost-api; frontend consumes jobpost-api. This helps map impact: if jobpost-api goes down, both are affected.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between spec.dependsOn and spec.dependencyOf?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Used on</th><th>Says</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>dependsOn</code></td><td>Component</td><td>"jobpost depends on postgres-db"</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>dependencyOf</code></td><td>Resource</td><td>"postgres-db is a dependency of jobpost"</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Same relationship, opposite sides — bidirectional linking lets you navigate either direction for impact analysis.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to model your engineering department hierarchy: Platform, Frontend, and Data teams all report to Engineering. Which entity kind(s) do you use?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">Group hierarchy — org chart as YAML</span></div><pre><code class="language-yaml">kind: Group
metadata:
  name: engineering
spec:
  type: department
  children: [platform, frontend, data]
---
kind: Group
metadata:
  name: platform
spec:
  type: team
  parent: engineering</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Group entities with <code>parent</code>/<code>children</code> — each Group can have one parent and multiple children, matching real org charts.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Which entity kinds require the owner field? Which don't?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>owner</th><th>Kinds</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Required</td><td>Component, API, System, Domain, Resource</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Optional</td><td>User, Group, Template</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>5 of 7 main kinds require owner — only User and Group don't (since they ARE the owners). Omitting owner on a required kind fails ingestion.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You want jobpost's Jenkins pipeline to show on its entity page. What entity kind do you create?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>None — you don't create a new entity. Add an <strong>annotation</strong> to jobpost's existing Component.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the annotation</span></div><pre><code class="language-yaml">metadata:
  annotations:
    jenkins.io/job-full-name: easypj/jobpost-build</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Integrations (Jenkins, PagerDuty, Sentry) don't create new entities — they enhance existing ones through annotations.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch8-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 8 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (8)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Real workplace situations — 8 scenarios mirroring how the CBA exam actually asks.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A teammate registers a new Redis cache and sets <code>dependencyOf: [component:jobpost]</code> but forgets <code>owner</code>. What happens?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Ingestion fails — <code>owner</code> is mandatory for Resource entities.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Missing field</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>owner</code> on a Resource</td><td>Validation failure, entity never appears</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§8.7's Gotcha — a missing owner is one of the most common real YAML mistakes.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Your org has ONE system (easypj-system) with 3 Components. Someone proposes creating a Domain for it right now. Good idea?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Not yet — a Domain groups MULTIPLE Systems. With only 1 System, there's nothing to group; wait until a second System exists in the same business area.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Systems in the business area</th><th>Create a Domain?</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>1 (easypj-system only)</td><td>❌ Not yet</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>2+ (e.g. + payroll-system)</td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§8.3's rule of thumb — creating a Domain prematurely adds a layer with no real grouping value.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A developer writes <code>consumesApis: [jobpost-api]</code> on the frontend Component but expects this to make frontend the API's owner. Correct?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>No — <code>consumesApis</code> just means frontend CALLS the API. Ownership is a completely separate field (<code>owner</code>) set on the API entity itself.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>consumesApis</code></td><td>"I call this API" — not ownership</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§8.6/8.7 — providesApis/consumesApis describe usage direction, never ownership.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You write <code>dependsOn: [postgres-db]</code> on jobpost (missing the <code>resource:</code> prefix). What breaks?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The reference can't resolve which entity <code>postgres-db</code> refers to — the dependency relationship silently fails to link.</p>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
dependsOn: [postgres-db]           →  ❌ unresolved (no kind prefix)
dependsOn: [resource:postgres-db]  →  ✅ resolves correctly
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Same class of bug as Chapter 3's entity-reference format — every relation reference needs its <code>kind:</code> prefix.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A reviewer asks: "why do we need BOTH dependsOn on jobpost AND dependencyOf on postgres-db — isn't one enough?"</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Backstage actually only needs ONE of them written — it auto-generates the mirror relation on the other side. Writing both manually is redundant but not wrong.</p>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
Write dependsOn on jobpost  →  Backstage AUTO-GENERATES dependencyOf on postgres-db
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Under the Hood §① — <code>dependsOn</code> auto-produces the paired <code>dependencyOf</code> graph relation; you don't have to hand-write both.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Someone wants to register their team's Terraform module as a Catalog entity so people know who owns it. Component or Resource?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Depends on what it represents: if the Terraform module itself IS the infrastructure being tracked (e.g. an S3 bucket it provisions), model that as a <strong>Resource</strong>. The Terraform code repo itself, if you want to track it as software, would be a Component.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>What you're modeling</th><th>Kind</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>The provisioned S3 bucket</td><td>Resource</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>The Terraform code repo itself</td><td>Component</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§8.8's decision tree — "is it infrastructure you use" (Resource) is a different question than "is it software you build and maintain" (Component); both can apply to different parts of the same Terraform setup.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">An exam question asks for the graph relation name generated by <code>spec.providesApis</code>. What do you answer?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>apiProvidedBy</code> / <code>providesApi</code> (singular, even though the YAML field is a plural array).</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>YAML field</th><th>Graph relation</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>providesApis</code> (plural)</td><td><code>apiProvidedBy</code> / <code>providesApi</code> (singular)</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Under the Hood §①'s field-to-relation map — the exam explicitly tests this naming mismatch.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Walk through the decision tree for a brand-new internal CLI tool your team wrote to manage feature flags.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
1. Piece of software you build? YES → 📦 COMPONENT (spec.type: CLI)
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§8.8 — the tree stops at the FIRST "yes." A CLI tool is software you build, so it's a Component with <code>type: CLI</code>, never reaching later questions.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch8-drill"></span>
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
