            <section class="section-block" id="b-s9-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">9.6 Validating catalog-info.yaml</h3>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Validating a catalog-info.yaml — the Official Method</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># OFFICIAL validation: the Catalog API's ValidateEntity endpoint</span>
POST http://localhost:7007/api/catalog/validate-entity
<span class="comment"># Body: the entity YAML (as JSON) — returns 200 OK or 400 + errors</span>

<span class="comment"># The CLI package is @backstage/cli (NOT @spotify/backstage-cli)</span>
npm install -g @backstage/cli

<span class="comment"># Example failure output:</span>
ERROR: data must have required property 'apiVersion'
ERROR: data.spec must have required property 'lifecycle'

<span class="comment"># Also: Backstage validates on ingestion automatically — check
# backend logs after a refresh for the SAME kind of schema errors</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning mb-0">
                    <h5>⚠️ A Package-Name Correction Worth Knowing</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">Older guides sometimes reference <code>@spotify/backstage-cli</code> — that package name is stale. The current, officially documented package is <strong>@backstage/cli</strong>. Reaching for the old name is a named CBA exam gotcha.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note mb-0"><h5>📋 JSON Schema Validation</h5><p class="mb-0">Backstage validates all catalog-info.yaml files against <strong>JSON Schema</strong> definitions. Each entity kind has its own schema that defines required fields, allowed values, and field types. If your YAML doesn't match the schema, ingestion fails. The schemas live in the Backstage repo at <code>plugins/catalog-backend/schema/</code>.</p></div>
            </section>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch9-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — the same JSON Schema check runs in 2 places</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>Backstage validates every catalog-info.yaml against a JSON Schema in two places.</p>
                    <h5>① ValidateEntity API and Ingestion Both Check the Same Schema</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
POST /api/catalog/validate-entity (you call it yourself, BEFORE commit)
                    ‖  same JSON Schema  ‖
Automatic check during ingestion (AFTER commit, on every refresh)
                    </pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout mb-0"><strong>🔑 Both report the same class of error</strong> — most commonly a missing required property such as <code>apiVersion</code> or <code>spec.lifecycle</code> — so a failure caught locally is the same failure you'd otherwise chase through backend logs later (Chapter 7 §7.7).</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch9-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Every catalog-info.yaml needs exactly 4 top-level fields:</strong> apiVersion, kind, metadata, spec.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>kind</code> must be one of 9 valid values</strong> (§9.1).</li>
                    <li><strong>Labels are structured key:value pairs for UI filtering;</strong> tags are plain strings for search; annotations link to external tools — don't treat them as interchangeable.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>name</code> is the only mandatory metadata field;</strong> <code>namespace</code> defaults to 'default'.</li>
                    <li><strong>Spec fields differ by kind</strong> — Component/API/Resource/System/Domain/User/Group each has its own set (§9.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Multi-entity files use a <code>---</code> separator;</strong> prefer one file per entity in large orgs for cleaner PR diffs (§9.5).</li>
                    <li><strong>Validate with the ValidateEntity API or <code>@backstage/cli</code></strong> — NOT <code>@spotify/backstage-cli</code>, which is not the current package.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch9-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Forgetting <code>apiVersion</code></strong> — it's always the literal <code>backstage.io/v1alpha1</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Reaching for <code>@spotify/backstage-cli</code></strong> instead of the correct <code>@backstage/cli</code> package name.</li>
                    <li><strong>Putting a field that belongs to one kind's spec</strong> (e.g. <code>providesApis</code>) <strong>under a different kind</strong> (e.g. a Resource).</li>
                    <li><strong>Assuming labels and tags are interchangeable</strong> — they serve different purposes (filtering vs. search).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch9-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Always add a description</strong> — it costs nothing and is the first thing shown on the entity page.</li>
                    <li><strong>Run validation locally before committing</strong> to catch schema errors before they reach ingestion.</li>
                    <li><strong>Default to one file per entity in large orgs;</strong> only combine files for tightly-coupled entities you'll always change together.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

