                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Ingestion fails — <code>kind</code> values are case-sensitive and must match one of the 9 valid values exactly.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Written</th><th>Valid?</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>component</code></td><td>❌ No</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>Component</code></td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§9.1 — the JSON Schema enforces exact casing for the 9 valid kind values.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A teammate uses <code>tags: { language: python }</code> (an object) instead of a list. What's wrong?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Wrong shape — <code>tags</code> is a plain STRING LIST, not key-value pairs. Key-value pairs belong under <code>labels</code>, not <code>tags</code>.</p>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
tags: { language: python }     ❌ wrong shape (that's a labels-shaped value)
tags: [python, django]         ✅ correct — a simple string list
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§9.2's Gotcha — labels and tags are NOT interchangeable, including their YAML shape.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You write a Resource entity with <code>spec.providesApis: [jobpost-api]</code>. What's wrong?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>providesApis</code> is a Component-only spec field — putting it on a Resource is invalid; Resources use <code>dependencyOf</code>/<code>dependsOn</code> instead.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Valid on</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>providesApis</code></td><td>Component only</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>dependencyOf</code></td><td>Resource</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§9.3's per-kind table + Gotcha — mixing a field from one kind's spec into another kind is a common, exam-tested mistake.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">jobpost's YAML validates fine via the ValidateEntity API, but the entity still doesn't show new fields after you push. What should you check?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Whether ingestion has actually re-run yet — validation passing doesn't mean the Catalog has re-fetched the latest version. Force a refresh or wait for the scheduled interval.</p>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
ValidateEntity: 200 OK (syntax/schema fine)  ≠  Catalog has re-ingested the new push yet
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Under the Hood §① — validation and ingestion are 2 separate checks/events; passing one doesn't trigger the other automatically.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Your team wants jobpost and jobpost-api to always be reviewed and merged together in the same PR. Should they live in one file or two?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>One multi-entity file, separated by <code>---</code> — they're a small, tightly-coupled pair that should always change together.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>File strategy</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>jobpost + jobpost-api (tightly coupled)</td><td>One file, <code>---</code> separated</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Large org, many independent entities</td><td>One file per entity</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§9.5's info box — the opposite case (large orgs, many independent entities) favors one file per entity for cleaner PR diffs instead.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A new engineer installs <code>@spotify/backstage-cli</code> globally per an old blog post and finds it doesn't work. What should they install instead?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Old (stale)</th><th>Current</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>@spotify/backstage-cli</code></td><td><code>@backstage/cli</code></td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§9.6's package-name correction — this exact stale reference is a named CBA exam gotcha.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">jobpost's catalog-info.yaml has a valid Component but is missing <code>spec.lifecycle</code>. What does the ValidateEntity API return?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">400 response</span></div><pre><code class="language-bash">ERROR: data.spec must have required property 'lifecycle'</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§9.6 — a 400 with a specific JSON Schema error message, the same class of error ingestion would report later.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write the minimum valid catalog-info.yaml for a new "notifications" Component owned by team-platform.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">minimum valid form</span></div><pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: backstage.io/v1alpha1
kind: Component
metadata:
  name: notifications
spec:
  type: service
  lifecycle: experimental
  owner: team-platform</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§9.1's 4 required fields, filled minimally — no annotations/tags/labels needed to pass validation, though they're recommended.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

