            <!-- ─────────── §10.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s10-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">10.1 The Kubernetes Plugin — What It Does</h3>
                <p>The K8s plugin connects to your Kubernetes clusters and displays <strong>live resource status</strong> on entity pages. When you view a Component in Backstage, the "Kubernetes" tab shows its Deployments, Pods, Services, and Ingresses — all in real time.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">K8s Plugin Architecture</div>
<pre>
  ┌─────────────────────┐        ┌──────────────────────────┐
  │  ☸️ K8s Cluster      │───────▶│  Backstage               │
  │                     │        │  Kubernetes Plugin        │
  │  Deployment: jobpost│        │                          │
  │   ├── Pod 1 (Ready) │        │  jobpost Entity Page:    │
  │   ├── Pod 2 (Ready) │        │  ┌─────────────────────┐ │
  │   └── Pod 3 (Ready) │        │  │ ☸️ KUBERNETES       │ │
  │                     │        │  │ Deployment: jobpost │ │
  │  Service: jobpost   │        │  │ 3/3 Pods Running   │ │
  │  Ingress: jobpost   │        │  │ Service: :8000     │ │
  │                     │        │  │ Ingress: URL       │ │
  └─────────────────────┘        │  └─────────────────────┘ │
                                  └──────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §10.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s10-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">10.2 Kubernetes Plugin Configuration</h3>

                <h4>Step 1: Install the Plugins</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">install K8s frontend + backend plugins</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Install frontend plugin (adds the "Kubernetes" tab to entity pages)</span>
$ yarn add --cwd packages/app @backstage/plugin-kubernetes

<span class="comment"># Install backend plugin (connects to K8s API servers)</span>
$ yarn add --cwd packages/backend @backstage/plugin-kubernetes-backend

<span class="comment"># Register in packages/app/src/plugins.ts:</span>
export { kubernetesPlugin } from '@backstage/plugin-kubernetes';

<span class="comment"># Register in packages/backend/src/index.ts:</span>
backend.add(import('@backstage/plugin-kubernetes-backend'));</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Step 2: Configure app-config.yaml</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">app-config.yaml — K8s Plugin Configuration</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># app-config.yaml
kubernetes:
  serviceLocatorMethod:
    type: 'multiTenant'                # ← How services are found
  clusterLocatorMethods:
    - type: 'config'                   # ← Static cluster list
      clusters:
        - name: 'easypj-cluster'       # ← Cluster display name
          url: 'https://k8s-api.easypj.com'  # ← API Server URL
          authProvider: 'serviceAccount'      # ← Auth method
          serviceAccountToken: ${K8S_SA_TOKEN}  # ← Token from env var
          skipTLSVerify: false         # ← TLS verification
          dashboardUrl: 'https://k8s-dash.easypj.com'  # ← K8s dashboard link</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note mb-0">
                    <h5>🔑 serviceLocatorMethod — 2 Values</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0"><code>multiTenant</code> (shown above) fans out and searches every configured cluster for a match. <code>singleTenant</code> pins an entity to exactly ONE named cluster via the <code>backstage.io/kubernetes-cluster</code> annotation, skipping the fan-out search entirely.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip mb-0">
                    <h5>💡 'config' Is Only ONE of 4 clusterLocatorMethods Types</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">The official Kubernetes plugin docs also list <code>catalog</code> (derive clusters from Catalog entities instead of static config), <code>gke</code> (auto-discover Google Kubernetes Engine clusters), and <code>localKubectlProxy</code> (use your local kubectl proxy — handy for local development). easypj's example above uses the static <code>config</code> method, the right choice for a small, fixed set of clusters.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Step 3: Create K8s ServiceAccount &amp; RBAC</h4>
                <p>Backstage needs <strong>read-only access</strong> to your K8s cluster. Create a ServiceAccount with appropriate RBAC permissions:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">K8s RBAC for Backstage Service Account</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># 1. Create ServiceAccount
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: backstage-reader
  namespace: backstage
---
# 2. Create ClusterRole (read-only)
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRole
metadata:
  name: backstage-reader
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["pods", "services", "configmaps"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
- apiGroups: ["apps"]
  resources: ["deployments", "replicasets"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
- apiGroups: ["networking.k8s.io"]
  resources: ["ingresses"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
---
# 3. Bind ClusterRole to ServiceAccount
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRoleBinding
metadata:
  name: backstage-reader
subjects:
- kind: ServiceAccount
  name: backstage-reader
  namespace: backstage
roleRef:
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: backstage-reader
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">get the token</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">$ kubectl create token backstage-reader -n backstage
<span class="comment"># → Copy this token to K8S_SA_TOKEN env var</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning mb-0">
                    <h5>⚠️ CBA Exam: Backstage K8s Permissions</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">Backstage needs <strong>get, list, watch</strong> on Pods, Services, Deployments, ReplicaSets, and Ingresses. It NEVER creates, updates, or deletes K8s resources — it's read-only. The CBA may ask what minimum RBAC permissions Backstage requires.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §10.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s10-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">10.3 Linking K8s Resources to Catalog Entities</h3>
                <p>Backstage matches K8s resources to Catalog entities using <strong>annotations</strong> in catalog-info.yaml. There are 3 primary linking methods, plus 1 more for pinning to a specific cluster:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">3 Methods to Link K8s to Entities</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># Method 1: By Name (simplest)
metadata:
  annotations:
    backstage.io/kubernetes-id: jobpost
# → Matches K8s Deployment named "jobpost"

# Method 2: By Namespace
metadata:
  annotations:
    backstage.io/kubernetes-namespace: easypj-prod
# → Shows ALL K8s resources in that namespace

# Method 3: By Label Selector (most flexible)
metadata:
  annotations:
    backstage.io/kubernetes-label-selector: app=jobpost
# → Matches any K8s resource with label "app=jobpost"</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip mb-0">
                    <h5>🔑 Which One Do I Use?</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">Use <code>kubernetes-id</code> for exact name matches (most common). Use <code>kubernetes-label-selector</code> when one entity maps to MULTIPLE K8s objects that share a label but not an exact name.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note mb-0">
                    <h5>🔑 One More Official Annotation — backstage.io/kubernetes-cluster</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">Lets an entity pin itself to ONE specific, named cluster. Used together with the <code>singleTenant</code> serviceLocatorMethod (§10.2), where Backstage must be told exactly which configured cluster a given entity's resources live in, rather than searching every configured cluster.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §10.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s10-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">10.4 Viewing K8s Resources in Backstage</h3>
                <p>When the K8s plugin is configured, every entity with a K8s annotation gets a <strong>"Kubernetes" tab</strong>. The data is <strong>live</strong> — it queries the K8s API directly each time you view the tab.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Kubernetes Tab — What You'll See on the Entity Page</div>
<pre>
  ☸️ KUBERNETES
  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  Deployment: jobpost          🟢 3/3 pods Running               │
  │  ReplicaSet: jobpost-7d9f8    🟢 Current                        │
  │  Service:    jobpost-svc      ClusterIP · :8000                 │
  │  Ingress:    jobpost.easypj.com  🔒 TLS active                  │
  │  Pod events: 2 min ago — Started container jobpost              │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>What You See</th><th>Details Shown</th><th>Where It Comes From</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Deployments</strong></td><td>Replicas (ready/desired), strategy, selector</td><td>apps/v1 Deployment API</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Pods</strong></td><td>Status (Running/Pending/CrashLoopBackOff), restart count, age</td><td>core/v1 Pod API</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Pod Logs</strong></td><td>Stdout/stderr streams (last 100 lines)</td><td>Pod logs sub-resource</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Pod Events</strong></td><td>Schedule, pull, start events with timestamps</td><td>core/v1 Event API</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Services</strong></td><td>Type (ClusterIP/LoadBalancer), ports, selector</td><td>core/v1 Service API</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Ingresses</strong></td><td>Host, path, TLS config, backend service</td><td>networking.k8s.io/v1 Ingress API</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Resource Usage</strong></td><td>CPU/memory requests &amp; limits (if metrics-server installed)</td><td>metrics.k8s.io API</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §10.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s10-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">10.5 Deploying easypj to Kubernetes — Complete Walkthrough</h3>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Dockerfile for easypj/jobpost Django App</div>
<pre><code class="language-dockerfile">FROM python:3.11-slim
ENV PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1
ENV PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1
WORKDIR /app
