            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 10 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the K8s plugin, configuration, and entity linking — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What does the Kubernetes plugin in Backstage do?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Connects Backstage to your Kubernetes clusters and displays <strong>live resource status</strong> on entity pages.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Shows</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Deployments, Pods, Services, Ingresses</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Frontend renders the K8s tab, backend communicates with the K8s API. Data is always live — no caching by default.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Where do you configure K8s cluster connection details for Backstage?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>In <code>app-config.yaml</code> under the <code>kubernetes</code> section — <code>clusterLocatorMethods</code> with name, URL, auth method, and token.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>serviceLocatorMethod</td><td>multiTenant / singleTenant</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>clusterLocatorMethods type</td><td>config / catalog / gke / localKubectlProxy</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Always use <code>${ENV_VAR}</code> for tokens and URLs — never hardcode K8s connection details.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What are the 3 primary ways to link a Catalog entity to K8s resources?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Annotation</th><th>Matches by</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>kubernetes-id</code></td><td>Exact Deployment name</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>kubernetes-namespace</code></td><td>All resources in a namespace</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>kubernetes-label-selector</code></td><td>K8s label query</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>kubernetes-id</code> is most common and must match the Deployment name EXACTLY (case-sensitive). You can combine annotations — Backstage ORs them together.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What authentication method does Backstage use to connect to K8s clusters?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Service Account tokens</strong> are the most common method (<code>authProvider: serviceAccount</code>). OIDC is also supported.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>authProvider</th><th>Use case</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>serviceAccount</td><td>Most common — static token</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>oidc</td><td>Cloud-managed clusters, dynamic tokens</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>ServiceAccount: create SA → grant RBAC → get token → pass via env var. OIDC: more complex, dynamic short-lived tokens, better for cloud-managed clusters.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write the complete app-config.yaml kubernetes section to connect a cluster named "easypj-cluster".</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">minimum single-cluster config</span></div><pre><code class="language-yaml">kubernetes:
