            <section class="section-block" id="b-s11-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">11.1 What are Software Templates? — The 2-Hour vs 2-Minute Problem</h3>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Templates — Before vs After Backstage</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  ❌ WITHOUT TEMPLATES (Manual Scaffolding)                   │
  │  1. Find a similar repo to copy from... 20 min               │
  │  2. Clone it and delete what you don't need... 15 min        │
  │  3. Rename everything (files, packages, configs)... 30 min   │
  │  4. Update CI/CD configs... 20 min                            │
  │  5. Create repo in GitHub and push... 10 min                  │
  │  6. Set up Docker/K8s manifests... 25 min                     │
  │  ────────────────────────────────────────────                 │
  │  TOTAL: ~2 HOURS of tedious manual work                       │
  │                                                               │
  │  ✅ WITH BACKSTAGE TEMPLATES (Automated Scaffolding)          │
  │  1. Click "Create" → Choose Template → Fill Form → Done      │
  │  ────────────────────────────────────────────                 │
  │  TOTAL: ~2 MINUTES — everything automated!                   │
  │  • GitHub repo created automatically                         │
  │  • CI/CD pipeline pre-configured                             │
  │  • K8s manifests generated                                   │
  │  • Catalog entity registered automatically                   │
  │  • TechDocs skeleton ready to go                             │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip mb-0"><h5>💡 Template = Blueprint + Parameters + Actions</h5><p class="mb-0">A template has 3 parts: <strong>Blueprint</strong> (the skeleton files — Dockerfile, CI config, K8s YAML), <strong>Parameters</strong> (user input form — service name, owner, description), and <strong>Actions</strong> (what happens — fetch skeleton, publish to GitHub, register in Catalog). Together they automate the entire project creation process.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §11.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s11-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">11.2 Template Anatomy — Complete template.yaml</h3>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Complete template.yaml for a Django Microservice</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: scaffolder.backstage.io/v1beta3
kind: Template
metadata:
  name: django-service
  title: Django Microservice
  description: Scaffold a new Django service for the easypj ecosystem
  tags: [django, python, microservice, recommended]
spec:
  owner: team-platform
  type: service         <span class="comment"># ← free-form string, not a fixed enum (see info box below)</span>

  <span class="comment"># ── USER INPUTS (the form developers fill in) ──</span>
  parameters:
    - title: Service Details
      required: [name, owner]
      properties:
        name:
          title: Service Name
          type: string
          description: Unique name for your service
          pattern: '^[a-z0-9-]+$'
          ui:autofocus: true
        description:
          title: Description
          type: string
          description: What does this service do?
        owner:
          title: Owner
          type: string
          ui:field: OwnerPicker
          ui:options:
            allowedKinds: [Group]

  <span class="comment"># ── ACTIONS TO EXECUTE ──</span>
  steps:
    - id: fetch-base
      name: Fetch Template Skeleton
      action: fetch:template
      input:
        url: ./skeleton
        values:
          name: ${{ parameters.name }}
          description: ${{ parameters.description }}
          owner: ${{ parameters.owner }}

    - id: publish
      name: Publish to GitHub
      action: publish:github
      input:
        allowedHosts: ['github.com']
        description: ${{ parameters.description }}
        repoUrl: github.com?owner=${{ parameters.owner }}&repo=${{ parameters.name }}
        defaultBranch: main

    - id: register
      name: Register in Catalog
      action: catalog:register
      input:
        repoContentsUrl: ${{ steps.publish.output.repoContentsUrl }}
        catalogInfoPath: /catalog-info.yaml</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note mb-0">
                    <h5>🔑 spec.type Is a Free-Form String, Not a Fixed Enum</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">Common values are <code>service</code> | <code>website</code> | <code>library</code> | <code>documentation</code> | <code>resource</code>, but Backstage does NOT restrict you to a fixed list — Chapter 13's infra-as-code template uses <code>resource</code>, and you can invent your own classification if it fits your org. Don't confuse <code>spec.type</code> (a free-form classification) with <code>kind: Template</code> (the entity kind itself) — they answer two different questions.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §11.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s11-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">11.3 Template Parameters — The User Input Form</h3>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Parameter Type</th><th>UI Field</th><th>Example</th><th>Validation</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>string</code></td><td>Text input field</td><td>Service name</td><td>pattern, minLength, maxLength</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>string</code></td><td><code>OwnerPicker</code></td><td>Select from Users/Groups</td><td>Must be an existing entity</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>string</code></td><td><code>RepoUrlPicker</code></td><td>Select GitHub org/repo</td><td>Valid host/owner/repo</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>string</code></td><td><code>EntityPicker</code></td><td>Select from Catalog entities</td><td>Must exist in Catalog</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>string</code> (enum)</td><td>Dropdown</td><td><code>pythonVersion: ['3.10','3.11','3.12']</code></td><td>One of the listed values</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>number</code></td><td>Number input</td><td>Port number</td><td>minimum, maximum</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>boolean</code></td><td>Checkbox toggle</td><td>Enable monitoring</td><td>—</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>array</code></td><td>Multi-select / tag input</td><td>Tags list</td><td>items, minItems, uniqueItems</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>object</code></td><td>Grouped fields</td><td>Nested config</td><td>properties, required</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip mb-0"><h5>💡 UI Fields — Beyond Basic Inputs</h5><p class="mb-0">Backstage provides special UI fields for common inputs: <code>OwnerPicker</code> (select from Users/Groups), <code>RepoUrlPicker</code> (select GitHub org/repo), <code>EntityPicker</code> (select from Catalog entities). These provide auto-complete and validation out of the box — they prevent typos that would otherwise break <code>catalog:register</code> later in the pipeline.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §11.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s11-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">11.4 Template Actions — What Happens When You Click "Create"</h3>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Template Execution Flow</div>
<pre>
  👤 User fills form
       │
       ▼
  📝 Parameters collected (name, owner, description)
       │
       ▼
  ⬇️ Step 1: fetch:template → Copy skeleton files, replace placeholders
       │
       ▼
  🔧 Step 2: (optional) Custom actions → Run cookiecutter, process templates
       │
       ▼
  🚀 Step 3: publish:github → Create GitHub repo, push code
       │
       ▼
  📦 Step 4: catalog:register → Register new entity in Catalog
       │
       ▼
  ✅ Done! New service visible in Backstage Catalog!
</pre>
                </div>

                <div id="b-s11-4a"><h4>fetch:template / fetch:plain</h4><p><code>fetch:template</code> copies a local <code>./skeleton</code> directory with placeholder replacement (<code>${{ values.name }}</code>). <code>fetch:plain</code> copies files from a URL without placeholder processing. Use fetch:template when you need customized files; use fetch:plain for static files like license templates.</p></div>

                <div id="b-s11-4b"><h4>publish:github / publish:gitlab</h4><p>Creates a new repository on GitHub/GitLab, pushes the skeleton code, and sets the default branch. Requires the GitHub/GitLab integration token configured in app-config.yaml. The <code>repoUrl</code> parameter uses query string format: <code>github.com?owner=ORG&amp;repo=NAME</code>.</p></div>

                <div id="b-s11-4c"><h4>catalog:register</h4><p>Registers the newly created repository in the Backstage Catalog by reading its <code>catalog-info.yaml</code>. This makes the new service immediately discoverable. The action reads <code>repoContentsUrl</code> from the publish step's output.</p></div>

                <div id="b-s11-4d"><h4>Custom Actions</h4><p>You can build custom actions for organization-specific needs — e.g., creating a Jira ticket, sending Slack notifications, provisioning cloud resources. Custom actions use <code>createTemplateAction()</code> from <code>@backstage/plugin-scaffolder-backend</code>. Other built-in actions worth knowing: <code>fetch:cookiecutter</code> (Cookiecutter-style templates) and <code>debug:wait</code> (used purely for testing template execution timing).</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §11.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s11-5">
