            <section class="section-block" id="b-s11-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">11.7 Template Creation Walkthrough — Building the easypj Django Template</h3>
                <p>Let's build a real Software Template from scratch — the same "Django Microservice" template used to scaffold the jobpost service. This end-to-end walkthrough covers every concept the CBA tests.</p>

                <h4>Part A: Create the Template Directory Structure</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Template Directory Layout</div>
<pre>templates/
└── django-service/
    ├── template.yaml          <span class="comment"># Template definition (metadata + parameters + steps)</span>
    └── skeleton/               <span class="comment"># Files copied to new project</span>
        ├── catalog-info.yaml   <span class="comment"># Auto-registers the new service</span>
        ├── Dockerfile           <span class="comment"># Container definition</span>
        ├── mkdocs.yml           <span class="comment"># TechDocs configuration</span>
        └── docs/
            └── index.md         <span class="comment"># Starter documentation</span></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Part B: Write template.yaml — The Template Definition</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">templates/django-service/template.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: scaffolder.backstage.io/v1beta3  <span class="comment"># ← Note: scaffolder.backstage.io!</span>
kind: Template
metadata:
  name: django-service
  title: Django Microservice
  description: Scaffolds a production-ready Django microservice
  tags: [recommended, python, django]
spec:
  owner: team-platform
  type: service

  <span class="comment"># ── PARAMETERS: The form the user fills out ──</span>
  parameters:
    - title: Service Details
      required: [name, owner]
      properties:
        name:
          title: Service Name
          type: string
          description: Unique name for the service
        owner:
          title: Owner
          type: string
          ui:field: OwnerPicker
          ui:options:
            allowedKinds: [Group]
        description:
          title: Description
          type: string
        port:
          title: Port
          type: number
          default: 8000

  <span class="comment"># ── STEPS: The actions executed in order ──</span>
  steps:
    - id: fetch
      name: Fetch Skeleton
      action: fetch:template
      input:
        url: ./skeleton
        values:
          name: ${{ parameters.name }}
          owner: ${{ parameters.owner }}
          description: ${{ parameters.description }}
          port: ${{ parameters.port }}

    - id: publish
      name: Publish to GitHub
      action: publish:github
      input:
        allowedHosts: [github.com]
        description: ${{ parameters.description }}
        repoUrl: github.com?owner=easypj&repo=${{ parameters.name }}

    - id: register
      name: Register in Catalog
      action: catalog:register
      input:
        repoContentsUrl: ${{ steps.publish.output.repoContentsUrl }}
        catalogInfoPath: /catalog-info.yaml</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Part C: Register the Template in Backstage</h4>
                <p>Add the template location to <code>app-config.yaml</code>:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">app-config.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">catalog:
  locations:
    - type: file
      target: ./templates/django-service/template.yaml
      rules:
        - allow: [Template]  <span class="comment"># ← REQUIRED, or Backstage won't recognize it as a Template!</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note mb-0">
                    <h5>🔑 CBA Key Concept — The 3-Step Pipeline</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">Every template follows: <strong>fetch:template</strong> (get skeleton code) → <strong>publish:github</strong> (create repo) → <strong>catalog:register</strong> (add to catalog). The CBA WILL test this order. Steps are sequential — step 2 cannot start until step 1 completes. Each step's outputs are available to subsequent steps via <code>${{ steps.STEP_ID.output.OUTPUT_NAME }}</code>.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §11.8 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s11-8">
                <h3 class="section-h">11.8 Template Task List &amp; Start Over — Managing Template Executions</h3>
                <p>Every template execution creates a <strong>unique task</strong> with its own ID. Backstage provides a Task List view and a "Start Over" feature for managing these executions.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div><h5>Template Task List</h5>
                        <p>View all previously executed template tasks. Access from the "Create" page via the kebab menu (⋮) in the upper right corner. Each task shows its status (completed, failed, processing), template name, and execution time.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔄</div><h5>Start Over</h5>
                        <p>Re-run a previously executed template with the same parameters pre-filled. Useful for: retrying failed templates, creating similar services, or re-running with minor parameter changes. Find it in the Task List context menu.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note mb-0">
                    <h5>🔑 CBA Key Concept — Task Lifecycle</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">Each template execution is an async task: <strong>open → processing → completed/failed</strong>. The Task List shows history. "Start Over" pre-fills parameters but allows editing before re-execution. In case of failure, you can click each step to see its log output for debugging. You can also cancel a running task — the current step is cancelled if it supports the abort signal.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Disabling "Register Existing Component"</h4>
                <p>If you want to force all components to be created through templates (not manually registered), disable the button in app-config.yaml:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">app-config.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">app:
  routes:
    bindings:
      scaffolder.registerComponent: false  <span class="comment"># ← Disables manual registration</span></code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch11-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — how a temporary workspace and output-chaining make the 3-step pipeline work</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>When a developer clicks Create, the scaffolder spins up a temporary workspace for that run.</p>
                    <h5>① Placeholder Substitution and Output Chaining</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
fetch:template writes files into the temp workspace
    → replaces every ${{ values.X }} placeholder with the submitted parameter
publish step's OUTPUT (e.g. repoContentsUrl)
    → becomes available to EVERY subsequent step as ${{ steps.&lt;id&gt;.output.&lt;field&gt; }}
                    </pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout mb-0"><strong>🔑 This output-chaining is exactly why a template can compose fetch → publish → register into one pipeline</strong> instead of three unrelated manual scripts — the register step's <code>repoContentsUrl</code> input flows in from the publish step without either action knowing the other exists.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch11-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>A Software Template = template.yaml</strong> (the blueprint) + parameters (the form) + steps (the actions).</li>
                    <li><strong>template.yaml always declares <code>apiVersion: scaffolder.backstage.io/v1beta3</code></strong> and <code>kind: Template</code> — different from catalog entities' <code>backstage.io/v1alpha1</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>spec.type</code> classifies what kind of thing gets scaffolded</strong> — common values are service/website/library/documentation/resource, but it's a free-form string, not a fixed enum.</li>
                    <li><strong>Parameters map directly onto form fields</strong> the developer fills in the Backstage UI; UI pickers like OwnerPicker/RepoUrlPicker add validation and catalog lookups on top of a plain text box.</li>
                    <li><strong>The near-universal 3-step shape is fetch → publish → register:</strong> copy the skeleton, push it to GitHub, register the result in the Catalog.</li>
                    <li><strong>Manual scaffolding took ~2 hours by hand;</strong> a template reduces this to ~2 minutes end to end.</li>
                    <li><strong>Once registered, the new service is immediately visible in the Catalog,</strong> picked up by the same ingestion pipeline (Chapter 7 §7.2) as every other entity.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch11-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Forgetting <code>rules: [{allow: [Template]}]</code></strong> on a <code>type: file</code> catalog.locations entry — without it Backstage won't recognize the file as a Template kind (§11.7).</li>
                    <li><strong>Confusing <code>spec.type</code></strong> (a free-form classification: service/website/library/documentation) <strong>with <code>kind: Template</code></strong> (the entity kind itself) — they answer two different questions (§11.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Leaving a required parameter out of <code>required: [...]</code></strong> — the form then lets a developer submit it empty, breaking a later step that assumes it's filled (§11.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Assuming <code>fetch:template</code> creates a GitHub repository</strong> — it only copies files into a local/temp workspace; <code>publish:github</code> is a separate, later step (§11.4).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch11-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Reuse the standard fetch → publish → register 3-step shape</strong> as your starting skeleton for any new template — usually only the <code>skeleton/</code> contents need to change.</li>
                    <li><strong>Use <code>ui:field: OwnerPicker</code>/<code>EntityPicker</code></strong> instead of a free-text string wherever the answer must resolve to an existing catalog entity — it prevents typos that would otherwise break <code>catalog:register</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Test a new template.yaml locally with <code>yarn dev</code></strong> before publishing it org-wide.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

