                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A team wants their template to notify #platform-alerts on Slack after every successful scaffold. Do they need a custom action, or does a built-in cover this?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A custom action is needed — no built-in action talks to Slack. This is exactly the case the "IF YOU NEED TO... REACH FOR..." table flags as custom-code territory.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Built-in available?</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Notify Slack</td><td>❌ No — write a custom action</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§12.3's decision table — most templates need zero custom code, but external-system notifications are the one common exception.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A custom action's schema doesn't mark <code>channel</code> as required. In production, a template omits it. What happens at runtime?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>ctx.input.channel</code> is <code>undefined</code> — the handler likely crashes or silently misbehaves, since nothing validated its presence before the handler ran.</p>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
required: []  (channel not required)  →  ctx.input.channel = undefined  →  handler crashes
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§12's Gotcha — always mark genuinely-needed input properties as <code>required</code> in the schema.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A template's Task completion page shows only raw step logs, no "Open in Catalog" link. What's missing from the template.yaml?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>spec.output.links</code> — without it, Backstage has nothing to render as a clickable link on the completion page.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Present?</th><th>Task page shows</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Missing</td><td>Raw step logs only</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Present</td><td>"Open in Catalog" clickable link</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§12.1's info box — this is a real, common omission that makes an otherwise-working template feel broken to end users.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Someone hardcodes a value instead of using <code>${{ steps.publish.output.repoContentsUrl }}</code> in the register step. What breaks if the repo naming logic ever changes?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The register step points at a stale/wrong location — hardcoding defeats the whole purpose of output-chaining, which exists specifically so steps stay correct even as upstream behavior changes.</p>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
Hardcoded URL          →  breaks silently when repo-naming logic changes
${{ steps.publish.output.repoContentsUrl }}  →  always correct, no matter how publish computes it
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Under the Hood §① — the shared workspace + output contract is designed so no step needs to hardcode another step's result.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A template ran successfully but the generated <code>catalog-info.yaml</code> still literally contains <code>{{ values.name }}</code> instead of the real service name. What's the most likely cause?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A typo — the property name referenced in the skeleton file doesn't exactly match the parameter's actual name in <code>spec.parameters</code>.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Cause</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Literal <code>{{ }}</code> in output</td><td>Typo'd placeholder name</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§12.4's common-errors table — this exact symptom (literal <code>{{ }}</code> in output) has one specific listed cause.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A teammate insists templates always execute steps in parallel for speed. Are they right?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>No — steps execute strictly in the order listed under <code>spec.steps</code>, sequentially. Parallel execution would break output-chaining entirely (register needs publish's output, which wouldn't exist yet).</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Claim</th><th>Reality</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>"Steps run in parallel"</td><td>False — strictly sequential</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§12.5's exam trap — action order is immutable and sequential, never parallel.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A step's output doesn't seem to be flowing into the next step as expected. What's the fastest way to see what value actually resolved?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">debug logging</span></div><pre><code class="language-bash">BACKSTAGE_LOG_LEVEL=debug yarn dev</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§12's Speed Tips — the debug log shows the actual resolved <code>{{ steps.* }}</code> values, faster than guessing.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
