            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch13-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — a Template is ingested exactly like any other entity</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>All 4 easypj templates are registered the same way every other Template entity is: a <code>type: file</code> catalog.locations entry pointing at each template.yaml, gated by <code>rules: [{allow: [Template]}]</code> (§13.5).</p>
                    <h5>① Templates Go Through the Same Ingestion Pipeline as Any Entity</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
type: file location  →  EntityProvider reads it  →  Processor validates + stores  →  queryable/searchable
                    </pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout mb-0"><strong>🔑 The Catalog's ingestion pipeline (Chapter 7 §7.2) treats a Template exactly like it treats a Component or a System.</strong> That is also why a broken template.yaml (bad YAML, missing required fields) fails the same way a broken catalog-info.yaml would — it simply won't appear in the Catalog until the syntax is fixed.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch13-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>easypj maintains exactly 4 templates today:</strong> django-service (service), react-frontend (website), db-migration-job (service), infra-as-code (resource).</li>
                    <li><strong>All 4 share the identical 3-step shape:</strong> fetch → publish:github → catalog:register — only the skeleton/ contents and parameters differ.</li>
                    <li><strong>django-service and db-migration-job are both owned by team-platform and typed service,</strong> despite scaffolding very different things (a Django app versus a one-off K8s Job).</li>
                    <li><strong>react-frontend is the only one of the 4 typed website and owned by team-fe.</strong></li>
                    <li><strong>infra-as-code is the only one typed resource</strong> — it scaffolds Terraform, not application code.</li>
                    <li><strong>All 4 templates are registered as type: file catalog.locations entries,</strong> each with <code>rules: [{allow: [Template]}]</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Templates become visible in the Create wizard as soon as their location is registered</strong> — no extra deploy step beyond a config reload.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch13-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Assuming every template must be type: service</strong> — infra-as-code is type: resource and react-frontend is type: website; spec.type is a free classification, not fixed to the action steps.</li>
                    <li><strong>Copy-pasting one template's owner</strong> (e.g. team-platform) into another without checking who actually owns it (react-frontend is team-fe).</li>
                    <li><strong>Forgetting the <code>rules: [{allow: [Template]}]</code> restriction</strong> on each catalog.locations entry — without it Backstage may misinterpret the file as a different kind.</li>
                    <li><strong>Missing a required parameter</strong> (e.g. targetService on db-migration-job, cloudProvider on infra-as-code) so the generated skeleton has no way to know what it's targeting.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch13-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Start any new easypj template from whichever of the 4 existing ones is structurally closest</strong> (same type/owner) and only change the skeleton/ and parameters.</li>
                    <li><strong>Keep the fetch → publish:github → catalog:register step IDs and shape identical across templates</strong> — consistency makes slotting in a custom action (§12.3) easy.</li>
                    <li><strong>Register a new template with a single extra catalog.locations entry</strong> — no code change to Backstage itself is required.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch13-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 13)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the 4 templates + generic registration</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># THE 4 easypj TEMPLATES (name / type / owner):
#   django-service     service   team-platform
#   react-frontend     website   team-fe
#   db-migration-job   service   team-platform
#   infra-as-code      resource  team-platform

# Generic registration pattern (app-config.yaml), repeat per template:
catalog:
  locations:
    - type: file
      target: ./templates/&lt;name&gt;/template.yaml
      rules:
        - allow: [Template]</code></pre>
