            <!-- ─────────── §14.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s14-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">14.1 What are TechDocs? — Documentation-as-Code</h3>
                <p>TechDocs follows the <strong>docs-as-code</strong> philosophy: documentation lives in the same Git repository as your source code, written in Markdown (<code>.md</code>) or MDX (<code>.mdx</code>), and is automatically built and published every time you push. Under the hood, TechDocs uses <strong>MkDocs</strong> — a Python-based static site generator that converts Markdown files into beautiful, searchable HTML documentation. MDX support allows embedding React components directly in your docs for interactive examples.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The TechDocs Pipeline — From Markdown to Beautiful Docs</div>
<pre>
  📝 Developer writes docs in Markdown
       │
       ▼
  📦 Docs live in same repo as code (/docs/)
       │  index.md, architecture.md, api-reference.md
       ▼
  🚀 Git push triggers TechDocs build
       │
       ▼
  ⚙️ Generator (MkDocs)
       │  • Converts Markdown → Static HTML/CSS/JS
       │  • Strips the JS afterward, for security
       │  • Creates search index, renders Mermaid diagrams
       ▼
  📤 Publisher
       │  • Stores the static files: local filesystem (Basic) or
       │    cloud storage — S3/GCS/Azure Blob (Recommended)
       ▼
  🔌 techdocs-backend
       │  • The single backend intermediary that retrieves docs
       │    from whichever storage type was configured and serves them
       ▼
  🖥️ Reader
          • Frontend piece — requests docs from techdocs-backend
            by entity ID, renders them on the "Docs" tab
          • Full-text search, navigation sidebar from mkdocs.yml
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note mb-0">
                    <h5>🔑 4 Official Architecture Pieces — Know All 4, Not Just 3</h5>
                    <p>Generator (runs MkDocs), Publisher (stores the output), <strong>techdocs-backend</strong> (retrieves + serves — the piece easiest to forget), and Reader (renders on the entity page). Backstage names two deployment models: <strong>'Basic'</strong> (docs generated on-demand on the Backstage server itself, stored locally) and <strong>'Recommended'</strong> (docs generated in CI/CD, stored in cloud storage, served on-demand by techdocs-backend).</p>
                    <p class="mb-0">The Reader never talks to storage directly — it always goes through techdocs-backend, regardless of which storage type is configured underneath.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §14.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s14-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">14.2 Setting Up TechDocs</h3>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Complete mkdocs.yml for jobpost Documentation</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># mkdocs.yml — Place at repo root, next to /docs/ folder
site_name: 'jobpost Documentation'
site_description: 'Job posting and recruitment application — Developer Docs'
repo_url: https://github.com/easypj/jobpost

nav:                                  <span class="comment"># ← Controls the sidebar navigation</span>
  - Home: index.md
  - Architecture: architecture.md
  - 'API Reference': api-reference.md
  - 'Getting Started': getting-started.md
  - Deployment: deployment.md
  - Contributing: contributing.md

plugins:
  - techdocs-core                     <span class="comment"># ← Required TechDocs plugin</span>

markdown_extensions:
  - admonition                        <span class="comment"># ← !!! note, !!! warning, !!! tip</span>
  - codehilite                        <span class="comment"># ← Syntax highlighting</span>
  - pymdownx.superfences:             <span class="comment"># ← Mermaid diagram support</span>
      custom_fences:
        - name: mermaid
          class: mermaid
          format: !!python/name:pymdownx.superfences.fence_div_format
  - toc:                              <span class="comment"># ← Auto-generate table of contents</span>
      permalink: true</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">docs/ Directory Anatomy — Generic MkDocs Project Layout</div>
<pre>
  jobpost/                     (repo root)
    mkdocs.yml                  &lt;- nav + plugins config (shown above)
    docs/
      index.md                   &lt;- required, the home page
      architecture.md
      api-reference.md
      assets/                      &lt;- images referenced by ![](assets/x.png)
      overrides/                     &lt;- optional, custom theme overrides

  Only docs/ + mkdocs.yml are required — assets/ and overrides/ are opt-in.
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §14.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s14-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">14.3 TechDocs Configuration in app-config.yaml</h3>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">app-config.yaml — TechDocs Section, Dev vs Production</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># Development
techdocs:
  builder: local
  generator: { runIn: local }
  publisher: { type: local }

# Production
techdocs:
  builder: external          <span class="comment"># CI/CD pre-builds docs</span>
  generator: { runIn: docker } <span class="comment"># isolated MkDocs container</span>
  publisher:
    type: awsS3
    awsS3: { bucketName: easypj-techdocs, region: us-east-1 }</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div id="b-s14-3a"><h4>Builder (local | external)</h4><p><code>local</code>: Backstage builds docs on-demand when viewing. Best for development. <code>external</code>: Docs are pre-built by CI/CD pipeline. Best for production — build in CI, publish to cloud storage, Backstage just serves.</p></div>
                <div id="b-s14-3b"><h4>Generator (local | docker)</h4><p><code>local</code>: Runs MkDocs directly on the Backstage server (requires Python + MkDocs installed). <code>docker</code>: Runs MkDocs in a Docker container — isolated, no Python needed on host, consistent builds across environments. <strong>Docker is recommended for production.</strong></p>
                <div class="comparison-table"><table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Generator option</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>dockerImage</code></td><td>Override the default MkDocs Docker image</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>pullImage</code></td><td>Pull a fresh copy of the image before each build</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>mkdocs.omitTechdocsCorePlugin</code></td><td>Skip auto-injecting the techdocs-core plugin</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>mkdocs.legacyCopyReadmeMdToIndexMd</code></td><td>Fallback: copy README.md to index.md if missing</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>mkdocs.disableExternalFonts</code></td><td>For air-gapped networks that can't reach font CDNs</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>mkdocs.defaultPlugins</code></td><td>Array of MkDocs plugin names injected by default</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table></div>
                </div>
                <div id="b-s14-3c"><h4>Publisher (local | GCS | S3 | Azure)</h4><p><code>local</code>: Stores generated HTML in <code>./techdocs-output/</code>. Only works for single-instance dev. <code>googleGcs</code>/<code>awsS3</code>/<code>azureBlobStorage</code>: Cloud storage — required for multi-replica production deployments so all pods can access the same docs.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table"><table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Publisher type</th><th>Required field</th><th>Notable optional fields</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>googleGcs</code></td><td><code>bucketName</code></td><td>bucketRootPath, projectId, credentials</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>awsS3</code></td><td><code>bucketName</code></td><td>region, bucketRootPath, endpoint, s3ForcePathStyle, sse ('aws:kms'|'AES256')</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>azureBlobStorage</code></td><td><code>containerName</code> ⚠️ NOT bucketName</td><td>connectionString, credentials</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table></div>
                <div class="info-box warning mb-0">
                    <h5>⚠️ CBA Trap — azureBlobStorage's Field Name</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">Unlike <code>googleGcs</code> and <code>awsS3</code> which both use <code>bucketName</code>, <code>azureBlobStorage</code> requires <code>containerName</code> instead. Mixing these up is a real, named exam gotcha.</p>
                </div>
                </div>
            </section>

