            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch14-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — why the Reader never touches storage directly</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>The 4 architecture pieces named in §14.1 do the real work: the Generator runs MkDocs to turn Markdown into static HTML/CSS/JS, then strips the JS afterward for security before handing off to the Publisher.</p>
                    <h5>① One Backend Intermediary for Every Storage Type</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
Generator (builds)  →  Publisher (stores: local OR S3 OR GCS OR Azure)
                                     ↓
                        techdocs-backend (the ONLY thing that reads storage)
                                     ↓
                        Reader (asks techdocs-backend for docs by entity id)
                    </pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout mb-0"><strong>🔑 The Reader never needs to know or care where the files actually live</strong> — it just asks techdocs-backend for an entity's docs by id, and techdocs-backend centralizes security handling across every storage type.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch14-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>TechDocs = documentation-as-code:</strong> Markdown/MDX docs versioned in the same repo, same PR, as the code they describe.</li>
                    <li><strong>The pipeline is Generator</strong> (MkDocs build) <strong>→ Publisher</strong> (storage) <strong>→ techdocs-backend</strong> (retrieval) <strong>→ Reader</strong> (render on entity page).</li>
                    <li><strong>'Basic' = docs built on the Backstage server itself, stored locally;</strong> 'Recommended' = docs built in CI/CD, stored in cloud storage, served on demand.</li>
                    <li><strong>mkdocs.yml needs a <code>nav:</code> list and <code>plugins: [techdocs-core]</code>;</strong> the docs/ directory needs at minimum an index.md.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>techdocs.publisher.type</code> chooses the storage backend:</strong> local (Dev only), googleGcs, awsS3, azureBlobStorage, or openStackSwift (Production).</li>
                    <li><strong>Good TechDocs practice:</strong> docs next to code, Mermaid over stale screenshots, a clear nav, admonitions for warnings/tips, and updating docs in the SAME PR as the code.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch14-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Assuming <code>techdocs.publisher: local</code> scales to Production</strong> — it does not; multiple pods can't share local storage (§14.5).</li>
                    <li><strong>Confusing the Generator</strong> (builds the static site) <strong>with the Publisher</strong> (stores it) — two distinct pipeline stages (§14.1).</li>
                    <li><strong>Forgetting azureBlobStorage's field is <code>containerName</code>, not <code>bucketName</code></strong> like googleGcs/awsS3 (§14.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Assuming the Reader talks to storage directly</strong> — it always goes through techdocs-backend, regardless of storage type (Under the Hood §①).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch14-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Default to <code>techdocs.generator.runIn: docker</code> in Production</strong> — it's the isolated, CI/CD-friendly path away from local.</li>
                    <li><strong>Reach for <code>legacyCopyReadmeMdToIndexMd</code> only as a fallback;</strong> a real docs/index.md written for TechDocs beats an auto-copied README.</li>
                    <li><strong>Use Mermaid diagrams over screenshots from the start</strong> — they're versionable text and never go stale.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

