            <section class="section-block" id="b-s16-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">16.2 Frontend Plugins</h3>
                <p>Frontend plugins render UI components. They use <code>createPlugin()</code> to define the plugin, <code>createRoutableExtension()</code> for page routing (lazy-loaded), and <code>createComponentExtension()</code> for embedding cards on entity pages. They access backend data via the <strong>API client proxy</strong> — never directly calling databases or external services.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container"><div class="diagram-title">Frontend Plugin Skeleton (Legacy API)</div>
<pre><code class="language-typescript">// plugins/easypj-dashboard/src/plugin.ts
import { createPlugin, createRoutableExtension } from '@backstage/core-plugin-api';
import { rootRouteRef } from './routes';

export const easypjDashboardPlugin = createPlugin({
  id: 'easypj-dashboard',
  routes: { root: rootRouteRef },
});

export const EasypjDashboardPage = easypjDashboardPlugin.provide(
  createRoutableExtension({
    name: 'EasypjDashboardPage',
    component: () => import('./components/DashboardPage')
      .then(m => m.DashboardPage),
    mountPoint: rootRouteRef,
  }),
);</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note mb-0">
                    <h5>🔑 createApiFactory() — the Missing Half of createApiRef()</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">A <code>createApiRef()</code> declaration alone is just an id/type — it does NOT do anything by itself. <code>createApiFactory()</code> ties that ApiRef definition to a concrete implementation, registered in <code>packages/app/src/apis.ts</code>. Only after the factory is registered does <code>useApi(someApiRef)</code> actually return a real object at runtime.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §16.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s16-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">16.3 Backend Plugins</h3>
                <p>Backend plugins provide API endpoints and data processing. They use <code>createRouter()</code> for HTTP routes, access databases via <code>PluginDatabaseManager</code>, read URLs via <code>UrlReader</code>, and communicate with other plugins through the <strong>event system</strong>. Backend plugins can also schedule recurring tasks (cron-like jobs).</p>
                <div class="diagram-container"><div class="diagram-title">Backend Plugin Skeleton (Legacy API)</div>
<pre><code class="language-typescript">// plugins/easypj-dashboard-backend/src/service/router.ts
import { createRouter } from '@backstage/backend-common';
import express from 'express';

export async function createEasypjRouter(): Promise&lt;express.Router&gt; {
  const router = express.Router();
  router.use(express.json());

  router.get('/job-stats', async (_req, res) => {
    res.json({ activePostings: 42, newToday: 3 });
  });

  return router;
}</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning mb-0">
                    <h5>⚠️ LEGACY NOTE — @backstage/backend-common Is Deprecated</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">The <code>createRouter()</code>/<code>@backstage/backend-common</code> pattern shown above is the OLD backend system. Official docs confirm <code>@backstage/backend-common</code> is now deprecated — new backend plugins should use <strong><code>createBackendPlugin()</code></strong> from <code>@backstage/backend-plugin-api</code> instead (the <strong>New Backend System</strong>, built on 5 concepts: Backend, Plugins, Services, Extension Points, and Modules). See Chapter 18 §18.3 for the modern pattern.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

