                    <p>The [PLUGIN FRAMEWORK] box in §16.1 lists five internal services every plugin rides on.</p>
                    <h5>① The 5 Shared Framework Services</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
Plugin Registry     → who's installed
Route Management    → which URL renders which page
API Client Proxy    → how a component reaches its backend without hardcoding a host
Component Mounting  → where a card actually renders
Event Bus           → cross-plugin signals
                    </pre>
                    <p>The Frontend/Backend split in §16.2/§16.3 is really just "which side of that framework does my code plug into" — and <code>createApiRef()</code> + <code>createApiFactory()</code> (§16.2) is the glue: the ApiRef is just an id/type, the factory (registered in <code>packages/app/src/apis.ts</code>) is what actually satisfies <code>useApi()</code> at runtime.</p>
                    <div class="uth-callout mb-0"><strong>🔑 Same underlying framework, whether you're on the legacy or current API surface</strong> — createFrontendPlugin() and createBackendPlugin() plug into these exact same 5 services, just through a different entry point.</div>
