                        <p><strong>200+ community plugins</strong> at backstage.io/plugins.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Category</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>CI/CD, Monitoring, Incident management, Project management, Communication</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Always check the marketplace first before building custom — the CBA expects you to know that 200+ plugins exist.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How does a frontend plugin access data from a backend plugin?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Through the <strong>API Client Proxy</strong> — <code>createApiRef()</code> + <code>useApi()</code>, routed through Backstage's backend.</p>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
Browser  →  /api/proxy/...  →  Backstage Backend  →  target API/service
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This avoids CORS issues and keeps API tokens on the server — they never reach the browser.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the Backstage event bus and how do plugins use it?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A publish-subscribe system — plugins never call each other directly.</p>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
Catalog ingests entity  →  publishes catalog.entity.created  →  Search plugin subscribes → indexes it
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This loose coupling means plugins can be added/removed independently without breaking each other.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What does createRoutableExtension() do?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Creates a <strong>lazy-loaded</strong> page component for a plugin, mounted at a specific route.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Without it</th><th>With it</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>All plugin code loads at startup</td><td>Code loads only when user navigates there</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Analogous to React.lazy() — code splitting at the route level, critical for performance with many plugins.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between a core plugin and a community plugin?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th></th><th>Maintained by</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Core</td><td>Backstage team</td><td>Catalog, Scaffolder, TechDocs, Search, K8s, Auth</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Community</td><td>External developers/companies</td><td>Jenkins, PagerDuty, Grafana, Sentry</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Core plugins are guaranteed to work and ship with every install; community plugins vary in quality/maintenance.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Where are frontend plugins registered to appear in Backstage?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>packages/app/src/App.tsx</td><td>Route registration</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>packages/app/src/components/Root.tsx</td><td>Sidebar navigation</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>A plugin can have a route without a sidebar link (internal pages), but most user-facing plugins need both.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is createComponentExtension() used for?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Creates a <strong>card component</strong> embedded on entity pages — smaller than a full page.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Creates</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>createRoutableExtension</td><td>A full page</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>createComponentExtension</td><td>A card widget</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Entity page cards (CI/CD status, K8s health, Sentry errors) are the most common plugin UI type.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You install a community plugin but it doesn't appear. What are 3 things to check?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Check</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>1</td><td>Installed in both packages/app AND packages/backend?</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>2</td><td>Registered in App.tsx (route) and/or Root.tsx (sidebar)?</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>3</td><td>Configured in app-config.yaml (tokens, URLs)?</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Most "plugin not appearing" issues are #2 — not registered. Always check the plugin's README for setup steps.</p></div>
