                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A new hire reads an old blog post using <code>createRouter()</code> and <code>@backstage/backend-common</code> and assumes that's still the recommended way to build backend plugins. What should they know?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>@backstage/backend-common</code> is deprecated — new backend plugins should use <code>createBackendPlugin()</code> from <code>@backstage/backend-plugin-api</code> (the New Backend System).</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Package</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>@backstage/backend-common</td><td>Deprecated (legacy)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>@backstage/backend-plugin-api</td><td>Current</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§16.3's Gotcha — this exact stale-blog-post scenario is a real, common onboarding trap.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A developer declares <code>const myApiRef = createApiRef&lt;MyApi&gt;({...})</code> and calls <code>useApi(myApiRef)</code> in a component, but the app crashes at runtime. What's the likely missing step?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>No <code>createApiFactory()</code> was registered in <code>packages/app/src/apis.ts</code> for that ApiRef — the ApiRef alone declares an id/type but supplies no real implementation.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Piece</th><th>Present?</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>createApiRef()</td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>createApiFactory() in apis.ts</td><td>❌ Missing</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§16.2's Gotcha — this is precisely why an ApiRef and its Factory are described as two separate, both-required pieces.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A team wants to show their GitHub README on jobpost's entity page — nothing else, no backend logic. What plugin shape is this?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Frontend-only — it renders UI and calls GitHub's API directly from the browser (or via proxy), with no dedicated backend package needed.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Plugin shape</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Display GitHub README</td><td>Frontend-only</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§16.6's Gotcha — not every plugin needs to be Full-Stack; this is the canonical Frontend-only example.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A plugin registers routes in App.tsx but a developer can't find it anywhere in the UI. mkdocs — wait, wrong chapter — Root.tsx has no SidebarItem for it. Is the plugin broken?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Not necessarily broken — it may be an internal page reachable only by direct URL, deliberately without a sidebar entry. Whether that's intended depends on the plugin's design.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Registers</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>App.tsx</td><td>Route (present)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Root.tsx</td><td>Sidebar link (absent)</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§16's Lab Q8 — a route and a sidebar link are 2 separate registrations; a plugin can validly have one without the other.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You're scanning an unfamiliar plugin's source tree looking for its declared id and routes. Which file do you open first?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>plugin.ts</code> — that's where <code>createPlugin()</code> (or <code>createFrontendPlugin()</code>) declares the id, routes, and APIs.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Declares</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>plugin.ts</td><td>id, routes, APIs</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§16's Speed Tip — this is the fastest orientation point for any unfamiliar plugin codebase.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A repo has both <code>plugins/easypj-dashboard/</code> and <code>plugins/easypj-dashboard-backend/</code>. What plugin type is this, without reading any code?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Full-Stack — the presence of a <code>-backend</code> sibling package is the fast signal.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Packages present</th><th>Type</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>easypj-dashboard + easypj-dashboard-backend</td><td>Full-Stack</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§16.6's Speed Tip — this naming convention alone reliably answers the frontend/backend/full-stack question.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Two plugins need to react to the same Catalog event. Should Plugin A call Plugin B's function directly to notify it?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>No — both should subscribe to the event bus independently. Plugins communicate via publish/subscribe, never by calling each other directly.</p>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
Plugin A  →  publishes event  →  Event Bus  →  Plugin B subscribes and reacts
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§16.7's core rule — direct plugin-to-plugin calls break the loose coupling the whole architecture depends on.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

