                    <div class="eq-question">A platform engineer sets <code>pagerDuty.apiToken</code> in app-config.yaml org-wide but forgets to add <code>pagerduty.com/service-id</code> to jobpost's catalog-info.yaml. What happens?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>No PagerDuty card appears on jobpost's entity page — the token alone is not enough; the entity-level annotation is what actually turns the card on for that specific entity.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Present</th><th>Missing</th><th>Card shows?</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>apiToken</td><td>service-id annotation</td><td>❌ No</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Under the Hood §① — this exact "token alone isn't enough" gap is the chapter's core Gotcha.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A developer adds <code>github.com/project-slug</code> to a new Resource entity for a database, expecting to see a GitHub card. Does this work?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Yes, technically — annotations aren't restricted by Kind. If the value points at a valid repo, the GitHub card renders on that Resource's entity page too, even though it's unusual to attach a repo to infrastructure.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Entity Kind</th><th>github.com/project-slug works?</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Component</td><td>✅ Yes (typical)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Resource</td><td>✅ Yes (unusual but valid)</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§17.1's mechanism is annotation-driven, not Kind-driven — the plugin doesn't care what Kind the entity is.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A security review asks why Grafana's dashboard token never appears in browser network requests even though the dashboard clearly renders. How is this possible?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Grafana is routed through app-config.yaml's <code>proxy.endpoints</code> — the browser calls Backstage's own proxy path, and Backstage injects the real Grafana token server-side before forwarding.</p>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
Browser  →  /api/proxy/grafana/api  →  Backstage Backend (adds token)  →  Grafana
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§17.3's Gotcha — this is exactly the same proxy pattern used for Jenkins/ArgoCD/GitLab CI.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A teammate adds <code>github.com/project-slug: easypj/jobpost</code> expecting it to also enable the Jenkins card. It doesn't. Why?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Each CI/CD tool has its own distinct annotation — GitHub Actions uses <code>github.com/project-slug</code>, Jenkins needs its own separate <code>jenkins.io/job-full-name</code>. One annotation doesn't cross-enable a different plugin's card.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Card</th><th>Needs its own annotation</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>GitHub Actions</td><td>github.com/project-slug</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Jenkins</td><td>jenkins.io/job-full-name</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§17.2's Gotcha — this exact mix-up is named explicitly as a common mistake.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Leadership wants deploy failures posted to #easypj-deploys automatically. Which plugin, and where does the config live?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Slack — configured via <code>slack.webhookUrl</code> in app-config.yaml, used by Scaffolder/custom actions (Chapter 12 §12.3) to post to the channel. No catalog-info.yaml annotation or entity-page card involved.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Plugin</th><th>Config location</th><th>Has entity card?</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Slack</td><td>slack.webhookUrl (app-config.yaml)</td><td>❌ No</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§17.6's Key Takeaway — Slack is the one plugin in this chapter with no card, PUSH not PULL.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A card doesn't render and a developer immediately starts digging through app-config.yaml's proxy settings. What should they check first instead?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The entity-level annotation in catalog-info.yaml — check that FIRST, then the app-config.yaml section, in that order.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Check order</th><th>Location</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>1st</td><td>catalog-info.yaml annotation</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>2nd</td><td>app-config.yaml section</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§17's Speed Tip — this ordering saves time since the annotation is the more common, simpler thing to get wrong.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

