            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch18-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — dependency injection in the New Backend System, and how frontend/backend find each other</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>§18.3's <code>createBackendPlugin()</code> is the New Backend System pattern referenced back in Chapter 16 §16.3.</p>
                    <h5>① Explicit Dependency Declaration</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
register(env) {
  env.registerInit({
    deps: { httpRouter: coreServices.httpRouter },   ← declared explicitly
    async init({ httpRouter }) { ... }                ← ONLY receives what it declared
  });
}
                    </pre>
                    <p>This is a dependency-injection model that replaces the legacy <code>createRouter()</code>/<code>backend-common</code> approach.</p>
                    <h5>② discoveryApiRef — Frontend and Backend Find Each Other</h5>
                    <p>§18.4's DashboardPage never hardcodes a backend URL: it asks <code>discoveryApiRef.getBaseUrl('easypj-dashboard')</code> for the address, which is how Backstage lets a plugin's frontend and backend halves find each other regardless of where they're actually deployed.</p>
                    <div class="uth-callout mb-0"><strong>🔑 Both patterns exist for the same reason: explicit contracts over implicit assumptions</strong> — a backend service declares exactly what it needs, and a frontend asks the platform for an address instead of assuming one.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch18-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>The decision tree in §18.1 is the gate:</strong> check backstage.io/plugins first, build custom only for org-specific, non-reusable workflows.</li>
                    <li><strong>A frontend plugin package lives under <code>plugins/&lt;name&gt;/src</code></strong> with index.ts, plugin.ts, api.ts, routes.ts, components/.</li>
                    <li><strong>A backend plugin registers with <code>createBackendPlugin({ pluginId, register(env) {...} })</code></strong> and must explicitly declare its dependencies.</li>
                    <li><strong>The easypj Dashboard plugin's frontend calls its own backend through <code>discoveryApiRef.getBaseUrl(pluginId)</code></strong> — never a hardcoded URL.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>renderInTestApp()</code> from <code>@backstage/test-utils</code></strong> is the standard way to unit-test a plugin component.</li>
                    <li><strong>Editing App.tsx/Root.tsx directly (§18.6) is ORG-SPECIFIC wiring,</strong> not a reusable plugin.</li>
                    <li><strong>MUI (v4 makeStyles legacy vs v5 sx prop)</strong> plus Backstage's own core-components (InfoCard, Table) cover almost all plugin UI needs.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch18-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Confusing "add a route in App.tsx" with "build a plugin"</strong> — only the latter produces reusable, installable code (§18.6).</li>
                    <li><strong>Forgetting to declare a backend dependency</strong> (<code>deps: { httpRouter: coreServices.httpRouter }</code>) — <code>registerInit</code>'s callback won't receive it (§18.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Hardcoding a backend URL in a frontend component</strong> instead of using <code>discoveryApiRef.getBaseUrl()</code> (§18.4).</li>
                    <li><strong>Mixing MUI v4 makeStyles patterns into a v5 (sx prop) codebase,</strong> or vice versa, inconsistently (§18.7).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

