                        <p>Checking backstage.io/plugins — a Jenkins plugin already exists (Chapter 17 §17.2), so building custom here is unnecessary duplicated effort.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Skipped?</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Check backstage.io/plugins marketplace</td><td>✅ Yes — skipped</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Scaffold custom plugin</td><td>❌ Jumped straight here</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§18.1's decision tree — the first branch is always "does an official/community plugin already do this?"</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A backend plugin's <code>register(env)</code> only declares <code>deps: {}</code> (empty) but its <code>init</code> callback tries to use <code>httpRouter</code>. What happens?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>httpRouter</code> is undefined inside <code>init</code> — the callback only receives what was explicitly declared in <code>deps</code>, and nothing was declared.</p>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
deps: {}                          →  init({ httpRouter }) { }  →  httpRouter === undefined
deps: { httpRouter: coreServices.httpRouter }  →  init({ httpRouter }) { }  →  httpRouter is usable
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§18.3's Gotcha — this dependency-injection model has no implicit fallback; every needed service must be named in <code>deps</code>.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A frontend component hardcodes <code>fetch('http://localhost:7007/api/easypj-dashboard/job-stats')</code>. It works locally but breaks in a multi-replica production deployment. Why?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The hardcoded host/port assumption doesn't hold once the backend runs on a different host/port in production (e.g. behind a load balancer or different service name) — it should have used <code>discoveryApiRef.getBaseUrl()</code> instead.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Survives redeploy?</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Hardcoded <code>localhost:7007</code></td><td>❌ No</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>discoveryApiRef.getBaseUrl(pluginId)</code></td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§18.4's Gotcha — this exact hardcoded-URL mistake is named explicitly as a common failure.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A developer adds a route directly in App.tsx for a one-off internal tool and calls it "a new plugin." A reviewer disagrees. Who's right?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The reviewer — adding a route in App.tsx is org-specific app-shell wiring, not a reusable, installable plugin package. Real plugins live in their own <code>plugins/&lt;name&gt;</code> directory.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Lives where?</th><th>Reusable elsewhere?</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>App.tsx route</td><td>packages/app/src/App.tsx</td><td>❌ No</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Real plugin</td><td>plugins/&lt;name&gt;/src/</td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§18.6's core distinction, also named as §18's Gotcha — this exact terminology confusion is common.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A component test uses plain React Testing Library's <code>render(&lt;DashboardPage /&gt;)</code> and fails with errors about missing theme/API context. What should it use instead?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>renderInTestApp()</code> from <code>@backstage/test-utils</code> — it wraps the component with the App context (theme, routing, APIs) a real Backstage plugin component expects.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Renderer</th><th>Provides App context?</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Plain <code>render()</code> (RTL)</td><td>❌ No</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>renderInTestApp()</code></td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§18.5's Speed Tip — plain <code>render()</code> is missing exactly the context Backstage components rely on.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A plugin's UI mixes a MUI v4 <code>makeStyles</code> hook in one component and an MUI v5 <code>sx</code> prop in another, within the same package. Is this a bug?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Not a functional bug, but a Gotcha-flagged inconsistency — mixing the two styling patterns inconsistently makes the codebase harder to maintain, even though both technically work during Backstage's v4→v5 migration period.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Version</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>makeStyles</code> hook</td><td>MUI v4</td><td>Legacy, still works</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>sx</code> prop</td><td>MUI v5</td><td>Preferred, newer</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§18.7's Gotcha — pick one pattern per package/component consistently where possible.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A developer wants jobpost's dashboard to always match the easypj custom theme (Chapter 4 §4.5) without hardcoding colors. What MUI approach achieves this "for free"?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Use MUI's theme-aware props (<code>palette.primary.main</code>, <code>spacing()</code>) instead of hardcoded hex values — components using these automatically adopt whatever theme is active.</p>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
sx={{ color: '#1976d2' }}            → hardcoded, ignores theme changes
sx={{ color: 'primary.main' }}       → theme-aware, follows createTheme()
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§18.7's CBA Tip — this is exactly why plugin authors rarely need to hardcode colors.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write the createBackendPlugin() skeleton for a plugin with id "easypj-dashboard" that needs the httpRouter service.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the skeleton</span></div><pre><code class="language-typescript">createBackendPlugin({
  pluginId: 'easypj-dashboard',
  register(env) {
    env.registerInit({
      deps: { httpRouter: coreServices.httpRouter },
      async init({ httpRouter }) { /* ... */ },
    });
  },
});</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§18.3 — this is the exact New Backend System shape every backend plugin follows today.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

