            <!-- ─────────── §19.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s19-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">19.1 Why Deploy Backstage on Kubernetes?</h3>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔄</div><h5>High Availability</h5><p>Run multiple replicas. If one pod crashes, K8s auto-restarts it. Traffic is load-balanced across healthy pods.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📈</div><h5>Auto-Scaling</h5><p>Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) scales Backstage pods based on CPU/memory. Handle traffic spikes automatically.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🏥</div><h5>Self-Healing</h5><p>K8s monitors pod health with liveness/readiness probes. Unhealthy pods are restarted or replaced automatically.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div><h5>GitOps Ready</h5><p>Everything declared in YAML. Deploy via ArgoCD/Flux. Git is the single source of truth for your Backstage config.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note mb-0"><h5>💡 Same Platform, Same Guarantees</h5><p class="mb-0">Backstage gets the exact same replicas, self-healing and rolling updates that jobpost itself gets elsewhere in this guide — you're not learning a new deployment model, just applying the one you already know to the tool that catalogs everything else.</p></div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">Deploying Backstage to K8s — the exact command sequence</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. Build and push Docker image
$ docker build -t registry.easypj.com/backstage:v1.0 .
$ docker push registry.easypj.com/backstage:v1.0

# 2. Create namespace and secrets
$ kubectl create namespace backstage
$ kubectl create secret generic backstage-secrets \
    --from-literal=POSTGRES_HOST=postgres.easypj.com \
    --from-literal=GITHUB_TOKEN=ghp_xxxx \
    -n backstage

# 3. Create ConfigMap from app-config.yaml
$ kubectl create configmap backstage-config \
    --from-file=app-config.yaml -n backstage

# 4. Apply deployment and service
$ kubectl apply -f k8s/deployment.yaml
$ kubectl apply -f k8s/service.yaml
$ kubectl apply -f k8s/ingress.yaml

# 5. Verify deployment
$ kubectl get pods -n backstage -w    # Watch pods start
$ kubectl logs deploy/backstage -n backstage
$ curl https://backstage.easypj.com/healthcheck

# 6. Scale up for production
$ kubectl scale deploy/backstage --replicas=3 -n backstage</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

