            <section class="section-block" id="b-s19-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">19.5 Helm Chart for Backstage</h3>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ There Is No Official Backstage Helm Chart or Docker Image</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">Backstage's own docs explain there are no official published Helm charts or Docker images, because "Backstage is not a packaged service that you can use out of the box" — every real deployment is built from your own customized <code>@backstage/create-app</code> output. Treat the community <code>backstage/charts</code> chart below as a <strong>starting point</strong>, not a drop-in production artifact.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container"><div class="diagram-title">Deploying with Helm</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Add Backstage Helm repo
$ helm repo add backstage https://backstage.github.io/charts
$ helm repo update

# Install with custom values
$ helm install backstage backstage/backstage \
    --namespace backstage --create-namespace \
    --set appConfig.app.title="easypj Developer Portal" \
    --set database.client=pg \
    --set database.connection.host=postgres.easypj.com \
    --set ingress.enabled=true \
    --set ingress.host=backstage.easypj.com

# Upgrade after editing values.yaml
$ helm upgrade backstage backstage/backstage -n backstage

# Rollback to the previous release
$ helm rollback backstage -n backstage</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch19-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — why the ConfigMap's baseUrl fields decide "healthy but broken"</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>The mounted ConfigMap is the whole reason §19.4's 4 fields matter so much. When a Pod starts, Kubernetes mounts the <code>backstage-config</code> ConfigMap at <code>/app/app-config.yaml</code> exactly as declared in the Deployment's <code>volumeMounts</code>.</p>
                    <h5>① Backstage Reads Config Once, at Boot</h5>
                    <p>Backstage reads that file ONCE at boot to decide what URL its own frontend should call for the backend, and what origin <code>backend.cors.origin</code> should accept requests from.</p>
                    <h5>② The Ingress Is the Only Reachable Address</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
Browser  ──▶  Ingress (backstage.easypj.com)  ──▶  Service (ClusterIP, internal-only)  ──▶  Pod
                    │
                    └── app.baseUrl / backend.baseUrl / backend.cors.origin must ALL say THIS host,
                        never the internal ClusterIP Service DNS name — the browser can never reach that.
                    </pre>
                    <p>Because the Ingress — not the ClusterIP Service — is the only address a browser can actually reach, every one of those fields has to point at the Ingress host, not at the internal Service DNS name.</p>
                    <div class="uth-callout mb-0"><strong>🔑 Get this wrong and the Pods report Healthy while the browser silently fails to load data</strong> — a classic "it's running but it's broken" trap, since the liveness/readiness probes only check that the process is up, not that its configured URLs are externally correct.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch19-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Backstage on Kubernetes follows the same shape as any other service:</strong> Deployment (replicas) + Service (ClusterIP) + Ingress (TLS termination) + ConfigMap/Secret (config & credentials).</li>
                    <li><strong>A multi-stage Dockerfile keeps the runtime image small:</strong> build with <code>yarn build</code> in one stage, copy only the backend dist and node_modules into a slim runtime stage.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>app.baseUrl</code>, <code>backend.baseUrl</code>, <code>backend.listen.port</code> and <code>backend.cors.origin</code></strong> must all resolve to the externally-reachable Ingress host — this is the #1 cause of a broken frontend/backend handshake.</li>
                    <li><strong>There is no official Backstage Helm chart or Docker image;</strong> Backstage's own docs say every real deployment is built from a customized <code>@backstage/create-app</code> output.</li>
                    <li><strong>A PersistentVolumeClaim is required for the TechDocs cache</strong> — without it, published docs vanish every time a Pod restarts.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>helm upgrade</code> and <code>helm rollback</code></strong> give you the same safety net for Backstage itself that Deployments give jobpost: a one-command way forward and a one-command way back.</li>
                    <li><strong>SQLite cannot survive multiple replicas</strong> — PostgreSQL is the only supported production database once you're running more than 1 Pod.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch19-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Forgetting to update app-config's baseUrl fields to the Ingress host</strong> after moving from local dev to Kubernetes — the #1 "it deployed but doesn't work" mistake (§19.4).</li>
                    <li><strong>Treating the community <code>backstage/charts</code> Helm chart</strong> as an official, production-ready artifact instead of a starting point you must customize (§19.5).</li>
                    <li><strong>Skipping resource requests/limits on the Deployment</strong> — a memory leak in one replica can starve the node instead of being contained.</li>
                    <li><strong>Forgetting the PersistentVolumeClaim for the TechDocs cache</strong> and wondering why published docs disappear after every rollout.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch19-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
