            <!-- ─────────── §20.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s20-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">20.4 Troubleshooting K8s Plugin</h3>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table><thead><tr><th>Error</th><th>Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                        <tr><td>"403 Forbidden"</td><td>RBAC: ServiceAccount lacks permissions</td><td>Add get/list/watch on Deployments, Pods, Services (§20.5)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>"Connection refused"</td><td>Wrong API server URL or network issue</td><td>Verify cluster URL and network connectivity</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>"TLS certificate"</td><td>Self-signed cert not trusted</td><td>Set skipTLSVerify: true (dev only) or add CA cert</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>No resources shown</td><td>Missing annotation OR missing matching LABEL</td><td>Add the annotation, AND the matching label (not name) on the Deployment (§20.1)</td></tr>
                    </tbody></table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §20.5 (NEW — from Backstage.txt, no HTML equivalent existed) ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s20-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">20.5 RBAC for the Kubernetes Plugin ServiceAccount</h3>
                <p>The plugin only needs <strong>READ</strong> access, never write. Use a least-privilege ClusterRole for the Backstage ServiceAccount — and scope it to specific namespaces instead of cluster-wide whenever the org's clusters are shared.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding — read-only, exactly what the plugin needs</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># ClusterRole - read-only, exactly what the K8s plugin needs
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRole
metadata: { name: backstage-k8s-plugin-reader }
rules:
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["pods", "services", "configmaps", "limitranges"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
  - apiGroups: ["apps"]
    resources: ["deployments", "replicasets"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
  - apiGroups: ["networking.k8s.io"]
    resources: ["ingresses"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
---
# Bind it to the ServiceAccount Backstage authenticates as
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRoleBinding
metadata: { name: backstage-k8s-plugin-binding }
roleRef: { kind: ClusterRole, name: backstage-k8s-plugin-reader, apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io }
subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: backstage
    namespace: backstage</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning mb-0"><h5>⚠️ Never Grant Write Verbs</h5><p class="mb-0">The plugin never needs <code>create</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>patch</code>, or <code>delete</code> — granting them "just in case" violates least privilege and provides no functional benefit, since the plugin is read-only by design (§20.6).</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §20.6 (NEW — from Backstage.txt, no HTML equivalent existed) ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s20-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">20.6 Kubernetes Plugin vs Full GitOps Tools</h3>
                <p>The K8s plugin is a <strong>READ-ONLY window</strong>, not a deploy mechanism. Knowing when a view is enough and when you need ArgoCD (Chapter 21) is itself an exam-relevant distinction.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table><thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>K8s Plugin Alone</th><th>Add ArgoCD / GitOps (Ch 21)</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                        <tr><td>See pod/deployment status</td><td>✅ YES — built for this</td><td>Also shown, via sync status</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>See recent rollout history</td><td>⚠️ Limited</td><td>✅ YES — full revision history</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Trigger a deployment</td><td>❌ NO — view only</td><td>✅ YES — sync button / auto-sync</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Roll back a bad release</td><td>❌ NO</td><td>✅ YES — one click, git revert</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Enforce Git as source of truth</td><td>❌ NO</td><td>✅ YES — that is the whole point</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>See CPU/memory of running pods</td><td>✅ YES (§20.3)</td><td>Complementary, not overlapping</td></tr>
                    </tbody></table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note mb-0"><h5>💡 A Window, Not a Steering Wheel</h5><p class="mb-0">The plugin can show you a rollout is stuck, but it cannot trigger, retry or roll one back — that is ArgoCD's job. Read Chapter 21 next to close that gap.</p></div>
            </section>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch20-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — why the whole plugin hinges on one lookup</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>The entire plugin hinges on one lookup: the backend reads the entity's <code>backstage.io/kubernetes-id</code> annotation (§20.1) and issues a label selector query against every cluster registered under <code>clusterLocatorMethods</code> (§20.2) — it never matches on the entity's NAME.</p>
                    <h5>① Why the Entity's Own Name Doesn't Matter</h5>
                    <p>That is why the plugin can show resources for jobpost even if the catalog entity is called something completely different: the only contract that matters is annotation-value equals label-value.</p>
                    <h5>② singleTenant Requires an Extra Pin</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
serviceLocatorMethod: multiTenant   → fans query out to EVERY configured cluster
serviceLocatorMethod: singleTenant  → REQUIRES backstage.io/kubernetes-cluster
                                        annotation to pin the entity to ONE cluster
                    </pre>
                    <p>When <code>serviceLocatorMethod</code> is <code>singleTenant</code>, the plugin additionally requires a <code>backstage.io/kubernetes-cluster</code> annotation to pin the entity to one specific cluster instead of fanning the query out to all of them — useful once you have dozens of clusters and know exactly where a given service lives.</p>
                    <div class="uth-callout mb-0"><strong>🔑 One contract, everywhere:</strong> annotation-value equals label-value. Everything else in this chapter — multi-cluster fan-out, RBAC scoping, troubleshooting — exists to support or protect that one lookup.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch20-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>The plugin is two npm packages:</strong> a frontend tab (<code>@backstage/plugin-kubernetes</code>) and a backend service (<code>@backstage/plugin-kubernetes-backend</code>) talking over a REST call.</li>
                    <li><strong>Discovery is annotation-to-LABEL, never by entity name</strong> — forget the label on the Deployment and the tab stays empty even with a perfectly correct annotation.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>multiTenant</code> fans a query out across every configured cluster;</strong> <code>singleTenant</code> pins an entity to one cluster via the <code>backstage.io/kubernetes-cluster</code> annotation.</li>
                    <li><strong>A custom dashboard card is just another React component</strong> using <code>useApi(kubernetesApiRef)</code> — the same pattern as any other plugin API from Chapters 16-18.</li>
                    <li><strong>The plugin's ServiceAccount needs ONLY get/list/watch</strong> — it should never be granted write verbs.</li>
                    <li><strong>The plugin is READ-ONLY:</strong> it can show you a rollout is stuck, but it cannot trigger, retry or roll one back — that is ArgoCD's job (Chapter 21).</li>
                    <li><strong>The real 4 cluster locator methods are config/gke/catalog/localKubectlProxy</strong> — eks and aks are authProvider values, not locator method types.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch20-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Assuming the plugin matches by catalog entity name</strong> instead of the annotation-to-label pair — the single most common reason "the Kubernetes tab is empty" (§20.1).</li>
                    <li><strong>Granting the ServiceAccount write verbs "just in case"</strong> — the plugin never needs them and it violates least privilege (§20.5).</li>
                    <li><strong>Using skipTLSVerify in production</strong> instead of only in dev, to paper over a self-signed certificate error (§20.4).</li>
                    <li><strong>Forgetting the <code>backstage.io/kubernetes-cluster</code> annotation</strong> when serviceLocatorMethod is singleTenant, then wondering why nothing resolves (§20.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Expecting the plugin to fix a broken rollout</strong> — it can only show you the rollout is broken (§20.6).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

