            <!-- ─────────── §21.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s21-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">21.1 What is GitOps?</h3>
                <div class="diagram-container"><div class="diagram-title">GitOps — Push vs Pull Deployment Models</div>
<pre>
  ❌ PUSH (Old Way):                           ✅ PULL / GitOps (Modern Way):
  ┌──────────┐    kubectl apply               ┌──────────┐    polls every 3min
  │ CI/CD    │──────────────▶ K8s Cluster      │ Git Repo │◀──────────────┐
  │ Pipeline │                                 │ (truth)  │               │
  └──────────┘                                 └────┬─────┘         ┌─────┴─────┐
  CI/CD needs cluster credentials               │               │ ArgoCD    │
  (SECURITY RISK!)                              ▼               │ Flux      │
                                            ┌──────────┐         └───────────┘
                                            │ K8s      │         Runs INSIDE
                                            │ Cluster  │         the cluster
                                            └──────────┘         (more secure!)
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><h5>📋 GitOps 4 Principles</h5><p>1. <strong>Git as source of truth</strong> — all config in Git<br>2. <strong>Declarative</strong> — everything YAML<br>3. <strong>Automated reconciliation</strong> — ArgoCD/Flux syncs<br>4. <strong>Pull-based</strong> — cluster pulls from Git</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>🔒 Why Pull-Based?</h5><p>CI/CD never touches the cluster directly. No external cluster credentials needed. The reconciliation agent runs inside the cluster and pulls from Git. This eliminates a major attack vector.</p></div>
                </div>
            </section>

