                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">📝</div><h5>Git = Source of Truth</h5><p>Every change flows through Git. No <code>kubectl apply</code> directly. The Kustomization file IS the desired state. ArgoCD enforces it.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔄</div><h5>Auto-Sync</h5><p>ArgoCD polls Git every 3 minutes. Detects drift and auto-heals. If someone manually edits a pod, ArgoCD reverts it back to Git state.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔙</div><h5>Instant Rollback</h5><p><code>git revert</code> the image tag change → ArgoCD syncs back to old version. Rollback = git history. No manual kubectl commands.</p></div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip mb-0"><h5>💡 CBA GitOps Insight</h5><p class="mb-0">The CBA tests the GitOps flow: developer pushes → CI builds image → updates manifest in Git → CD tool (ArgoCD/Flux) syncs → Backstage displays status. Know that ArgoCD uses <strong>pull-based</strong> deployment (cluster pulls from Git), not push-based. The annotation <code>argocd/app-name</code> links the ArgoCD application to the Backstage entity.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §21.4 (NEW — from Backstage.txt, no HTML equivalent existed) ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s21-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">21.4 ArgoCD Application Health and Sync States</h3>
                <p>Two independent axes: <strong>SYNC status</strong> and <strong>HEALTH status</strong>. Backstage's entity page mirrors both badges straight from the ArgoCD API — reading only one is a real Gotcha (§21.6).</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th></th><th>HEALTHY</th><th>PROGRESSING</th><th>DEGRADED</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Synced</strong></td><td>All good, done (green in UI)</td><td>Rollout in flight (blue, wait)</td><td>Deployed but crashing (red — investigate NOW)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>OutOfSync</strong></td><td>Manual drift exists in cluster (yellow — review)</td><td>New commit landed mid-rollout (blue, will sync)</td><td>Bad manifest pushed, pods failing to start (red — fix + re-sync)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning mb-0"><h5>⚠️ Only ONE Safe Combination</h5><p class="mb-0">Backstage's entity page mirrors these two badges straight from the ArgoCD API, so <strong>"Synced + Healthy" is the ONLY combination that means fully done and safe</strong> — every other combination on the entity page means something is mid-flight or broken.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §21.5 (NEW — from Backstage.txt, no HTML equivalent existed) ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s21-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">21.5 Flux vs ArgoCD — Choosing a GitOps Engine</h3>
                <p>Both implement the same pull-based GitOps model (§21.1). Backstage has first-class plugins for both. Picking one is an org decision, not a Backstage limitation.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>ArgoCD</th><th>Flux</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>UI</td><td>Rich built-in web UI</td><td>CLI-first, no bundled UI</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Backstage integration</td><td><code>@roadiehq/backstage-plugin-argo-cd</code></td><td><code>@weaveworksoss/backstage-plugin-flux</code> (Entity FluxDeploymentsCard, EntityFluxSourcesCard)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Multi-tenancy</td><td>AppProjects</td><td>Kustomization + tenant RBAC/impersonation</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Progressive delivery</td><td>Argo Rollouts (separate)</td><td>Flagger (separate)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Best fit</td><td>Teams that want a visual dashboard non-experts use</td><td>Teams already deep in Kubernetes-native tooling</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §21.6 (NEW — from Backstage.txt, no HTML equivalent existed) ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s21-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">21.6 Common GitOps Pitfalls at easypj</h3>
                <p>Mistakes teams make once ArgoCD is wired into Backstage, and how each one shows up on the entity page.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Pitfall</th><th>How It Shows Up</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>kubectl apply run by hand</td><td>OutOfSync forever, fights the next auto-sync</td><td>Never kubectl apply directly; always commit to the repo</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Image tag "latest" in manifests</td><td>ArgoCD sees no diff, never redeploys new code</td><td>Use immutable tags (git SHA) so every change is a real diff</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Secrets committed in plaintext</td><td>Security scanner fails, drifted from the vault</td><td>Use Sealed Secrets or External Secrets Operator (Chapter 30)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Two repos out of sync (app vs infra)</td><td>CI updates one, forgets the other</td><td>Automate the manifest-repo bump as part of the CI job (Chapter 25)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch21-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — why pull is more secure, and why 2 badges instead of 1</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>The push-vs-pull distinction in §21.1 is the whole reason GitOps is considered more secure: in the push model, a CI job needs cluster credentials shipped INTO it, so a compromised CI pipeline can reach the cluster directly.</p>
                    <h5>① Pull Means No External System Holds Credentials</h5>
                    <p>In the pull model, ArgoCD runs INSIDE the cluster and only reaches OUT to poll Git (roughly every 3 minutes, per §21.3) — no external system ever holds cluster credentials.</p>
                    <h5>② Backstage Talks Only to the ArgoCD API</h5>
                    <p>Crucially, per §21.2's own note, Backstage never talks to Kubernetes directly for this card: every Sync Status and Health Status badge on the entity page comes straight from the ArgoCD API, configured once via <code>app-config.yaml</code>'s <code>argocd.baseUrl/username/password</code>.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
Sync Status  → does the cluster MATCH Git?
Health Status → is what's running actually WORKING?
                  Both must be green — either alone is not enough.
                    </pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout mb-0"><strong>🔑 That is why §21.4's two independent axes exist</strong> — sync tells you whether the cluster matches Git; health tells you whether what's running is actually working, and only "Synced + Healthy" together mean fully done and safe.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch21-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>GitOps makes Git the single source of truth;</strong> ArgoCD/Flux reconcile the cluster to match it automatically (pull, not push).</li>
                    <li><strong>The <code>argocd/app-name</code> annotation is the only link</strong> Backstage needs to show an entity's ArgoCD Application status.</li>
                    <li><strong>Sync Status and Health Status are two independent axes</strong> — read both together, never just one.</li>
                    <li><strong>Rollback in a GitOps world is a <code>git revert</code>,</strong> not a manual kubectl command — ArgoCD auto-syncs the reverted state.</li>
                    <li><strong>ArgoCD and Flux implement the same pull-based model;</strong> the choice is an org decision (UI vs CLI-first, AppProjects vs Kustomization+Flagger), not a Backstage limitation.</li>
                    <li><strong>Manual kubectl apply and "latest" image tags</strong> are the two most common ways teams accidentally fight or defeat their own GitOps pipeline.</li>
                    <li><strong>Secrets should never be committed in plaintext</strong> — use Sealed Secrets or an External Secrets Operator instead (Chapter 30).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch21-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Running kubectl apply by hand once ArgoCD is wired in</strong> — it goes OutOfSync forever and fights the next auto-sync (§21.6).</li>
                    <li><strong>Tagging images "latest"</strong> instead of an immutable tag (git SHA) — ArgoCD sees no manifest diff and never redeploys new code (§21.6).</li>
                    <li><strong>Reading only the Sync badge and ignoring Health</strong> — "Synced + Degraded" means deployed but crashing, not safe (§21.4).</li>
                    <li><strong>Letting the app repo and the k8s-manifests repo drift out of sync</strong> because CI updates only one of them (§21.6).</li>
                    <li><strong>Assuming Backstage talks to Kubernetes for this feature</strong> — it talks only to the ArgoCD API (§21.2).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

