            <!-- ─────────── §22.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s22-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">22.1 Authentication in Backstage — The Sign-In Flow</h3>
                <div class="diagram-container"><div class="diagram-title">Authentication Flow</div>
<pre>
  👤 User clicks "Sign In"
       │
       ▼
  🔀 Redirect to Auth Provider (GitHub/Okta/Google)
       │
       ▼
  ✅ User authenticates with provider
       │
       ▼
  🎫 Provider returns token to Backstage callback URL
       │
       ▼
  🔑 Backstage creates session (JWT)
       │  • Identity from provider (name, email, avatar)
       │  • Mapped to Backstage User entity
       │  • Group memberships synced
       ▼
  🖥️ User is logged in! Session stored in browser cookie.
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §22.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s22-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">22.2 Auth Provider Comparison</h3>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table class="comparison-table">
                        <thead><tr><th>Provider</th><th>Type</th><th>Best For</th><th>Setup Effort</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr id="b-s22-2a"><td><strong>GitHub</strong></td><td>OAuth2</td><td>Dev teams on GitHub. Auto-maps teams→Groups</td><td>🟢 Easy</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>GitLab</strong></td><td>OAuth2</td><td>Dev teams on GitLab. Similar to GitHub auth</td><td>🟢 Easy</td></tr>
                            <tr id="b-s22-2b"><td><strong>Okta / Azure AD</strong></td><td>OIDC</td><td>Enterprise SSO. Group sync via claims</td><td>🟡 Medium</td></tr>
                            <tr id="b-s22-2c"><td><strong>Google</strong></td><td>OAuth2</td><td>GSuite/Google Workspace orgs</td><td>🟢 Easy</td></tr>
                            <tr id="b-s22-2d"><td><strong>Auth0 / Keycloak</strong></td><td>OIDC</td><td>Flexible identity hub / Self-hosted</td><td>🟡-🔴 Medium-Complex</td></tr>
                            <tr id="b-s22-2e"><td><strong>Guest (No Auth)</strong></td><td>None</td><td>⚠️ Development ONLY — NEVER in production</td><td>🟢 None</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip mb-0"><h5>💡 Recommendation</h5><p class="mb-0">Start with GitHub (or GitLab) auth for dev teams, Okta/Azure AD for enterprise. NEVER use guest in production!</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §22.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s22-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">22.3 GitHub Authentication — Step by Step</h3>
                <div class="diagram-container"><div class="diagram-title">Setting Up GitHub OAuth</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. Create GitHub OAuth App
#    GitHub → Settings → Developer settings → OAuth Apps → New
#    Application name: easypj-backstage-dev
#    Homepage URL: http://localhost:3000
#    Callback URL: http://localhost:7007/api/auth/github/handler/frame

# 2. Configure app-config.yaml
auth:
  environment: development
  providers:
    github:
      development:
        clientId: ${GITHUB_CLIENT_ID}
        clientSecret: ${GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET}

# 3. Set env vars and start
$ export GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=abc123
$ export GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=xyz789
$ yarn dev

# 4. Test: Open http://localhost:3000
#    Click "Sign In" → "GitHub" → Authorize → You're logged in!
#    Your GitHub username/avatar appears in the top right.</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">app-config.yaml — production, with an explicit sign-in resolver</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">auth:
  environment: production
  providers:
    github:
      production:
        clientId: ${GITHUB_CLIENT_ID}
        clientSecret: ${GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET}
        signIn:
          resolvers:
            - resolver: usernameMatchingUserEntityName

# This resolver maps the GitHub username to an existing
# User entity with the same metadata.name</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note mb-0"><h5>🔑 What a Sign-In Resolver Actually Does</h5><p class="mb-0"><code>signIn.resolvers</code> is the piece of config that turns a provider identity into a Backstage User entity. <code>usernameMatchingUserEntityName</code> is the simplest case — it just matches the GitHub username against an existing User entity's <code>metadata.name</code>. Without an explicit resolver, new users may be unable to map to an existing User entity at all.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §22.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s22-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">22.4 Enterprise SSO (Okta / Azure AD)</h3>
                <p>For enterprise organizations, Okta and Azure AD (now Microsoft Entra ID) are the most common SSO providers. They use <strong>OpenID Connect (OIDC)</strong> — a modern, simpler protocol built on OAuth 2.0 — rather than the older SAML protocol.</p>

                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ Correction — No Built-In SAML Provider, OIDC Only</h5><p class="mb-0">Backstage's built-in <code>okta</code> and <code>microsoft</code> auth providers are <strong>OAuth2/OIDC only</strong> — there is <strong>no built-in named SAML provider</strong>. Backstage instead ships a generic authentication provider for OAuth2 and SAML that reduces the code needed to build a <em>custom</em> provider adhering to those standards. If your Okta or Azure AD tenant is set up for SAML rather than OIDC, you need to build a custom auth provider on top of the generic SAML provider scaffold rather than using the <code>okta</code>/<code>microsoft</code> config blocks shown below.</p></div>

                <div class="comparison-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Okta Configuration</th><th>Azure AD (Entra ID) Configuration</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>1. Create App</strong></td><td>Okta Admin → Applications → Create App Integration → OIDC → Web App</td><td>Azure Portal → Entra ID → App Registrations → New Registration</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>2. Redirect URI</strong></td><td><code>https://backstage.easypj.com/api/auth/okta/handler/frame</code></td><td><code>https://backstage.easypj.com/api/auth/microsoft/handler/frame</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>3. Client ID/Secret</strong></td><td>Copy from Okta App → General tab</td><td>Copy from Azure App → Certificates &amp; Secrets</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>4. Group Claims</strong></td><td>Okta → API → Authorization Servers → Add "groups" claim</td><td>Azure → Token Configuration → Add "groups" claim (set to "All groups")</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>5. Assign Users</strong></td><td>Assign users/groups to the Okta Application</td><td>Enterprise Applications → Users and Groups → Add</td></tr>
                </tbody></table></div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">app-config.yaml — Okta OIDC Provider</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">auth:
  environment: production
  providers:
    okta:
      production:
        clientId: ${OKTA_CLIENT_ID}
        clientSecret: ${OKTA_CLIENT_SECRET}
        audience: https://easypj.okta.com
        callbackUrl: "https://backstage.easypj.com/api/auth/okta/handler/frame"

# Sign-in resolver maps Okta profile → Backstage User
signInPage: okta
auth:
  signInResolvers:
    okta:
      resolver: emailMatchingUserEntityProfileEmail</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">app-config.yaml — Azure AD / Entra ID Provider</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">auth:
  environment: production
  providers:
    microsoft:
      production:
        clientId: ${AZURE_CLIENT_ID}
        clientSecret: ${AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET}
        tenantId: ${AZURE_TENANT_ID}
        callbackUrl: "https://backstage.easypj.com/api/auth/microsoft/handler/frame"

# Custom resolver for Azure AD groups
signInResolvers:
  microsoft:
    resolver: emailMatchingUserEntityProfileEmail</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>💡 Group Mapping — Automatic Team Syncing</h5><p class="mb-0">Both Okta and Azure AD support <strong>group claims</strong> — when a user logs in, their Okta/Azure group memberships are included in the OIDC token. Backstage's sign-in resolver automatically maps these to Backstage Group entities. This means <strong>zero manual group management</strong> — when someone joins the "platform-team" group in Okta, they automatically get access to platform-team resources in Backstage.</p></div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🏢</div><h5>Okta</h5><p>Best for: companies using Okta as their primary identity provider. Strong group management. OIDC-native. Widely adopted among tech companies.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">☁️</div><h5>Azure AD / Entra ID</h5><p>Best for: Microsoft 365 shops. Deep integration with Office, Teams, and Azure. Combined app registration + enterprise app model. Free with most M365 plans.</p></div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §22.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s22-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">22.5 Multiple Auth Providers — One Backstage, Many Identities</h3>
                <p>Backstage supports running <strong>multiple auth providers simultaneously</strong>. This is critical for organizations where some teams use GitHub, others use Okta, and contractors use a separate identity provider.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container"><div class="diagram-title">Multi-Provider Auth Architecture</div>
<pre>
    ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
    │              MULTI-PROVIDER AUTH ARCHITECTURE                      │
    │                                                                  │
    │                     ┌─────────────────┐                          │
    │                     │  SIGN-IN PAGE    │                          │
    │                     │                 │                          │
    │                     │  [GitHub]  [Okta]│  ← Multiple buttons     │
    │                     │  [Azure] [Google]│                          │
    │                     └───┬───┬───┬───┬─┘                          │
    │                         │   │   │   │                            │
    │              ┌──────────┘   │   │   └──────────┐                │
    │              ▼              ▼   ▼              ▼                │
    │         ┌─────────┐  ┌─────────┐  ┌─────────┐  ┌─────────┐    │
    │         │ GitHub   │  │ Okta    │  │ Azure   │  │ Google  │    │
    │         │ Provider │  │ Provider│  │ Provider│  │ Provider│    │
    │         └────┬─────┘  └────┬────┘  └────┬────┘  └────┬────┘    │
    │              │             │            │            │          │
    │              └─────────────┼────────────┼────────────┘          │
    │                            │            │                       │
    │                            ▼            ▼                       │
    │                    ┌─────────────────────────┐                  │
    │                    │   SIGN-IN RESOLVERS     │                  │
    │                    │   (One per provider)    │                  │
    │                    │                         │                  │
    │                    │  GitHub → username match│                  │
    │                    │  Okta   → email match   │                  │
    │                    │  Azure  → email match   │                  │
    │                    └───────────┬─────────────┘                  │
    │                                │                                │
    │                                ▼                                │
    │                    ┌─────────────────────────┐                  │
    │                    │   BACKSTAGE USER ENTITY  │                  │
    │                    │   (Single unified user)  │                  │
    │                    │   Same user, regardless  │                  │
    │                    │   of which provider used │                  │
    │                    └─────────────────────────┘                  │
    └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">app-config.yaml — Multiple Auth Providers</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">auth:
  environment: production
  providers:
    github:
      production:
        clientId: ${GITHUB_CLIENT_ID}
        clientSecret: ${GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET}
    okta:
      production:
        clientId: ${OKTA_CLIENT_ID}
        clientSecret: ${OKTA_CLIENT_SECRET}
        audience: "api://default"
    microsoft:
      production:
        clientId: ${AZURE_CLIENT_ID}
        clientSecret: ${AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET}
        tenantId: ${AZURE_TENANT_ID}
    google:
      production:
        clientId: ${GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID}
        clientSecret: ${GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET}

# All 4 providers appear on the sign-in page.
# User picks whichever one they prefer.
# Sign-in resolvers map each identity to the same Backstage User.</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note"><h5>🔑 The Critical Role of Sign-In Resolvers</h5><p class="mb-0">When the same person logs in via GitHub (using <code>alice@easypj.com</code>) AND via Okta (also <code>alice@easypj.com</code>), Backstage needs to know they're the SAME user. This is the sign-in resolver's job — it matches by email/username and creates/links to a single Backstage User entity. <strong>Without proper resolvers, the same person gets TWO separate Backstage accounts</strong> — one per provider — causing duplicate ownership and permission issues.</p></div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔗</div><h5>GitHub</h5><p>Developers, contractors with GitHub accounts. Auto-maps GitHub teams.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🏢</div><h5>Okta</h5><p>Full-time employees. Enterprise SSO with group claims for team mapping.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">☁️</div><h5>Azure AD</h5><p>Microsoft 365 orgs. Integrated with Office, Teams, Azure RBAC.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🌐</div><h5>Google</h5><p>G Suite orgs. Simple OAuth setup. Good for Google-first companies.</p></div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §22.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s22-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">22.6 Guest Access — Development Only!</h3>
                <p>Guest access allows anyone to browse Backstage <strong>without logging in</strong>. It's designed for local development convenience, NOT for production.</p>

                <div class="scenario-box"><h5>⚠️ The Security Risk of Guest Access in Production</h5><ul>
                    <li>🔴 <strong>No authentication:</strong> Anyone on the internet can access your Backstage instance</li>
                    <li>🔴 <strong>Full catalog exposure:</strong> All services, APIs, systems, and their metadata are visible</li>
                    <li>🔴 <strong>Documentation leaks:</strong> TechDocs may contain internal architecture, API keys in examples, and sensitive design decisions</li>
                    <li>🔴 <strong>Plugin data exposure:</strong> CI/CD status, K8s pod info, Sentry errors — all publicly visible</li>
                    <li>🔴 <strong>No audit trail:</strong> Can't track who viewed what. Zero accountability</li>
                    <li>🔴 <strong>Template abuse:</strong> Anyone can execute software templates — creating repos, consuming resources</li>
                </ul></div>

                <div class="info-box danger"><h5>🚫 THE GOLDEN RULE</h5><p class="mb-0"><strong>NEVER enable guest access in production.</strong> Guest access is configured via <code>auth.environment: development</code> with a bare <code>guest: {}</code> provider block. If you see this in a production config, it's an immediate security failure — guests get a fixed "guest" identity with no real permission scoping. Production MUST have at least one real auth provider configured.</p></div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">Guest config (DEV ONLY) — and what NOT to do in production</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># app-config.yaml (DEV ONLY - never in production)
auth:
  environment: development
  providers:
    guest: {}

# Guests get a fixed "guest" identity with no real permissions
# scoping - this is why it must NEVER be enabled in production</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Development (Guest OK)</th><th>Production (Guest NEVER)</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>auth.environment</strong></td><td><code>development</code></td><td class="winner"><code>production</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>auth.providers</strong></td><td>Empty or minimal</td><td class="winner">At least 1 (GitHub/Okta/Azure)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Who can access?</strong></td><td>Anyone on localhost</td><td class="winner">Only authenticated users</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Permissions</strong></td><td>None (everything is open)</td><td class="winner">RBAC enforced</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Audit logging</strong></td><td>Optional</td><td class="winner">Required for compliance</td></tr>
                </tbody></table></div>

                <div class="diagram-container"><div class="diagram-title">Wrong vs Correct — Production auth.environment</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># ❌ WRONG — Never do this in production!
auth:
  environment: development    # ← Guest access enabled!
  # providers:               # ← No providers = NO AUTH!
  #   (empty)

# ✅ CORRECT — Production configuration
auth:
  environment: production     # ← Guest access DISABLED
  providers:
    github:
      production:
        clientId: ${GITHUB_CLIENT_ID}
        clientSecret: ${GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET}
    # Always have at least ONE provider in production</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch22-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — why OIDC-only matters, and what a sign-in resolver actually resolves</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>The correction in §22.4 matters more than it looks: the built-in <code>okta</code> and <code>microsoft</code> auth providers only speak OAuth2/OIDC.</p>
                    <h5>① If Your IdP Is SAML, You Build a Custom Provider</h5>
                    <p>If your IdP is configured for SAML, Backstage does not ship a ready-made SAML provider — you build a custom one on top of its generic OAuth2/SAML provider scaffold instead of using the config blocks in §22.4.</p>
                    <h5>② Why the Flow Diagram Has a "Resolver" Step</h5>
                    <p>This is also why the sign-in flow diagram in §22.1 has a "resolver" step: whatever protocol delivers the token, the resolver is the one piece of code that decides which existing Backstage User entity that token belongs to (<code>usernameMatchingUserEntityName</code> is the simplest case — it just matches on name).</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
Provider token arrives  →  signIn.resolvers decides  →  which Backstage User entity?
  (any protocol)             (usernameMatching...        (metadata.name match, or
                               / emailMatching...)         profile.email match)
                    </pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout mb-0"><strong>🔑 One mechanism, every protocol:</strong> regardless of whether the token came from OAuth2, OIDC, or a custom SAML provider, the resolver is always the single piece of code deciding which User entity it maps to.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch22-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Sign-in flow:</strong> redirect to provider → user authenticates → provider returns a token → Backstage issues a JWT session (§22.1 diagram).</li>
                    <li><strong>Built-in okta/microsoft providers are OAuth2/OIDC only</strong> — SAML tenants need a custom provider built on the generic SAML provider.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>signIn.resolvers</code></strong> (e.g. <code>usernameMatchingUserEntityName</code>) is what actually turns a provider identity into a Backstage User entity.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>auth.environment</code></strong> (development vs production) selects which block of provider config is active.</li>
                    <li><strong>Multiple providers can run side by side</strong> — Backstage shows a picker and each provider keeps its own resolver.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>guest: {}</code></strong> gives every visitor the same fixed identity with no real permission scoping — dev only, never production.</li>
                    <li><strong>This chapter is beyond-exam depth,</strong> not one of the 19 scored CBA competencies — same as Chapters 23 and 24, which continue this same auth → permissions → identity-sync arc.</li>
