                <h4>🧠 Chapter 22 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of auth providers, configuration, and sign-in flow — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the Backstage auth flow from sign-in to logged-in?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>User clicks Sign In → Redirect to provider (OAuth/OIDC) → User authenticates → Provider returns token to Backstage callback URL → Backstage creates JWT session → User is logged in with identity and group memberships.</p>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
click → redirect → authenticate → token → JWT session → logged in
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Key: the callback URL is /api/auth/{provider}/handler/frame. Backstage issues its own JWT after validating the provider token. CBA: memorize the 6 steps in order.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Which auth provider is best for a team already using GitHub?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>GitHub OAuth</strong> — it auto-maps GitHub teams to Backstage Groups, uses existing GitHub accounts, and is the easiest to set up (just create an OAuth App). Perfect for dev teams already on GitHub.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Provider</th><th>Setup effort</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>GitHub</td><td>🟢 Easy</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Auto-maps teams to Groups (no manual config). Uses existing accounts (no new passwords). Create a GitHub OAuth App, set client ID/secret in app-config.yaml.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Can you use guest access in production?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>NO — never.</strong> Guest access exposes your entire Backstage instance without authentication. Anyone can see all your services, APIs, docs, and potentially sensitive information. Production MUST have auth configured.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Environment</th><th>Guest OK?</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Development</td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Production</td><td>❌ Never</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Dev only (auth.environment: development). Production must have auth.environment: production with at least one provider. This is a frequently tested concept.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the callback URL for GitHub OAuth in Backstage?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>http://localhost:7007/api/auth/github/handler/frame</code> for development. For production, replace localhost with your Backstage domain. This URL must be registered in the GitHub OAuth App settings.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">callback URL pattern</span></div><pre><code class="language-bash">https://backstage.easypj.com/api/auth/github/handler/frame</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Callback URL = /api/auth/{provider}/handler/frame. Must match exactly between GitHub OAuth App settings and Backstage's app.baseUrl. Mismatch = redirect_uri error.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How does Backstage store user sessions after authentication?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>As a <strong>JWT (JSON Web Token)</strong> stored in a browser cookie. The JWT contains the user's identity, group memberships, and expiration time. This token is sent with every request to the Backstage backend for authorization.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>JWT contains</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Identity</td></tr><tr><td>Group memberships</td></tr><tr><td>Expiration time</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Sessions use JWT cookies, not server-side sessions. CBA: know what the JWT contains (identity + groups + expiration).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is a sign-in resolver and what does it do?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A sign-in resolver maps the identity from an auth provider to a Backstage User entity. It takes the provider's profile (email, username, display name) and creates or matches a Backstage User.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the simplest resolver</span></div><pre><code class="language-yaml">signIn:
  resolvers:
    - resolver: usernameMatchingUserEntityName</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Custom resolvers needed when default mapping doesn't fit your org's identity model. CBA: know what a sign-in resolver does.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do GitHub teams map to Backstage Groups?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Backstage automatically reads the user's GitHub team memberships from the OAuth token and creates/matches Backstage Group entities. Team names become Group names, and the hierarchy (parent/child teams) is preserved.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>GitHub</th><th>Backstage</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Team</td><td>Group entity</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Parent/child team</td><td>Preserved hierarchy</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The sign-in resolver handles this mapping. CBA: GitHub teams auto-map to Groups — no manual group creation needed.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Can you use multiple auth providers simultaneously in Backstage?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Yes. Configure each provider in <code>auth.providers</code> in app-config.yaml. Users see all configured providers on the sign-in page and can choose which one to use. Each provider needs its own sign-in resolver.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Providers configured</th><th>Sign-in page shows</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>github + okta</td><td>A picker with both buttons</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Multiple providers = multiple sign-in options. Each needs its own config block in auth.providers + its own sign-in resolver. CBA: Backstage supports multiple simultaneous auth providers.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Where do you configure auth providers in Backstage?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>In <code>app-config.yaml</code> under <code>auth.providers</code>. Each provider has its own configuration block with clientId, clientSecret, and provider-specific settings. Secrets MUST use <code>${ENV_VAR}</code> syntax — never hardcode client secrets.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">multi-provider config</span></div><pre><code class="language-yaml">auth:
