            <!-- ─────────── §23.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s23-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">23.1 The Backstage Permission Framework</h3>
                <div class="diagram-container"><div class="diagram-title">Permission System</div>
<pre>
  ┌─────────────────────┐
  │   Permission Policy │ ← Define what actions exist & who can do them
  └────────┬────────────┘
           │
           ▼
  ┌─────────────┐   ┌─────────────┐
  │ Allow Rules │   │ Deny Rules  │
  │ "If owner,  │   │ "If not in  │
  │  allow del" │   │  group, deny"│
  └──────┬──────┘   └──────┬──────┘
         └────────┬────────┘
                  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────┐
  │    Permission Evaluator      │
  │ Checks each request against  │
  │ the policy rules             │
  └──────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ There Are 3 Outcomes, Not 2 — ALLOW, DENY, CONDITIONAL</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">Every permission check resolves to one of three <code>AuthorizeResult</code> values. <strong>CONDITIONAL</strong> is returned when the policy can't yet tell if a request should be allowed — e.g. it depends on whether the current user owns a specific entity. The condition travels with the response so the caller can apply it once the actual resource is known, which is what lets list/read endpoints filter results instead of allowing or denying outright.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Outcome</th><th>Means</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>ALLOW</strong></td><td>Access granted, unconditionally</td><td>Everyone can read catalog entities</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>DENY</strong></td><td>Access blocked, unconditionally</td><td>Interns can never delete anything</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CONDITIONAL</strong></td><td>"Can't decide yet" — a condition travels with the response</td><td>"Can delete IF owner" — depends on the specific entity</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §23.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s23-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">23.2 Configuring Permission Policies</h3>
                <div class="diagram-container"><div class="diagram-title">Permission Policy — Conceptual Example</div>
<pre><code class="language-typescript">// Conceptual illustration of an allow/deny policy shape
export default createPermissionPolicy({
  allow: [
    { // Anyone can READ entities
      permission: catalogEntityReadPermission,
      resourceType: 'catalog-entity',
      action: 'read',
    },
    { // Only OWNERS can DELETE their entities
      permission: catalogEntityDeletePermission,
      resourceType: 'catalog-entity',
      action: 'delete',
      conditions: {
        rule: 'IS_ENTITY_OWNER',
        resourceType: 'catalog-entity',
      },
    },
  ],
});</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note"><h5>🔑 The Real, Current Implementation Pattern</h5><p class="mb-0">The illustration above shows the SHAPE of allow/deny thinking, but the real, current Backstage API implements <code>PermissionPolicy</code> as a class with one <code>handle()</code> method, registered via <code>policyExtensionPoint</code>. See the exact code below.</p></div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">packages/backend/src/extensions/permissionsPolicyExtension.ts — the real, current implementation</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-typescript">import { AuthorizeResult } from '@backstage/plugin-permission-common';
import {
  catalogConditions,
  createCatalogConditionalDecision,
} from '@backstage/plugin-catalog-backend/alpha';
import { catalogEntityDeletePermission } from
  '@backstage/plugin-catalog-common/alpha';
import { PermissionPolicy, PolicyQuery, PolicyQueryUser }
  from '@backstage/plugin-permission-node';

class MyPermissionPolicy implements PermissionPolicy {
  async handle(request: PolicyQuery, user?: PolicyQueryUser) {
    if (request.permission.name ===
        catalogEntityDeletePermission.name) {
      return createCatalogConditionalDecision(
        request.permission,
        catalogConditions.isEntityOwner({
          claims: user?.info.ownershipEntityRefs ?? [],
        }),
      );
    }
    return { result: AuthorizeResult.ALLOW };
  }
}

// Registered via policyExtensionPoint in
// packages/backend/src/extensions/permissionsPolicyExtension.ts,
// added with backend.add(import('./extensions/
// permissionsPolicyExtension')) in packages/backend/src/index.ts</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip mb-0"><h5>💡 Notice: This Call Returns CONDITIONAL, Not ALLOW</h5><p class="mb-0"><code>createCatalogConditionalDecision()</code> doesn't return a flat ALLOW or DENY — it returns a CONDITIONAL decision carrying <code>catalogConditions.isEntityOwner(...)</code>, which the catalog plugin evaluates per-entity later. This is §23.1's third outcome in real code.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §23.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s23-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">23.3 RBAC Configuration — Mapping Groups to Roles</h3>
                <p>In Backstage, RBAC is implemented by mapping <strong>Backstage Groups</strong> (teams) to <strong>permission policies</strong>. There are no built-in "roles" like admin/developer/viewer — instead, you create permission rules that check group membership, commonly via a <code>roleForGroup</code> mapping.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container"><div class="diagram-title">RBAC — How Groups Map to Permissions</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                    BACKSTAGE RBAC MODEL                       │
  │                                                              │
  │  Group: team-platform (admins)                               │
  │    │                                                         │
  │    └──▶ Allow: ALL permissions (create, read, update, delete)│
  │                                                              │
  │  Group: team-fe (developers)                                 │
  │    │                                                         │
  │    ├──▶ Allow: catalog.entity.read (all entities)            │
  │    ├──▶ Allow: catalog.entity.create (with isEntityOwner)    │
  │    └──▶ Deny:  catalog.entity.delete (can't delete anything) │
  │                                                              │
  │  Group: interns (viewers)                                    │
  │    │                                                         │
  │    └──▶ Allow: catalog.entity.read (all entities)            │
  │         (Everything else is implicitly DENIED)               │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  RULE OF THUMB:
  • Admin team → Allow ALL
  • Dev team → Allow read/create/update (with ownership checks)
  • Viewer team → Allow read only
  • Unauthenticated → Deny ALL (unless guest access enabled)
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">roleForGroup — the real pattern</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-typescript">// packages/backend/src/extensions/permissionsPolicyExtension.ts
const roleForGroup = {
  'group:default/team-platform-admins': 'admin',
  'group:default/team-platform': 'developer',
};

// admin role: full catalog + scaffolder access
// developer role: read catalog, run templates, edit own entities
// viewer role (default): read-only across the board</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Common RBAC Patterns</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table class="comparison-table">
                        <thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Allow Rule</th><th>Condition</th><th>Who Gets It</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Admin Access</strong></td><td>ALL permissions</td><td>via roleForGroup: team-platform-admins</td><td>Platform team</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Owner-Only Delete</strong></td><td>catalog.entity.delete</td><td>isEntityOwner</td><td>Entity owners</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Team-Scoped Access</strong></td><td>catalog.entity.read</td><td>hasAnnotation: team=XYZ</td><td>Specific team members</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Read-Only</strong></td><td>catalog.entity.read</td><td>None (everyone)</td><td>All authenticated users</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §23.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s23-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">23.4 Conditional Permissions — Fine-Grained Access Control</h3>
                <p>Conditions are the power behind Backstage's permission system. Without conditions, permissions are all-or-nothing. With conditions, you can say "allow delete IF you're the owner" or "allow read IF the entity has a specific annotation."</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><h5>isEntityOwner</h5><p>The most common condition. Checks if the current user matches the entity's <code>spec.owner</code>. Used for: "Owners can delete their own services."</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>isEntityKind</h5><p>Checks whether the entity is a specific kind (e.g. Component). Used for: "This rule only applies to Components, not APIs or Systems."</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>hasAnnotation</h5><p>Checks if an entity has a specific annotation and value. Used for: "Only show entities with <code>visibility: public</code> to external users."</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><h5>hasLabel</h5><p>Checks if an entity has a specific label. Used for: "Developers can only see entities labeled with their team name."</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>hasSpec</h5><p>Checks fields within the entity's spec. Used for: "Only production services can be deployed; experimental services are blocked."</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><h5>hasMetadata</h5><p>Checks a specific metadata field/value. Used for: entity-level checks beyond just annotations/labels — e.g. matching on <code>metadata.name</code> patterns.</p></div>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Rule (catalogConditions.*)</th><th>Checks if…</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>isEntityOwner</code></td><td>User/Group is the entity owner</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>isEntityKind</code></td><td>Entity is a specific kind (e.g. Component)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>hasAnnotation</code></td><td>Entity has a specific annotation</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>hasLabel</code></td><td>Entity has a specific label</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>hasSpec</code></td><td>Entity has a specific spec field/value</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>hasMetadata</code></td><td>Entity has a specific metadata field/value</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip mb-0">
                    <h5>💡 Combining Conditions with AND/OR</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">You can combine multiple conditions: "Allow delete IF (owner AND lifecycle is experimental)" or "Allow read IF (hasAnnotation visibility=public OR hasLabel team=platform)". AND requires ALL conditions to match; OR requires ANY condition to match. This enables complex access policies without custom code.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §23.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s23-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">23.5 Testing Permissions</h3>
                <p>Test permissions thoroughly before deploying to production. A misconfigured permission can either block legitimate users (false positive) or allow unauthorized access (false negative).</p>

                <div class="comparison-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Test Approach</th><th>Tool</th><th>How It Works</th><th>CBA Note</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Permission Evaluator</strong></td><td><code>PermissionEvaluator</code> API</td><td>Programmatically test if a user/group can perform an action. Returns <code>ALLOW</code>, <code>DENY</code>, or <code>CONDITIONAL</code>.</td><td>Most reliable test method</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Role-Based Testing</strong></td><td>Different test users</td><td>Create test users in different groups (admin, dev, viewer). Test each one's access to protected resources.</td><td>Simulates real-world scenarios</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Backend Log Analysis</strong></td><td><code>env.logger</code></td><td>Check backend logs for permission denial reasons. Set log level to <code>debug</code> to see full permission evaluation trace.</td><td>Debugging production issues</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Negative Testing</strong></td><td>Manual / Automated</td><td>Deliberately try to access resources without permission. Verify the request is denied with proper error messaging.</td><td>Always test deny rules!</td></tr>
                </tbody></table></div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">The real curl authorize command</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Check what a specific user can do
curl -X POST localhost:7007/api/permission/authorize \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $USER_TOKEN" \
  -d '{"items":[{"permission":"catalog.entity.delete",
                 "resourceRef":"component:default/jobpost"}]}'

# Response: { result: "ALLOW" } or { result: "DENY" }
# Debugging denials: check backend logs for which rule matched</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">__tests__/permissions.test.ts — Permission Testing Example</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-typescript">import { PermissionEvaluator } from '@backstage/plugin-permission-common';
import { catalogEntityReadPermission } from '@backstage/plugin-catalog-common/alpha';

describe('Catalog Permissions', () => {
  let permissionEvaluator: PermissionEvaluator;

  beforeEach(() => {
    permissionEvaluator = createMockPermissionEvaluator();
  });

  it('allows admin user to read any entity', async () => {
    const result = await permissionEvaluator.authorize(
      [{ permission: catalogEntityReadPermission }],
      { credentials: { 'user:default/admin': [] } }
    );
    expect(result[0].result).toBe('ALLOW');
  });

  it('denies viewer from deleting entities', async () => {
    const result = await permissionEvaluator.authorize(
      [{ permission: catalogEntityDeletePermission }],
      { credentials: { 'user:default/viewer': [] } }
    );
    expect(result[0].result).toBe('DENY');
  });

  it('allows owner to update their own component', async () => {
    const result = await permissionEvaluator.authorize(
      [{ permission: catalogEntityUpdatePermission }],
      { credentials: { 'user:default/easypj-owner': [] } }
    );
    expect(result[0].result).toBe('ALLOW');
  });
});</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">✅</div><h5>Permission Evaluator</h5><p>Backstage's built-in API for testing permissions programmatically. Returns ALLOW/DENY/CONDITIONAL for any permission + user combination.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">👥</div><h5>User Simulation</h5><p>Test as different users with different roles. Simulate: admin, developer, viewer, team-lead. Verify each role gets correct access.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div><h5>Deny-First Testing</h5><p>Start with ALL denied, then add allows. This prevents accidental over-permission. Default-deny is the secure default.</p></div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning mb-0"><h5>⚠️ CBA-Style Permission Testing Pattern</h5><p class="mb-0">A typical scenario: <em>"Admin Alice can delete entities, but Developer Bob cannot — how do you verify this?"</em> Answer: Use <code>PermissionEvaluator.authorize()</code> or the curl <code>/api/permission/authorize</code> endpoint with Alice's credentials (expect ALLOW) and Bob's credentials (expect DENY). Always test BOTH positive AND negative cases before deploying.</p></div>
            </section>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch23-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — why CONDITIONAL exists, and how it lets one policy show different users different results</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>CONDITIONAL is the subtle piece of this chapter. A policy can't always answer "allowed or not" the moment a request arrives.</p>
                    <h5>① The Policy Doesn't Always Know the Specific Resource Yet</h5>
                    <p>"Can this user delete this entity" depends on whether they own the SPECIFIC entity being requested, which the policy layer may not know yet for a list/read call. Instead of guessing, <code>handle()</code> returns CONDITIONAL and attaches the condition itself (via <code>createCatalogConditionalDecision</code> + <code>catalogConditions.isEntityOwner</code> in §23.2's example).</p>
                    <h5>② The Caller Applies the Condition Per-Entity</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
policy.handle() → CONDITIONAL + isEntityOwner condition attached
                        │
        catalog plugin evaluates the condition ONCE PER ENTITY
                        │
   User A's 3 owned services shown  |  User B's other 12 hidden
                    </pre>
                    <p>The caller — often the catalog plugin evaluating a list of entities — applies that condition once each entity is known, which is what lets a single policy correctly show User A their own 3 owned services while hiding User B's 12.</p>
                    <div class="uth-callout mb-0"><strong>🔑 CONDITIONAL is not a maybe — it's a deferred, precise answer:</strong> the policy is certain of the RULE (isEntityOwner), just not yet certain of the RESULT for every individual resource.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch23-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Every permission check resolves to ALLOW, DENY, or CONDITIONAL</strong> — never anything else.</li>
                    <li><strong>CONDITIONAL means "can't decide yet"</strong> — the condition travels with the response so list/read endpoints can filter per-entity.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>PermissionPolicy.handle(request, user)</code></strong> is the one method you implement; it's registered via <code>policyExtensionPoint</code> / <code>backend.add(...)</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>roleForGroup</code> maps a Backstage Group ref straight to a role name</strong> (admin/developer/viewer) — permission rules key off the role, not individual users.</li>
                    <li><strong>Built-in conditions cover the common cases:</strong> isEntityOwner, isEntityKind, hasAnnotation, hasLabel, hasSpec, hasMetadata.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>POST /api/permission/authorize</code></strong> lets you test exactly what a given user's token can do, without touching the UI.</li>
                    <li><strong>When a permission denies unexpectedly,</strong> the backend logs show which rule matched — that's the first place to look.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch23-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Treating CONDITIONAL as a synonym for DENY</strong> — it is neither allow nor deny (§23.1).</li>
                    <li><strong>Forgetting to actually register the custom policy</strong> (policyExtensionPoint) — the default (usually allow-all) silently stays active (§23.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Mapping the wrong Group to the admin role</strong> in roleForGroup, granting far more access than intended (§23.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Writing a rule that looks correct in isolation but was never tested AS the affected user</strong> (§23.5).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

