                <h4>🧠 Chapter 23 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the permission framework, RBAC, and conditions — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between Allow and Deny rules in the permission framework?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Allow rules:</strong> Grant access when conditions are met. "If owner, allow delete."<br><strong>Deny rules:</strong> Block access when conditions are met. "If not in group X, deny." Deny rules take precedence over Allow rules. (There's also a third outcome, CONDITIONAL — see §23.1.)</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Outcome</th><th>Wins when both match?</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Deny</td><td>✅ Always</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Allow</td><td>❌ Only if no Deny matches</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Allow = grant, Deny = block. Deny ALWAYS wins over Allow. This follows security best practice: default deny, explicit allow.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What does the isEntityOwner condition do?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>isEntityOwner</code> checks if the current user is the owner of the entity being accessed. Used to restrict actions like delete, update, or unregister to only the entity's owner.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">real usage</span></div><pre><code class="language-typescript">catalogConditions.isEntityOwner({ claims: user?.info.ownershipEntityRefs ?? [] })</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>isEntityOwner = most common permission condition. Checks if user matches entity's owner field. Used with Allow (or a CONDITIONAL decision) for owner-only access.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Where is the permission policy file located?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>packages/backend/src/extensions/permissionsPolicyExtension.ts</code>. This file defines the <code>PermissionPolicy</code> class's <code>handle()</code> method, registered via <code>policyExtensionPoint</code>. Any changes require restarting the Backstage backend to take effect.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Registration mechanism</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>extensions/permissionsPolicyExtension.ts</td><td>policyExtensionPoint / backend.add(...)</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Changes need backend restart. This is the New Backend System registration pattern (same family as Chapter 16's createBackendPlugin).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Do Deny rules or Allow rules take precedence?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Deny rules take precedence.</strong> If any Deny rule matches a request, access is blocked — even if an Allow rule also matches. This follows the security principle of "deny by default, allow explicitly."</p>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
Allow matches + Deny matches  →  DENY wins
Allow matches + no Deny       →  ALLOW
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Deny &gt; Allow always. Single Deny match = access blocked. Always test Deny rules carefully — they can accidentally block legitimate access.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

