                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A developer reads the permission docs and concludes "CONDITIONAL basically means deny, since the user can't do it yet." Is this correct?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>No — CONDITIONAL is neither allow nor deny. It means the policy doesn't yet know the specific resource well enough to decide, so it attaches a condition (e.g. isEntityOwner) that gets evaluated once the resource is known — often resulting in ALLOW for some entities and effective denial for others, within the same request.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>CONDITIONAL means…</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>❌ Not "deny for now"</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>✅ "Decision deferred until the resource is known"</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§23's Gotcha — treating CONDITIONAL as a synonym for DENY is explicitly named as a common misconception.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A team writes a custom PermissionPolicy class implementing strict rules, but forgets to register it via policyExtensionPoint. What happens to their easypj Backstage instance?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The default policy (usually allow-all) silently stays active — the custom class exists in the codebase but is never actually wired in, so none of its restrictions take effect.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Effective policy</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Class written, not registered</td><td>Default allow-all — restrictions do nothing</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Registered via policyExtensionPoint</td><td>Custom class actually enforced</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§23.2's Gotcha — a real, easy-to-miss failure mode: writing correct code that's simply never registered.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

