            <section class="section-block" id="b-s24-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">24.1 LDAP / Active Directory Integration</h3>
                <div class="diagram-container"><div class="diagram-title">LDAP Sync Architecture</div>
<pre>
  ┌─────────────────┐         ┌─────────────────┐         ┌─────────────────┐
  │  LDAP / AD      │──pull──▶│  Backstage       │──store─▶│  PostgreSQL     │
  │  Directory      │ (30min) │  LDAP Module     │         │  (Users/Groups) │
  │                 │         │                  │         │                 │
  │  Users: 5000    │         │  @backstage/     │         │  kind: User     │
  │  Groups: 200    │         │  plugin-catalog- │         │  kind: Group    │
  │  OUs: 50        │         │  backend-module- │         │                 │
  │                 │         │  ldap            │         │                 │
  └─────────────────┘         └─────────────────┘         └─────────────────┘

  Config: app-config.yaml → catalog.providers.ldapOrg
  Schedule: Configurable (default every 30-60 min)
  Filter: users.options.filter: "(objectClass=person)"
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">app-config.yaml — the real ldapOrg config</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">catalog:
  providers:
    ldapOrg:
      default:
        target: ldaps://ldap.easypj.com
        bind: { dn: ${LDAP_BIND_DN}, secret: ${LDAP_BIND_SECRET} }
        users:
          - dn: ou=people,dc=easypj,dc=com
            options: { filter: (objectClass=person) }
        groups:
          - dn: ou=groups,dc=easypj,dc=com
        schedule: { frequency: PT30M, timeout: PT5M }</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container"><div class="diagram-title">LDAP to Backstage Entity Resolution</div>
<pre>
  LDAP directory (ou=people,dc=easypj,dc=com)
    entry: uid=jdoe, cn=Jane Doe, mail=jane@easypj.com
                              │  matched by the users.dn + filter (24.1)
                              ▼
  Ingestion runs on schedule (every 30 min, configured above)
                              │
                              ▼
  Backstage User entity created/updated:
    kind: User, metadata.name: jdoe, spec.profile.email: jane@easypj.com

  Groups resolve the same way, one level up, from ou=groups (24.1).
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note mb-0"><h5>🔑 LDAP Sync Is Scheduled (Not Real-Time)</h5><p class="mb-0">Changes in LDAP appear in Backstage after the next sync cycle. For real-time updates, use Okta/Azure AD group claims instead. LDAP is best for initial bulk import and periodic sync of existing directory users.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §24.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s24-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">24.2 Okta / Azure AD Group Sync</h3>
                <div class="diagram-container"><div class="diagram-title">Real-Time Group Sync via OIDC Claims</div>
<pre>
  ┌─────────────────┐         ┌─────────────────┐
  │  Okta / Azure AD│──login─▶│  Backstage      │
  │                 │         │                 │
  │  User signs in  │         │  Reads group    │
  │  → ID Token     │         │  claims from    │
  │  contains:      │         │  auth token     │
  │  groups: [      │         │  → Auto-updates │
  │   "platform",   │         │  Group entities │
  │   "engineering" │         │  → Instant sync │
  │  ]              │         │                 │
  └─────────────────┘         └─────────────────┘

  ADVANTAGE: Real-time! Changes reflect on next sign-in.
  No scheduled sync needed unlike LDAP.
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">app-config.yaml — the real Okta group-sync config</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">catalog:
  providers:
    okta:
      default:
        orgUrl: https://easypj.okta.com
        token: ${OKTA_API_TOKEN}
        groupKey: 0oa1b2c3d4e5f6g7h8
        schedule: { frequency: { minutes: 30 } }

# Auto-creates Backstage Group entities from Okta groups,
# and User entities from Okta org members</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><h5>🔑 Okta</h5><p>Group claims included in OIDC token. Configure in app-config.yaml under <code>catalog.providers.okta</code>. Backstage auto-creates Group entities from claims.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>🔑 Azure AD</h5><p>Group membership via Microsoft Graph API or OIDC claims. Supports nested groups. Configure under <code>auth.providers.microsoft</code>.</p></div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §24.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s24-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">24.3 Custom Sign-In Resolvers</h3>
                <div class="diagram-container"><div class="diagram-title">Sign-In Resolver — Mapping External Identity to Backstage User</div>
<pre>
  ┌─────────────────┐         ┌─────────────────┐         ┌─────────────────┐
  │  Auth Provider  │──token─▶│  Sign-In        │──maps──▶│  Backstage      │
  │  (GitHub/Okta)  │         │  Resolver       │         │  User Entity    │
  │                 │         │                 │         │                 │
  │  Profile:       │         │  Input:         │         │  name: jane     │
  │  email, name,   │         │  provider       │         │  email: ...     │
  │  avatar, groups │         │  profile data   │         │  memberOf: [...]│
  └─────────────────┘         └─────────────────┘         └─────────────────┘

  Use Custom Resolver When:
  • Non-standard claims from identity provider
  • Need to transform usernames/emails
  • Multiple identity sources need merging
  • External API call needed during sign-in
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">packages/backend/src/extensions/auth.ts — real custom resolver code</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-typescript">const customResolver = async (info, ctx) => {
  const { profile } = info.result;
  const userEntityRef = stringifyEntityRef({
    kind: 'User',
    name: profile.email.split('@')[0],
  });
  return ctx.issueToken({
    claims: { sub: userEntityRef, ent: [userEntityRef] },
  });
};</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip mb-0"><h5>💡 Built-in vs Custom</h5><p class="mb-0">Built-in resolvers handle GitHub, Okta, Google, Azure AD out of the box. Build custom only when your identity mapping doesn't fit the standard patterns. Custom resolvers receive the full provider profile and return a Backstage User reference via <code>ctx.issueToken()</code>.</p></div>
            </section>

