            <section class="section-block" id="b-s24-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">24.5 Identity Provider Troubleshooting</h3>
                <p>The most common failures when wiring an external IdP, and where to look first.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely Cause</th><th>Where to Look</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Users never appear in the Catalog</td><td>LDAP schedule has not run yet</td><td>Check catalog processing logs, force a manual refresh</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Sign-in works but no Groups</td><td>bind DN lacks read access to the groups OU</td><td>Verify bind account can read ou=groups (§24.1)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Login loops back to the sign-in page</td><td>Custom resolver threw and issueToken never ran</td><td>Check backend logs for the resolver stack trace (§24.3)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Okta sync stops updating</td><td>API token expired or revoked</td><td>Rotate OKTA_API_TOKEN, confirm scope is still valid</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch24-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — why sync is scheduled, not login-triggered</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>Both the LDAP (ldapOrg) and Okta providers in this chapter run as scheduled catalog providers, not on-demand calls triggered by login.</p>
                    <h5>① The Schedule Is a Catalog Processing Task</h5>
                    <p>The schedule field (<code>PT30M</code> in §24.1, <code>{ minutes: 30 }</code> in §24.2) is a catalog processing task that periodically re-lists the directory and reconciles User/Group entities the same way any other catalog provider reconciles Components — it diffs what it just fetched against what's already in the catalog and creates/updates/removes entities to match.</p>
                    <h5>② Why a New Hire Doesn't Appear Instantly</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
AD account created  →  waits for next scheduled ldapOrg run  →  appears in catalog
                        (NOT the moment the account is created)
                    </pre>
                    <p>That's why a brand-new hire doesn't appear in easypj's catalog the instant their AD account is created: they appear on the next scheduled run, which is also why §24.5's first troubleshooting question is always "has the schedule actually run yet?"</p>
                    <div class="uth-callout mb-0"><strong>🔑 Same reconciliation model as any catalog provider:</strong> fetch → diff → create/update/remove — LDAP and Okta sync aren't special cases, they're catalog providers like any other.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch24-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>ldapOrg ingests Users and Groups from two separate DN queries</strong> (ou=people / ou=groups) sharing one bind credential and one schedule.</li>
                    <li><strong>An LDAP entry becomes a Backstage entity keyed by a stable field</strong> (e.g. uid → metadata.name) — Groups resolve the same way, one level up.</li>
                    <li><strong>Okta group sync needs orgUrl + an API token + groupKey,</strong> and creates both Group and User entities from org membership.</li>
                    <li><strong>A custom sign-in resolver builds a userEntityRef by hand</strong> and calls <code>ctx.issueToken()</code> directly — use it only when built-in resolvers can't express the mapping.</li>
                    <li><strong>Decision rule (§24.4):</strong> on-prem AD/LDAP → ldapOrg; Okta/Azure AD → group sync; unusual/homegrown identity → custom resolver; no IdP yet → guest (Chapter 22).</li>
                    <li><strong>Nearly every sync failure traces to one of three things:</strong> the schedule hasn't run, the bind account lacks read access to the target OU, or an API token expired.</li>
                    <li><strong>This chapter is beyond-exam depth,</strong> not one of the 19 scored CBA competencies — closing the same arc as Chapters 22 and 23.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch24-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Expecting a new user to appear immediately after they sign in</strong> — ingestion is scheduled, not login-triggered (§24.1).</li>
                    <li><strong>A bind DN that can authenticate but has no read grant on ou=groups</strong> — users sync, groups silently don't (§24.1).</li>
                    <li><strong>A custom resolver that throws</strong> — the symptom is a login loop back to sign-in, not a visible error (§24.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>An expired/revoked OKTA_API_TOKEN</strong> — group sync stops silently until someone checks the logs (§24.2).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

