                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of LDAP, Okta group sync, and custom resolvers — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How does Backstage sync users from LDAP?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Using the <code>@backstage/plugin-catalog-backend-module-ldap</code> module via the <code>ldapOrg</code> provider. Configure LDAP server URL, bind DN, and search filters in app-config.yaml. Users and Groups are ingested as Catalog entities on a configurable schedule.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Config key</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>catalog.providers.ldapOrg.target</td><td>ldaps://ldap.easypj.com</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>LDAP sync uses the ldapOrg catalog provider. Config: server URL, bind DN, search filters, schedule. Users/Groups become Catalog entities.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is a sign-in resolver?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A sign-in resolver maps the identity from an auth provider (GitHub, Okta, etc.) to a Backstage User entity. It takes the provider's profile data and determines which Backstage user this corresponds to. Custom resolvers are used when the built-in mapping doesn't fit your needs.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">custom resolver skeleton</span></div><pre><code class="language-typescript">const customResolver = async (info, ctx) => { ... return ctx.issueToken({...}); };</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Sign-in resolver = identity mapper (provider → Backstage User). Takes profile data, returns User entity. Custom when built-in doesn't fit.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do group memberships stay in sync with Okta/Azure AD?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Group claims are included in the auth token from the provider. Backstage reads these claims on every sign-in and updates the user's Group memberships automatically. Changes in Okta/Azure AD are reflected the next time the user signs in.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Timing</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>OIDC group claims</td><td>Real-time, next sign-in</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Okta/Azure sync via OIDC group claims in auth token. Updated on every sign-in (real-time). No scheduled sync needed.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What module is needed for LDAP integration?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>@backstage/plugin-catalog-backend-module-ldap</code>. Install it and configure the LDAP server URL, bind DN, bind credentials, and search filters in app-config.yaml under <code>catalog.providers.ldapOrg</code>.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the module</span></div><pre><code class="language-bash">@backstage/plugin-catalog-backend-module-ldap</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>LDAP module = @backstage/plugin-catalog-backend-module-ldap. Config in app-config.yaml. Runs on schedule.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How often does Backstage sync users from LDAP?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The sync schedule is configurable in app-config.yaml (<code>schedule: { frequency: PT30M, timeout: PT5M }</code>). Default is typically every 30-60 minutes. Changes in LDAP appear in Backstage after the next sync cycle — not instantly.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>frequency</td><td>PT30M (every 30 min)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>timeout</td><td>PT5M</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>LDAP sync schedule = configurable (default 30-60 min). Not real-time. This contrasts with Okta group claims.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between LDAP sync and Okta group claims?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table><thead><tr><th></th><th>LDAP sync</th><th>Okta group claims</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Model</td><td>Scheduled bulk import (pull)</td><td>Real-time via token (push-ish)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Delay</td><td>Up to schedule interval</td><td>Next sign-in</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>LDAP = scheduled (30-60 min delay). Okta = real-time (next sign-in). LDAP pulls on timer; Okta pushes via token claims.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">When would you build a custom sign-in resolver?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>When built-in resolvers don't fit: (1) non-standard claims from identity provider, (2) need to transform usernames/emails, (3) multiple identity sources need merging, (4) external API call needed during sign-in.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Scenario</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Non-standard claims</td></tr><tr><td>Username/email transform</td></tr><tr><td>Multi-source merging</td></tr><tr><td>External API enrichment</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>4 scenarios for custom resolver — matches §24.4's decision guide's "homegrown/unusual" row.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you configure the LDAP search filter for users?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>In app-config.yaml under the ldapOrg provider config: <code>users.options.filter: "(objectClass=person)"</code>. Customize to filter by department, group membership, or any LDAP attribute.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">custom filter example</span></div><pre><code class="language-yaml">options: { filter: "(&(objectClass=person)(department=Engineering))" }</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>LDAP filter in app-config.yaml. Default: (objectClass=person). Uses LDAP filter syntax with & (AND), | (OR).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What happens when a user is removed from LDAP but still exists in Backstage?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The user entity remains in the Backstage Catalog but is marked as orphaned. On the next LDAP sync, Backstage can be configured to either (1) remove the user (clean sync), or (2) keep the user but flag them (preserve history).</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Option</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Clean sync — remove</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Preserve — flag, keep history</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Orphaned users = removed from LDAP, still in Backstage. Choose based on compliance requirements.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to sync users from both LDAP and Okta into Backstage. How would you architect this?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Configure BOTH: (1) ldapOrg catalog provider for bulk sync of existing users/groups, (2) Okta as a catalog provider for real-time group sync. LDAP handles initial import and periodic sync; Okta handles real-time group updates.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>LDAP</td><td>Initial import + periodic sync</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Okta</td><td>Real-time group updates</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Dual identity: LDAP for bulk import + Okta for real-time sync. Both are configured as catalog.providers entries simultaneously.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch24-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 24 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (8)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Real workplace situations — 8 scenarios mirroring how the CBA exam actually asks.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">easypj hires a new engineer whose AD account is created at 2:15 PM. The ldapOrg schedule runs every 30 minutes. When should IT expect the new hire to appear in the Backstage catalog?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>On the next scheduled run after 2:15 PM — not instantly. Ingestion is scheduled, not login-triggered, so there's an unavoidable window (up to the schedule's frequency) before the entity appears.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Event</th><th>Time</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>AD account created</td><td>2:15 PM</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Appears in catalog</td><td>Next PT30M run (up to 2:45 PM)</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§24's Gotcha — "expecting a new user to appear immediately after they sign in" is named explicitly as a common misconception.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A new hire's User entity appears correctly after LDAP sync, but their Group memberships never show up. The bind DN successfully authenticates. What's the likely cause?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The bind DN can authenticate but lacks read access to the <code>ou=groups</code> OU specifically — authentication succeeding doesn't guarantee read authorization on every OU. Verify the bind account's permissions on the groups branch.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Cause</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Users sync, groups don't</td><td>bind DN lacks read grant on ou=groups</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§24.5's troubleshooting table — this exact symptom/cause pair is named explicitly.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Users report being stuck in a redirect loop back to the sign-in page after easypj deployed a new custom sign-in resolver. Where should the team look first?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Backend logs, not the browser — a custom resolver that throws an exception produces exactly this symptom (a silent login loop), and the actual stack trace is only visible in the backend logs, never surfaced to the user's browser.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Where to look</th><th>Shows the stack trace?</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Browser</td><td>❌ No — just a login loop</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Backend logs</td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§24.3's Gotcha + §24's Speed Tip — "check backend logs FIRST for a custom resolver."</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">easypj's Okta group sync worked fine for months, then silently stopped updating Group memberships with no error visible to users. What's the most likely cause, and how do you confirm it?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>An expired or revoked <code>OKTA_API_TOKEN</code> — group sync fails silently rather than surfacing a user-facing error. Confirm by checking backend logs, then rotate the token and verify its scope is still valid.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Okta sync stops updating</td><td>API token expired/revoked</td><td>Rotate OKTA_API_TOKEN</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§24.5's troubleshooting table — "Okta sync stops updating" → "API token expired or revoked."</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">easypj is a small startup with 8 engineers, no LDAP, no Okta — just local dev accounts for now. Per §24.4's decision guide, what should they use?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Guest auth (Chapter 22) — the decision guide's row for "No IdP at all yet (local dev, small team)" points directly to guest access, with the standing caveat that it must never reach production.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Org has…</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>No IdP yet, small team</td><td>Guest auth (dev only)</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§24.4's decision guide's final row, tying back directly to Chapter 22 §22.6's golden rule.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A platform engineer wants to verify a new ldapOrg config without waiting the full 30-minute schedule interval. What's the fastest way?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Force a manual catalog refresh instead of waiting out the schedule — this is explicitly named as the recommended workflow for testing, rather than sitting through the full PT30M interval.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Wait time</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Wait for scheduled run</td><td>Up to 30 min</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Force manual refresh</td><td>Immediate</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§24's Speed Tip — "force a manual catalog refresh while testing instead of waiting out the schedule."</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">easypj wants a User entity's metadata.name to be derived from the email prefix (e.g. jane@easypj.com → jane) for a homegrown SSO system with non-standard claims. Which mechanism fits, and roughly how?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A custom sign-in resolver — build the <code>userEntityRef</code> by hand using <code>profile.email.split('@')[0]</code> as the name, then call <code>ctx.issueToken()</code> with that ref in the claims.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the transform</span></div><pre><code class="language-typescript">name: profile.email.split('@')[0]</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§24.3's exact real code — email-prefix-as-username is precisely the kind of transform a custom resolver exists for.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write the minimal catalog.providers.ldapOrg config for easypj's directory at ou=people,dc=easypj,dc=com with a 30-minute sync schedule.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">minimal config</span></div><pre><code class="language-yaml">catalog:
  providers:
    ldapOrg:
      default:
        target: ldaps://ldap.easypj.com
        bind: { dn: ${LDAP_BIND_DN}, secret: ${LDAP_BIND_SECRET} }
        users:
          - dn: ou=people,dc=easypj,dc=com
            options: { filter: (objectClass=person) }
        schedule: { frequency: PT30M, timeout: PT5M }</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§24.1's exact real config — target, bind, users DN+filter, schedule are the required pieces.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

