            <!-- ─────────── §25.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s25-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">25.3 Jenkins Integration</h3>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Correction — Moved to @backstage-community, and Uses Native Config</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">Install <code>@backstage-community/plugin-jenkins</code> and its backend package (also moved from <code>@backstage/*</code> in the same April 2024 migration). The real, current config uses the plugin's own native <code>jenkins:</code> block in app-config.yaml — not a raw <code>proxy.endpoints</code> entry.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">app-config.yaml — the real, current Jenkins plugin config</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">jenkins:
  baseUrl: https://jenkins.easypj.com
  username: ${JENKINS_USERNAME}
  apiKey: ${JENKINS_API_KEY}

# Annotation on the entity
metadata:
  annotations:
    jenkins.io/job-full-name: easypj/jobpost-build</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note"><h5>🔑 The Proxy Pattern Still Applies Underneath</h5><p class="mb-0">Even with the native <code>jenkins:</code> config block, the mechanism is the same proxy pattern used throughout this chapter: the backend plugin holds the <code>apiKey</code> and calls Jenkins server-side — the frontend never sees credentials, only rendered build data.</p></div>

