                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div><h5>Build History</h5><p>Displays last N builds with status, duration, timestamp. Each row links to the full Jenkins build page.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">▶️</div><h5>Trigger Jobs</h5><p>"Run Build" button on the entity page triggers a Jenkins job via the plugin's backend. No need to open Jenkins.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🧪</div><h5>Test Results</h5><p>Shows pass/fail counts, test duration trend, and links to individual test reports from Jenkins.</p></div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning mb-0"><h5>⚠️ Jenkins API Key Security</h5><p class="mb-0">The <code>JENKINS_API_KEY</code> must be a Jenkins API token (NOT your password). Create it at <strong>Jenkins → User → Configure → API Token</strong>. It is stored as an environment variable and used server-side only — it <strong>never leaves the backend</strong>.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §25.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s25-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">25.4 GitLab CI Integration</h3>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Correction — There Is No First-Party @backstage/plugin-gitlab</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">There is <strong>no first-party <code>@backstage/plugin-gitlab</code></strong> package — that name does not exist. The Backstage plugin directory instead recommends the third-party <code>@immobiliarelabs/backstage-plugin-gitlab</code> (plus its matching <code>@immobiliarelabs/backstage-plugin-gitlab-backend</code> package). Using the wrong package name silently fails install.</p>
                </div>

                <p>Install <code>@immobiliarelabs/backstage-plugin-gitlab</code> + its backend package. This plugin displays <strong>pipeline status</strong>, <strong>job logs</strong>, and <strong>merge request overview</strong> from GitLab CI/CD on the entity page. Add the <code>gitlab.com/project-slug</code> annotation to link entities to their GitLab projects.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">app-config.yaml + catalog-info.yaml — the real, current GitLab config</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># app-config.yaml
integrations:
  gitlab:
    - host: gitlab.com
      token: ${GITLAB_TOKEN}

# catalog-info.yaml
metadata:
  annotations:
    gitlab.com/project-slug: easypj/jobpost
    gitlab.com/merge-request: 'true'  # Enable MR overview</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>What It Shows</th><th>CBA Relevance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Pipeline Status</strong></td><td>✅ passed / ❌ failed / 🔄 running / ⏸️ pending for the latest pipeline on each branch</td><td>Know the annotation <code>gitlab.com/project-slug</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Job Logs</strong></td><td>Individual job output within a pipeline. Helps debug which step failed.</td><td>Understand that logs are fetched via the backend plugin</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Merge Request Overview</strong></td><td>Open MRs with pipeline status per MR. Shows whether an MR is ready to merge.</td><td>Know that <code>gitlab.com/merge-request</code> enables this</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Kubernetes Integration</strong></td><td>If using GitLab K8s agent, shows deployment status alongside pipeline status.</td><td>Bonus: combines CI + CD in one view</td></tr>
                </tbody></table></div>

                <div class="info-box note mb-0"><h5>📖 GitLab CI vs GitHub Actions in Backstage</h5><p class="mb-0">Both show CI/CD status, but GitLab's plugin also shows <strong>merge request pipelines</strong> — a unique feature for GitLab Flow users. GitHub Actions focuses on workflow runs per branch. Unlike GitHub Actions and Jenkins, the GitLab plugin was never a first-party package to begin with — it's third-party from the start.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §25.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s25-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">25.5 ArgoCD / Flux (GitOps CD)</h3>
                <p>The ArgoCD plugin shows <strong>application sync status</strong>, <strong>deployment history</strong>, and <strong>health</strong> directly on the entity page. Flux integration provides similar GitOps visibility. Both answer the critical question: <strong>"Does the live state match the desired state in Git?"</strong></p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>🔑 This Config Block Is SHARED With Chapter 21</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">The <code>argocd:</code> config block is the <strong>same one</strong> configured back in Chapter 21 §21.2 for GitOps deployment status — configure it ONCE and both use cases (GitOps deploy status and CI/CD visibility) read from it. Re-configuring ArgoCD twice is a real, named Gotcha.</p>
                </div>

