            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch25-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — why annotation + central config lets 4 unrelated systems appear side by side</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>Every one of these cards works the same way under the hood: it reads an annotation off the entity (<code>github.com/project-slug</code>, <code>jenkins.io/job-full-name</code>, <code>gitlab.com/project-slug</code>, <code>argocd/app-name</code>) to learn WHICH external project to ask about.</p>
                    <h5>① Lookup Key on the Entity, Credentials in Central Config</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
Entity annotation  →  "which project to ask about" (per-entity)
app-config.yaml    →  "how to authenticate" (once, centrally)
                    </pre>
                    <p>Then it calls that system's own API using credentials stored once in app-config.yaml (a token, an apiKey, a baseUrl). Because the lookup key lives on the entity and the credentials live in central config, the same easypj component can show GitHub Actions, Jenkins, GitLab CI and ArgoCD data side by side without any of those systems knowing about each other.</p>
                    <div class="uth-callout mb-0"><strong>🔑 Same shape as the Kubernetes plugin (Chapter 20):</strong> annotation-to-lookup-key, credentials-in-config — this is a recurring Backstage integration pattern, not unique to CI/CD.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

