            <!-- ─────────── §26.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s26-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">26.1 Production Checklist — The Complete Readiness Assessment</h3>
                <p>Before deploying Backstage to production, EVERY item on this checklist must be verified. The CBA exam tests this checklist heavily — expect scenario questions like "A team deployed Backstage with SQLite and 3 replicas. What will happen?"</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><h5>☑ 1. PostgreSQL (not SQLite)</h5><p>SQLite corrupts with concurrent writes. PostgreSQL via managed service (RDS/Cloud SQL) or K8s StatefulSet.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>☑ 2. HTTPS/TLS Enabled</h5><p>All traffic encrypted. Use cert-manager + Let's Encrypt or enterprise CA. No plain HTTP in production.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><h5>☑ 3. Auth Configured</h5><p>At least one provider (GitHub/Okta/Azure AD). Guest access disabled. <code>auth.environment: production</code>.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>☑ 4. Secrets Externalized</h5><p>K8s Secrets or a vault — NEVER hardcoded in YAML. This is its own checklist row, distinct from Auth.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><h5>☑ 5. Cloud Storage for TechDocs</h5><p>GCS, S3, or Azure Blob. NOT local publisher. All pods share same docs. External builder in CI/CD.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>☑ 6. Elasticsearch/OpenSearch</h5><p>Not SQLite search. Clustered engine for fast full-text search across all entities and TechDocs.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><h5>☑ 7. Monitoring (Prometheus + Grafana)</h5><p>Scrape /metrics. Dashboards for request rate, errors, latency. Alerts for downtime and error spikes.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>☑ 8. Centralized Logging</h5><p>Ship logs to Loki/ELK/Splunk. Include request IDs for tracing. Log level: info (not debug).</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><h5>☑ 9. Automated Backups</h5><p>PostgreSQL daily backups with point-in-time recovery. Test restore procedure monthly.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>☑ 10. 3+ Replicas (HA)</h5><p>Minimum 3 replicas across multiple AZs/nodes. Pod anti-affinity for resilience.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ CBA Exam Trap — The "SQLite in Production" Question</h5><p class="mb-0">This is the MOST tested production concept. SQLite supports only ONE concurrent writer. With 3 Backstage replicas, concurrent writes WILL corrupt data. The CBA WILL ask: "Can you use SQLite with 3 replicas?" Answer: NO. Always PostgreSQL.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §26.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s26-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">26.2 PostgreSQL Setup — Production Database Configuration</h3>
                <p>PostgreSQL is the <strong>only supported production database</strong> for Backstage. SQLite works for local development but fails catastrophically with multiple concurrent writers. Here's the complete production setup:</p>

                <div class="diagram-container"><div class="diagram-title">PostgreSQL Deployment Options — Decision Flow</div>
<pre>
                        ┌─────────────────────────────┐
                        │  "I need PostgreSQL for     │
                        │   Backstage production"     │
                        └─────────────┬───────────────┘
                                      │
                    ┌─────────────────┴─────────────────┐
                    ▼                                   ▼
        ┌───────────────────────┐         ┌───────────────────────┐
        │  OPTION A: MANAGED    │         │  OPTION B: STATEFULSET│
        │  (⭐⭐⭐ Recommended)  │         │  (⭐⭐ Self-Managed)   │
        │                       │         │                       │
        │  AWS RDS PostgreSQL   │         │  K8s StatefulSet      │
        │  Google Cloud SQL     │         │  + PersistentVolume    │
        │  Azure PostgreSQL     │         │  + PgBouncer sidecar   │
        │                       │         │                       │
        │  ✅ Auto backups      │         │  ✅ Full control       │
        │  ✅ Point-in-time     │         │  ✅ No vendor lock-in  │
        │     recovery          │         │  ❌ Ops overhead       │
        │  ✅ Built-in pooling  │         │  ❌ You manage backups │
        │  ✅ Monitoring        │         │  ❌ You handle failover│
        │  ✅ Minimal ops       │         │                       │
        └───────────────────────┘         └───────────────────────┘
</pre></div>

                <h4>Option A: Managed Service (Recommended)</h4>
                <p>Use AWS RDS, Google Cloud SQL, or Azure Database for PostgreSQL. Benefits: automated backups, point-in-time recovery, connection pooling, monitoring, and zero maintenance.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Provider</th><th>Service Name</th><th>Connection Pooling</th><th>CBA Note</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>AWS</strong></td><td>RDS for PostgreSQL / Aurora</td><td>RDS Proxy (built-in option)</td><td>Most commonly referenced on exam</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Google Cloud</strong></td><td>Cloud SQL for PostgreSQL</td><td>Cloud SQL Auth Proxy</td><td>Alternative; know it exists</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Azure</strong></td><td>Azure Database for PostgreSQL</td><td>PgBouncer (built-in)</td><td>Flexible Server includes pooling</td></tr>
                </tbody></table></div>

                <h4>Option B: Kubernetes StatefulSet</h4>
                <p>Run PostgreSQL in-cluster with a StatefulSet and PersistentVolumeClaim. More ops overhead but full control over version, extensions, and configuration.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">postgres-statefulset.yaml — In-cluster PostgreSQL for Backstage</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: backstage-postgres-secret
  namespace: backstage
stringData:
  POSTGRES_USER: backstage
  POSTGRES_PASSWORD: changeme123!  # Use Sealed Secrets or Vault!
  POSTGRES_DB: backstage
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: postgres-data
  namespace: backstage
spec:
  accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 20Gi  # Size based on catalog size + template history
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: StatefulSet
metadata:
  name: postgres
  namespace: backstage
spec:
  serviceName: postgres
  replicas: 1  # Single master (use Patroni/Stolon for HA)
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: postgres
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: postgres
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: postgres
          image: postgres:16-alpine
          ports:
            - containerPort: 5432
          envFrom:
            - secretRef:
                name: backstage-postgres-secret
          volumeMounts:
            - name: postgres-data
              mountPath: /var/lib/postgresql/data
          resources:
            requests:
              cpu: 500m
              memory: 512Mi
            limits:
              cpu: "2"
              memory: 2Gi
      volumes:
        - name: postgres-data
          persistentVolumeClaim:
            claimName: postgres-data
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: postgres
  namespace: backstage
spec:
  selector:
    app: postgres
  ports:
    - port: 5432
  clusterIP: None  # Headless service for StatefulSet</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">app-config.yaml — Backstage database configuration, incl. pluginDivisionMode</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">backend:
  database:
    client: pg
    connection:
      host: ${POSTGRES_HOST}           # postgres.backstage.svc.cluster.local
      port: ${POSTGRES_PORT}           # 5432
      user: ${POSTGRES_USER}           # backstage
      password: ${POSTGRES_PASSWORD}   # From K8s Secret
      database: ${POSTGRES_DB}         # backstage
      pool:
        min: 2       # Minimum idle connections
        max: 10      # Maximum connections per Backstage instance
      ssl:
        rejectUnauthorized: true  # Always use SSL in production
    # 🔑 pluginDivisionMode controls how plugins share the database
    pluginDivisionMode: database   # DEFAULT: every plugin gets its own DB</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note"><h5>🔑 pluginDivisionMode — Database vs Schema</h5><p class="mb-0">The default, <code>database</code>, gives every plugin its own separate PostgreSQL database. Set it to <code>schema</code> instead if you need every plugin sharing one database via separate schemas — useful when a managed service limits how many databases you can create.</p></div>

                <!-- Connection Pooling Deep Dive -->
                <h4>🔑 Connection Pooling — Critical for Production</h4>
                <p>Backstage opens many database connections — <strong>one per plugin</strong> that accesses the database. Without connection pooling (PgBouncer or built-in), you can easily exhaust PostgreSQL's connection limit (default: 100). Managed services include pooling; for self-hosted PostgreSQL, deploy <strong>PgBouncer</strong> as a sidecar.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container"><div class="diagram-title">Connection Pooling — PgBouncer Sidecar Pattern</div>
<pre>
    ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
    │                 Backstage Pod (1 of 3)                      │
    │                                                            │
    │  ┌──────────────────┐      ┌──────────────────────────┐   │
    │  │  Backstage       │      │  PgBouncer (Sidecar)     │   │
    │  │  Backend         │      │                          │   │
    │  │                  │      │  20 Backstage ──────┐    │   │
    │  │  Catalog Plugin  │──DB──▶  connections         │    │   │
    │  │  Scaffolder      │──DB──▶  multiplexed into    │    │   │
    │  │  Search Plugin   │──DB──▶  5 PostgreSQL ───────┼────┼──▶ PostgreSQL
    │  │  Auth Plugin     │──DB──▶  connections         │    │   │   (max 100
    │  │  User Settings   │──DB──▶                      │    │   │    connections)
    │  │                  │      │  localhost:5432      │    │   │
    │  └──────────────────┘      └──────────────────────┘   │   │
    │                                                            │
    │  WITHOUT PgBouncer: 3 pods × 20 conns each = 60 conns    │
    │  WITH PgBouncer:    3 pods × 5 conns each  = 15 conns    │
    │  💡 75% reduction in database connections!                 │
    └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre></div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔌</div><h5>Why Pooling Matters</h5><p>Each Backstage plugin opens its own DB connection. 10 plugins × 3 replicas = 30+ connections. PostgreSQL defaults to 100 max — you'll hit the limit fast without pooling.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div><h5>PgBouncer</h5><p>Lightweight connection pooler. Deploy as a sidecar in the Backstage pod. Configure Backstage to connect to <code>localhost:5432</code> (PgBouncer) instead of the PostgreSQL Service.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">☁️</div><h5>Managed Pooling</h5><p>AWS RDS Proxy, Cloud SQL Auth Proxy, Azure PgBouncer — all handle pooling automatically. No sidecar needed. Recommended for production simplicity.</p></div>
                </div>

                <!-- Backups -->
                <h4>📋 Automated Backups — Non-Negotiable</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">💾</div><h5>Managed Backups</h5><p>RDS/Cloud SQL/Azure: enable automated daily backups with 7-35 day retention. Enable point-in-time recovery (PITR). Test restore <strong>monthly</strong> — a backup you haven't tested isn't a backup.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🛠️</div><h5>Self-Managed Backups</h5><p>Use <code>pg_dump</code> as a CronJob, ship to S3/GCS. For PITR, configure WAL archiving.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># K8s CronJob: daily PostgreSQL backup to S3
apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: CronJob
metadata:
  name: postgres-backup
spec:
  schedule: "0 2 * * *"  # 2 AM daily
  jobTemplate:
    spec:
      template:
        spec:
          containers:
            - name: backup
              image: postgres:16-alpine
              command: ["pg_dump", "-h", "postgres",
                        "-U", "backstage", "-Fc",
                        "backstage"]
              env:
                - name: PGPASSWORD
                  valueFrom:
                    secretKeyRef:
                      name: backstage-postgres-secret
                      key: POSTGRES_PASSWORD
          restartPolicy: OnFailure</code></pre></div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger mb-0"><h5>🚨 CBA Exam Trap — "SQLite in Production"</h5><p class="mb-0">This is the <strong>MOST tested production concept</strong> on the CBA. SQLite supports only <strong>ONE concurrent writer</strong>. With 3 Backstage replicas, concurrent writes <strong>WILL</strong> corrupt data. The exam WILL ask: <em>"Can you use SQLite with multiple replicas?"</em> Answer: <strong>NO. Always PostgreSQL in production.</strong></p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §26.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s26-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">26.3 TechDocs in Production — Cloud Storage Architecture</h3>
                <p>In production with multiple replicas, TechDocs MUST use cloud storage. Here's why and how:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container"><div class="diagram-title">TechDocs Production Architecture</div>
<pre>
  CI/CD PIPELINE                     CLOUD STORAGE              BACKSTAGE PODS
  ┌──────────────┐                 ┌──────────────┐           ┌──────────────┐
  │ GitHub Push  │                 │  GCS / S3 /  │◀──────────│  Pod 1       │
  │ (docs/*.md)  │──build──▶      │  Azure Blob  │◀──────────│  Pod 2       │
  │              │                 │              │◀──────────│  Pod 3       │
  │ mkdocs build │                 │ techdocs/    │           │              │
  │ → HTML       │──upload──▶     │  easypj/     │           │ All pods read│
  │              │                 │  jobpost/    │           │ same bucket  │
  └──────────────┘                 └──────────────┘           └──────────────┘

  KEY CONFIG: builder: external | generator.runIn: docker | publisher: awsS3/googleGcs/azureBlobStorage
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">app-config.yaml — the real production TechDocs config</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">techdocs:
  builder: external          # CI/CD builds, not Backstage itself
  generator: { runIn: docker }   # Docker-based build for security isolation
  publisher:
    type: awsS3
    awsS3: { bucketName: easypj-techdocs-prod, region: us-east-1 }</code></pre>
                </div>

                <p><strong>Why not local publisher?</strong> Each pod has its own filesystem. Pod A builds docs → only Pod A has them. Requests to Pod B or Pod C = no docs. Cloud storage = single shared source, all pods serve the same content.</p>
                <div class="info-box note mb-0"><h5>🔑 Why generator.runIn: docker Matters</h5><p class="mb-0">Running the mkdocs generator inside a Docker container isolates the build from the Backstage backend process itself — a malicious or broken docs repo can't affect the running instance, only its own sandboxed container.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §26.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s26-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">26.4 Search in Production — Elasticsearch Deep Dive</h3>
                <p>Backstage's default search uses an in-memory Lunr index, which works for single-pod dev but FAILS at scale. For production, swap in Elasticsearch or OpenSearch:</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table class="comparison-table">
                        <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Setup Complexity</th><th>Performance</th><th>Best For</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Elasticsearch/OpenSearch</strong></td><td>Medium (separate cluster)</td><td>Excellent — clustered, full-text</td><td>Large catalogs (1000+ entities), high traffic</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>PostgreSQL Search</strong></td><td>Low (same DB)</td><td>Good — single node, basic text</td><td>Small-medium catalogs (&lt;500 entities)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">app-config.yaml — real production Search config</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">search:
  collators: { catalog: { schedule: { frequency: { minutes: 10 } } } }

# For production scale, swap the default in-memory Lunr index for
# @backstage/plugin-search-backend-module-elasticsearch or OpenSearch</code></pre>
                </div>
                <p>Configure in <code>app-config.yaml</code> under <code>search.elasticsearch</code> with node URLs, auth credentials, and index settings. Elasticsearch requires a separate cluster deployment.</p>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §26.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s26-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">26.5 Backstage Upgrades — Safe Upgrade Process</h3>
                <p>Upgrading Backstage versions is a multi-step process that MUST be followed in order. Skipping steps can corrupt your database or break plugins.</p>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">1</div><h5>Bump Versions</h5><p><code>backstage-cli versions:bump</code> — updates all @backstage packages together, then read the upgrade helper diff at <code>backstage.github.io/upgrade-helper</code> for breaking config changes.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">2</div><h5>Backup PostgreSQL</h5><p><code>pg_dump backstage > backup_$(date +%Y%m%d).sql</code> — ALWAYS backup before any upgrade.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">3</div><h5>Run Migrations</h5><p><code>yarn backstage-cli migrate</code> — applies DB schema changes. NEVER skip this step.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">4</div><h5>Test in Staging</h5><p>Run <code>yarn tsc</code> + <code>yarn test</code>, deploy to staging. Test all plugins, templates, and auth flows. Fix issues BEFORE production.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">5</div><h5>Deploy in a Low-Traffic Window</h5><p><code>helm upgrade backstage backstage/backstage -n backstage</code>, timed to minimize impact. Monitor logs/metrics for 24 hours.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">6</div><h5>Keep a Rollback Plan Ready</h5><p>The previous image tag must be ready to redeploy immediately — a rollback plan is safety net, not an afterthought.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box danger mb-0"><h5>🔴 If Upgrade Fails — Rollback Procedure</h5><p class="mb-0">1. <code>helm rollback backstage</code> (revert K8s deployment). 2. Restore PostgreSQL from backup (if migrations corrupted data). 3. Check logs for the specific error. 4. Fix the issue in staging. 5. Retry. NEVER upgrade production without a tested rollback plan.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §26.6 (NEW — from Backstage.txt, no HTML equivalent existed) ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s26-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">26.6 Zero-Downtime Deployment Strategy</h3>
                <p>Rolling updates so users never see a gap. Readiness probes are what make this safe — neither piece alone is enough.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">Deployment — rolling update with a readiness gate</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:
  replicas: 3
  strategy:
    type: RollingUpdate
    rollingUpdate: { maxUnavailable: 0, maxSurge: 1 }
  template:
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: backstage
          readinessProbe:
            httpGet: { path: /healthcheck, port: 7007 }
            initialDelaySeconds: 10
            periodSeconds: 5

# maxUnavailable: 0 means the old pod is only killed AFTER the new one
# passes its readiness probe - zero requests are ever dropped.</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning mb-0"><h5>⚠️ Neither Piece Alone Is Enough</h5><p class="mb-0"><code>maxUnavailable: 0</code> without a <code>readinessProbe</code> does not guarantee zero dropped requests — Kubernetes has no way to know the new pod isn't actually ready to serve traffic yet without the probe telling it so.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §26.7 (NEW — from Backstage.txt, no HTML equivalent existed) ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s26-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">26.7 Disaster Recovery Runbook</h3>
                <p>What to do when production Backstage goes down hard. This is an ORDER of operations, not a wall of options.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container"><div class="diagram-title">DR Runbook — Follow in Order</div>
<pre>
  1. Confirm the blast radius: is it Backstage, or is PostgreSQL itself down?
  2. If DB is down: restore the latest automated PostgreSQL snapshot (§26.2)
  3. If Backstage pods are crash-looping: roll back to the last known-good
     image tag via ArgoCD/GitOps revert (Chapter 21) - do NOT hotfix in prod
  4. Re-run the upgrade helper diff (§26.5) if the crash follows a version bump
  5. Once healthy: verify Catalog count matches pre-incident baseline
  6. Write the incident timeline while it is fresh - feed it into Chapter 27 alerts
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box danger mb-0"><h5>🚨 Never Hotfix Production Directly</h5><p class="mb-0">Skipping this order and trying to fix a crash-looping deployment by hand — instead of rolling back via GitOps (Chapter 21) — is the single biggest DR mistake. Rollback first, investigate second.</p></div>
            </section>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch26-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — why RollingUpdate and readinessProbe must cooperate</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>The zero-downtime rollout in §26.6 only works because of how a RollingUpdate and a readinessProbe cooperate.</p>
                    <h5>① The Old Pod Waits for the New One to Prove Itself</h5>
                    <p>With <code>maxUnavailable: 0</code>, Kubernetes is not allowed to kill an old easypj pod until a new one has come up AND passed its readinessProbe against <code>/healthcheck</code> on port 7007.</p>
                    <h5>② The Service Keeps Routing to Old Pods Until Then</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
New pod starts → readinessProbe checks /healthcheck → NOT ready yet
                                                          │
                        Service routes 100% traffic to OLD pods (still up)
                                                          │
                        readinessProbe passes → new pod added to Service
                                                          │
                        ONLY NOW does maxUnavailable:0 allow an old pod to die
                    </pre>
                    <p>Until that probe returns healthy, the Service (§26.2's PostgreSQL-backed backend included) keeps routing 100% of traffic to the old, still-working pods. Skip the readinessProbe and <code>maxUnavailable: 0</code> buys you nothing — Kubernetes has no way to know the new pod isn't ready yet, so it may still drop requests.</p>
                    <div class="uth-callout mb-0"><strong>🔑 Two mechanisms, one guarantee:</strong> maxUnavailable:0 controls WHEN old pods die; readinessProbe controls WHETHER a new pod is trusted with traffic — you need both for the "zero dropped requests" promise to actually hold.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch26-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>The production checklist (§26.1) is the CONTRACT</strong> — database, auth, secrets, monitoring, backups, TLS and scaling all have to be real before you call it "production."</li>
                    <li><strong>PostgreSQL (§26.2) replaces SQLite</strong> because SQLite is a single local file — it cannot be shared safely by multiple replicas.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>pluginDivisionMode: database</code></strong> (the default) gives every plugin its own separate PostgreSQL database; set it to <code>schema</code> instead if every plugin should share one database via separate schemas.</li>
                    <li><strong>TechDocs (§26.3) and Search (§26.4) both need to move off their local defaults</strong> — external Docker-based builder + cloud storage for docs, Elasticsearch/OpenSearch instead of in-memory Lunr for search.</li>
                    <li><strong>Upgrades (§26.5) follow a fixed order:</strong> bump versions, read the upgrade helper diff, test in staging, then deploy in a low-traffic window with a rollback plan ready.</li>
                    <li><strong>Zero-downtime (§26.6) needs BOTH <code>maxUnavailable: 0</code> AND a real readinessProbe</strong> — neither one alone is enough.</li>
                    <li><strong>The DR runbook (§26.7) is an ORDER of operations, not a menu</strong> — confirm blast radius first, never hot-fix production directly.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch26-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Treating SQLite as "good enough for now" in production</strong> — it cannot be shared across replicas and has no real backup story (§26.1, §26.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Forgetting the readinessProbe when trying for zero-downtime</strong> — maxUnavailable: 0 without it does not guarantee no dropped requests (§26.6).</li>
                    <li><strong>Leaving TechDocs on its local/dev builder in production</strong> instead of switching to builder: external with a cloud publisher (§26.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Skipping the DR runbook order</strong> and trying to fix a crash-looping deployment by hand instead of rolling back via GitOps (§26.7).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch26-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Run the §26.1 checklist as a literal gate</strong> before any go-live — treat any unchecked row as a launch blocker.</li>
                    <li><strong>Always test an upgrade in staging</strong> with the upgrade helper diff BEFORE touching production (§26.5).</li>
                    <li><strong>Write the DR runbook (§26.7) BEFORE you need it</strong> — a 3 AM incident is the worst time to design a recovery order from scratch.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch26-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 26)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">DB + TechDocs + Search + upgrade + rollout + DR order</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">backend.database: { client: pg, connection: {...}, ssl.rejectUnauthorized }
techdocs: { builder: external, generator.runIn: docker, publisher.type: awsS3 }
search.collators.catalog.schedule.frequency.minutes: 10

# backstage-cli versions:bump              # bump all @backstage packages

spec.strategy: { type: RollingUpdate, rollingUpdate: { maxUnavailable: 0,
  maxSurge: 1 } }, readinessProbe: { httpGet: /healthcheck :7007 }

# DR order: confirm blast radius -> restore PG snapshot -> ArgoCD revert ->
#   re-check upgrade diff -> verify Catalog count -> write the timeline</code></pre>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch26-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 26 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of production database, TechDocs, search, and upgrades — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why must you use PostgreSQL (not SQLite) in production?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>SQLite can't handle concurrent writes — with 3+ Backstage replicas, you'd get data corruption. PostgreSQL supports concurrent connections and is the only supported production database for Backstage.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">production database config</span></div><pre><code class="language-yaml">backend:
  database:
    client: pg                          # ← PostgreSQL (NOT sqlite3!)
    connection:
      host: ${POSTGRES_HOST}            # ← RDS/Cloud SQL endpoint
      password: ${POSTGRES_PASSWORD}    # ← NEVER hardcode!</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>SQLite = single writer lock. 3 replicas = concurrent writes = corruption. This is the #1 production requirement.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What should the TechDocs builder be set to in production?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>builder: external</code> with <code>generator: { runIn: docker }</code> — docs are pre-built by CI/CD in an isolated Docker container and published to cloud storage. Backstage only serves them.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>builder</td><td>external</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>generator.runIn</td><td>docker</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>External builder = CI/CD builds docs, Backstage serves. Docker isolation keeps the build sandboxed. Keeps pods stateless.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the upgrade process for Backstage?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>backstage-cli versions:bump</code> → read the upgrade helper diff → backup PostgreSQL → run <code>yarn backstage-cli migrate</code> → test in staging → deploy with Helm upgrade in a low-traffic window.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">upgrade flow</span></div><pre><code class="language-bash">backstage-cli versions:bump
pg_dump backstage > backstage_backup_$(date +%Y%m%d).sql
yarn backstage-cli migrate
helm upgrade backstage backstage/backstage -n backstage</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>versions:bump updates all @backstage packages together; migrate handles DB schema changes. Always backup before major upgrades.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What items are on the production checklist for Backstage?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Database (PostgreSQL), Auth (real SSO), Secrets (externalized), Monitoring, Backups, TLS, Scaling (3+ replicas) — plus TechDocs cloud storage, production search, and centralized logging.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Core 7</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Database</td></tr><tr><td>Auth</td></tr><tr><td>Secrets</td></tr><tr><td>Monitoring</td></tr><tr><td>Backups</td></tr><tr><td>TLS</td></tr><tr><td>Scaling</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The checklist is the contract — every row must be real before calling something "production."</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why should you use Elasticsearch/OpenSearch for production search?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The default in-memory Lunr index doesn't scale and only works on a single pod. Elasticsearch/OpenSearch provides distributed full-text search, high availability, fast queries, and shared access for all Backstage replicas.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Search backend</th><th>Multi-pod safe?</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>In-memory Lunr (default)</td><td>❌ No</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Elasticsearch/OpenSearch</td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Lunr = single pod only, doesn't scale. Production requirement for multi-replica setups.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What does <code>yarn backstage-cli migrate</code> do, and how does it relate to <code>versions:bump</code>?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>migrate</code> applies database schema migrations that come with new Backstage versions. <code>versions:bump</code> updates the package versions first; <code>migrate</code> then updates PostgreSQL tables to match. Always backup before running either.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>versions:bump</td><td>Updates @backstage package versions</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>migrate</td><td>Applies DB schema changes</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Two distinct steps in the fixed upgrade order — bump comes before migrate.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you configure a managed PostgreSQL service for Backstage, including plugin database division?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>In app-config.yaml: <code>backend.database.client: pg</code>, connection details via env vars, SSL enabled, and <code>pluginDivisionMode: database</code> (default) or <code>schema</code>.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the config</span></div><pre><code class="language-yaml">backend:
  database:
    client: pg
    pluginDivisionMode: database</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>pluginDivisionMode controls whether plugins get separate databases (default) or share one via schemas.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Your Backstage upgrade failed and the app won't start. How do you recover?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>1. <code>helm rollback backstage</code> to previous version<br>2. Restore PostgreSQL from backup if migrations corrupted data<br>3. Check logs for the specific error<br>4. Fix the issue in staging before retrying.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Step</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>helm rollback</td></tr><tr><td>Restore DB backup if needed</td></tr><tr><td>Check logs</td></tr><tr><td>Fix in staging, retry</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Failed upgrade recovery follows the same "never hotfix production" principle as the §26.7 DR runbook.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
