            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch27-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — how Backstage's health story quietly shifted twice</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>Backstage's health story has quietly shifted twice: the older prom-client-based <code>/metrics</code> (or <code>/api/metrics</code>) endpoint is being phased out in favor of OpenTelemetry-based instrumentation, which exposes metrics on a SEPARATE port (<code>localhost:9464/metrics</code> by default) rather than under the main API.</p>
                    <h5>① /healthcheck Was Never a Default</h5>
                    <p>Similarly, <code>/healthcheck</code> is not a built-in route at all — the current default Root Health Service exposes readiness and liveness under <code>/.backstage/health/v1/readiness</code> and <code>/liveness</code>; <code>/healthcheck</code> only exists if a team registers it themselves as a custom backend route (as easypj does in §27.1's examples).</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
Assumed:   /healthcheck (default)         /metrics or /api/metrics :7007 (default)
Reality:   /.backstage/health/v1/*        localhost:9464/metrics (separate port)
           (readiness + liveness)          (OpenTelemetry-based)
                    </pre>
                    <p>Knowing which endpoint is actually wired up matters more than assuming the "obvious" one exists.</p>
                    <div class="uth-callout mb-0"><strong>🔑 Both shifts point the same direction:</strong> Backstage moved from ad-hoc/legacy conventions toward standardized, officially-supported mechanisms (Root Health Service, OpenTelemetry) — always check current docs rather than assuming an older tutorial's endpoint still applies.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch27-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong><code>/healthcheck</code> is NOT guaranteed by default</strong> — the built-in routes are <code>/.backstage/health/v1/readiness</code> and <code>/liveness</code> (§27.1).</li>
                    <li><strong>The officially recommended metrics path today is OpenTelemetry on a separate port (:9464),</strong> not the older /api/metrics (§27.1).</li>
                    <li><strong>Structured JSON logging (§27.2)</strong> is what makes shipping logs to Loki/ELK practical — Backstage itself just writes to stdout.</li>
                    <li><strong>Alertmanager rules (§27.3) should cover at minimum:</strong> Backstage down, high error rate, and stalled catalog ingestion.</li>
                    <li><strong>Usage analytics (§27.4)</strong> — weekly active users, most-viewed entities, template runs, search queries — answer a DIFFERENT question than uptime: is anyone actually using this platform?</li>
                    <li><strong>Readiness and liveness are different questions</strong> — "can it take traffic" vs "is it alive at all."</li>
                    <li><strong>Monitoring and analytics are not the same thing</strong> — you need both, for different reasons.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

