                    <div class="eq-question">easypj's catalog silently stops ingesting new GitHub repos for 20 minutes, but no alert fires because the team only alerts on HTTP 5xx rates. What alert is missing?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A dedicated "catalog ingestion stalled" alert (failed &gt;5 min → critical) — HTTP error-rate alerts don't catch a silently stale catalog, which is its own distinct production failure mode.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Alert type</th><th>Catches stale ingestion?</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>HTTP 5xx rate</td><td>❌ No</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Catalog ingestion stalled (&gt;5 min)</td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§27's Speed Tip — "alert on catalog ingestion failures, not just HTTP errors" is explicitly called out as often-missed.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">easypj's Backstage uptime has been 99.99% all quarter, but leadership asks "is anyone actually using this thing?" Which dashboard answers that, and why doesn't the uptime dashboard?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The usage-analytics dashboard (weekly active users, template executions, search queries) — uptime measures whether the service is available, not whether anyone is actually opening it. These are genuinely different questions requiring different metrics.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Answered by</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>"Is it up?"</td><td>Uptime/monitoring dashboard</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>"Is anyone using it?"</td><td>Usage-analytics dashboard</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§27's Gotcha — "treating monitoring and analytics as the same thing" is explicitly named; this scenario is exactly why they're distinct.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write the Alertmanager rule that pages on-call after Backstage has been down for 2 minutes.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the rule</span></div><pre><code class="language-yaml">groups:
  - name: backstage
    rules:
      - alert: BackstageDown
        expr: up{job='backstage'} == 0
        for: 2m
        labels: { severity: critical }
        annotations: { summary: 'Backstage is down' }</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§27.3's exact real rule — the `for: 2m` clause prevents alerting on a brief blip.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A junior engineer sets backend.logger.level to debug in the production app-config.yaml "just to see more detail while investigating an issue," and forgets to revert it. What are the 2 real risks?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Excessive log volume (cost and noise) and potential exposure of sensitive data that debug-level logging may include but info-level would not.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Risk</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Excessive log volume</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Sensitive data exposure</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§27.2's explicit warning — "NEVER use debug in production."</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Design the minimum observability setup for easypj using the 4 pillars from this chapter, naming the correct CURRENT endpoint/tool for each.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Pillar</th><th>Current tool/endpoint</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Health</td><td>/.backstage/health/v1/readiness + /liveness</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Metrics</td><td>OpenTelemetry, localhost:9464/metrics</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Logs</td><td>backend.logger: { level: info, format: json } → Loki</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Alerts</td><td>Alertmanager rule (BackstageDown, for: 2m)</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>All 4 pillars named with their CURRENT, corrected endpoints — the whole point of this chapter's fact-checking pass.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

