            <section class="section-block" id="b-s28-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">28.1 Backstage REST API Overview</h3>
                <p>Base URL: <code>http://localhost:7007/api/</code> (dev) or <code>https://backstage.easypj.com/api/</code> (prod). All API calls require authentication via a <strong>Bearer token</strong> in the Authorization header.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table"><table><thead><tr><th>API Category</th><th>Base Path</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Key Endpoints</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Catalog</strong></td><td><code>/api/catalog</code></td><td>Query + register entities</td><td><code>/entities</code>, <code>/locations</code>, <code>/entity-facets</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Scaffolder</strong></td><td><code>/api/scaffolder/v2</code></td><td>Execute templates</td><td><code>/tasks</code>, <code>/tasks/:id</code>, <code>/tasks/:id/eventstream</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Search</strong></td><td><code>/api/search</code></td><td>Query search index</td><td><code>/query</code> — accepts <code>?term=</code> and filter params</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>TechDocs</strong></td><td><code>/api/techdocs</code></td><td>Access documentation</td><td><code>/metadata/:kind/:ns/:name</code>, <code>/static/docs/:ns/:kind/:name</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Auth</strong></td><td><code>/api/auth</code></td><td>Token management</td><td><code>/:provider/start</code>, <code>/:provider/refresh</code></td></tr>
                </tbody></table></div>
                <p><strong>Authentication:</strong> All API calls require <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code>. Tokens come from the auth provider (e.g., GitHub OAuth) for user-driven calls. For service-to-service automation, use a Backstage-generated service token configured in <code>app-config.yaml</code> under <code>backend.auth.externalAccess</code> (§28.4).</p>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §28.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s28-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">28.2 Catalog API — Query &amp; Register Entities</h3>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Correction — There Is No Direct POST /api/catalog/entities</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">The official Catalog API docs list <strong>no direct create route</strong>. Entities are always created by registering a <strong>Location</strong> (<code>POST /api/catalog/locations</code>) that points at a catalog-info.yaml — never by POSTing an entity JSON body directly to <code>/entities</code>. This ensures every entity goes through the same validation/processing pipeline (Chapter 7) regardless of how it arrived.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container"><div class="diagram-title">Catalog API — Real Endpoints</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                         CATALOG REST API                                   │
  │  Base: /api/catalog                                                       │
  │                                                                          │
  │  📖 READ (GET)                                                            │
  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
  │  │ GET /entities                     → List all entities (+ filters)   │  │
  │  │ GET /entities/by-name/:k/:ns/:n   → Get entity by kind/ns/name     │  │
  │  │ GET /entity-facets                → Aggregate counts by kind/life..│  │
  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ✏️ REGISTER (POST) — via Location, NOT a direct entity create           │
  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
  │  │ POST /locations                   → Register URL/file location      │  │
  │  │   (Backstage then reads + validates + creates the entity)           │  │
  │  │   ❌ POST /entities does NOT exist — a common false assumption      │  │
  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
  │                                                                          │
  │  🗑️ DELETE (DELETE)                                                       │
  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
  │  │ DELETE /entities/by-uid/:uid      → Remove entity (needs permission)│  │
  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
  │                                                                          │
  │  🔄 REFRESH (POST)                                                        │
  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
  │  │ POST /refresh                     → Force re-read all locations     │  │
  │  │ POST /locations/:id               → Refresh single location         │  │
  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">Catalog API — real curl examples</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># List all entities
curl localhost:7007/api/catalog/entities

# Get one specific entity
curl localhost:7007/api/catalog/entities/by-name/component/default/jobpost

# Register a new entity via a Location (catalog has NO direct
# POST /entities endpoint — see the software-catalog-api docs)
curl -X POST localhost:7007/api/catalog/locations \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"type":"url","target":"https://github.com/org/repo/blob/main/catalog-info.yaml"}'

# Delete an entity by uid
curl -X DELETE localhost:7007/api/catalog/entities/by-uid/abc-123</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight" id="b-s28-2a"><h5>📖 List &amp; Get</h5><p><code>GET /entities</code> with <code>?filter=kind=Component,spec.lifecycle=production</code>. Get single: <code>/entities/by-name/:kind/:namespace/:name</code>.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card" id="b-s28-2b"><h5>✏️ Register &amp; Update</h5><p><code>POST /locations</code> (register URL — the only way to create) — GitOps style. <code>DELETE /entities/by-uid/:uid</code> removes; updates happen by editing catalog-info.yaml in Git and letting the location re-sync.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight" id="b-s28-2c"><h5>🗑️ Delete &amp; Refresh</h5><p><code>DELETE /entities/by-uid/:uid</code> (needs delete permission). <code>POST /refresh</code> (force re-read all). <code>POST /locations/:id</code> (refresh one).</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card" id="b-s28-2d"><h5>📊 Facets &amp; Stats</h5><p><code>GET /entity-facets</code> returns <code>{"kind":[{"value":"Component","count":42}]}</code>. Build dashboards from aggregate counts.</p></div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §28.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s28-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">28.3 Scaffolder REST API — Templates as Code</h3>
                <p>The Scaffolder API lets you trigger software templates from any system — CI/CD pipelines, Slack bots, internal CLIs, or even scheduled jobs.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container"><div class="diagram-title">Scaffolder API — Execute &amp; Track Templates</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                      SCAFFOLDER v2 API FLOW                                │
  │                                                                          │
  │  1. SUBMIT TASK                                                           │
  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
  │  │ POST /api/scaffolder/v2/tasks                                       │  │
  │  │ Body: { templateRef: "template:default/django-service",             │  │
  │  │         values: { name: "my-svc", owner: "team-plat" } }            │  │
  │  │ Response: { "id": "abc-123", "status": "open" }                     │  │
  │  └────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────┘  │
  │                                   │                                      │
  │  2. TRACK PROGRESS                ▼                                      │
  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
  │  │ GET /api/scaffolder/v2/tasks/abc-123                                │  │
  │  │ Response: { "status": "processing" | "completed" | "failed" }       │  │
  │  └────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────┘  │
  │                                   │                                      │
  │  3. STREAM LOGS (SSE)             ▼                                      │
  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
  │  │ GET /api/scaffolder/v2/tasks/abc-123/eventstream                    │  │
  │  │ → Server-Sent Events — real-time step-by-step log output            │  │
  │  │ → event: log | data: "Fetching template skeleton..."                │  │
  │  │ → event: complete | data: "Template execution finished"             │  │
  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">Scaffolder API — real curl examples</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Trigger a template run
curl -X POST localhost:7007/api/scaffolder/v2/tasks \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"templateRef":"template:default/django-service","values":{"name":"new-svc"}}'

# Poll the task status
curl localhost:7007/api/scaffolder/v2/tasks/a1b2c3d4-...

# Stream live task events (build logs as they happen) — note the -N flag
curl -N localhost:7007/api/scaffolder/v2/tasks/a1b2c3d4-.../eventstream</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight" id="b-s28-3a"><h5>🚀 Execute a Template</h5><p><code>POST /api/scaffolder/v2/tasks</code>. Body: <code>templateRef</code> + <code>values</code>. Returns <code>taskId</code>. Async — template runs in background.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card" id="b-s28-3b"><h5>📊 Track Task Status</h5><p><code>GET /tasks/:taskId</code> → status (open/processing/completed/failed). <code>GET /tasks/:taskId/eventstream</code> → SSE real-time logs (use <code>curl -N</code> to disable buffering).</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note mb-0"><h5>🔑 CBA Key Concept — Scaffolder v2 (Not v1!)</h5><p class="mb-0">The CBA exam tests on <strong>v2</strong> (<code>/api/scaffolder/v2/</code>). v1 is deprecated. v2 introduced async task-based execution: submit a task → get taskId → poll/stream for results. This is a critical architectural improvement because templates can take minutes to run. Always reference v2 endpoints in exam answers.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §28.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s28-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">28.4 Automation Examples — CI/CD &amp; Beyond</h3>
                <p>Here are real-world examples you can use in your pipelines. Automation is not a separate mechanism — it is the exact same API calls a human makes, just carrying a Bearer token instead of a browser session.</p>
                <div id="b-s28-4a"><h4>Auto-Register a Service in CI/CD — via Location, corrected</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-container"><pre><code class="language-bash"># Register a new entity via CI/CD pipeline (register a Location —
# there is no direct POST /entities create endpoint)
curl -X POST \
  https://backstage.easypj.com/api/catalog/locations \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $BACKSTAGE_TOKEN" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "type": "url",
    "target": "https://github.com/easypj/new-service/blob/main/catalog-info.yaml"
  }'</code></pre></div>
                </div>
                <div id="b-s28-4b"><h4>Trigger a Template from CI/CD</h4><div class="diagram-container"><pre><code class="language-bash"># Trigger a Django service template from CI/CD
curl -X POST \
  https://backstage.easypj.com/api/scaffolder/v2/tasks \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $BACKSTAGE_TOKEN" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "templateRef": "template:default/django-service",
    "values": {
      "name": "new-service",
      "owner": "team-platform",
      "description": "Created via CI pipeline"
    }
  }'</code></pre></div></div>
                <div id="b-s28-4c"><h4>Sync External Data into Catalog</h4><p>Use the API to push data from external sources (CMDB, asset management, spreadsheets) into Backstage's catalog. A scheduled script pulls data from an internal CMDB and writes catalog-info.yaml files (or registers Locations pointing at generated files) on a cron schedule — this is how organizations bridge legacy inventory systems into Backstage without a native EntityProvider.</p></div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">app-config.yaml — the real static CI/CD token config</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># How the CI/CD static token above is actually configured on the backend
backend:
  auth:
    externalAccess:
      - type: static
        options:
          token: ${CICD_TOKEN}
          subject: cicd-system
          accessRestrictions:
            - plugin: catalog
            - plugin: scaffolder</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning mb-0"><h5>⚠️ Always Scope CI/CD Tokens</h5><p class="mb-0"><code>accessRestrictions</code> narrows the token to only the plugins it actually needs (here: catalog + scaffolder). A leaked, unscoped token could call every plugin — always scope narrowly.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §28.5 (NEW — from Backstage.txt, no HTML equivalent existed) ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s28-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">28.5 Webhooks and Event-Driven Automation</h3>
                <p>Reacting to Backstage events instead of polling the API on a timer. There is <strong>no global <code>catalogEventBus</code></strong> API in Backstage — event-driven automation is built on the <code>EventsService</code> instead.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">The real event-driven mechanism</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-typescript">// Event-driven automation is built on the EventsService
// (@backstage/plugin-events-node, ref: eventsServiceRef),
// which supports publish() and subscribe() per topic.

// External systems can push events in over HTTP with no custom bus:
//   POST /api/events/http/{topic}  (see @backstage/plugin-events-backend)

// The signals plugin (real-time UI push) needs NO experimentalFeatures
// flag — once @backstage/plugin-signals(-backend) is installed it is
// auto-discovered, no extra config required.</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><h5>📡 EventsService</h5><p><code>publish()</code> and <code>subscribe()</code> per topic. This is the real mechanism — not a global event bus.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>🔌 External Push</h5><p><code>POST /api/events/http/{topic}</code> lets external systems push events in without a custom bus.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip mb-0"><h5>💡 Reach for Events Before Building a Polling Loop</h5><p class="mb-0">Webhooks/events are cheaper and faster to react to than polling the API on a timer — reach for §28.5's mechanism before writing a polling loop against §28.2's or §28.3's endpoints.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §28.6 (NEW — from Backstage.txt, no HTML equivalent existed) ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s28-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">28.6 Building a Custom CLI Tool Against the Backstage API</h3>
                <p>Wrapping the REST API in a small internal CLI. A custom CLI is just a thin wrapper — same endpoints, same token, one command instead of a multi-line curl.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">easypj-cli.js — a one-line "register this repo" command</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-javascript">#!/usr/bin/env node
// easypj-cli register <repo-url> --owner team-platform
const [,, cmd, repoUrl, , owner] = process.argv;
if (cmd === "register") {
  await fetch("https://backstage.easypj.com/api/catalog/locations", {
    method: "POST",
    headers: { Authorization: "Bearer " + process.env.BACKSTAGE_TOKEN,
               "Content-Type": "application/json" },
    body: JSON.stringify({ type: "url", target: repoUrl + "/catalog-info.yaml" }),
  });
  console.log("Registered " + repoUrl + " (owner: " + owner + ")");
}</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note mb-0"><h5>🔑 Not New Power, Just Convenience</h5><p class="mb-0">This CLI calls the exact same <code>POST /api/catalog/locations</code> endpoint from §28.2/§28.4 — a teammate types <code>easypj-cli register &lt;url&gt;</code> instead of remembering a multi-line curl incantation. Build one once more than one teammate needs to run the same command.</p></div>
            </section>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch28-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — why there's no direct create endpoint, and why automation is "the same calls, plus a token"</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>Every one of these five APIs sits behind the same Backstage backend (port 7007 in dev).</p>
                    <h5>① The Catalog API Has No Direct Create Endpoint On Purpose</h5>
                    <p>Creation always flows through registering a Location that points at a catalog-info.yaml, so the same validation/processing pipeline from Chapter 7 runs no matter how the entity arrived.</p>
                    <h5>② Scaffolder Is Task-Based, Not Synchronous</h5>
                    <p>POST creates a task, and you poll its taskId or stream its eventstream for progress, because template runs can take minutes.</p>
                    <h5>③ Automation Is Not a Separate Mechanism</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
Human clicks UI  →  same REST endpoints, browser session
CI/CD calls API  →  same REST endpoints, Bearer token instead

  Both paths hit the identical validation pipeline — no shortcut, no bypass.
                    </pre>
                    <p>It is the exact same API calls a human makes, just carrying a Bearer token instead of a browser session, scoped narrowly via <code>backend.auth.externalAccess.accessRestrictions</code> so a leaked token cannot call every plugin. Webhooks and events (§28.5) flip the model from polling to push, built on the EventsService's <code>publish()</code>/<code>subscribe()</code> rather than any global "catalogEventBus." And the CLI in §28.6 is nothing more than a thin wrapper calling <code>fetch()</code> against those same endpoints — convenience, not new power.</p>
                    <div class="uth-callout mb-0"><strong>🔑 One truth, many doors:</strong> the entity page, the CLI, the CI/CD pipeline, and a chat bot are all just different front doors onto the identical set of REST endpoints and their identical validation rules.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch28-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Five REST APIs</strong> — Catalog, Scaffolder, Search, TechDocs, Auth — cover everything the Backstage UI can do (§28.1).</li>
                    <li><strong>There is no direct <code>POST /api/catalog/entities</code></strong> — entities are created by registering a Location that points at a catalog-info.yaml (§28.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>The Scaffolder API is task-based:</strong> POST creates a task, then you poll <code>/tasks/:taskId</code> or stream <code>/eventstream</code> for progress (§28.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Automation (§28.4) is the same API calls a human makes,</strong> just with a Bearer token — scope that token narrowly with <code>backend.auth.externalAccess.accessRestrictions</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Event-driven automation (§28.5)</strong> is built on the EventsService (publish()/subscribe()), and external systems can push events in over <code>POST /api/events/http/{topic}</code> with no custom bus.</li>
                    <li><strong>A custom CLI (§28.6) is just a thin wrapper</strong> — same endpoints, same token, one command instead of a multi-line curl.</li>
                    <li><strong>The signals plugin auto-discovers</strong> once installed — no experimentalFeatures flag required.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch28-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Assuming a first-party <code>POST /api/catalog/entities</code> exists</strong> — it does not; register a Location instead (§28.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Forgetting the Scaffolder API is asynchronous</strong> — a single POST does not return the finished result; you must poll the taskId or stream its events (§28.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Giving a CI/CD static token unrestricted access</strong> instead of scoping it with accessRestrictions to only the plugins it actually needs (§28.4).</li>
                    <li><strong>Thinking event-driven automation needs an experimentalFeatures flag</strong> — the signals plugin auto-discovers once installed, no flag required (§28.5).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

