            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch28-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 28 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the 5 REST APIs, the Catalog/Scaffolder APIs, and automation — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you list all entities via the Catalog API?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>GET /api/catalog/entities</code>. Returns JSON array of all entities. Supports filtering: <code>?filter=kind=Component,spec.lifecycle=production</code>.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">list + filter</span></div><pre><code class="language-bash">curl "http://localhost:7007/api/catalog/entities?filter=kind=Component,spec.lifecycle=production"</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The <code>filter</code> query parameter uses Backstage's entity filter syntax. This is the most common API call for building automation scripts.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you create a new entity via the Catalog API?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>There is <strong>no direct create endpoint</strong>. You register a <strong>Location</strong> instead: <code>POST /api/catalog/locations</code> with a URL/file path pointing at a catalog-info.yaml — Backstage then ingests and processes it through the same validation pipeline as any other entity.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Exists?</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>POST /api/catalog/entities</td><td>❌ Does not exist</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>POST /api/catalog/locations</td><td>✅ The only real way</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the single most-corrected fact in this chapter. Registering a Location is "pull-based" — Backstage reads and validates from the source, following GitOps: the catalog-info.yaml lives in Git and Backstage auto-discovers it.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why doesn't Backstage offer a direct entity-create endpoint?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>So that every entity — regardless of how it arrived — goes through the identical validation/processing pipeline (the same one from Chapter 7) that Location-based ingestion always runs. A direct create endpoint would let entities bypass that pipeline.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>Runs Ch7's validation pipeline?</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>A hypothetical direct create</td><td>❌ Could bypass it</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Register a Location (real)</td><td>✅ Always runs it</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Think of it like a librarian who only accepts new books through the intake desk (Locations), never handed directly by a patron — one path, one set of checks, no matter the source.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What endpoint refreshes the entire catalog? When should you use it?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>POST /api/catalog/refresh</code>. Use after adding/updating catalog locations or after changes to catalog-info.yaml files in Git. Forces immediate re-processing. For a single location, use <code>POST /api/catalog/locations/:id</code> instead.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Endpoint</th><th>Scope</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>POST /refresh</td><td>ALL locations — expensive on large catalogs</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>POST /locations/:id</td><td>One location — cheaper, preferred</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>By default, Backstage refreshes periodically. <code>POST /refresh</code> forces immediate ingestion of ALL locations — expensive on large catalogs, so prefer the single-location refresh when possible.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you execute a software template via the Scaffolder API?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>POST /api/scaffolder/v2/tasks</code> with a JSON body containing <code>templateRef</code> (e.g., <code>"template:default/django-service"</code>) and <code>values</code>. Returns a <code>taskId</code>. Track progress with <code>GET /api/scaffolder/v2/tasks/:taskId</code>.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">execute + poll</span></div><pre><code class="language-bash">curl -X POST -d '{"templateRef":"template:default/django-service","values":{"name":"my-service"}}' \
  http://localhost:7007/api/scaffolder/v2/tasks
curl http://localhost:7007/api/scaffolder/v2/tasks/abc-123</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Async: returns immediately with a taskId. Always check task status before assuming success.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you authenticate Backstage API calls for CI/CD automation?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Configure a static token under <code>backend.auth.externalAccess</code> in app-config.yaml, scoped with <code>accessRestrictions</code> to only the plugins it needs. Never share user OAuth tokens in scripts.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">scoped static token</span></div><pre><code class="language-yaml">backend:
  auth:
    externalAccess:
      - type: static
        options: { token: ${CICD_TOKEN}, subject: cicd-system,
          accessRestrictions: [{plugin: catalog}, {plugin: scaffolder}] }</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Static tokens bypass OAuth and are scoped narrowly via accessRestrictions — ideal for pipelines.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What are the 5 main API categories in Backstage?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>1. <strong>Catalog</strong> (<code>/api/catalog</code>). 2. <strong>Scaffolder</strong> (<code>/api/scaffolder/v2</code>). 3. <strong>Search</strong> (<code>/api/search</code>). 4. <strong>TechDocs</strong> (<code>/api/techdocs</code>). 5. <strong>Auth</strong> (<code>/api/auth</code>).</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>API</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>1</td><td>Catalog</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Scaffolder</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Search</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Remember: Scaffolder uses /v2/ (not v1!), and Search is /query (not /search — that's the UI route).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you get entity counts grouped by kind (facets)?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>GET /api/catalog/entity-facets</code>. Returns JSON with aggregate counts: <code>"kind":[{"value":"Component","count":42}]</code>. Also supports faceting by <code>spec.lifecycle</code>, <code>spec.owner</code>, <code>metadata.namespace</code>.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Facet by</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>kind</td></tr><tr><td>spec.lifecycle</td></tr><tr><td>spec.owner</td></tr><tr><td>metadata.namespace</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Facets are pre-computed aggregates — fast even on 10,000+ entity catalogs. Doesn't return entity details, just counts.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you track the status of a Scaffolder task in real time?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>GET /api/scaffolder/v2/tasks/:taskId/eventstream</code> (SSE — Server-Sent Events). For a simple poll, use <code>GET /api/scaffolder/v2/tasks/:taskId</code> every 2-3 seconds instead.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">streaming with -N</span></div><pre><code class="language-bash">curl -N http://localhost:7007/api/scaffolder/v2/tasks/abc-123/eventstream</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Task lifecycle: open → processing → completed/failed. -N disables curl's output buffering for live streams.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between Scaffolder API v1 and v2?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>v2 (<code>/api/scaffolder/v2/</code>) is current, task-based async execution with event streaming. v1 is deprecated and used a synchronous "job" model. The CBA exam tests on v2 — any answer mentioning v1 is a distractor.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Version</th><th>Model</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>v1 (deprecated)</td><td>Synchronous "job"</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>v2 (current)</td><td>Async task + poll/stream</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Critical architectural improvement since templates can take minutes to run.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch28-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 28 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (8)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Real workplace situations — 8 scenarios mirroring how the CBA exam actually asks.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A new engineer writes a CI/CD script that POSTs a full entity JSON body directly to <code>/api/catalog/entities</code>, based on an old blog post. What happens?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The request fails — that endpoint does not exist. The script needs to register a Location instead (<code>POST /api/catalog/locations</code>) pointing at a catalog-info.yaml, letting Backstage ingest and validate it through the normal pipeline.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Endpoint used</th><th>Exists?</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>POST /api/catalog/entities</td><td>❌ No</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>POST /api/catalog/locations</td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§28.2's core correction — this is the single most important fact-check in the chapter.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">easypj's CI pipeline triggers a Scaffolder template and immediately assumes success because the POST returned HTTP 200. Is this a safe assumption?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>No — a 200 from <code>POST /tasks</code> only means the task was accepted (status "open"), not that it succeeded. The pipeline must poll <code>GET /tasks/:taskId</code> (or stream <code>/eventstream</code>) and check for "completed" before declaring success.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Response</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>200 + status: "open"</td><td>Task accepted, NOT finished</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Polled status: "completed"</td><td>Actually succeeded</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§28's Gotcha — "forgetting the Scaffolder API is asynchronous" is named explicitly as a common mistake.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A platform team configures a CI/CD static token with no accessRestrictions at all, giving it full access to every plugin. What's the risk, concretely?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>If that token leaks (e.g., checked into a repo, exposed in a log), an attacker gets full API access to every Backstage plugin, not just catalog/scaffolder — accessRestrictions exists precisely to cap the blast radius of a leaked token.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Token config</th><th>Blast radius if leaked</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>No accessRestrictions</td><td>Every plugin</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>accessRestrictions: [catalog, scaffolder]</td><td>Only those 2 plugins</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§28.4's Gotcha — "giving a CI/CD static token unrestricted access" is named explicitly as a real risk.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A developer wants to notify a Slack channel the instant a new Component is registered, and starts writing a script that polls <code>GET /entities</code> every 10 seconds. Is there a better approach?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Yes — use the EventsService instead of polling: subscribe to the relevant topic (or have the registering system push via <code>POST /api/events/http/{topic}</code>) and react when the event fires, rather than repeatedly querying the API on a timer.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Efficiency</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Poll GET /entities every 10s</td><td>❌ Wasteful, laggy</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>EventsService subscribe()</td><td>✅ Reacts instantly, cheap</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§28's Speed Tip — "reach for webhooks/events before building a polling loop" is explicitly recommended.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A teammate assumes enabling the signals plugin (real-time UI push) requires flipping an experimentalFeatures flag in app-config.yaml. Correct or not?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Not correct — once <code>@backstage/plugin-signals(-backend)</code> is installed, it is auto-discovered with no extra config required. No experimentalFeatures flag is needed.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Required?</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Install @backstage/plugin-signals(-backend)</td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Set an experimentalFeatures flag</td><td>❌ No</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§28.5's Gotcha — this exact misconception is named explicitly.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">easypj's 5 platform engineers each independently write their own multi-line curl command to register a new repo, and they occasionally get the JSON body wrong. What's the recommended fix?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Wrap the repeated curl command in a small internal CLI (§28.6) — e.g. <code>easypj-cli register &lt;repo-url&gt;</code> — once more than one teammate needs to run the same command, so everyone gets the correct call automatically.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Before</th><th>After</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>5 hand-written curl commands</td><td>1 shared <code>easypj-cli register</code></td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§28's Speed Tip — "wrap repeated curl incantations in a tiny internal CLI" is explicitly recommended for exactly this scenario.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write the curl command an external system would use to push a custom event into Backstage's "deployment-completed" topic, without any custom event bus.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the push</span></div><pre><code class="language-bash">curl -X POST https://backstage.easypj.com/api/events/http/deployment-completed \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{...}'</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§28.5 — <code>POST /api/events/http/{topic}</code> is the real, documented mechanism, backed by <code>@backstage/plugin-events-backend</code>.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Design the minimum backend.auth.externalAccess block for a CI/CD token that should ONLY be able to register Locations and trigger Scaffolder templates.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the config</span></div><pre><code class="language-yaml">backend:
  auth:
    externalAccess:
      - type: static
        options:
          token: ${CICD_TOKEN}
          subject: cicd-system
          accessRestrictions:
            - plugin: catalog
            - plugin: scaffolder</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§28.4's exact real config — the accessRestrictions array is the whole point of the scoping.</p></div>
                    </details>
