            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch29-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — why stateless scaling is trivial, and why caching is a separate lever</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>Backstage scales the same way any stateless web backend does: because session state lives in the JWT and not in pod memory (§29.2), any replica can answer any request, which is what makes adding a fourth or fifth pod trivial.</p>
                    <h5>① HA Is "More of the Same, Spread Out"</h5>
                    <p>High availability (§29.3) is really "more of the same, spread out" — more replicas across more zones, plus a database layer (PostgreSQL primary + read replica) that can survive a zone going down.</p>
                    <h5>② Caching Is a Different Kind of Lever Entirely</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
Horizontal scaling  →  ADDS capacity (more pods to share the same work)
Caching             →  REMOVES repeated work (same lookup answered once, reused)

  Neither substitutes for the other — a caching problem needs caching,
  not more replicas; an under-provisioned DB pool needs pool tuning,
  not more caching.
                    </pre>
                    <p>Caching (§29.4) doesn't add capacity, it removes repeated work — the same session check, the same catalog lookup — so the capacity you already have goes further. Load testing (§29.5) is how you find out whether §29.2-29.4 actually worked before real users do, and capacity planning (§29.6) is how you avoid needing any of this as an emergency in the first place.</p>
                    <div class="uth-callout mb-0"><strong>🔑 Diagnose, then pull exactly one lever:</strong> §29.1's table exists precisely because scaling, caching, and HA solve three genuinely different problems, and pulling the wrong one wastes time without fixing anything.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch29-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Diagnose the bottleneck first (§29.1)</strong> — horizontal scaling never fixes a caching problem, and caching never fixes an under-provisioned DB connection pool.</li>
                    <li><strong>Horizontal scaling (§29.2) works because Backstage is stateless</strong> — session state lives in the JWT, not pod memory.</li>
                    <li><strong>Service separation</strong> — splitting the backend by plugin behind a custom DiscoveryService — is a more advanced lever than replicas; reach for it only after replicas and caching are already maxed out.</li>
                    <li><strong>High availability (§29.3) means 3+ replicas across 2+ zones,</strong> PostgreSQL with automated failover, and a tested DR restore drill.</li>
                    <li><strong>Redis caching (§29.4) covers session tokens, catalog lookups, and plugin-level API responses</strong> — configured once under <code>backend.cache</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Load test with k6 (§29.5) BEFORE go-live,</strong> and use the capacity-planning table (§29.6) to size replicas/DB/cache to your actual org size.</li>
                    <li><strong>This chapter maps to Infrastructure 22%,</strong> not Customizing 32% — a domain-tag correction from the original ported content.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

