            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 29 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of scaling, HA, caching, and capacity planning — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why can Backstage be horizontally scaled across multiple replicas?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Backstage pods are <strong>stateless</strong> — session state lives in the JWT, not pod memory. All real state lives in external services: PostgreSQL (catalog, users) and cloud storage (TechDocs). Any pod can handle any request.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Where session lives</th><th>Enables replicas?</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Pod memory</td><td>❌ No — breaks interchangeability</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>JWT (client-held)</td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Every pod is interchangeable — no sticky sessions needed. This is why Kubernetes HPA works seamlessly with Backstage.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What Kubernetes resource enables automatic scaling of Backstage pods?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA)</strong>. Configure with: <code>kubectl autoscale deployment backstage --cpu-percent=70 --min=3 --max=10</code>. HPA monitors CPU/memory metrics and automatically adds/removes pods based on load.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the command</span></div><pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl autoscale deployment backstage --cpu-percent=70 --min=3 --max=10</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>HPA works because Backstage is stateless. Minimum 3 replicas ensures HA (survive 1 pod failure).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What makes a Backstage deployment truly highly available, beyond just running multiple pods?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>3+ replicas across 2+ availability zones, PostgreSQL with streaming replication + automatic failover, cloud storage with cross-region replication for TechDocs, and a tested DR restore drill.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>HA requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Backstage pods</td><td>3+ replicas, 2+ zones</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>PostgreSQL</td><td>Primary + replica, auto-failover</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Multiple Backstage pods alone is NOT sufficient — a single PostgreSQL instance is still a single point of failure regardless of pod count.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What caching options are available for Backstage? Which is most impactful?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Redis (backend cache — most impactful, 60-80% DB load reduction), CDN (static assets + TechDocs), Service Worker (frontend JS/CSS), PostgreSQL shared_buffers, Catalog in-memory cache. <strong>Redis is the most impactful</strong> for API performance.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Impact</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Redis</td><td>🔽 60-80% DB load</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>CDN</td><td>Faster static/TechDocs delivery</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Redis sits between the Backstage backend and PostgreSQL — cache hits skip the database entirely.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you configure Redis caching in Backstage?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>In <code>app-config.yaml</code>, set <code>backend.cache.store: redis</code> and <code>backend.cache.connection: redis://redis-service:6379</code>. Optional: <code>backend.cache.defaultTtl: 300</code> (5 minutes).</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the config</span></div><pre><code class="language-yaml">backend:
  cache:
    store: redis
    connection: redis://redis-service:6379</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Always use <code>redis</code> (shared) in multi-pod deployments, never <code>memory</code> (per-pod, inconsistent).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the "service separation" scaling lever, and when should you reach for it?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Splitting the backend into multiple services, each running a different set of plugins, routed via a custom <code>DiscoveryService</code> implementation. Reach for it only AFTER simple horizontal scaling and caching are already maxed out — it's more complex than adding replicas.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Lever</th><th>Complexity</th><th>Try first?</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Add replicas</td><td>Low</td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Service separation</td><td>High</td><td>❌ Only after replicas+caching maxed</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>An advanced lever per the official scaling docs — not the default first move.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What are the 5 performance optimization layers in Backstage?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>1. <strong>Frontend:</strong> Code splitting, lazy loading. 2. <strong>Backend API:</strong> Query optimization, connection pooling. 3. <strong>Catalog Ingestion:</strong> Processing interval tuning, scoped provider scans. 4. <strong>Search:</strong> Off-peak reindexing, collator batch caps. 5. <strong>TechDocs:</strong> External build storage, CDN, incremental builds.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Layer</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Frontend</td></tr><tr><td>Backend API</td></tr><tr><td>Catalog</td></tr><tr><td>Search</td></tr><tr><td>TechDocs</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Match the problem to the solution — a frontend bottleneck needs a different fix than a catalog bottleneck.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why does Backstage's stateless design matter for scaling?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Because ANY pod can serve ANY request, enabling true horizontal scaling without sticky sessions. Compare with a stateful app that keeps local state per instance — you can't just add more of those.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">scaling is trivial</span></div><pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl scale deployment backstage --replicas=5</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>No session affinity needed — the load balancer can use plain round-robin.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the disaster recovery plan for a highly available Backstage?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Since all config is in Git (GitOps), redeploy to a new region in &lt;30 minutes. Nightly PostgreSQL backup (retain 30 days) with a tested restore drill. Cloud storage (TechDocs) should use cross-region replication.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Step</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Create new K8s cluster</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Restore PostgreSQL from backup</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>kubectl apply Backstage manifests</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Cross-region cloud storage replication means TechDocs data is already in the backup region — no separate restore needed.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you prove your sizing before real traffic finds the limit?</div>
