            <section class="section-block" id="b-s30-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">30.1 Production Security Checklist</h3>
                <div class="comparison-table"><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Check</th><th>Requirement</th><th>Priority</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                    <tr><td>1</td><td>HTTPS Everywhere</td><td>TLS 1.2+ on all endpoints. Redirect HTTP→HTTPS.</td><td>🔴 Critical</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>2</td><td>Auth Enforced</td><td>SSO required. No guest access in production.</td><td>🔴 Critical</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>3</td><td>Secrets External</td><td>No secrets in app-config.yaml or Git. Use K8s Secrets/Vault.</td><td>🔴 Critical</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>4</td><td>RBAC — Least Privilege</td><td>Users get minimum permissions needed. Default deny.</td><td>🟡 High</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>5</td><td>Image Updates</td><td>Regularly update Backstage images. Scan for CVEs.</td><td>🟡 High</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>6</td><td>WAF + Rate Limiting</td><td>Web Application Firewall on Ingress. Rate limit API calls.</td><td>🟡 High</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>7</td><td>Audit Logging</td><td>Enable and ship audit logs to SIEM (Splunk/Datadog).</td><td>🟢 Medium</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>8</td><td>Network Policies</td><td>Restrict pod-to-pod traffic. Backstage pods → DB only.</td><td>🟢 Medium</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>9</td><td>CORS Configured</td><td>Restrict API origins. Never use <code>*</code> in production.</td><td>🟢 Medium</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>10</td><td>Dependency Scanning</td><td>Automated yarn audit / Snyk / Dependabot in CI.</td><td>🟢 Medium</td></tr>
                </tbody></table></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §30.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s30-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">30.2 Secret Management — Never in Git</h3>
                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ The Golden Rule</h5><p class="mb-0"><strong>NEVER</strong> put secrets (tokens, passwords, keys) in <code>app-config.yaml</code> or any file committed to Git. Always use environment variable substitution: <code>${GITHUB_TOKEN}</code>. The CBA exam WILL ask about this.</p></div>

                <div id="b-s30-2a">
                    <h4>Environment Variables (K8s)</h4>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">Kubernetes Secret — store sensitive values</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: backstage-secrets
type: Opaque
stringData:
  GITHUB_TOKEN: "ghp_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"
  POSTGRES_PASSWORD: "supersecretpassword"
  BACKSTAGE_SECRET: "random-generated-secret"
---
# Deployment — reference secrets as env vars
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
spec:
  template:
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: backstage
        env:
        - name: GITHUB_TOKEN
          valueFrom:
            secretKeyRef:
              name: backstage-secrets
              key: GITHUB_TOKEN</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">kubectl — baseline creation command</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create secret generic backstage-secrets \
  --from-literal=GITHUB_TOKEN=xxx -n backstage</code></pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div id="b-s30-2b">
                    <h4>External Vaults (Advanced) — Real ExternalSecret Config</h4>
                    <p>For enterprise: <strong>HashiCorp Vault</strong> (dynamic secrets, auto-rotation), <strong>AWS Secrets Manager</strong> (auto-rotation, IAM integration), or <strong>Azure Key Vault</strong>. Use the <strong>External Secrets Operator (ESO)</strong> to sync vault secrets into K8s Secrets automatically.</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">External Secrets Operator — syncs from a real vault</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: external-secrets.io/v1beta1
kind: ExternalSecret
spec:
  secretStoreRef: { name: vault-backend, kind: ClusterSecretStore }
  target: { name: backstage-secrets }</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <p><strong>Token rotation:</strong> Use fine-grained GitHub PATs (scoped to specific repos) and rotate every 90 days. Automate rotation via CI pipeline.</p>
                    <div class="info-box note mb-0"><h5>🔑 Backstage Service Tokens — Scope Narrowly</h5><p class="mb-0">Scope every Backstage-generated service token narrowly with <code>backend.auth.externalAccess.accessRestrictions</code> (Chapter 28 §28.4) — e.g. limit a CI/CD token to only the catalog/scaffolder plugins — so a leaked token can't call every plugin in the backend.</p></div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §30.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s30-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">30.3 Network Security — Defense in Depth</h3>
                <div id="b-s30-3a">
                    <h4>TLS &amp; Ingress</h4>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">Ingress with TLS + WAF annotations (NGINX example)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: backstage-ingress
  annotations:
    cert-manager.io/cluster-issuer: "letsencrypt-prod"
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/ssl-redirect: "true"
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/limit-rps: "20"
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/whitelist-source-range: "10.0.0.0/8"
spec:
  tls:
  - hosts: [backstage.easypj.com]
    secretName: backstage-tls
  rules:
  - host: backstage.easypj.com
    http:
      paths:
      - path: /
        pathType: Prefix
        backend:
          service:
            name: backstage
            port:
              number: 7007</code></pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div id="b-s30-3b">
                    <h4>NetworkPolicies &amp; CORS</h4>
                    <p><strong>NetworkPolicies:</strong> Restrict Backstage pods to only talk to PostgreSQL, Redis, and Elasticsearch. Block all other egress. Deny all ingress except from the load balancer.</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the real NetworkPolicy — default deny + narrow allow</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># NetworkPolicy - only allow traffic from the ingress controller
spec:
  podSelector: { matchLabels: { app: backstage } }
  ingress: [{ from: [{ namespaceSelector: { matchLabels:
    { name: ingress-nginx } } }] }]</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <p><strong>CORS Configuration in app-config.yaml:</strong></p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">app-config.yaml — CORS + CSP</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">backend:
  cors:
    origin: "https://backstage.easypj.com"  # NEVER use "*" in prod!
    methods: [GET, POST, PUT, DELETE]
    credentials: true
  csp:
    connect-src: ["'self'", "https://api.easypj.com"]
    img-src: ["'self'", "data:", "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com"]</code></pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §30.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s30-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">30.4 Vulnerability Management — Scan Everything</h3>
                <div class="comparison-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What It Scans</th><th>Integration</th><th>Frequency</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>yarn audit</strong></td><td>Node.js dependencies</td><td>CI pipeline (GitHub Actions)</td><td>Every commit</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Snyk</strong></td><td>Dependencies + base image</td><td>CI + GitHub integration</td><td>Every PR</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Dependabot</strong></td><td>npm/yarn packages</td><td>GitHub native (auto-PRs)</td><td>Daily</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Trivy</strong></td><td>Container images</td><td>CI pipeline (image build)</td><td>Every build</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Grype</strong></td><td>Container images + SBOM</td><td>CI pipeline</td><td>Every build</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Backstage Updates</strong></td><td>Platform itself</td><td>Manual upgrade cycle (Chapter 26)</td><td>Monthly</td></tr>
                </tbody></table></div>
                <p><strong>Workflow:</strong> 1) CI builds Backstage image → 2) Trivy/Grype scans it → 3) If critical CVEs found, fail the build → 4) Dependabot auto-opens PRs for vulnerable npm packages → 5) Monthly Backstage version upgrades for platform-level fixes.</p>
                <div class="info-box warning mb-0"><h5>⚠️ Both Layers Together, Not Either/Or</h5><p class="mb-0">Scanning container images but skipping dependency scanning (or vice versa) leaves a real gap — this chapter's scanning requirement means BOTH layers together, not one or the other.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §30.5 (NEW — from Backstage.txt, no HTML equivalent existed) ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s30-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">30.5 Threat Model — What Could Go Wrong</h3>
                <p>A STRIDE-style pass over the Backstage attack surface. Because knowing the checklist (§30.1) matters less than knowing WHY.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Threat</th><th>Example in Backstage</th><th>Mitigation</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Spoofing</strong></td><td>Fake sign-in as another user</td><td>Real SSO, no guest in prod</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Tampering</strong></td><td>Edited catalog-info.yaml granting self extra ownership</td><td>RBAC + PR review on infra repos (Chapter 23)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Repudiation</strong></td><td>No audit trail for who deleted an entity</td><td>Enable audit logging (§30.1)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Info disclosure</strong></td><td>Secrets leaked via app-config committed to Git</td><td>Never inline secrets in YAML (§30.2)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Denial of service</strong></td><td>Unbounded scaffolder task spam</td><td>Rate limit the Ingress (§30.3)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Elevation of privilege</strong></td><td>Broad ClusterRole on the K8s plugin ServiceAccount</td><td>Least-privilege RBAC (Chapter 20 §20.5)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §30.6 (NEW — from Backstage.txt, no HTML equivalent existed) ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="b-s30-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">30.6 Security Incident Response Runbook</h3>
                <p>What to do in the first hour of a suspected security incident.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container"><div class="diagram-title">Incident Response — Follow in Order</div>
<pre>
  1. Contain: revoke the suspected compromised token/credential immediately
  2. Assess: check audit logs (§30.1) for what that identity actually touched
  3. Rotate: rotate ALL secrets that identity had access to, not just the one
  4. Patch: if a CVE was the entry point, apply the fix from §30.4 now
  5. Restore: use the DR runbook (Chapter 26 §26.7) if data was altered, not just accessed
  6. Report: write the postmortem, feed new detections into monitoring (Chapter 27)
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box danger mb-0"><h5>🚨 Rotate Everything That Identity Touched, Not Just the One Credential</h5><p class="mb-0">Responding to an incident by rotating only the one credential known to be compromised — instead of everything that identity had access to — is a common, dangerous shortcut.</p></div>
            </section>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch30-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — why the checklist, threat model, and runbook are one system, not three separate things</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>The security checklist (§30.1) and the defense-in-depth diagram above it aren't two different things — the checklist is the CONTRACT, and each of its rows is satisfied by one specific mechanism elsewhere in the chapter.</p>
                    <h5>① Every Checklist Row Has a Named Mechanism</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
Checklist row "Secrets External"     →  satisfied by §30.2 (vault/K8s Secrets)
Checklist row "Network + Rate Limit" →  satisfied by §30.3 (Ingress config)
Checklist row "Image Updates/Scan"   →  satisfied by §30.4 (Trivy/Snyk/etc)
                    </pre>
                    <p>Secret management (§30.2) satisfies the "never in Git" row, network security (§30.3) satisfies transport and rate-limiting, and vulnerability management (§30.4) satisfies the "regular updates, scanned images" row.</p>
                    <h5>② Why the Threat Model Exists on Top of the Checklist</h5>
                    <p>The threat model (§30.5) exists because a checklist alone doesn't teach you WHY each row matters — walking Backstage through STRIDE shows that RBAC (Chapter 23) still matters even with real SSO enforced, because SSO only proves WHO you are, never WHAT you're allowed to do. And the incident runbook (§30.6) is the plan for the day one of these layers fails anyway — contain first, understand blast radius second, never skip straight to "fix and move on."</p>
                    <div class="uth-callout mb-0"><strong>🔑 Checklist = contract, threat model = why, runbook = what happens when it fails anyway.</strong> All three are one system covering prevention, understanding, and response.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch30-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Defense in depth means six layers</strong> — network, auth, RBAC, secrets, supply chain, audit — each one assumes the layer before it CAN fail.</li>
                    <li><strong>The 7-item checklist (§30.1) is the contract:</strong> HTTPS, external secrets, real SSO, least-privilege RBAC, scanned images, WAF+rate-limiting, audit logging.</li>
                    <li><strong>Secrets (§30.2) always come from Kubernetes Secrets or an External Secrets Operator</strong> synced from a real vault — never hardcoded in app-config.yaml.</li>
                    <li><strong>Scope every service token narrowly</strong> with <code>backend.auth.externalAccess.accessRestrictions</code> so a leaked token can't call every plugin.</li>
                    <li><strong>A STRIDE pass (§30.5) shows RBAC still matters even with SSO enforced</strong> — SSO proves identity, not authorization.</li>
                    <li><strong>The incident runbook (§30.6) is an ORDER</strong> — contain, assess, rotate, patch, restore, report — not a menu to pick from.</li>
                    <li><strong>This chapter maps to Infrastructure 22%,</strong> not Customizing 32% — a domain-tag correction from the original ported content.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch30-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Treating SSO alone as "secure enough"</strong> and skipping least-privilege RBAC — SSO answers WHO, not WHAT they can do (§30.5).</li>
                    <li><strong>Committing a secret inline in app-config.yaml "just for now"</strong> instead of referencing an environment variable backed by a vault (§30.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Scanning container images but skipping dependency scanning</strong> (or vice versa) — §30.4 requires both layers together.</li>
                    <li><strong>Responding to an incident by rotating only the one credential known to be compromised</strong> instead of everything that identity had access to (§30.6).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch30-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Run the §30.1 checklist as a literal go/no-go gate</strong> — treat any unchecked row as a launch blocker, same discipline as Chapter 26 §26.1.</li>
                    <li><strong>Automate scanning (§30.4) into CI</strong> so a new CVE is caught on the next build, not on the next audit.</li>
                    <li><strong>Rehearse the incident runbook (§30.6) BEFORE an incident</strong> — a live breach is the worst time to invent a response order.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch30-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 30)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">checklist + secrets + network + scanning + incident order</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># Checklist: HTTPS, vault secrets, real SSO, least-priv RBAC, scanned
#   images, WAF+rate-limit, audit logging                        (30.1)
# kubectl create secret generic backstage-secrets --from-literal=... (30.2)
# ExternalSecret: secretStoreRef: vault-backend -> target: backstage-secrets
# Ingress: cert-manager.io/cluster-issuer + limit-rps: '20'      (30.3)
# Scan: yarn audit / Snyk / Dependabot / Trivy / Grype            (30.4)
# Incident: contain -> assess -> rotate -> patch -> restore -> report (30.6)</code></pre>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch30-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 30 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of secrets, network security, and vulnerability management — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Where should secrets NEVER be stored? What's the correct approach?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Never in <code>app-config.yaml</code> or any file committed to Git. Use environment variable substitution (<code>${GITHUB_TOKEN}</code>), Kubernetes Secrets mapped to env vars, or external vaults (HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager).</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Location</th><th>Safe?</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Inline in app-config.yaml (Git)</td><td>❌ No — permanent Git history</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>${ENV_VAR} + K8s Secret/vault</td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>A secret committed to Git is compromised permanently — even "removing" it later leaves it in Git history.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you reference a secret in app-config.yaml without exposing it?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Use <code>${ENV_VAR_NAME}</code> syntax. Example: <code>integrations.github[0].token: ${GITHUB_TOKEN}</code>. The actual token value is injected at runtime via environment variables (from K8s Secrets or vault).</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the pattern</span></div><pre><code class="language-yaml">integrations:
  github:
    - token: ${GITHUB_TOKEN}</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>app-config.yaml stays safe to commit — it only contains references, never actual secrets.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What tools can scan Backstage for vulnerabilities?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Dependencies:</strong> yarn audit, Snyk, Dependabot. <strong>Container images:</strong> Trivy, Grype. <strong>Platform:</strong> Regular Backstage version upgrades + GitHub Security Advisories.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Tools</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>yarn audit, Snyk, Dependabot</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Images</td><td>Trivy, Grype</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The CBA expects automated CI scanning at both layers, not manual checks.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you restrict which origins can call Backstage's API?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Configure CORS in app-config.yaml: <code>backend.cors.origin: "https://backstage.easypj.com"</code>. This restricts API access to your Backstage domain only. <strong>Never use <code>"*"</code></strong> in production.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>origin value</th><th>Production-safe?</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>"*"</td><td>❌ No</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>"https://backstage.easypj.com"</td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>origin: "*"</code> means ANY website can call your API — including malicious sites.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What are the top items on a Backstage security checklist?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>HTTPS everywhere, real SSO (no guest access), secrets external (never in Git), RBAC least privilege, and regular image updates + vulnerability scanning — plus WAF/rate-limiting, audit logging, NetworkPolicies, and CORS.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Priority</th><th>Item</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>🔴 Critical</td><td>HTTPS, Auth, Secrets</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>🟡 High</td><td>RBAC, Image updates, WAF</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>HTTPS and Auth are table stakes; secrets management prevents leaks; RBAC limits blast radius if compromised.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you manage GitHub tokens securely for Backstage?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Use <strong>fine-grained Personal Access Tokens (PATs)</strong> scoped to specific repositories and permissions. Store in K8s Secrets. Rotate every 90 days. Automate rotation via CI pipeline or use GitHub App installation tokens.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Token type</th><th>Scope</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Fine-grained PAT</td><td>Specific repos + permissions</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>GitHub App token</td><td>Auto-expires after 1 hour</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>If a fine-grained token leaks, the blast radius is limited to its scoped repos/permissions.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What NetworkPolicy should you apply to Backstage pods?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Allow ingress only from the Ingress controller namespace; allow egress only to PostgreSQL, Redis, Elasticsearch, and required external APIs. Default deny all other traffic.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the real policy</span></div><pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:
  podSelector: { matchLabels: { app: backstage } }
  ingress: [{ from: [{ namespaceSelector: { matchLabels:
    { name: ingress-nginx } } }] }]</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>"Default deny" is critical — start by blocking everything, then explicitly allow only what Backstage needs.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you enable TLS for Backstage on Kubernetes?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Use cert-manager with Let's Encrypt to auto-provision TLS certificates. Configure the Ingress resource with a TLS section pointing to the cert-manager generated secret, and the annotation <code>cert-manager.io/cluster-issuer: "letsencrypt-prod"</code>.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the annotation</span></div><pre><code class="language-yaml">annotations:
  cert-manager.io/cluster-issuer: "letsencrypt-prod"</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Backstage itself runs plain HTTP internally — the Ingress handles HTTPS termination externally.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between K8s Secrets and a real external vault?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>K8s Secrets:</strong> Base64-encoded (NOT encrypted by default), cluster-scoped, no auto-rotation. <strong>Vault/cloud secret managers:</strong> Dynamic secrets, auto-rotation, audit logging. Use an External Secrets Operator to sync a vault into K8s Secrets.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Encrypted at rest?</th><th>Auto-rotation?</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>K8s Secrets (default)</td><td>❌ No — just base64</td><td>❌ No</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Vault / cloud secret manager</td><td>✅ Yes</td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>K8s Secrets are "good enough" for small deployments; enterprises should use Vault or cloud secret managers.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you enable audit logging in Backstage?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Ship logs to stdout/stderr (collected by Fluentd/Filebeat) and forward to a SIEM (Splunk, Datadog, ELK). Audit events include: entity creation/deletion, template execution, user login/logout, and permission changes.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Audit event</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Entity created/deleted</td></tr><tr><td>Template executed</td></tr><tr><td>Permission changed</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Critical for SOC2/ISO27001 compliance and security investigations.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch30-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 30 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (8)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Real workplace situations — 8 scenarios mirroring how the CBA exam actually asks.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">easypj enforces real SSO (Chapter 22) but never configured the permission policy from Chapter 23, leaving the default allow-all in place. Is this deployment secure?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>No — SSO only proves WHO a user is, not WHAT they're allowed to do. Without a real permission policy, any signed-in user (intern or admin) has full access. This is exactly the "Elevation of privilege"/RBAC gap the §30.5 STRIDE pass warns about.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Configured?</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>SSO (identity)</td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>RBAC (authorization)</td><td>❌ No — allow-all</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§30's Gotcha — "treating SSO alone as 'secure enough' and skipping least-privilege RBAC."</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A developer commits a real database password directly into app-config.yaml "just to get the demo working today," planning to fix it later. What's wrong with this plan?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The secret is compromised the moment it's committed — Git history is permanent, so even reverting the commit later leaves the password recoverable from history. The fix should have been an env var reference from the start, not a "temporary" inline value.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Action</th><th>Recoverable from Git history?</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Commit then revert</td><td>✅ Still recoverable — compromised</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Never commit (env var from the start)</td><td>❌ Never exposed</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§30.2's Gotcha — "committing a secret inline... 'just for now'" is named explicitly as a common, dangerous shortcut.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">easypj's CI pipeline runs Trivy on container images but has no yarn audit / Dependabot step for npm dependencies. Is their vulnerability management complete?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>No — image scanning alone misses vulnerable npm dependencies that never make it into a container layer scan the same way. §30.4 requires BOTH dependency scanning AND image scanning together, not either one alone.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Scan layer</th><th>Configured?</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Image scanning (Trivy)</td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Dependency scanning (yarn audit/Dependabot)</td><td>❌ No — gap</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§30's Gotcha — "scanning container images but skipping dependency scanning (or vice versa)."</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A CI/CD static token (Chapter 28) is discovered leaked in a public repo. The on-call engineer rotates just that one token and closes the incident. What did they miss?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>They should have rotated ALL secrets that identity had access to (per its accessRestrictions scope), not just the one leaked token — an attacker with even brief access could have already used it to extract other credentials or data reachable by that identity's permissions.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Action taken</th><th>Complete per §30.6?</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Rotate only the leaked token</td><td>❌ No</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Rotate everything that identity could reach</td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§30.6's runbook step 3 ("Rotate: rotate ALL secrets that identity had access to") + §30's Gotcha on incomplete rotation.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Walk the incident response runbook in order for a scenario where a CI/CD static token was just leaked in a public GitHub repo.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>1</td><td>Contain: revoke the leaked token immediately</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>2</td><td>Assess: check audit logs for what it actually touched</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>3</td><td>Rotate: rotate everything that identity could reach</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>4</td><td>Patch: fix the leak's root cause if a CVE enabled it</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>5</td><td>Restore: use the DR runbook if data was altered</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>6</td><td>Report: write the postmortem</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§30.6's exact 6-step order — this is the chapter's own named Practice Drill scenario.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Someone edits a catalog-info.yaml in a shared infra repo to name themselves as the owner of a service they don't actually maintain, without going through PR review. Which STRIDE category does this fall under, and what mitigates it?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Tampering</strong> — unauthorized modification of a trusted source-of-truth file. Mitigated by RBAC plus mandatory PR review on infra repos (Chapter 23), so a self-granted ownership change can't merge silently.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>STRIDE category</th><th>Mitigation</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Tampering</td><td>RBAC + PR review (Ch23)</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§30.5's STRIDE table — this exact scenario is the table's own named example for Tampering.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">easypj's K8s plugin ServiceAccount (Chapter 20) was granted a broad ClusterRole with write access "to be safe." A pod is later compromised. Which STRIDE threat does this enable, and what should have prevented it?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Elevation of privilege</strong> — the compromised pod inherits whatever broad permissions the ServiceAccount holds. Least-privilege RBAC (Chapter 20 §20.5's read-only ClusterRole) should have been applied from the start, limiting the blast radius of any single pod compromise.</p>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>ClusterRole</th><th>Compromise blast radius</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Broad, write access</td><td>❌ Large</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Least-privilege, read-only</td><td>✅ Limited</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>§30.5's STRIDE table names this exact scenario — a broad ClusterRole on the K8s plugin ServiceAccount.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Design the minimum defense-in-depth stack for easypj using this chapter's 6 layers, naming one concrete mechanism per layer.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Mechanism</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                            <tr><td>Network</td><td>HTTPS + WAF + rate limiting on Ingress</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Auth</td><td>Real SSO, no guest access</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>RBAC</td><td>Least privilege, deny by default</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Secrets</td><td>Vault/K8s Secrets, never in Git</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Supply chain</td><td>Trivy + yarn audit in CI</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Audit</td><td>Logging shipped to SIEM</td></tr>
                        </tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The defense-in-depth diagram's 6 layers, each with its real, named mechanism from this chapter.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
