            <section class="section-block" id="b-s31-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">31.7 Practice Exam — 30 Questions (Timed: 45 Minutes)</h3>
                <p>Take this practice exam under timed conditions. The domain split matches the real exam weighting exactly: 10 Customizing (32%), 7 Development Workflow (24%), 7 Infrastructure (22%), 6 Catalog (22%). Cover the questions before checking answers — that is the whole point.</p>

                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📝 Practice Exam Tips</h5><ul class="mb-0">
                    <li>⏱️ Set a timer for 45 minutes (half the real exam)</li>
                    <li>📖 Keep backstage.io/docs open — practice finding answers quickly</li>
                    <li>❌ Don't Google — only use docs.backstage.io</li>
                    <li>📊 Score yourself: 21/30 = 70% (passing). Aim for 27-30 (exam ready)</li>
                    <li>🔍 After scoring, review every wrong answer and re-study the topic</li>
                    <li>🔄 Take the practice exam again 3 days later — aim for improvement</li>
                </ul></div>

                <h4>Domain 1: Customizing Backstage (Q1-Q10)</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table"><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Question</th><th>Answer</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                    <tr><td>1</td><td>A plugin renders a UI tab but never touches a database or runs server-side code. What KIND of plugin is it? A) Backend-only B) Frontend-only C) Full-stack D) Dynamic</td><td><strong>B</strong></td><td>Frontend-only plugins render UI and call external APIs directly from the browser — no backend package required.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>2</td><td>Which import provides the Backstage-styled card component most commonly used to wrap custom plugin content? A) Card from @material-ui/core B) InfoCard from @backstage/core-components C) Panel from @backstage/theme D) Container from @material-ui/core</td><td><strong>B</strong></td><td>InfoCard is the Backstage-specific wrapper that matches the platform's look — plain MUI Card also works but is less idiomatic.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>3</td><td>You need to add a brand-new page reachable from the sidebar that is specific to your org and will never be reused elsewhere. What is the correct approach? A) Publish it as a community plugin on npm B) Edit App.tsx to add a route and Root.tsx to add a sidebar item C) Add it inside the Catalog plugin's source code D) Create a new Backstage instance just for this page</td><td><strong>B</strong></td><td>Org-specific, non-reusable wiring belongs directly in the App shell (App.tsx/Root.tsx), not in a redistributable plugin (Chapter 18 §18.6).</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>4</td><td>What is the correct order of steps in a standard scaffolder template pipeline? A) publish:github → fetch:template → catalog:register B) catalog:register → fetch:template → publish:github C) fetch:template → publish:github → catalog:register D) fetch:template → catalog:register → publish:github</td><td><strong>C</strong></td><td>You must fetch the skeleton, THEN publish it to a real repo, THEN register it — you cannot register a repo that does not exist yet.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>5</td><td>Which techdocs.builder setting should a MULTI-REPLICA production deployment use? A) local B) external C) docker D) inline</td><td><strong>B</strong></td><td>external means CI/CD pre-builds the docs once; local would make each pod (re)build on demand, wasting resources and risking inconsistent output across pods (Chapter 26 §26.3).</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>6</td><td>What is the correct lifecycle order for a Backstage plugin? A) Register → Configure → Install → Run B) Install → Configure → Register → Run C) Configure → Install → Run → Register D) Install → Register → Run → Configure</td><td><strong>B</strong></td><td>npm/yarn install the package, configure it in app-config.yaml, register it via createPlugin(), then it runs.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>7</td><td>What does createRoutableExtension() do? A) Creates a new Catalog entity B) Registers a lazy-loaded page/route for a frontend plugin C) Creates a new backend Express router D) Extends the permission framework</td><td><strong>B</strong></td><td>It wires a plugin's page component to a route and enables code-splitting so the plugin's code loads only on demand.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>8</td><td>A template parameter needs the user to pick an EXISTING Backstage Group as the owner. Which ui:field should you use? A) RepoUrlPicker B) EntityPicker C) OwnerPicker D) GroupSelect</td><td><strong>C</strong></td><td>OwnerPicker is purpose-built for selecting an owning User or Group; EntityPicker is the more general-purpose version.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>9</td><td>Inside a custom scaffolder action's handler, how do you report a value for later steps to consume? A) return {value} B) ctx.output('key', value) C) console.log(value) D) ctx.logger.info(value)</td><td><strong>B</strong></td><td>ctx.output() is how a step exposes data that later steps reference via {{ steps.&lt;id&gt;.output.&lt;key&gt; }}.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>10</td><td>What is the main benefit of DYNAMIC plugins over standard ones? A) They run faster B) They can be installed/updated at runtime without a rebuild C) They do not need app-config.yaml entries D) They bypass the permission framework</td><td><strong>B</strong></td><td>Dynamic plugins load from a container image at runtime — no Backstage rebuild or redeploy needed to add one.</td></tr>
                </tbody></table></div>

                <h4>Domain 2: Development Workflow (Q11-Q17)</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table"><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Question</th><th>Answer</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                    <tr><td>11</td><td>What is the key difference between yarn tsc and yarn build? A) They are identical commands B) tsc only type-checks; build compiles AND bundles for prod C) tsc is for the backend only; build is for the frontend only D) build only works in Docker</td><td><strong>B</strong></td><td>yarn tsc verifies types across the monorepo without producing deployable output; yarn build does full production compilation and bundling.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>12</td><td>In a Backstage monorepo, where does yarn.lock live and how many should exist? A) One per package, several total B) One at the repo root, covering all workspaces C) None — Backstage does not use Yarn D) One per plugin only</td><td><strong>B</strong></td><td>Yarn workspaces share ONE root-level yarn.lock that pins versions across every package/plugin in the monorepo.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>13</td><td>Why do production Backstage Dockerfiles use a multi-stage build? A) It is required by Kubernetes B) To keep the final runtime image small by excluding build tools and devDependencies C) It makes yarn dev faster D) It avoids needing a package.json</td><td><strong>B</strong></td><td>Stage 1 compiles with full devDependencies; stage 2 copies only the compiled output, dramatically shrinking image size (Chapter 19 §19.3).</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>14</td><td>Which command scaffolds a brand-new Backstage application? A) yarn create backstage-app B) npx @backstage/create-app@latest C) yarn backstage-cli new D) npm init backstage</td><td><strong>B</strong></td><td>npx @backstage/create-app@latest is the official scaffolding command for a fresh Backstage instance.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>15</td><td>What is the app-config.yaml merge precedence, highest last? A) local → production → base B) base → local → production C) base → production → local D) production → base → local</td><td><strong>C</strong></td><td>app-config.yaml (base) is overridden by app-config.production.yaml, which is overridden by the gitignored app-config.local.yaml (highest priority).</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>16</td><td>In yarn dev mode, are TypeScript type errors guaranteed to stop the server from running? A) Yes, dev mode always type-checks strictly B) No — dev mode transpiles on the fly without full type-checking C) Only backend type errors block yarn dev D) Only frontend type errors block yarn dev</td><td><strong>B</strong></td><td>yarn dev prioritizes fast iteration; only yarn tsc (or yarn build/CI) rigorously enforces types.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>17</td><td>Why should CI pipelines use "yarn install --frozen-lockfile" instead of plain "yarn install"? A) It installs faster in all cases B) It fails loudly if package.json and yarn.lock have drifted, instead of silently rewriting the lockfile C) It skips devDependencies automatically D) It is required for Docker builds only</td><td><strong>B</strong></td><td>Frozen-lockfile installs catch dependency drift as a hard CI failure rather than silently changing resolved versions.</td></tr>
                </tbody></table></div>

                <h4>Domain 3: Backstage Infrastructure (Q18-Q24)</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table"><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Question</th><th>Answer</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                    <tr><td>18</td><td>What are the three layers of Backstage's architecture? A) Frontend, Middleware, Database B) Frontend (React), Backend (Node.js), Database (PostgreSQL) C) Client, Proxy, Server D) UI, API Gateway, Cache</td><td><strong>B</strong></td><td>React frontend talks to a Node.js/Express backend, which is the ONLY layer allowed to talk to the database.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>19</td><td>Why is SQLite unsuitable for a production Backstage deployment with 3 replicas? A) SQLite cannot store YAML data B) SQLite only supports a single concurrent writer C) SQLite is slower than PostgreSQL for reads D) SQLite requires a paid license in production</td><td><strong>B</strong></td><td>Multiple pods writing concurrently would corrupt a SQLite file; PostgreSQL is built for concurrent access.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>20</td><td>Which Kubernetes resource holds non-sensitive Backstage configuration like app-config.yaml? A) Secret B) ConfigMap C) PersistentVolumeClaim D) Ingress</td><td><strong>B</strong></td><td>ConfigMap stores non-sensitive config; Secret is reserved for credentials and tokens.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>21</td><td>Your org already runs Okta company-wide for SSO. Which auth provider is the best fit for Backstage? A) Guest access B) GitHub OAuth C) Okta (OIDC) D) None needed</td><td><strong>C</strong></td><td>Reusing the existing enterprise identity provider (Okta) avoids a second identity system and enables group sync.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>22</td><td>In the Backstage permission framework, if an Allow rule and a Deny rule both match a request, which wins? A) Allow always wins B) Deny always wins C) The most recently defined rule wins D) Whichever rule is alphabetically first</td><td><strong>B</strong>*</td><td>*In practice, a PermissionPolicy should be written so Deny takes precedence — but Backstage's core framework does NOT ship separate "Allow rules"/"Deny rules" that auto-combine. A PermissionPolicy is a single function returning ONE decision (ALLOW, DENY, or CONDITIONAL) per request; deny-wins is something YOU implement as a design convention (Chapter 23), not a built-in rule engine.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>23</td><td>Why can Backstage scale horizontally just by adding replicas? A) Each pod has its own database B) The app is stateless — session state lives in the JWT, not pod memory C) Kubernetes automatically shares memory between pods D) Horizontal scaling is not actually supported</td><td><strong>B</strong></td><td>Because no session state is kept in a given pod, any replica can handle any request (Chapter 29 §29.2).</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>24</td><td>Which of these is NOT part of the standard production checklist? A) PostgreSQL instead of SQLite B) Guest access enabled for convenience C) TLS/HTTPS everywhere D) Secrets stored outside of Git</td><td><strong>B</strong></td><td>Guest access must NEVER be enabled in production — it is a dev-only convenience with no real permission scoping.</td></tr>
                </tbody></table></div>

                <h4>Domain 4: Backstage Catalog (Q25-Q30)</h4>
