            <section class="section-block" id="b-s32-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">32.6 The New Backend System: Plugins, Modules, Extension Points &amp; Service-to-Service Auth</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>A backend instance does no work itself — it just wires plugins/modules together</li>
                    <li>Plugins run as isolated, network-only "microservices" inside one backend instance</li>
                    <li>A Module can only extend ONE plugin, via that plugin's Extension Points</li>
                    <li>Extension points are created with <code>createExtensionPoint()</code> and registered in a plugin's <code>register(env)</code> via <code>env.registerExtensionPoint()</code>; a Module then declares the extension point as a dependency in its own <code>registerInit({ deps })</code> block</li>
                    <li>Plugin-to-plugin calls are secure <strong>BY DEFAULT</strong> — no config needed. Each plugin gets the <code>auth</code> and <code>httpAuth</code> services (from <code>coreServices</code>) and self-signed tokens are verified via the caller's public key set</li>
                </ul>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Backend System Building Blocks (New Backend System)</div>
<pre>
   COMPONENT           ROLE
   ------------------  ------------------------------------------------
   Backend instance    Deployment unit that wires plugins/modules together
   Plugin              Provides one feature; isolated, network-only comms
   Service              Shared utility injected via coreServices (logger, auth...)
   Module               Extends ONE plugin via that plugin's Extension Point
   Extension Point      Customization surface a plugin exposes to its modules
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">extension-point.ts</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-typescript">// Plugin side: create + register an extension point
export const easypjEnrichmentExtensionPoint =
  createExtensionPoint&lt;EasypjEnrichmentExtensionPoint&gt;({
    id: 'easypj.enrichment',
  });

export const easypjPlugin = createBackendPlugin({
  pluginId: 'easypj',
  register(env) {
    env.registerExtensionPoint(easypjEnrichmentExtensionPoint, extensionPointImpl);
    env.registerInit({ deps: { /* ... */ }, async init() { /* ... */ } });
  },
});

// Module side: extend that ONE plugin via its extension point
export const easypjModuleAudit = createBackendModule({
  pluginId: 'easypj',
  moduleId: 'audit',
  register(env) {
    env.registerInit({
      deps: { enrichment: easypjEnrichmentExtensionPoint },
      async init({ enrichment }) { enrichment.addEnricher(auditEnricher); },
    });
  },
});</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>🔐 Service-to-Service Auth — Plugin to Plugin (Secure by Default)</h5>
                    <p><code>auth</code> and <code>httpAuth</code> services are injected via <code>coreServices</code> — no config needed. A plugin requests a token to call another plugin:</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">plugin-to-plugin-token.ts</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-typescript">const { token } = await auth.getPluginRequestToken({
  onBehalfOf: await auth.getOwnServiceCredentials(),
  targetPluginId: 'catalog',
});</code></pre></div>
                    <p class="mb-0">Self-signed tokens are verified via the caller's public JWKS. External callers (CI/CD, webhooks) use <code>backend.auth.externalAccess</code>: static tokens or JWKS-validated JWTs, each scoped with optional <code>accessRestrictions</code> for least-privilege.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch32-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — why plugin-to-plugin calls need no config</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>§32.6 states that <code>auth</code> and <code>httpAuth</code> are injected into every plugin via <code>coreServices</code> with self-signed tokens verified against the caller's public key set (JWKS). This is what makes the security model "secure by default."</p>
                    <h5>① No Shared Secret, Ever</h5>
                    <p>A plugin calling another plugin (e.g. a custom processor from §32.1 calling the catalog plugin) never has to be handed a shared secret or manage a credential — it asks its own <code>auth</code> service for a scoped token naming the target plugin, and the receiving plugin verifies that token's signature itself.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
Plugin A                                Plugin B (target)
   │  auth.getPluginRequestToken()          │
   │  ({ targetPluginId: 'catalog' })       │
   ▼                                        │
 self-signed token  ──────────────────────▶ verifies via caller's public JWKS
                    </pre>
                    <p>External callers (CI/CD, webhooks) are the one case that DOES need explicit config, via <code>backend.auth.externalAccess</code> static tokens or JWKS-validated JWTs — because an external caller has no in-process auth service to ask.</p>
                    <div class="uth-callout mb-0"><strong>🔑 In-process calls are automatic; only external callers need explicit config</strong> — that boundary is the entire security model.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch32-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>A CatalogProcessor's <code>postProcessEntity</code> hook</strong> is the standard way to enrich every entity of a given kind during ingestion (§32.1).</li>
                    <li><strong>A CatalogProcessor differs from an EntityProvider:</strong> the former enriches entities already in the catalog, the latter introduces entities from an external system with no <code>catalog-info.yaml</code> (§32.1).</li>
                    <li><strong>Dynamic plugins load at RUNTIME</strong> from a container image — no Backstage rebuild or redeploy needed to add or update one (§32.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Self-hosted gives full control at high ops burden;</strong> Roadie and Spotify Portal trade some control for a managed, low-burden offering (§32.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Cortex and Port are NOT Backstage</strong> — they're rival internal developer portal products, not managed Backstage hosting (§32.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Multi-tenancy in Backstage is built from namespaces plus catalog rules and permission policies,</strong> not a separate product (§32.4).</li>
                    <li><strong>Backstage's CNCF status is Incubating,</strong> on the path toward Graduation — not yet Graduated (§32.5).</li>
                    <li><strong>In the New Backend System, a backend instance does no work itself</strong> — it only wires Plugins and Modules together (§32.6).</li>
                    <li><strong>A Module can extend exactly ONE plugin,</strong> through that plugin's Extension Points created with <code>createExtensionPoint()</code> (§32.6).</li>
                    <li><strong>Plugin-to-plugin auth is secure by default</strong> via <code>coreServices</code>' <code>auth</code>/<code>httpAuth</code> — no shared secrets, no extra config (§32.6).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch32-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Confusing a Module with a plugin.</strong> A Module cannot stand alone; it only ever extends ONE existing plugin's Extension Point.</li>
                    <li><strong>Assuming dynamic plugins skip app-config entirely.</strong> They still need a <code>dynamicPlugins</code> config block (§32.2), they just skip the rebuild/redeploy step.</li>
                    <li><strong>Thinking multi-tenancy requires separate Backstage instances.</strong> §32.4 does it inside ONE instance with namespaces + catalog rules.</li>
                    <li><strong>Assuming plugin-to-plugin calls need manually issued API keys.</strong> §32.6's whole point is that this is automatic via <code>coreServices</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Assuming Cortex or Port run Backstage under the hood.</strong> They don't — they're competing products, not managed hosting of Backstage (§32.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Assuming Backstage is already CNCF Graduated.</strong> It's Incubating, on the path toward Graduation (§32.5).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="b-ch32-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Reach for a CatalogProcessor before a custom EntityProvider</strong> if you only need to enrich/transform entities that already exist — it's the smaller, simpler extension point.</li>
                    <li><strong>Prototype with dynamic plugins in non-prod first</strong> (§32.2 marks the feature Beta) before relying on it for a production rollout.</li>
                    <li><strong>When designing multi-tenancy, decide the namespace + rule + policy shape</strong> (§32.4) BEFORE onboarding a second team, not after.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

