                    <tr><td><code>gitlab.com/project-id</code></td><td>GitLab project</td><td>GitLab</td><td><code>12345</code></td></tr>
                </tbody></table></div>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="sh-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">H.4 Template Actions — Quick Reference</h3>
                <p>Template actions do the actual work. The CBA expects you to know which action to use for each step in a template pipeline.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Action</th><th>What It Does</th><th>Pipeline Position</th><th>Key Input</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>fetch:template</code></td><td>Copy skeleton files + replace placeholders</td><td>Step 1 (always first)</td><td><code>url</code>, <code>values</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>fetch:plain</code></td><td>Copy files from URL (no placeholders)</td><td>Step 1 (alternative)</td><td><code>url</code>, <code>targetPath</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>fetch:cookiecutter</code></td><td>Execute Cookiecutter template</td><td>Step 1 (alternative)</td><td><code>url</code>, <code>values</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>publish:github</code></td><td>Create GitHub repo + push code</td><td>Step 2 (publish)</td><td><code>repoUrl</code>, <code>defaultBranch</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>publish:gitlab</code></td><td>Create GitLab repo + push code</td><td>Step 2 (alternative)</td><td><code>repoUrl</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>catalog:register</code></td><td>Register entity in Backstage Catalog</td><td>Step 3 (always last)</td><td><code>catalogInfoUrl</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>catalog:write</code></td><td>Write entity YAML to Catalog</td><td>Step 3 (alternative)</td><td><code>entity</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>fs:delete</code></td><td>Delete files during template run</td><td>Anywhere</td><td><code>files</code></td></tr>
                </tbody></table></div>
                <div class="cka-speed-tip"><h5>⚡ Speed Tip</h5><p class="mb-0">The 3-step pipeline is ALWAYS: <code>fetch:template</code> → <code>publish:github</code> → <code>catalog:register</code>. This order is fixed — you can't register before publishing, and you can't publish before fetching. The CBA will test this sequence.</p></div>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="sh-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">H.5 Permission Conditions — Quick Reference</h3>
                <p>The Backstage permission framework uses conditions to restrict access. Know these 6 conditions for the CBA.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Checks If...</th><th>CBA Scenario</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>IS_ENTITY_OWNER</code></td><td>User/Group is the entity's owner</td><td>"Team A should only see their own services"</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>IS_ENTITY_KIND</code></td><td>Entity is a specific kind</td><td>"Only Components should be visible, not APIs"</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>HAS_ANNOTATION</code></td><td>Entity has a specific annotation</td><td>"Only entities with pagerduty.com/service-id"</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>HAS_LABEL</code></td><td>Entity has a specific label</td><td>"Only entities labeled 'internal'"</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>HAS_SPEC</code></td><td>Entity has a specific spec field</td><td>"Only entities with spec.type: service"</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>HAS_TAG</code></td><td>Entity has a specific tag</td><td>"Only entities tagged 'production'"</td></tr>
                </tbody></table></div>
                <div class="cka-exam-tip"><h5>🎯 CBA Exam Tip — Permission Conditions Aren't a Rule Engine</h5><p class="mb-0">These 6 conditions are building blocks used <strong>inside</strong> a single <code>PermissionPolicy.handle()</code> function (Chapter 23) that returns exactly ONE decision — <code>ALLOW</code>, <code>DENY</code>, or <code>CONDITIONAL</code> — per request. Backstage does <strong>not</strong> ship separate auto-combining "Allow rules" and "Deny rules" where Deny automatically overrides Allow; any such precedence is logic YOU write inside that one function. Don't let a scenario question about "Allow read for all, Deny read for kind:API" imply a built-in rule engine — trace through what the policy function itself would return.</p></div>
            </section>
        </article>
